INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (1, 1, 'Anatomical Terminology', 1, '# Anatomical Terminology Summary

This section establishes anatomical terminology as a standardized system derived from ancient Greek and Latin to eliminate ambiguity in describing body structures, with anatomical terms composed of roots (referring to organs, tissues, or conditions) modified by prefixes and suffixes. The subsection on directional terms presents ten essential terms—anterior (ventral), posterior (dorsal), superior (cranial), inferior (caudal), lateral, medial, proximal, distal, superficial, and deep—that describe the relative locations of structures throughout the body. Body planes and sections are introduced as fundamental tools for anatomical interpretation, with sections defined as two-dimensional surfaces created by cutting through three-dimensional structures and planes as imaginary surfaces passing through the body; the three primary planes are the sagittal plane (dividing into right and left sides, including midsagittal/median and parasagittal variants), the frontal or coronal plane (dividing into anterior and posterior portions), and the transverse plane (dividing into upper and lower portions). The major body cavities are organized into the dorsal cavity (subdivided into the cranial cavity and spinal/vertebral cavity) and the ventral cavity (subdivided into the thoracic cavity and abdominopelvic cavity, the latter further distinguished into abdominal and pelvic cavities). The thoracic cavity houses the lungs and heart within the mediastinum and is separated from the abdominopelvic cavity by the diaphragm, while the abdominopelvic cavity contains digestive organs and reproductive organs. Serous membranes (serosa) consisting of parietal layers lining cavity walls and visceral layers covering organs enclose three serous cavities—the pleural cavity (surrounding lungs), the pericardial cavity (surrounding heart), and the peritoneal cavity (surrounding abdominal and pelvic organs)—and secrete serous fluid to');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (2, 1, 'Homeostasis', 2, 'The Homeostasis section explains how the body maintains stable internal conditions through set points, normal ranges, and feedback mechanisms. Negative feedback systems maintain homeostasis using three components: a sensor (receptor) that monitors physiological values, a control center that compares values to the set point, and an effector that reverses deviations to restore the normal range. Blood glucose control exemplifies negative feedback, with pancreatic beta cells detecting excess glucose and releasing insulin to signal skeletal muscle fibers, adipocytes, and liver cells to take up glucose, while pancreatic alpha cells subsequently detect decreased blood glucose and stop insulin release. Temperature regulation involves the brain''s heat-loss center (stimulating blood vessel dilation, sweat gland activation, and increased respiration) and heat-gain center (reducing skin blood flow, triggering shivering, and stimulating thyroid hormone and epinephrine release) to maintain the set point of approximately 37°C. In contrast, positive feedback intensifies physiological deviations and occurs only when a definite endpoint exists, as demonstrated in childbirth when cervical stretch-sensitive nerve cells trigger pituitary oxytocin release, which strengthens uterine contractions until the baby is born. Positive feedback also operates in blood clotting, where reduced blood perfusion triggers clotting protein release from the injured blood vessel wall, with each clotting step accelerating the cascade to seal the damaged area.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (3, 1, 'Medical Imaging', 3, 'The Medical Imaging section covers X-rays and modern medical imaging techniques used to visualize body structures and functions. X-rays, discovered by Wilhelm Röntgen in 1895, are high-energy electromagnetic radiation with short wavelength that penetrate solids and ionize gases; they are directed from an X-ray machine toward a metallic plate positioned behind the patient, producing images where soft tissues appear gray and hard tissues such as bone appear as light-toned shadows, making X-rays particularly useful for visualizing fractures, bones, and teeth despite their potential to damage cells and initiate cancerous changes. Modern medical imaging encompasses noninvasive techniques including Computed tomography (CT or CAT) scanners, which use cross-sectional X-rays to reveal soft tissue details in the brain and thoracic and abdominal viscera; Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), based on nuclear physics principles where matter exposed to magnetic fields and radio waves emits radio signals that differ between malignant and normal tissue; Functional MRI (fMRI), which detects blood flow concentration to study brain activity and abnormalities; Positron emission tomography (PET), which uses radiopharmaceuticals to illustrate physiologic activity including nutrient metabolism and blood flow; and Ultrasonography, which uses high-frequency sound waves converted into real-time images to study heart function, blood flow, and fetal growth and development. These modern techniques produce three-dimensional or functional images analyzed by computers, offering advantages over traditional X-rays while maintaining their clinical utility in medical practice.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (4, 1, 'Requirements for Human Life', 4, '# Requirements for Human Life

This section establishes the essential physiological requirements for human survival, beginning with three basic classes of nutrients: water (comprising approximately 70 percent of adult body mass and serving as the medium for chemical reactions), energy-yielding nutrients (carbohydrates and lipids converted to ATP, and proteins providing amino acids), and micronutrients (vitamins and minerals). Oxygen, comprising approximately 20 percent of atmospheric air, is identified as critical for ATP production, with brain cells being especially sensitive to oxygen deprivation. The section emphasizes that human survival requires body temperature to remain within a narrow range from just below to just above 37°C (98.6°F), as enzyme function and metabolism depend on this range; the body responds to temperature deviations through sweating, shivering, and altered blood circulation to extremities. Controlled hypothermia is introduced as a clinical application in which metabolic rate is reduced during open-heart surgery and cardiac arrest treatment by cooling the heart muscle below 15°C (60°F) using an external heart-lung pump. Atmospheric pressure is established as critical for maintaining dissolved gases in body fluids and enabling breathing through precise gas exchange of oxygen intake and carbon dioxide release; altitude sickness occurs when high-altitude atmospheric pressure is reduced. Finally, the section addresses decompression sickness (DCS or "the bends"), caused by rapid reduction in barometric pressure that causes dissolved nitrogen to form bubbles in blood and tissues, treatable through hyperbaric chamber repressurization; blood pressure is identified as a critical dynamic pressure of body fluids required to reach all tissues while remaining low enough for delicate blood vessels to withstand pulsating pressurized blood.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (5, 1, 'Structural Organization of the Human Body', 5, 'This subsection presents the hierarchical levels of structural organization in the human body, from simplest to most complex. The chemical level comprises subatomic particles (protons, electrons, neutrons), atoms, and molecules (water, proteins, sugars) that form the chemical building blocks of body structures. The cellular level includes cells with flexible membranes enclosing cytoplasm and organelles, where all physiologic functions are performed or initiated. Tissues represent groups of similar cells performing specific functions, while organs are anatomically distinct structures composed of two or more tissue types. Organ systems consist of groups of organs working together to perform major functions, with the textbook identifying eleven distinct organ systems in the human body. The organism level represents the highest level of organization—a living being with cellular structure capable of independently performing all physiologic functions necessary for life. In multicellular organisms including humans, all cells, tissues, organs, and organ systems work together to maintain the life and health of the organism.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (6, 2, 'Circulation and the Central Nervous System', 1, '# Circulation and the Central Nervous System

This section covers the specialized circulatory structures and protective mechanisms of the central nervous system, including blood supply, cerebrospinal fluid circulation, and protective membranes. The blood supply to the brain and spinal cord originates from the aorta via the common carotid arteries (internal and external branches entering through the carotid canal) and vertebral arteries (protected by transverse foramina and entering via the foramen magnum), which converge to form the circle of Willis to maintain cerebral perfusion; venous drainage occurs through dural sinuses (superior sagittal, transverse, and sigmoid sinuses) and jugular veins, with the orthostatic reflex maintaining blood pressure upon standing. The meninges—consisting of the dura mater (thick fibrous outer layer), arachnoid mater (thin middle layer with trabeculae), and pia mater (thin innermost membrane)—enclose the brain and spinal cord, with the subarachnoid space between the arachnoid and pia mater containing cerebrospinal fluid that provides cushioning and is reabsorbed via arachnoid granulations into dural sinuses. The ventricular system comprises four interconnected ventricles (lateral, third, and fourth ventricles) derived from the neural tube''s central canal, connected by the interventricular foramina, cerebral aqueduct, and apertures that open into the subarachnoid space, with cerebrospinal fluid produced by choroid plexuses circulating approximately 500 milliliters daily to remove metabolic wastes. Stroke—disruption of blood supply caused by an embolus—results in tissue death and loss of specific neurological functions depending on location, while meningitis, inflammation of the meninges caused by bacterial or viral pathogens, is diagnosed via lumbar puncture and treated with antib');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (7, 2, 'The Central Nervous System', 2, '# Summary: The Central Nervous System

The Central Nervous System section covers the major divisions and structures of the brain and spinal cord. The Cerebrum subsection describes the cerebral cortex and subcortical structures, including the cerebral hemispheres separated by the longitudinal fissure and connected by the corpus callosum, with gyri and sulci creating landmarks that delineate the frontal, parietal, temporal, and occipital lobes; Brodmann''s areas correlate specific cytoarchitecturally distinct regions with localized functions including visual perception, auditory sensation, somatosensory processing, motor control, and language production, while subcortical nuclei (basal nuclei, hippocampus, amygdala) augment cortical processes. The Brain Stem subsection explains how the midbrain, pons, and medulla form a tapering cone connecting the brain to the spinal cord, with the tectum and tegmentum separated by the cerebral aqueduct, colliculi integrating sensory information, and the reticular formation regulating sleep, wakefulness, and attention; cranial nerves and major ascending and descending pathways pass through the brain stem to regulate cardiovascular and respiratory systems. The Cerebellum subsection details how this "little brain" compares motor commands from the cerebrum with sensory feedback from the inferior olive nucleus to coordinate movement and correct discrepancies through midbrain projections to the spinal cord. The Myth of Left Brain/Right Brain subsection clarifies that while hemispheres show functional lateralization, functions are distributed globally, and introduces the diencephalon (thalamus, hypothalamus, epithalamus, subthalamus) as the relay center between the cerebrum and rest of the nervous system. The Spinal Cord subsection describes the neural tube structure divided into cervical, thoracic, lumbar, and sacral regions, with');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (8, 2, 'The Embryologic Perspective', 3, '# The Embryologic Perspective: Summary

This section explains how the nervous system develops from embryonic germ layers to establish the adult nervous system''s organization and structure. The ectoderm differentiates into neuroectoderm, which forms the neural plate that folds into the neural tube—the embryonic origin of the brain and spinal cord—while the neural crest separates laterally to form peripheral nervous system structures and non-nervous tissues. Beginning at 25 days of development, the anterior neural tube enlarges into three primary vesicles: the prosencephalon (forebrain), mesencephalon (midbrain), and rhombencephalon (hindbrain), establishing the basic divisions of the developing brain. These primary vesicles subsequently differentiate into five secondary vesicles: the telencephalon and diencephalon (from the prosencephalon), the mesencephalon (unchanged), and the metencephalon and myelencephalon (from the rhombencephalon), which give rise to the cerebrum, thalamus, hypothalamus, midbrain, pons, cerebellum, and medulla oblongata. The cephalic flexure reorients the neuraxis so the cerebrum occupies an anterior position, and embryonic origins explain key anatomical relationships, such as the cerebellum''s largest connection to the pons (both from the metencephalon) and the retina''s connection to the diencephalon via the optic tract and thalamus. The hollow center of the neural tube persists as the ventricles, and developmental defects such as spina bifida (failure of neural tube closure) result in varying degrees of neurological impairment, while white matter development continues into adolescence, with the brain completing development around 20 years of age.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (9, 3, 'Embryonic Development of the Axial Skeleton', 1, '# Summary: Embryonic Development of the Axial Skeleton

The axial skeleton develops from embryonic tissues including the notochord, neural tube, and somite-derived sclerotomes containing mesenchyme that differentiates into connective tissues, cartilages, and bones. Skull development occurs through two ossification mechanisms: intramembranous ossification produces brain case bones (top and sides) from direct mesenchymal differentiation, while endochondral ossification produces facial bones and the brain case floor from hyaline cartilage models. During fetal development, fontanelles—large areas of dense connective tissue at bone junctions such as between the frontal and parietal bones—separate skull bones and allow brain growth; these fontanelles decrease in size by age 2, while sutures containing dense fibrous connective tissue persist between bones. At birth, the brain case and orbits are disproportionally large compared to the underdeveloped maxilla, mandible, and small paranasal sinuses and nasal cavity; early childhood growth includes enlargement of the mastoid process, fusion of mandibular and frontal bone halves, expansion of paranasal sinuses and jaws, and rapid facial growth with tooth eruption. Vertebral development begins with mesenchyme accumulation from sclerotomes around the notochord, which forms hyaline cartilage models undergoing endochondral ossification; the notochord largely disappears except for persistent areas contributing to intervertebral disc formation. Ribs develop from vertebral cartilage models, separate by week eight, and ossify except for the anterior costal cartilage portion. The sternum develops from paired hyaline cartilage models beginning in week five; rib cartilages attach to the developing sternum, the sternal halves fuse and ossify with the manubrium and body converting to bone first while the xiphoid process remains cartil');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (10, 3, 'The Skull', 2, '# Summary: The Skull

This section on the skull establishes the sphenoid bone as the central keystone bone articulating with most other skull bones and introduces the major anatomical divisions and structures of the skull. The anterior view of the skull reveals the facial bones supporting the eyes and facial structures, with the orbit and nasal cavity as dominant features; the nasal septum, formed by the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid bone (upper) and vomer bone (lower), divides the nasal cavity into halves, while the nasal conchae project from lateral walls to warm and filter incoming air. The lateral view highlights the zygomatic arch, formed by the temporal process of the zygomatic bone and zygomatic process of the temporal bone, which separates the temporal and infratemporal fossae containing muscles of mastication. Skull sutures are immobile joints filled with dense fibrous connective tissue that interlock bones; major sutures include the coronal, sagittal, and lambdoid sutures, with the pterion representing the weakest region where four bones meet. The brain case comprises eight bones—paired parietal and temporal bones plus unpaired frontal, occipital, sphenoid, and ethmoid bones—that protect the brain within the cranial cavity, which is subdivided into anterior, middle, and posterior cranial fossae of increasing depth. The 14 facial bones form the upper and lower jaws, nose, nasal cavity, and orbit, with the maxilla forming the upper jaw and hard palate and the mandible serving as the only moveable skull bone, articulating with the temporal bone at the temporomandibular joint. The orbit is a cone-shaped socket formed by seven skull bones that houses the eyeball and contains muscles for eye movement, with the optic canal and superior orbital fissure providing passage for the optic nerve and associated structures. Developmental defects include cleft lip and cleft');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (11, 3, 'The Vertebral Column', 3, 'The Vertebral Column section covers the anatomical organization and functional characteristics of the spine across five regions: cervical (C1–C7), thoracic (T1–T12), lumbar (L1–L5), sacrum, and coccyx, totaling 24 individual vertebrae plus fused sacral and coccygeal segments in adults. The vertebral column exhibits four curvatures—two primary curves (thoracic and sacrococcygeal) retained from fetal development and two secondary curves (cervical and lumbar) that develop postnatally—which enhance strength, flexibility, and shock absorption, though excessive curvatures result in pathological conditions including kyphosis, lordosis, and scoliosis. A typical vertebra consists of an anterior weight-bearing body, a posterior vertebral arch composed of pedicles and laminae enclosing the vertebral foramen, and seven processes (paired transverse and articular processes plus a single spinous process) that serve as attachment sites and articulation points. Regional modifications of vertebrae reflect functional demands: cervical vertebrae possess transverse foramina and the atlas (C1) and axis (C2) enable skull articulation; thoracic vertebrae feature costal facets for rib attachment; lumbar vertebrae bear the greatest body weight with correspondingly large bodies; and the sacrum and coccyx form from fused vertebrae. Intervertebral discs, composed of a tough outer anulus fibrosus and gel-like nucleus pulposus, anchor adjacent vertebral bodies and provide cushioning, with herniation of the nucleus pulposus potentially compressing spinal nerves and causing pain. Supporting ligaments including the anterior and posterior longitudinal ligaments, supraspinous ligament, nuchal ligament, and ligamentum flavum stabilize the vertebral column and resist excessive motion in various directions.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (12, 4, 'Bone Formation and Development', 1, '# Bone Formation and Development

Bone Formation and Development encompasses four key processes that establish skeletal structure from fetal development through adolescence. Intramembranous ossification forms flat bones of the face, cranial bones, and clavicles directly from mesenchymal connective tissue sheets, with osteoblasts clustering at ossification centers to secrete osteoid that calcifies and traps cells as osteocytes, while the periosteum develops a protective compact bone layer superficial to trabecular bone containing red marrow. Endochondral ossification, occurring in skull base and long bones, replaces hyaline cartilage templates through invasion of blood vessels and osteogenic cells that transform the perichondrium into the periosteum and establish primary and secondary ossification centers, leaving cartilage only as articular cartilage and the epiphyseal plate. Longitudinal bone growth occurs at the epiphyseal plate, a hyaline cartilage layer with four functionally distinct zones—the reserve zone, proliferative zone, zone of maturation and hypertrophy, and zone of calcified matrix—where chondrocyte division and maturation drive growth until hormonal signals cease proliferation and the epiphyseal line remains. Bone remodeling is the continuous process in which osteoblasts deposit new bone where resorption of old or damaged bone occurs, with approximately 5–10 percent of the skeleton remodeled annually in response to injury, exercise, and other stimuli. Osteogenesis imperfecta (brittle bone disease) is a genetic disorder caused by mutations impairing collagen production in bone matrix, resulting in fragile bones prone to fractures, deformities, and systemic complications including fragile skin, weak muscles, loose joints, blue sclera, and hearing loss, with treatment focused on maximizing independence and mobility rather than cure.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (13, 4, 'Bone Structure', 2, '# Bone Structure Summary

The Bone Structure section comprehensively covers the anatomical organization and cellular composition of bone tissue. The Gross Anatomy of Bone subsection describes long bones, which consist of the diaphysis (containing a medullary cavity with yellow marrow bounded by compact bone), epiphysis (wider ends with spongy bone and red marrow), and metaphysis (containing the epiphyseal plate that ossifies into the epiphyseal line), along with the periosteum and endosteum that support bone nourishment and remodeling. Bone Markings introduces surface features classified as articulations (where bone surfaces meet at joints), projections (areas indicating attachment forces), and holes (openings for vessels and nerves), with specific examples including condyles, spinous processes, foramina, and sinuses. The Bone Cells and Tissue subsection identifies four cell types—osteoblasts (synthesizing matrix), osteocytes (maintaining mature bone in lacunae), osteogenic cells (undifferentiated precursors), and osteoclasts (performing bone resorption)—embedded in an extracellular matrix of collagen fibers and hydroxyapatite crystals that provide flexibility and hardness. Compact and Spongy Bone describes the histological distinction between dense compact bone organized into osteons (Haversian systems) with concentric lamellae and central canals, and spongy bone with trabeculae arranged along stress lines containing red marrow for hematopoiesis. The Blood and Nerve Supply subsection explains vascular and neural pathways entering through nutrient foramina and periosteal vessels, and introduces Paget''s disease as an osteoclast-mediated condition diagnosed through imaging and alkaline phosphatase levels, treatable with bisphosphonates.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (14, 4, 'Exercise, Nutrition, Hormones, and Bone Tissue', 3, 'This section examines the multifactorial regulation of bone tissue through mechanical, nutritional, and hormonal mechanisms. The Exercise and Bone Tissue subsection explains how mechanical stress stimulates deposition of mineral salts and collagen fibers, while lack of mechanical stress causes bone loss and atrophy; bone remodeling maintains ideal size and weight for activity level, with resistance training proving more effective than cardiovascular activities for preventing age-related bone loss and osteoporosis. The Nutrition and Bone Tissue subsection identifies essential nutrients including calcium, vitamin D, vitamin K, magnesium, fluoride, and omega-3 fatty acids, establishing that vitamin D is required for calcium absorption from the small intestine, fluoride stabilizes hydroxyapatite crystals by forming fluorapatite, and omega-3 fatty acids reduce inflammation to enhance osteoblast function. The Hormones and Bone Tissue subsection describes endocrine regulation through growth hormone (stimulating chondrocyte proliferation in epiphyseal plates and osteoblastic activity), thyroxine (promoting osteoblastic activity and bone matrix synthesis), sex hormones (promoting osteoblastic activity and conversion of epiphyseal plates to epiphyseal lines), and calcitriol (stimulating calcium and phosphate absorption). The Skeletal System subsection addresses osteoporosis as decreased bone mass when resorption exceeds formation, detailing the opposing actions of parathyroid hormone (stimulating osteoclast activity and calcium release) and calcitonin (inhibiting osteoclasts), the protective role of estrogen in osteoblastic activity, and prevention strategies including adequate childhood calcium and vitamin D intake plus weight-bearing exercise.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (15, 4, 'Fractures: Bone Repair', 4, '# Fractures: Bone Repair Section Summary

This section on bone fracture repair covers two primary subsections addressing fracture classification and the healing process. The Types of Fractures subsection categorizes eight fracture types—transverse, oblique, spiral, comminuted, impacted, greenstick, open/compound, and closed/simple—based on complexity, location, and structural features, noting that single fractures may exhibit multiple type characteristics simultaneously. Treatment approaches discussed include supportive devices (braces, canes, walkers, wheelchairs), immobilization methods (casts, splints, wraps), and surgical interventions (metal rod implantation), with smoking and being overweight identified as risk factors. The Bone Repair subsection describes the sequential healing process beginning with fracture hematoma formation from blood clotting in the periosteum, osteons, and medullary cavity within six to eight hours. Within 48 hours, chondrocytes from the endosteum and periosteum create internal and external calli composed of fibrocartilaginous matrix, hyaline cartilage, and bone, stabilizing the fracture. Over subsequent weeks, osteoclasts resorb dead bone while osteogenic cells differentiate into osteoblasts, and cartilage in the calli is replaced by trabecular bone through endochondral ossification. The internal and external calli unite, compact bone replaces spongy bone at fracture margins, and bone remodeling eventually eliminates external evidence of the fracture.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (16, 4, 'The Functions of the Skeletal System', 5, 'The skeletal system performs three primary gross functions: support, movement, and protection. Bones and cartilage form a scaffold that supports body weight and provide attachment points for muscles, which act as levers with joints serving as fulcrums to enable movement through muscle contraction spanning joints. The skeletal system protects vital internal organs, including the lungs and heart protected by the ribs, the spinal cord protected by the vertebral column, and the brain protected by the cranium. An orthopedist is a medical specialist who diagnoses and treats disorders and injuries of the musculoskeletal system. Common orthopedic conditions include scoliosis (lateral curvature of the spine), kyphosis (excessive dorsoventral curvature), and spina bifida (failure of the neural canal to close during fetal development). Age-related spinal curvatures may result from accumulated injuries or osteoporosis, conditions often managed by orthopedic specialists and sports medicine professionals.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (17, 5, 'Adjustments of the Infant at Birth and Postnatal Stages', 1, 'Chapter 28, Section "Adjustments of the Infant at Birth and Postnatal Stages" covers the physiological transitions newborns undergo immediately after birth across four major subsystems. The Respiratory Adjustments subsection describes how labor contractions constrict umbilical blood vessels, reducing oxygenated blood flow and elevating carbon dioxide levels to trigger the respiratory center in the brain, initiating the first breath within 10 seconds of birth; the first breaths inflate the lungs, decrease lung pressure, cause circulatory reconfiguration, and enable pulmonary alveoli and alveolar capillaries to assume the placenta''s role in gas exchange. The Circulatory Adjustments subsection explains how clamping the umbilical cord collapses umbilical blood vessels and causes the ductus venosus to degenerate into the ligamentum venosum, while the newborn''s first breath decreases pulmonary blood pressure and reverses blood flow through the foramen ovale, with tissue flaps fusing to form the fossa ovalis within one year; simultaneously, increased oxygen concentration causes the ductus arteriosus to constrict and become the ligamentum arteriosum. The Thermoregulatory Adjustments subsection addresses how newborns transition from the warm amniotic fluid (approximately 98.6°F) to a cooler external environment and compensate through nonshivering thermogenesis via breakdown of brown adipose tissue distributed over the back, chest, and shoulders, which contains specialized mitochondria that produce more heat than standard cellular respiration. The Homeostasis in the Newborn: Apgar Score subsection introduces the five-criterion assessment tool (skin color, heart rate, reflex, muscle tone, and respiration), scored 0–2 at 1 and 5 minutes after birth, with scores of 8 or above indicating normal status and');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (18, 5, 'Changes During Pregnancy, Labor, and Birth', 2, '# Summary: Changes During Pregnancy, Labor, and Birth

This section comprehensively covers the anatomical, hormonal, and physiological transformations occurring during pregnancy and parturition. The introduction establishes that fetal hypoxia triggers meconium passage in utero through vagus nerve stimulation of gastrointestinal peristalsis and anal sphincter relaxation, with meconium aspiration causing serious neonatal complications including airway obstruction, alveolar collapse, and increased infection risk. The hormonal subsection explains how estrogens, progesterone, hCG, and relaxin—produced initially by the corpus luteum then by the placenta—mediate uterine enlargement, fetal tissue maturation, suppression of ovulation, and pelvic ligament elasticity, while anterior pituitary hormones (thyrotropin, prolactin, ACTH) and parathyroid hormone support maternal metabolic demands and fetal calcium acquisition. Maternal weight gain (10–16.3 kg total) results from fetal mass, placental and membrane growth, amniotic fluid, breast tissue expansion, increased blood volume, fat storage, and uterine enlargement, requiring an additional 300 calories daily during the second and third trimesters. Organ system changes include decreased intestinal peristalsis and gastric reflux, urinary bladder compression, 30% blood volume increase, elevated pulse and blood pressure, varicose veins, 50% increase in respiratory minute volume, and integumentary changes (striae, linea nigra, chloasma). Labor physiology involves the rising estrogen-to-progesterone ratio increasing myometrial oxytocin receptor sensitivity, fetal cortisol boosting placental estrogen, and oxytocin and prostaglandins establishing a positive feedback loop that produces increasingly powerful contractions. Childbirth progresses through three stages: cervical');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (19, 5, 'Embryonic Development', 3, '# Embryonic Development Summary

This section covers the three major gestational periods of prenatal human development—the pre-embryonic stage (weeks 1–2), embryonic stage (weeks 3–8), and fetal stage (week 9 until birth—characterized by cell division, migration, and differentiation. Pre-implantation development begins with the conceptus transported via the Fallopian tube, undergoing rapid mitotic divisions to form blastomeres that organize into a morula and then a blastocyst containing an inner cell mass (future embryo) and outer trophoblasts (future chorionic sac and fetal placenta). The trophoblast degrades the zona pellucida, allowing the blastocyst to implant in the uterine wall by week 1, where syncytiotrophoblast cells digest endometrial tissue and the trophoblast secretes human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) to maintain progesterone and estrogen production. During week 2, extra-embryonic membranes form—the amnion (containing amniotic fluid for protection and movement), yolk sac (providing early nutrients and blood cells), allantois (contributing to the umbilical cord and urinary bladder), and chorion—while gastrulation in week 3 transforms the two-layered embryonic disc into three germ layers (ectoderm, mesoderm, endoderm) via the primitive streak. The placenta develops by weeks 14–16 from the decidua basalis (maternal portion) and syncytiotrophoblast, cytotrophoblast cells, and chorionic villi (embryonic portion), with the umbilical cord containing two umbilical arteries and one umbilical vein surrounded by Wharton''s jelly. The placenta performs nutrition, respiration, and endocrine functions through selective permeability: oxygen');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (20, 5, 'Fertilization', 4, '# Summary: Fertilization

The Fertilization section of Chapter 28 covers the complete process of sperm-oocyte union and zygote formation. The Transit of Sperm subsection describes how sperm navigate the female reproductive tract through the vagina, cervix, uterus, and uterine tubes while encountering barriers including cervical mucus, vaginal acidity, and uterine leukocytes that reduce millions of ejaculated sperm to thousands of viable contenders. This subsection introduces capacitation, a process in which female reproductive tract fluids enhance sperm motility, deplete cholesterol from the sperm head membrane, and enable lysosomal enzyme release necessary for oocyte penetration. The Contact Between Sperm and Oocyte subsection explains the anatomical structures involved—the secondary oocyte, corona radiata, zona pellucida, acrosome, and cortical granules—and describes how capacitated sperm are chemically attracted to the corona radiata, penetrate both protective layers, and trigger the acrosomal reaction to fuse with the oocyte plasma membrane. This subsection details two polyspermy-prevention mechanisms: the fast block (sodium ion permeability change causing membrane depolarization) and the slow block/cortical reaction (calcium ion influx triggering cortical granules to release zonal inhibiting proteins and form the fertilization membrane). The Zygote subsection describes completion of meiosis II, formation of the second polar body, and transformation of the oocyte into an ovum, followed by pronuclei decondensation, DNA replication, and fusion into a single diploid zygote; it also distinguishes dizygotic twins (from two fertilized eggs) from monozygotic twins (from zygote splitting). The In Vitro Fertilization subsection describes this assisted reproductive technology, including gonadotropin administration, ultrasound');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (21, 5, 'Fetal Development', 5, '# Fetal Development Summary

The Fetal Circulatory System section describes how the fetus maintains oxygenation and nutrient delivery through placental integration via the umbilical cord, featuring the umbilical vein (carrying oxygenated blood from placenta) and umbilical arteries (returning deoxygenated blood and wastes). Three critical shunts bypass immature fetal organs: the ductus venosus diverts blood from the umbilical vein around the liver to the inferior vena cava, the foramen ovale connects the right and left atria to bypass the pulmonary trunk, and the ductus arteriosus diverts blood from the pulmonary artery into the aorta. Blood flows from the umbilical vein through the inferior vena cava to the right atrium, then splits between the foramen ovale and right ventricle before reaching the pulmonary trunk and ductus arteriosus, ultimately distributing through coronary and carotid arteries and descending aorta to the lower body and umbilical arteries. The Other Organ Systems section tracks development from weeks 9–40 across multiple systems, including ossification and brain expansion, bone marrow assumption of erythrocyte production from the liver, and eye development with eyelid changes. Sensory organ development (weeks 13–16) encompasses eye convergence, blinking, sucking motions, and ear repositioning, while integumentary changes include lanugo coverage (weeks 17–20), vernix caseosa secretion, nail development, and subcutaneous fat deposition (weeks 31–birth). Additional developments include spinal cord axon myelination and lung surfactant production (weeks 21–30), testis descent into the scrotum in males (weeks 21–30), kidney development, and meconium accumulation in the intestines.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (22, 5, 'Lactation', 6, 'The Lactation section covers the hormonal regulation and anatomical mechanisms of milk production and delivery. Prolactin from the anterior pituitary establishes and maintains breast milk supply by mobilizing maternal micronutrients, while estrogen, progesterone, and placental hormones inhibit milk synthesis during pregnancy until placental expulsion removes this inhibition. Oxytocin from the posterior pituitary stimulates myoepithelial cells to contract, forcing milk from lactocytes in the alveoli through lactiferous ducts, lactiferous sinuses, and nipple pores in the let-down reflex triggered by sensory stimulation of the areola. Growth hormone, cortisol, parathyroid hormone, and insulin facilitate transport of maternal amino acids, fatty acids, glucose, and calcium into breast milk. Breast milk composition changes across lactation stages: colostrum (48–72 hours postpartum) is high in protein and immunoglobulins, transitional milk appears by postpartum day 3, and mature milk develops by postpartum day 10, with foremilk and hindmilk differing in lactose, protein, and fat content within single feedings. Breast milk''s laxative properties help expel meconium and clear bilirubin through bile excretion, preventing hyperbilirubinemia and jaundice in newborns, whereas cow''s milk is unsuitable for infants due to protein composition incompatible with the immature digestive system.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (23, 5, 'Patterns of Inheritance', 7, '# Patterns of Inheritance

This section on Patterns of Inheritance covers the fundamental mechanisms by which traits pass from parents to offspring through genes located on the 23 chromosome pairs (22 autosomes and sex chromosomes XX/XY). The subsection "From Genotype to Phenotype" establishes that genotype (complete genetic makeup) determines phenotype (expressed characteristics) through allele interactions—dominant alleles mask recessive alleles, while incomplete dominance and codominance allow alternative expression patterns—and that multiple genes commonly interact to produce single traits, with clinical applications in predicting risk for heritable genetic disorders. Mendel''s Theory of Inheritance, derived from mid-1800s garden pea crosses, demonstrates that traits transmit in discrete patterns through the principles of random segregation (parents pass one allele from each pair to haploid gametes with equal probability) and independent assortment (allele pairs sort independently during gamete formation), producing characteristic phenotypic ratios such as 3:1 in heterozygous crosses. Autosomal Dominant Inheritance describes disorders caused by a single dominant allele on the 22 autosomes (e.g., neurofibromatosis type I, achondroplastic dwarfism, Marfan syndrome, Huntington''s disease), where heterozygous parents have a 50 percent chance of passing the disorder to offspring. Autosomal Recessive Inheritance occurs when heterozygous carriers do not display symptoms because their unaffected gene compensates; examples include cystic fibrosis, sickle-cell anemia, Tay–Sachs disease, and phenylketonuria. X-linked Dominant or Recessive Inheritance involves genes on the X chromosome of the 23rd pair, with X-linked recessive inheritance (color blindness, hemophilia, muscular dystrophy) more common, allowing females to');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (24, 6, 'Acid-Base Balance', 1, '# Acid-Base Balance Summary

The Acid-Base Balance section covers three major regulatory mechanisms that maintain blood pH through complementary processes operating at different timescales. The respiratory regulation subsection explains how the lungs control blood carbonic acid levels by modulating CO2 exhalation in response to chemoreceptors (peripheral sensors in the aorta and carotid arteries; central sensors in the brain) that signal the respiratory center in the medulla oblongata to adjust breathing rate based on CO2 and pH changes, with hyperventilation causing alkalosis and hypoventilation causing hypercapnia. The buffer systems subsection identifies three major buffer systems—protein buffers (accounting for two-thirds of blood buffering power and operating predominantly intracellularly), phosphate buffers, and the bicarbonate-carbonic acid buffer system (maintaining a 20:1 ratio in normal blood)—which operate at different rates from seconds for chemical buffering to minutes for respiratory adjustment to hours or days for renal adjustment, with hemoglobin playing a key role in buffering hydrogen ions during CO2 conversion to bicarbonate. The renal regulation subsection describes how renal tubule cells conserve bicarbonate through a four-step mechanism involving Na+ and H+ antiport at apical membranes, carbonic anhydrase-driven formation of carbonic acid from CO2, and HCO3− reabsorption into peritubular capillaries, while also explaining how decreased blood bicarbonate results from diuretics, diarrhea, Addison''s disease, chronic nephritis, or uncontrolled diabetes mellitus, and how potassium and chloride levels influence H+ secretion and bicarbonate conservation.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (25, 6, 'Body Fluids and Fluid Compartments', 2, 'This section on Body Fluids and Fluid Compartments covers the fundamental principles and structures governing fluid distribution and movement throughout the body. The introduction establishes key concepts including solutes (proteins, carbohydrates, and electrolytes such as sodium ions [Na+] and chloride ions [Cl−]), osmosis across semi-permeable membranes, and the critical importance of solute balance for normal cellular function. The section identifies two major fluid compartments—intracellular fluid (ICF), comprising approximately 60 percent of total body water and enclosed by plasma membranes, and extracellular fluid (ECF), comprising one-third of body water with constituents including plasma, interstitial fluid (IF), cerebrospinal fluid, lymph, synovial fluid, pleural fluid, pericardial fluid, peritoneal fluid, and aqueous humor. Composition varies significantly across compartments: ECF (plasma and IF) contains high sodium, chloride, and bicarbonate, while ICF contains elevated potassium, phosphate, and magnesium; sodium-potassium pumps maintain these concentration gradients using ATP energy. Solute movement between compartments occurs through active transport (requiring ATP and moving against concentration gradients), passive transport (moving down concentration gradients), and facilitated transport (using protein channels for polar molecules like glucose and ions), with water moving via osmosis through aquaporins. Fluid movement between plasma, IF, and ICF is driven by hydrostatic pressure (capillary blood pressure generated by heart pumping) and osmotic pressure (produced by solute concentration differences), with the lymphatic system draining excess interstitial fluid. Edema—the pathological accumulation of excess water in tissues—results from water leakage due to medical conditions, injury, or altered pressure gradients, with pulmonary edema representing a serious complication of heart and kidney failure that compromises gas exchange.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (26, 6, 'Disorders of Acid-Base Balance', 3, '# Disorders of Acid-Base Balance

This section classifies acid-base disorders into metabolic and respiratory categories, explaining how both acidosis and alkalosis result from disturbances in CO₂ levels, carbonic acid concentration, and bicarbonate reabsorption by the renal system. Ketoacidosis (diabetic acidosis) occurs when tissues break down fatty acids to form ketone bodies—acetoacetic acid, beta-hydroxybutyric acid, and acetone—causing increased blood acidity, deep rapid breathing, fruity-smelling breath, and potentially fatal diabetic coma if untreated. Metabolic alkalosis results from primary bicarbonate excess due to ingestion of excessive bicarbonate or antacids, Cushing''s disease (chronic ACTH hypersecretion elevating aldosterone and potassium loss), loss of hydrochloric acid through vomiting, or diuretic use. Metabolic acidosis is defined by blood pH below 7.35 from primary bicarbonate deficiency disrupting the normal 20:1 bicarbonate-to-carbonic acid buffer ratio, with causes including diarrhea, uremia, diabetic ketoacidosis, strenuous exercise, and toxic ingestions (methanol, ethylene glycol, salicylate). Respiratory acidosis (excess carbonic acid and CO₂ from pneumonia, emphysema, or congestive heart failure) and respiratory alkalosis (deficiency in carbonic acid and CO₂ from hyperventilation caused by emotional upset, fever, infections, or aspirin overdose) are also covered. Compensation mechanisms maintain blood pH within 7.35–7.45 through respiratory compensation (adjusting CO₂ exhalation to readjust the bicarbonate-to-carbonic acid ratio) and renal compensation (conserving bicarbonate, secreting H⁺');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (27, 6, 'Electrolyte Balance', 4, '# Electrolyte Balance Summary

The Electrolyte Balance section covers the hormonal regulation and physiological roles of key electrolytes essential for homeostasis. The Regulation of Sodium and Potassium subsection explains how aldosterone and angiotensin II control sodium reabsorption and potassium excretion in the distal tubule and collecting ducts via the sodium-potassium pump, with aldosterone release triggered by increased blood potassium, decreased blood sodium, or decreased blood pressure, while angiotensin II causes vasoconstriction and increases glomerular filtration rate and aldosterone secretion from the adrenal cortex; a negative feedback loop operates when increased extracellular fluid osmolality inhibits further aldosterone release. The Roles of Electrolytes subsection details six critical electrolytes: sodium, the major extracellular cation responsible for half the osmotic pressure gradient between intracellular and extracellular fluid, freely filtered and reabsorbed in the proximal convoluted tubule; potassium, the major intracellular cation establishing resting membrane potential, maintained by sodium-potassium pumps and excreted via the distal convoluted tubule and collecting ducts under aldosterone control; chloride, the predominant extracellular anion contributing to osmotic pressure and electrical neutrality; bicarbonate, the second most abundant blood anion produced from CO2 via carbonic anhydrase in red blood cells and functioning in acid-base buffering; and calcium and phosphate, which serve as mineral reserves in bones and teeth while supporting muscle contraction, enzyme activity, blood coagulation, neurotransmitter release, and hormone secretion.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (28, 6, 'Water Balance', 5, '# Water Balance

This section covers water balance regulation through multiple integrated mechanisms involving the kidneys, hypothalamus, and hormonal systems. The introduction establishes that approximately 2500 mL of water leaves the body daily through urine (primary route), skin via insensible water loss through evaporation, and lungs through expired air, with the kidneys regulating water levels by adjusting filtrate and urine composition. Water intake regulation involves osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus''s thirst center monitoring plasma osmolality; when osmolality increases, the hypothalamus triggers thirst awareness and releases antidiuretic hormone (ADH) from the posterior pituitary gland to promote water reabsorption in the kidneys. The sympathetic nervous system decreases serous salivary output and increases mucus output to reinforce thirst sensation, while baroreceptors in the aortic arch and carotid arteries detect reduced blood pressure from water loss and activate the renin-angiotensin hormonal system, which produces angiotensin II to stimulate thirst and trigger aldosterone release from the adrenal glands, promoting sodium and water reabsorption in the distal tubules. ADH (vasopressin) controls water reabsorption from collecting ducts and tubules by signaling epithelial cells to move aquaporins (water channel proteins) to cell membranes, increasing water permeability; when plasma concentration decreases, ADH levels decline and aquaporins are removed, reducing water reabsorption. Diuretics—compounds including alcohol and caffeine that increase urine output—are used clinically to treat hypertension, congestive heart failure, and fluid retention by inhibiting ADH release or function.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (29, 7, 'Anatomy of Selected Synovial Joints', 1, '# Anatomy of Selected Synovial Joints

This section surveys six major synovial joint types classified by axes of motion: pivot joints (atlantoaxial, proximal radioulnar), hinge joints (knee, elbow, ankle, interphalangeal), condyloid joints (metacarpophalangeal, radiocarpal), saddle joints (first carpometacarpal, sternoclavicular), plane joints (intertarsal, vertebral articular processes), and ball-and-socket joints (shoulder, hip), with ligaments and muscles restricting range of motion. The vertebral column contains zygapophysial (facet) joints formed between articular processes of adjacent vertebrae, with regional variations: cervical vertebrae enable flexion, extension, lateral flexion, and rotation; thoracic vertebrae allow greatest rotation but are limited by spinous processes and thoracic cage; lumbar vertebrae permit extension, flexion, and lateral flexion but prohibit rotation. The atlantoaxial joint comprises paired articulations between the axis''s superior articular processes and atlas''s inferior articular processes, plus a pivot joint between the dens and anterior arch of the atlas, allowing head rotation. The temporomandibular joint (TMJ), formed between the mandibular fossa and articular tubercle of the temporal bone with the mandibular condyle, contains an articular disc that enables mandibular depression, elevation, and protraction/retraction; TMJ dislocation and temporomandibular joint disorder represent common clinical concerns. The shoulder (glenohumeral) joint, a ball-and-socket articulation between the humeral head and shallow glenoid cavity deepened by the glenoid labrum, possesses the greatest range of motion but minimal ligament support, relying instead on four rotator cuff muscles (supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, subscapularis) as "dynamic lig');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (30, 7, 'Classification of Joints', 2, 'The Classification of Joints section presents two complementary organizational systems for categorizing joints based on structural composition and functional capacity. Structural classification divides joints into fibrous, cartilaginous, and synovial types based on the connective tissue anchoring adjacent bones or the presence of a fluid-filled joint cavity. Functional classification categorizes joints as synarthroses (immobile), amphiarthroses (slightly moveable), or diarthroses (freely moveable) according to the degree of mobility between bones. Synarthroses, exemplified by skull sutures and the manubriosternal joint, provide strong unions for protection and stability. Amphiarthroses, including intervertebral joints united by fibrocartilage discs and the pubic symphysis, permit limited mobility while maintaining structural support. Diarthroses are freely moveable synovial joints subdivided by axes of motion: uniaxial joints (such as the elbow) allow movement in one plane, biaxial joints (such as metacarpophalangeal joints) allow movement in two planes, and multiaxial joints (such as the shoulder and hip joints) allow movement in all three anatomical planes including anterior-posterior, medial-lateral, and rotational movements. The text establishes that joint mobility correlates with functional requirements, with immobile and slightly mobile joints prioritizing protection and stability while freely moveable joints enable extensive body and limb movements.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (31, 7, 'Synovial Joints', 3, 'Synovial joints are defined by a joint cavity bounded by the articular capsule, with bones articulating on surfaces covered by articular cartilage and lubricated by synovial fluid secreted by the synovial membrane. Ligaments—classified as extrinsic, intrinsic, or intracapsular—connect bones and limit excessive motion, while tendons and muscles crossing the joint provide dynamic support, functioning as "dynamic ligaments" particularly at joints with weak ligaments such as the shoulder. Additional supportive structures include articular discs and menisci (fibrocartilage structures providing shock absorption and cushioning at joints like the knee and temporomandibular joint), bursae (thin connective tissue sacs classified as subcutaneous, submuscular, or subtendinous that reduce friction), and tendon sheaths surrounding tendons with lubricating fluid. Bursitis—inflammation of a bursa causing pain, swelling, and stiffness—occurs commonly at the shoulder, hip, knee, or elbow from muscle overuse, trauma, or infection, and is treated with NSAIDs, corticosteroids, or fluid drainage. Synovial joints are structurally classified into six types based on articulating bone surface shapes: pivot joints (atlantoaxial, proximal radioulnar) allowing uniaxial rotation; hinge joints (elbow, knee, ankle) permitting uniaxial bending; condyloid joints (metacarpophalangeal, radiocarpal) enabling biaxial anterior-posterior and side-to-side movements; saddle joints (first carpometacarpal, sternoclavicular) with concave-convex surfaces allowing biaxial thumb opposition; plane joints (intercarpal, intertarsal, acromioclavicular) functionally limited by ligaments; and ball-and-socket joints (hip, glenohumeral shoulder) permitting');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (32, 7, 'Types of Body Movements', 4, '# Types of Body Movements

This section covers fundamental body movements and their anatomical bases, beginning with anatomical position as the reference standard for describing movement, including key ankle movements of dorsiflexion and plantarflexion. Flexion and extension are primary sagittal plane movements occurring at the vertebral column (intervertebral discs and articular processes), shoulder, elbow, wrist, metacarpophalangeal and interphalangeal joints, hip, and knee, with hyperextension and hyperflexion representing abnormal excessive movements at hinge joints and in the cervical region. Abduction and adduction are coronal plane movements at condyloid, saddle, and ball-and-socket joints, with circumduction representing circular sequential combination of these movements, while rotation occurs at the atlantoaxial joint (atlas rotating around the dens of the axis), proximal radioulnar joint, and multiaxial shoulder and hip joints, producing medial and lateral rotation of the humerus and femur. Supination and pronation are forearm movements between anatomical (supinated) and pronated positions, produced by radius rotation at the proximal radioulnar joint (a pivot joint) accompanied by distal radioulnar joint movement. Superior and inferior rotation are scapular movements defined by glenoid cavity direction, with superior rotation required for full upper limb abduction to prevent the greater tubercle of the humerus from contacting the acromion. Opposition and reposition are thumb-specific movements at the first carpometacarpal saddle joint (formed between the trapezium and first metacarpal bone), with opposition combining flexion and abduction to bring the thumb tip into contact with a finger tip.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (33, 8, 'Carbohydrate Metabolism', 1, '# Summary: Carbohydrate Metabolism

Carbohydrate metabolism encompasses the integrated pathways of glycolysis, the Krebs cycle, and oxidative phosphorylation that convert glucose into usable cellular energy. Glycolysis, occurring in the cytoplasm, oxidizes glucose through sequential enzyme-catalyzed reactions—including hexokinase, phosphofructokinase-1, and aldolase—to produce pyruvate, yielding a net gain of two ATP and two NADH per glucose molecule. In the presence of oxygen, pyruvate enters the mitochondrial matrix where pyruvate dehydrogenase converts it to acetyl CoA, which enters the Krebs cycle (citric acid cycle/tricarboxylic acid cycle) and combines with oxaloacetate via citrate synthase to form citrate. The Krebs cycle proceeds through sequential enzymatic conversions catalyzed by aconitase, isocitrate dehydrogenase, α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, succinyl CoA dehydrogenase, succinate dehydrogenase, fumarase, and malate dehydrogenase, generating three NADH, one ATP (via GTP), and one FADH2 per cycle turn. NADH and FADH2 subsequently enter the electron transport chain in the inner mitochondrial membrane, where four enzyme complexes and coenzymes (ubiquinone and Cytochrome c) transfer electrons and pump H+ ions to create a proton gradient, with molecular oxygen serving as the terminal electron acceptor to form water. ATP synthase harnesses the H+ gradient to phosphorylate ADP and produce approximately 36 ATP per glucose molecule in aerobic respiration. When oxygen is limited, pyruvate enters fermentation and is converted to lactic acid; additionally, gluconeogenesis synthesizes new glucose');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (34, 8, 'Energy and Heat Balance', 2, 'This section on Energy and Heat Balance covers thermoregulation and the mechanisms by which the body maintains core body temperature. The hypothalamus functions as the master thermoregulatory control center, initiating heat dissipation through increased blood circulation to skin and sweating when core temperature exceeds set point, and generating heat through shivering and increased energy use when temperature falls below set point. Thyroid hormone acts as a key regulator that stimulates increased energy use and heat production by cells throughout the body. The thermoneutral environment, approximately 84 °F for a naked human, represents the condition where the body expends no energy to maintain core temperature. The body employs four mechanisms of heat exchange to maintain homeostasis: conduction (approximately 3% of heat loss), the direct transfer of heat between skin and objects in contact; convection (approximately 15% of heat loss), the transfer of heat to surrounding air or water; radiation (approximately 60% of heat loss), the transfer of heat via infrared waves to the environment; and evaporation (approximately 20% of heat loss at rest), the transfer of heat through sweat evaporation from skin, with evaporation rate dependent on relative humidity. All four heat exchange mechanisms operate according to the principle that heat flows from higher to lower concentration and vary in rate based on environmental temperature and conditions.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (35, 8, 'Lipid Metabolism', 3, 'This section on Lipid Metabolism covers the complete pathway of lipid digestion, transport, and utilization in the body. Pancreatic lipases and bile salts break down ingested triglycerides in the small intestine, with cholecystokinin (CCK) regulating pancreatic lipase release and gallbladder contraction; the resulting chylomicrons (phospholipid vesicles containing triglycerides and cholesterol) are formed in enterocytes and transported via lacteals into the lymphatic and circulatory systems to reach the liver or adipocytes. Lipolysis hydrolyzes triglycerides into fatty acids and glycerol in the cytoplasm, while β-oxidation converts fatty acids into acetyl CoA in the mitochondrial matrix for energy production through the Krebs cycle. When excess acetyl CoA accumulates, ketone body synthesis occurs, producing β-hydroxybutyrate, acetoacetate, and acetone; β-hydroxybutyrate is the primary ketone body oxidized back to acetoacetate and acetyl CoA for use by the brain and other tissues when glucose is limited. Lipogenesis converts excess acetyl CoA into fatty acids, triglycerides, cholesterol, steroids, and bile salts in adipocytes and hepatocytes through the citrate-malate shuttle mechanism, where pyruvate carboxylase and pyruvate dehydrogenase generate citrate that crosses the mitochondrial membrane and is reconverted to acetyl CoA and malonyl CoA in the cytoplasm for fatty acid synthesis. The section also explains that metabolic rate decreases approximately 2% per decade after age 30 due to reduced lean muscle mass, with the most dramatic decline occurring between ages 50–70, and notes that excessive ketone production can cause diabetic ketoacidosis and produce acetone, which is removed');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (36, 8, 'Metabolic States of the Body', 4, 'The Metabolic States of the Body section examines two distinct physiological conditions: the absorptive (fed) state and the postabsorptive (fasting) state. During the absorptive state, anabolism exceeds catabolism as nutrients are digested and absorbed; carbohydrates, proteins, and fats are processed through the digestive tract, with sugars and amino acids entering the bloodstream and fats entering the lymphatic system for transport to the liver, adipose tissue, and muscle cells. Rising blood glucose stimulates pancreatic beta cells to release insulin, which promotes glucose uptake by hepatocytes, adipose cells, and muscle cells through glucose-6-phosphate formation, while insulin simultaneously stimulates glycogen storage and protein synthesis. The section addresses phenylketonuria (PKU), a metabolic disorder caused by deficient phenylalanine hydroxylase enzyme activity that prevents conversion of phenylalanine to tyrosine, resulting in toxic accumulation and neurological damage; treatment includes dietary modification, vitamin supplementation, and gene therapy. The postabsorptive state occurs when stored nutrients must sustain the body, with pancreatic alpha cells releasing glucagon as blood glucose drops below 80–120 mg/dL, triggering glycogen breakdown in liver cells and inhibition of glycogen synthesis. During prolonged fasting, gluconeogenesis in the liver replaces glucose while muscles switch to fatty acid metabolism, exporting pyruvate, lactate, and alanine to the liver for glucose synthesis. After several days of starvation, ketone bodies derived from fatty acids and triglyceride stores become the major fuel source for the heart and other organs, preserving protein until fat stores deplete and muscle proteins are finally broken down for gluconeogenesis.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (37, 8, 'Nutrition and Diet', 5, 'The "Nutrition and Diet" section of Chapter 24 covers caloric measurement and energy balance, establishing that the nutritional Calorie measures heat energy and that daily caloric needs (1500–2000 calories) depend on body mass, age, height, gender, activity level, and exercise frequency. The subsection introduces MyPlate dietary guidelines, which categorize foods into six groups (fruits, vegetables, grains, protein foods, dairy, and oils), and explains that fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K) are absorbed with lipids in chylomicrons and can accumulate in body lipid stores, while water-soluble vitamins (B vitamins and vitamin C) are absorbed with water and excreted in urine without storage. Obesity is defined by body mass index (BMI), with healthy BMI at 18–24.9 kg/m², overweight at 25–29.9 kg/m², and obesity at >30 kg/m², and arises from overeating, poor diet, sedentary lifestyle, limited sleep, genetic factors, and diseases or drugs. The section details fat-soluble and water-soluble vitamins with their sources, recommended daily allowances, and functions such as vitamin A in eye and bone development, vitamin D in calcium absorption, and vitamin B1 in carbohydrate metabolism, along with deficiency conditions including night blindness, rickets, and pernicious anemia. Major minerals (potassium, sodium, calcium, phosphorus, magnesium, chloride) and trace minerals (iron, zinc, copper, iodine, sulfur, fluoride, manganese, cobalt, selenium, chromium, molybdenum) are presented with their sources, recommended daily allowances, and critical functions in bone structure, hemoglobin formation, and electrolyte balance. Obesity complications include coronary heart disease, type 2 diabetes,');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (38, 8, 'Overview of Metabolic Reactions', 6, '# Overview of Metabolic Reactions

This section covers the fundamental metabolic processes that sustain life, beginning with catabolic and anabolic reactions and their complementary roles in energy management. Catabolic reactions break down large organic molecules (carbohydrates, lipids, proteins, and nucleic acids) into smaller molecules, transferring approximately 40% of released chemical bond energy to adenosine triphosphate (ATP)—the energy currency of cells consisting of adenine, ribose, and three phosphate groups—while releasing 60% as heat; ATP is continuously cycled through energy-releasing and energy-building reactions as catabolic enzymes cleave the high-energy bond between the second and third phosphate groups to produce adenosine diphosphate (ADP) and phosphate (Pi). Glucose serves as the primary monosaccharide for ATP production and is regulated by endocrine control mechanisms, with excess glucose stored as glycogen in the liver and skeletal muscles or converted to triglyceride in adipocytes; triglycerides undergo β-oxidation for energy, while proteins are broken down into amino acids for use as building blocks or further catabolized for ATP production. Hormonal regulation of metabolism involves catabolic hormones (cortisol, glucagon, adrenaline/epinephrine, and cytokines) that stimulate molecular breakdown and energy production, and anabolic hormones (growth hormone, insulin-like growth factor, insulin, testosterone, and estrogen) that promote molecular synthesis. The section concludes by examining metabolic diseases caused by hormonal imbalances, specifically Cushing syndrome (excessive cortisol production from the adrenal gland or pituitary tumors producing adrenocorticotropic hormone) and Addison''s disease (adrenal insufficiency characterized by reduced cortisol production), demonstrating how disruptions in metabolic regulation produce significant physiological consequences.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (39, 8, 'Protein Metabolism', 7, 'Protein Metabolism encompasses the digestion, absorption, and utilization of proteins throughout the body. Protein digestion begins in the stomach, where pepsin and hydrochloric acid denature proteins at pH 1.5–3.5, and continues in the small intestine following the release of sodium bicarbonate, proteases (trypsin, chymotrypsin, elastase), and digestive hormones (secretin, CCK) from the pancreas. Enterokinase activates trypsinogen to trypsin, which then activates chymotrypsinogen to chymotrypsin, enabling proteolysis to break large proteins into smaller peptides. Amino acids are transported across the intestinal mucosa via sodium-amino acid transporters and delivered to the liver and body cells for protein synthesis and structural/functional roles in collagen, hemoglobin, and enzymes. The Urea Cycle, occurring primarily in the liver and secondarily in the kidney, converts ammonium ions produced from amino acid breakdown through transamination into urea and water, preventing toxic accumulation of ammonium and enabling nitrogen excretion through urine. Excess amino acids cannot be stored and are instead converted into glucose or ketones through metabolic intermediates including pyruvate, acetyl CoA, acetoacyl CoA, oxaloacetate, and α-ketoglutarate, particularly during starvation, allowing amino acids to serve as an energy source through the Krebs cycle.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (40, 9, 'Exercise and Muscle Performance', 1, '# Exercise and Muscle Performance

This section examines how different exercise types induce specific physiological adaptations in muscle tissue. Endurance exercise primarily recruits slow-twitch (SO) fibers and stimulates aerobic metabolism through increased mitochondria production, myoglobin accumulation in the sarcoplasm, and angiogenesis to enhance oxygen delivery and waste removal, with postural muscles serving as examples of tissues naturally rich in SO fibers. Resistance exercise recruits fast glycolytic (FG) fibers to generate powerful contractions through high ATP hydrolysis and cross-bridge formation rates, causing muscle hypertrophy via increased myofibril and sarcomere formation that requires progressive intensity increases and dietary protein supplementation. Unlike endurance training, resistance training increases connective tissue development and tendon strength to contain powerful contractions and prevent tendon damage during force transfer to bone, though cellular damage to the sarcolemma and myofibrils contributes to post-exercise soreness during the repair and mass-building phase. Both exercise types carry risks of overuse injuries including stress fractures and tendon inflammation if training is excessive or recovery is inadequate. The section also addresses performance-enhancing substances—including anabolic steroids, erythropoietin (EPO), human growth hormone (hGH), and creatine—that athletes use to increase muscle mass, power output, oxygen availability for aerobic respiration, and ATP production, though most are banned by governing bodies and carry serious health risks including infertility, cardiovascular disease, and potentially fatal physiological strain.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (41, 9, 'Muscle Fiber Contraction and Relaxation', 2, '# Summary: Muscle Fiber Contraction and Relaxation

This section explains the molecular mechanisms of muscle contraction and relaxation, centered on calcium ion signaling, the sliding filament model, ATP availability, and ATP regeneration. Key structures include the sarcoplasmic reticulum, T-tubules, sarcolemma, sarcomeres, myofibrils, thick filaments (anchored at the M-line), thin filaments (anchored at Z-discs), myosin heads, actin filaments, troponin, and tropomyosin. The sliding filament model describes how Ca²⁺ binds to troponin, causing tropomyosin to expose myosin-binding sites on actin, allowing myosin heads to form cross-bridges and pull thin filaments toward the sarcomere center. The cross-bridge cycle—consisting of myosin head attachment, inorganic phosphate release, the power stroke (~10 nm movement), and ATP-dependent re-cocking—enables repeated thin filament sliding; ATP binding causes myosin head detachment, while ATP hydrolysis re-cocks the myosin head into a high-energy configuration. Muscle contraction requires continuous ATP regeneration through three mechanisms: creatine phosphate metabolism (powering the first ~15 seconds), anaerobic glycolysis (providing ATP for ~1 minute), and aerobic respiration in mitochondria (generating ~95% of ATP in resting or moderately active muscles). Contraction ceases when motor neuron signaling ends, allowing Ca²⁺ to be pumped back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum and tropomyosin to reshield actin-binding sites; muscle fatigue occurs when ATP reserves decline, lactic acid accumulates, or Na⁺/K⁺ imbalances disrupt calcium flow.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (42, 9, 'Nervous System Control of Muscle Tension', 3, 'This section on Nervous System Control of Muscle Tension covers the mechanisms by which the nervous system regulates skeletal muscle contraction force and types. The introduction establishes three contraction types—concentric (shortening muscle to move load), eccentric (lengthening muscle with diminished tension), and isometric (producing tension without changing joint angle)—and introduces motor units as the fundamental control unit, consisting of a motor neuron and all skeletal muscle fibers it innervates; the all-or-none principle dictates that muscle fibers in a motor unit contract together or not at all. The Motor Units subsection explains that small motor units (in extraocular and hand muscles) contain lower-threshold motor neurons for fine control, while large motor units (in thigh and back muscles) contain higher-threshold motor neurons for gross movements, and that recruitment—progressive activation of additional motor units—allows the nervous system to generate forces from minimal to maximal while preventing fatigue through alternating motor unit activity. The Length-Tension Range of a Sarcomere subsection describes the length-tension relationship, establishing that maximal tension occurs at 80–120 percent of resting sarcomere length with optimal myosin head and actin-binding site overlap, and that tension decreases when sarcomeres are stretched beyond 120 percent or shortened beyond 80 percent due to insufficient filament overlap. Muscle Tone explains how cyclical motor unit activation maintains low-level sustained contraction for joint stabilization and posture, with hypotonia (flaccid appearance from cerebellum or innervation damage) and hypertonia (excessive tone from upper motor neuron damage, presenting as rigidity or spasticity) representing pathological deviations. The Frequency of Motor Neuron Stimulation subsection details how stimulation frequency controls tension through the twitch (latent, contraction, and relaxation phases), wave summation, incomplete tetanus, complete tetanus, and');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (43, 9, 'Skeletal Muscle', 4, '# Skeletal Muscle Section Summary

The skeletal muscle section covers the structural and functional organization of skeletal muscle tissue, beginning with the sarcomere as the functional contractile unit composed of actin and myosin microfilaments organized between Z-discs, along with regulatory proteins troponin and tropomyosin that control myosin-actin interactions. Skeletal muscle fibers are organized within three connective tissue layers called mysia: the epimysium (outer dense, irregular connective tissue), perimysium (organizing fibers into fascicles), and endomysium (encasing individual fibers), which collectively transfer contractile force through tendons to the periosteum for skeletal movement. The section describes how somatic motor neurons are the exclusive mechanism for signaling skeletal muscle contraction, with axon branches innervating individual muscle fibers and enabling targeted, voluntary movements while also stabilizing joints and protecting internal organs. Excitation-contraction coupling is detailed as the process initiated when motor neurons transmit action potentials to the neuromuscular junction, where acetylcholine binds to ACh receptors on the motor end-plate, causing sarcolemmal depolarization and opening of voltage-gated sodium channels. The propagating action potential travels along the sarcolemma and down T-tubules, which form triads with sarcoplasmic reticulum membranes surrounding myofibrils, triggering calcium channel opening and Ca²⁺ release into the sarcoplasm. Calcium ions interact with the troponin-tropomyosin complex on thin filaments, exposing actin-binding sites that allow myosin heads to attach and pull actin toward the sarcomere center, shortening the muscle fiber and generating contractile force while producing heat for homeostatic regulation.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (44, 10, 'Bones of the Lower Limb', 1, '# SECTION SUMMARY: Bones of the Lower Limb

The lower limb contains 30 bones organized into seven categories: the femur, patella, tibia, fibula, tarsal bones, metatarsal bones, and phalanges. The femur, the longest and strongest bone of the body, articulates with the acetabulum at the hip joint and features the greater trochanter, lesser trochanter, linea aspera, and distal condyles that form the knee joint with the tibia. The patella, the largest sesamoid bone, is incorporated into the quadriceps femoris tendon and articulates with the patellar surface of the femur to increase muscle leverage and prevent tendon damage. The seven tarsal bones—organized into proximal (talus and calcaneus), intermediate (navicular and cuboid), and distal (three cuneiforms) groups—form the posterior foot, with the talus articulating with the tibia and fibula to form the ankle joint and transferring body weight through the calcaneus to the ground. The metatarsal bones form the anterior foot between the tarsal bones and phalanges, with each metatarsal articulating with a proximal phalanx to form metatarsophalangeal joints; the hallux (big toe) contains two phalanges while toes 2–5 each contain three phalanges connected by interphalangeal joints. The foot''s three arches—the transverse arch formed by cuneiforms and metatarsal bases, and the medial and lateral longitudinal arches—distribute body weight and absorb shock through elastic ligaments and foot muscles. Clinical conditions affecting the lower limb include runner''s knee (patellofemoral syndrome), caused by abnormal patella tracking related to Q-angle and quadriceps muscle imbalances, and pes planus (flat foot), resulting from excessive');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (45, 10, 'Bones of the Upper Limb', 2, '# Summary: Bones of the Upper Limb

The upper limb contains 30 bones organized into four regions: the humerus, ulna and radius, carpal bones, metacarpal bones, and phalanx bones, with key articulations including the glenohumeral joint (humerus head with glenoid cavity), elbow joint (humerus trochlea with ulna trochlear notch; humerus capitulum with radius), and radiocarpal joint (radius with proximal carpal bones). The eight carpal bones are arranged in proximal (scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform) and distal (trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, hamate) rows, with the radiocarpal and midcarpal joints enabling wrist movements, and the flexor retinaculum forming the carpal tunnel through which forearm tendons and a nerve pass; carpal tunnel syndrome results from compression of this nerve. The five metacarpal bones articulate proximally with distal carpal bones at carpometacarpal joints and distally with proximal phalanges at metacarpophalangeal joints, with the first metacarpal allowing independent thumb mobility while the fourth and fifth metacarpals demonstrate limited anterior-posterior mobility that increases grip strength. Common fractures of the upper limb include humeral fractures at the surgical neck, shaft, and distal end with potential artery and nerve complications; Colles fractures of the distal radius (most frequent forearm fracture, especially in osteoporotic individuals over age 50) producing characteristic "dinner fork" deformity; and scaphoid fractures (most common carpal bone fracture) with risk of bone necrosis and degenerative joint disease due to poor blood supply.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (46, 10, 'Development of the Appendicular Skeleton', 3, '# Development of the Appendicular Skeleton

This section covers the embryological and postnatal development of appendicular skeleton structures, including congenital abnormalities and ossification processes. Clubfoot (talipes), the most common lower limb deformity occurring in approximately 1 of every 1,000 births, results from shortened or abnormally tight ligaments and muscles that pull the foot into an abnormal position, causing inversion of the heel and lateral foot displacement; treatment via corrective casting must begin within the first 2 weeks after birth. Limb development begins with limb buds—small mesenchymal bulges covered by ectoderm—appearing near the end of the fourth week (upper limb) and shortly thereafter (lower limb), with the apical ectodermal ridge stimulating proximal-to-distal outgrowth. During the sixth week, distal regions expand into paddle shapes (handplate and footplate) with hyaline cartilage differentiating to form bone models, while programmed cell death creates finger and toe separations; the seventh week brings 90-degree lateral rotation of the upper limb and 90-degree medial rotation of the lower limb. Ossification of appendicular bones occurs primarily through endochondral ossification (except the clavicle, which develops via intramembranous ossification), involving primary ossification centers in the diaphysis by week 12 and secondary centers in epiphyses usually after birth. The epiphyseal plate, a layer of hyaline cartilage between diaphysis and epiphysis, drives bone lengthening until fusion occurs at skeletal maturity; the femur demonstrates this process with primary center at week 7 and sequential secondary center fusion between ages 11–19. Clinically, epiphyseal plates appear dark on radiographs due to lower cartilage density and may be mistaken for fractures');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (47, 10, 'The Pelvic Girdle and Pelvis', 4, 'The pelvic girdle and pelvis section covers the anatomy and functional significance of the bony pelvis, which is formed by the two hip bones, sacrum, and coccyx and functions as a largely immobile, weight-bearing structure that transfers weight from the vertebral column through the hip joints to the lower limbs. The hip bone (coxal bone) consists of three fused components—the ilium, ischium, and pubis—each with distinct anatomical landmarks including the iliac crest, anterior and posterior superior iliac spines, iliac fossa, and auricular surface on the ilium; the ischial tuberosity, ischial spine, and lesser sciatic notch on the ischium; and the pubic body, pubic tubercle, pectineal line, and superior and inferior pubic rami on the pubis. The ilium, pubis, and ischium converge to form the acetabulum (hip joint cavity), while the inferior pubic ramus joins the ischial ramus to form the ischiopubic ramus and define the subpubic angle, with the obturator foramen located between the ischium and pubis. The pelvis is stabilized by ligaments including the anterior and posterior sacroiliac ligaments, sacrospinous ligament, and sacrotuberous ligament, which help define the greater and lesser sciatic foramina. The section also introduces forensic anthropology and forensic pathology as complementary disciplines that utilize skeletal anatomy, dental evidence, and human osteology to identify remains and assist in death investigation, with forensic anthropologists responsible for excavation, age and sex determination, and postmortem interval estimation while forensic pathologists determine cause of death.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (48, 11, 'Autonomic Reflexes and Homeostasis', 1, '# Autonomic Reflexes and Homeostasis

This section examines how autonomic reflexes maintain homeostasis through the coordinated activity of sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions. The structure of visceral reflexes differs fundamentally from somatic reflexes: visceral reflexes employ a two-step efferent pathway involving preganglionic fibers from the lateral horn (spinal cord) or cranial nuclei (brainstem) synapsing in ganglia before postganglionic fibers reach target effectors, whereas somatic reflexes have a single-step pathway directly to skeletal muscle. Key visceral sensory structures include baroreceptors in the aorta and carotid sinuses that detect blood pressure changes via the vagus and glossopharyngeal nerves; visceral sensations typically remain unconscious but can produce referred pain when visceral and somatosensory fibers enter the spinal cord at the same level (exemplified by Kehr''s sign—referred shoulder pain following splenic rupture due to diaphragmatic pressurization). The section distinguishes long reflexes (involving CNS afferent and efferent branches) from short reflexes (completely peripheral, involving the enteric nervous system and myenteric plexuses with no CNS involvement). Homeostasis is maintained through dual innervation and competing autonomic inputs: postganglionic sympathetic fibers release norepinephrine (binding adrenergic receptors) while postganglionic parasympathetic fibers release ACh (binding muscarinic receptors), exemplified in heart rate control and the pupillary light reflex. Autonomic tone—the resting tendency toward sympathetic or parasympathetic dominance—varies by organ system; the heart exhibits parasympathetic tone at rest (60–80 bpm) with an intrinsic rate of 100 bpm. Finally, the sympathetic reflex maintaining');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (49, 11, 'Central Control', 2, '# Summary: Central Control

The Central Control section of the autonomic nervous system establishes how the CNS regulates autonomic reflexes through hierarchical integration of sensory information and coordinated motor output. The pupillary light reflex exemplifies autonomic control, wherein unilateral stimuli produce bilateral responses through parasympathetic pathways involving the retina, oculomotor nerve, ciliary ganglion, and iris circular fibers—a defining feature distinguishing autonomic from somatic reflexes. The hypothalamus serves as the primary control center for homeostatic mechanisms and autonomic balance, integrating visceral reflex information from forebrain structures including the olfactory cortex, septal nuclei, and amygdala through the medial forebrain bundle, while transmitting autonomic output via the dorsal longitudinal fasciculus and medial forebrain bundle to the Edinger–Westphal nucleus and thoracolumbar spinal cord. The amygdala projects to the hypothalamus to stimulate sympathetic fight-or-flight responses during anxiety or fear, triggering stress hormone release through endocrine control. The medulla contains the cardiovascular center, which coordinates homeostatic responses through the solitary nucleus (receiving sensory input from the glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves), the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus nerve (providing parasympathetic output that decreases heart rate and activates digestion), and the nucleus ambiguus (contributing parasympathetic cardiac tone and innervating pharyngeal and laryngeal muscles). Descending hypothalamic inputs stimulate sympathetic activity through cardiac accelerator nerves and vasomotor nerves to modulate heart rate and blood vessel constriction in response to stress.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (50, 11, 'Divisions of the Autonomic Nervous System', 3, '# Summary: Divisions of the Autonomic Nervous System

The autonomic nervous system comprises two divisions—sympathetic and parasympathetic—that control cardiac muscle, smooth muscle, and glandular tissue through involuntary responses to maintain homeostasis. The sympathetic division, or thoracolumbar system, originates from thoracic and upper lumbar spinal cord and includes sympathetic chain ganglia (23 per side), collateral ganglia (celiac, superior mesenteric, and inferior mesenteric), and the adrenal medulla; preganglionic fibers are short and myelinated while postganglionic fibers are long and unmyelinated, allowing divergent effects across multiple organ systems via white and gray rami communicantes and splanchnic nerves. The parasympathetic division, or craniosacral system, originates from brain stem nuclei (Edinger–Westphal nucleus, salivatory nuclei, dorsal nucleus of the vagus nerve, nucleus ambiguus) and sacral spinal cord, with long preganglionic fibers traveling in cranial nerves III, VII, IX, and X and sacral spinal nerves to terminal ganglia located near or within target organs. Chemical signaling in the autonomic nervous system involves cholinergic synapses releasing acetylcholine and adrenergic synapses releasing norepinephrine, with all preganglionic fibers releasing ACh to nicotinic receptors on ganglionic neurons, postganglionic parasympathetic fibers releasing ACh to muscarinic receptors, and postganglionic sympathetic fibers releasing norepinephrine to α- or β-adrenergic receptors. The sympathetic division mediates the fight-or-flight response through dual innervation at target effectors, while the parasympathetic division mediates rest-and-digest responses; postganglionic');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (51, 11, 'Drugs that Affect the Autonomic System', 4, '# Summary: Drugs that Affect the Autonomic System

This section examines pharmacological agents that modify autonomic nervous system function across sympathetic, parasympathetic, and integrated autonomic pathways. Nicotine acts as an acetylcholine analog, binding to nicotinic receptors on ganglionic neurons in both sympathetic and parasympathetic ganglia; while opposing effects typically cancel in most organs, the cardiovascular system experiences unbalanced sympathetic predominance, increasing vascular tone and blood pressure and creating risk for arrhythmias. Sympathomimetic drugs enhance adrenergic function through agonists (phenylephrine, pseudoephedrine, cocaine, caffeine) that bind to α-adrenergic or β-adrenergic receptors or block norepinephrine reuptake, while sympatholytic drugs such as β-blockers (metoprolol, propranolol) and α-blockers block these receptors to treat cardiovascular disease and hypertension. Parasympathomimetic drugs (muscarinic agonists) like pilocarpine enhance cholinergic effects at postganglionic terminals where acetylcholine binds to muscarinic receptors (M1–M5), while anticholinergic drugs (muscarinic antagonists) such as atropine and scopolamine derived from the Atropa genus block parasympathetic function and increase sympathetic tone. Motion sickness results from conflicting visual and vestibular information that stimulates the area postrema (located next to the fourth ventricle outside the blood–brain barrier), which connects via the solitary nucleus and dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus nerve to trigger emesis and autonomic symptoms; scopolamine (administered via transdermal patch) and antihistamines such as dimenhydrinate treat motion sickness by modulating these autonomic responses.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (52, 12, 'An Overview of Blood', 1, '# An Overview of Blood

This section comprehensively covers blood composition, functions, and characteristics essential to understanding cardiovascular physiology. Blood composition consists of formed elements (erythrocytes, leukocytes, and thrombocytes) and plasma, which can be separated and quantified through centrifugation; the hematocrit test measures erythrocyte percentage (approximately 41% in females and 47% in males), while plasma comprises approximately 59% in females and 53% in males. Blood plasma is approximately 92 percent water containing three major protein groups—albumin (54%, produced by the liver, serves as a binding protein and contributes to osmotic pressure), globulins (38%, subdivided into alpha, beta, and gamma globulins that transport lipids and vitamins or function as antibodies), and fibrinogen (7%, essential for blood clotting)—along with electrolytes, dissolved gases, organic nutrients, and metabolic wastes. Blood performs multiple critical functions including oxygen and nutrient delivery to body cells, waste removal via the kidneys and liver, defense against pathogens and mutated cells through leukocytes and platelets, heat distribution through negative-feedback regulation, and homeostatic maintenance via pH buffering and water content regulation. Blood characteristics include color variation based on hemoglobin oxygen saturation (bright red when oxygenated, dusky red when deoxygenated), viscosity approximately five times greater than water, temperature averaging 38°C, and pH averaging 7.4 (slightly alkaline), with blood constituting approximately 8 percent of adult body weight (5–6 liters in males, 4–5 liters in females). Phlebotomy—the practice of drawing blood—involves three collection procedures: venipuncture from surface arm veins, capillary stick from finger or heel, and arterial stick for blood gas analysis, with collected samples analyzed by medical');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (53, 12, 'Blood Typing', 2, '# Blood Typing

The Blood Typing section covers the ABO and Rh blood group systems, the two most clinically significant erythrocyte antigen classifications among more than 50 identified antigens. The ABO blood group is defined by the presence or absence of A and B glycoprotein antigens on erythrocyte membranes, with naturally-formed anti-A and anti-B antibodies (immunoglobulins) produced by plasma cells; agglutination and hemolysis occur when incompatible blood types are transfused, as antibodies bind to erythrocyte antigens and cause clumping that blocks blood vessels and releases hemoglobin into the bloodstream. The Rh blood group is classified by the presence or absence of the Rh D antigen, with approximately 85% of Americans being Rh positive; unlike preformed ABO antibodies, anti-Rh antibodies develop only after sensitization through transfusion or birth of an Rh+ baby to an Rh− parent, potentially causing hemolytic disease of the newborn (HDN or erythroblastosis fetalis) in subsequent pregnancies. RhoGAM (Rh immune globulin) prevents anti-Rh antibody development by destroying fetal Rh+ erythrocytes that cross the placental barrier, reducing HDN incidence from 13–14% to 0.1% since 1968. Safe transfusion protocols designate type O− blood as the universal donor and type AB+ blood as the universal recipient, with type O erythrocytes lacking A and B antigens to prevent agglutination and type AB+ patients able to receive any blood type. In mass casualty situations, intravenous saline solution or artificial blood substitutes such as hemoglobin- and perfluorocarbon-based oxygen carriers may replace lost blood volume when typed blood');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (54, 12, 'Erythrocytes', 3, '# Summary: Erythrocytes

Erythrocytes are small cells (mean diameter 7–8 micrometers) comprising approximately 25 percent of total body cells, with primary functions of transporting oxygen from the lungs to body tissues and transporting approximately 24 percent of carbon dioxide waste to the lungs. Mature erythrocytes are biconcave disks lacking nucleus and mitochondria that rely on anaerobic respiration, with spectrin enabling flexibility through narrow capillaries and hemoglobin (composed of four globin chains each bound to heme containing Fe²⁺ iron) transporting four oxygen molecules per molecule while approximately 23–24 percent of CO₂ binds as carbaminohemoglobin. Erythrocyte production occurs in bone marrow at >2 million cells/second and requires essential nutrients including iron (absorbed and stored in ferritin and hemosiderin, transported by transferrin), copper (component of hephaestin and ceruloplasmin for iron oxidation), zinc (co-enzyme for heme synthesis), folate, and vitamin B₁₂ (co-enzymes for DNA synthesis). After a 120-day lifespan, macrophages phagocytose erythrocytes in bone marrow, liver, and spleen; globin is recycled into amino acids, iron is stored or transported to red bone marrow, and the non-iron heme portion degrades into biliverdin and bilirubin, which binds to albumin, travels to the liver for bile manufacture, and is converted to urobilinogen and stercobilin for fecal elimination. Disorders of erythrocytes include anemia (deficient RBCs or hemoglobin classified by kinetic and morphological approaches examining mean corpuscle volume), with specific types including sickle cell anemia, iron deficiency anemia,');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (55, 12, 'Hemostasis', 4, '# Hemostasis Summary

The Hemostasis section of Chapter 18 covers the mechanisms and disorders of blood clotting. The Formation of the Platelet Plug subsection describes how platelets bind to exposed collagen and endothelial lining with assistance from von Willebrand factor, with platelet-released chemicals (adenosine diphosphate, serotonin, prostaglandins, and phospholipids) promoting additional platelet adhesion and maintaining vasoconstriction to temporarily seal vessel openings. This subsection details three coagulation pathways—the extrinsic pathway (triggered by trauma), the intrinsic pathway (triggered by internal vessel damage or foreign materials), and the common pathway—all dependent on clotting factors (I–XIII) secreted primarily by the liver and platelets, with vitamin K and calcium ion (factor IV) required as cofactors. The extrinsic pathway involves factor III release and sequential activation of factors VII and X, while the intrinsic pathway involves factor XII activation by negatively charged molecules and sequential activation of factors XI and IX, with factor VIII combining with factor IX to activate factor X. In the common pathway, prothrombinase converts prothrombin (factor II) to thrombin, which converts fibrinogen (factor I) to fibrin, with factor XIII stabilizing the fibrin clot; fibrinolysis then gradually degrades the clot through conversion of plasminogen to plasmin and release of bradykinin, a vasodilator. The Disorders of Clotting subsection addresses coagulation failure disorders (thrombocytopenia and hemophilia A, B, and C) and excessive clot formation disorders (thrombocytosis and thrombophilia/hypercoagulation), defining thrombi as aggregations of platelets, erythrocytes, and WBCs trapped in fibrin strands, distinguishing m');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (56, 12, 'Leukocytes and Platelets', 5, '# Section Summary: Leukocytes and Platelets

This section covers the characteristics, classification, and disorders of leukocytes and platelets as formed elements of blood. Leukocytes originate from hematopoietic stem cells in bone marrow and differ from erythrocytes by being larger, less numerous (5,000–10,000 per µL), nucleated, and capable of emigration (diapedesis) through capillary walls to perform defensive functions in tissues through positive chemotaxis. Leukocytes are classified into two major groups: granular leukocytes (neutrophils, eosinophils, basophils) produced in red bone marrow with lobed nuclei, and agranular leukocytes (monocytes, lymphocytes) arising from lymphoid and myeloid stem cells that develop in lymphatic tissues. Granular leukocytes include neutrophils (50–70% of count) containing lysozyme and defensins for bacterial defense, eosinophils (2–4%) targeting parasitic worms with antihistamines and toxins, and basophils (less than 1%) releasing histamines and heparin; agranular leukocytes include lymphocytes (20–30%) comprising natural killer cells, B cells producing antibodies via humoral immunity, and T cells providing cellular-level immunity, plus monocytes (2–8%) maturing into macrophages. Leukocyte disorders include leukopenia (too few leukocytes), leukocytosis (excessive proliferation), leukemia (myelocytic or lymphocytic cancer), and lymphoma (malignant lymphocytes in lymph nodes and spleen). Platelets are cytoplasmic fragments derived from megakaryocytes stimulated by thrombopoietin from kidneys and liver, circulate for approximately');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (57, 12, 'Production of the Formed Elements', 6, '# Production of the Formed Elements

This section covers hematopoiesis, the development of all blood formed elements from hematopoietic stem cells (hemocytoblasts) located in red bone marrow. Hematopoietic stem cells occupy the lowest level of a hierarchal stem cell system and differentiate into two lineages: lymphoid stem cells, which give rise to lymphocytes (T cells, B cells, natural killer cells) that mature in lymphatic tissues, and myeloid stem cells, which produce erythrocytes, megakaryocytes, monocytes, and granular leukocytes (neutrophils, eosinophils, basophils) through intermediate precursor cells identified by the -blast suffix. Hemopoietic growth factors—including erythropoietin (EPO) secreted by kidney interstitial fibroblasts in response to low oxygen, thrombopoietin produced by the liver and kidneys, and cytokines (colony-stimulating factors and interleukins) secreted by bone marrow and other tissues—initiate and regulate hematopoiesis by stimulating stem cell proliferation and differentiation. The section also addresses blood doping practices, including transfusion of supplemental RBCs and injection of synthetic EPO to increase hematocrit and oxygen delivery, both of which carry significant health risks. Bone marrow transplantation, in which donor stem cells replace faulty patient marrow via intravenous infusion following radiation and/or chemotherapy, treats severe anemias and leukemia, with donor matching essential to prevent tissue rejection.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (58, 13, 'Cardiac Cycle', 1, '# Cardiac Cycle Summary

The Cardiac Cycle section covers the sequential phases of heart contraction and relaxation, along with diagnostic assessment of cardiac function through sound analysis. During atrial systole (P wave on ECG), the atrial kick contributes 20–30% of ventricular filling to reach end diastolic volume (EDV) of approximately 130 mL. Ventricular systole (QRS complex, 270 ms total) consists of isovolumic contraction, during which atrioventricular valves (tricuspid and mitral valves) close as pressure rises, followed by the ventricular ejection phase when semilunar valves (pulmonary and aortic valves) open and stroke volume (70–80 mL) is ejected, leaving end systolic volume (ESV) of 50–60 mL. Ventricular diastole (T wave, 430 ms total) begins with isovolumic ventricular relaxation, during which semilunar valves close and produce the dicrotic notch as pressure falls, followed by late ventricular diastole when atrioventricular valves reopen and blood flows from the superior and inferior venae cavae, coronary sinus, and pulmonary veins into the relaxed atria and ventricles. Heart sounds are assessed through auscultation using a stethoscope: S1 results from atrioventricular valve closure during ventricular contraction, while S2 results from semilunar valve closure during ventricular diastole; S3 and S4 may indicate pathological conditions such as congestive heart failure or left ventricular failure. Murmurs, caused by turbulent blood flow and graded on a scale of 1 to 6, are recorded using phonocardiograms or auscult');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (59, 13, 'Cardiac Muscle and Electrical Activity', 2, '# Summary: Cardiac Muscle and Electrical Activity

This section examines cardiac muscle structure and the heart''s electrical conduction system. Cardiac muscle possesses autorhythmicity and consists of two cell types: myocardial contractile cells (99 percent of atrial and ventricular cells) that conduct impulses and generate contractions, and myocardial conducting cells (1 percent) that form the conduction system and propagate action potentials throughout the heart. Cardiomyocytes are shorter than skeletal muscle cells with striations formed by sarcomeres; T tubules occur only at Z discs, and the sarcoplasmic reticulum stores minimal calcium, requiring extracellular calcium influx and resulting in slower contraction onset. Intercalated discs containing desmosomes, gap junctions, and tight junctions link adjacent cardiomyocytes and synchronize contractions, while the extended refractory period prevents tetany. The conduction system comprises the sinoatrial (SA) node (pacemaker in the right atrium), atrioventricular (AV) node (in the atrioventricular septum), atrioventricular bundle (bundle of His), left and right bundle branches, and Purkinje fibers, which sequentially depolarize and conduct impulses to ventricular contractile cells. ECG abnormalities including ectopic foci, arrhythmias, and fibrillation result from localized ischemia, drugs, or autonomic stimulation, with specific waveform changes indicating pathology such as myocardial infarction, ventricular enlargement, and hypoxia. Heart blocks are classified by location and severity (first-, second-, and third-degree), and treatments include defibrillation, artificial pacemakers, and life support interventions.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (60, 13, 'Cardiac Physiology', 3, '# Cardiac Physiology Summary

The Cardiac Physiology section examines the determinants and regulation of cardiac output and heart function. Resting cardiac output (CO = HR × SV) averages 5.25 L/min, calculated from mean stroke volume of approximately 70 mL and heart rate of 75 bpm, with stroke volume determined by the difference between end-diastolic volume (EDV) and end-systolic volume (ESV); ejection fraction, representing the percentage of blood ejected per contraction, ranges from 55–70 percent. The relationship between heart rate and cardiac output varies with physiological conditions: increased heart rate initially elevates stroke volume and cardiac output, but excessive heart rates (above 160 bpm) reduce diastolic filling time and subsequently decrease stroke volume, with the optimal aerobic exercise range of 120–160 bpm maintaining stable cardiac output. The cardiovascular center in the medulla oblongata receives input from visceral receptors—proprioreceptors, baroreceptors (located in the aortic sinus, carotid bodies, and venae cavae), and chemoreceptors—which modulate sympathetic and parasympathetic stimulation through the cardiac plexus; the baroreceptor reflex and atrial (Bainbridge) reflex maintain cardiac homeostasis by adjusting heart rate and contractility in response to pressure, stretch, and metabolic byproducts. Sympathetic stimulation via cardioaccelerator nerves releases norepinephrine at beta-1 receptors to increase heart rate and contractility, while parasympathetic stimulation through the vagus nerve releases acetylcholine to decrease these parameters. Stroke volume is regulated by three primary factors: preload (EDV), contractility (controlled by positive and negative inotropic factors including sympathetic/parasympathetic stimulation, hormones, and electrolytes),');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (61, 13, 'Heart Anatomy', 4, '# Heart Anatomy Section Summary

This section on heart anatomy establishes the quantitative significance of cardiac output (approximately 70 mL stroke volume, 5.25 liters per minute) and describes the heart''s gross anatomy, including its pinecone shape (12 cm length, 8 cm width, 6 cm thickness) and sex-dependent weight (250–300 grams in females, 300–350 grams in males), with discussion of cardiac hypertrophy in athletes versus pathological enlargement in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. The heart''s location within the mediastinum between the lungs, enclosed by the pericardium (fibrous pericardium and serous pericardium with parietal and visceral layers), positions it between vertebral bodies and sternum to enable cardiopulmonary resuscitation. The four chambers—right atrium, left atrium, right ventricle, and left ventricle—facilitate two linked circuits: the pulmonary circuit transports deoxygenated blood to the lungs via the pulmonary trunk and returns oxygenated blood via pulmonary veins, while the systemic circuit distributes oxygenated blood through the aorta and returns deoxygenated blood via the superior and inferior venae cavae. The heart wall comprises three layers (epicardium, myocardium, and endocardium), with internal partitioning by septa (interatrial, interventricular, and atrioventricular) containing the fossa ovalis and supporting atrioventricular and semilunar valves anchored by the cardiac skeleton. The coronary circulation supplies cardiac muscle through the left coronary artery (with circumflex and anterior interventricular/LAD branches), right coronary artery, and coronary veins draining into the coronary sinus, with blockage causing myocard');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (62, 14, 'Cell Growth and Division', 1, '# Cell Growth and Division

This section examines the cellular mechanisms of growth and division, beginning with somatic cells, which contain 23 homologous pairs of chromosomes organized as diploid organisms, and introduces the cell cycle as the sequence of events from cell creation through division. The cell cycle consists of interphase (G1, S, and G2 phases) and mitosis followed by cytokinesis, with cells spending most time in interphase performing metabolic functions and replicating DNA during S phase to create sister chromatids attached at the centromere. Mitosis progresses through four sequential stages—prophase (chromatin condenses into chromosomes; centrioles form centrosomes extending microtubules to create the mitotic spindle; kinetochores attach to spindle microtubules), metaphase (sister chromatids align on the metaphase plate), anaphase (sister chromatids separate and move to opposite cell ends), and telophase (daughter nuclei form and the mitotic spindle disassembles)—followed by cytokinesis, during which a cleavage furrow divides the cytoplasm. Cell cycle progression is regulated by checkpoints controlled by cyclins and cyclin-dependent kinases (CDKs), which provide stop and go signals; the metaphase checkpoint ensures sister chromatids are properly attached to microtubules before anaphase separation. Cancer arises from mutations disrupting homeostatic balance between proto-oncogenes (which promote DNA replication and cell division through growth factor signaling) and tumor suppressor genes (which halt division, trigger apoptosis, or enforce contact inhibition), with proto-oncogenes functioning as an accelerator and tumor suppressor genes as a brake pedal in the cell cycle''s cruise control system.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (63, 14, 'Cellular Differentiation', 2, '# Cellular Differentiation

This section examines stem cells and their applications in regenerative medicine. The Stem Cells subsection categorizes stem cells by differentiation potential: totipotent stem cells (arise from zygote division and can differentiate into any cell type), pluripotent stem cells (differentiate into any human tissue type), multipotent stem cells (differentiate into cell types within a given lineage), oligopotent stem cells (limited to a few cell types), and unipotent cells (fully specialized). The subsection describes stem cells present at different life stages—embryonic stem cells, fetal stem cells, and adult stem cells—including epithelial stem cells (give rise to keratinocytes), hematopoietic stem cells in bone marrow (give rise to red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets), endothelial stem cells (give rise to endothelial cells lining blood and lymph vessels), and mesenchymal stem cells (give rise to muscle cell types). Differentiation involves changes in cell size, shape, metabolic activity, and function through unique genetic expression controlled by transcription factors, proteins that bind to specific genes and promote or inhibit their transcription. The Stem Cell Research subsection explores therapeutic applications using adult stem cells, human embryonic stem cells (hESCs), umbilical cord blood stem cells, deciduous teeth stem cells, and induced pluripotent stem cells (iPSCs)—genetically reprogrammed adult cells that function like embryonic stem cells. Cell-based therapy involves inducing stem cells to differentiate in vitro and injecting them into patients to repair damaged tissues for diseases such as diabetes and heart disease. The subsection contrasts stem cell sources: hESCs offer nearly unlimited differentiation potential but trigger immune rejection and raise ethical concerns, while adult stem cells avoid immunological and ethical pitfalls but have limited differentiation capacity; iPSCs are presented as a promising');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (64, 14, 'Protein Synthesis', 3, '# Protein Synthesis Summary

Protein synthesis is a fundamental cellular process that converts genetic information into functional proteins through gene expression, beginning with genes (functional DNA segments) and involving the transformation of DNA base sequences into amino acid sequences via triplet codons. Transcription, the first step, occurs in the nucleus where RNA polymerase unwinds the DNA coding strand at the promoter sequence and synthesizes messenger RNA (mRNA)—a single-stranded intermediate containing uracil and ribose sugar—through initiation, elongation, and termination stages. Post-transcriptional modification converts pre-mRNA to mature mRNA through splicing, wherein a spliceosome removes introns (non-coding regions) and joins exons (coding segments), enabling multiple protein variations from a single gene. The mature mRNA then travels to the cytoplasm for translation, the second step of protein synthesis. Translation synthesizes polypeptides (amino acid chains) using the ribosome (composed of ribosomal RNA and proteins) as the substrate and transfer RNA (tRNA) molecules as translators, with tRNA anticodons recognizing complementary mRNA codons to attach specific amino acids to the growing chain. Translation proceeds through initiation (ribosomal subunits bind mRNA), elongation (tRNA delivers amino acids as the mRNA shifts one codon), and termination (stop codon releases the completed protein), with multiple ribosomes simultaneously translating a single mRNA strand in a polyribosome to increase efficiency. Many ribosomes associate with rough endoplasmic reticulum to synthesize proteins destined for the Golgi apparatus.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (65, 14, 'The Cell Membrane', 4, 'The Cell Membrane section covers the structural and functional organization of the cell membrane and its role in regulating cellular transport. The phospholipid bilayer, composed of amphipathic molecules with hydrophilic phosphate heads and hydrophobic lipid tails, forms the fundamental structure, with cholesterol contributing to membrane fluidity and maintaining the fluid mosaic state between the intracellular fluid (ICF) and extracellular fluid (ECF). Membrane proteins are classified as integral proteins—including channel proteins that selectively transport ions and receptors that bind ligands to induce cellular responses—and peripheral proteins that function on membrane surfaces or as digestive enzymes. Glycoproteins with attached carbohydrates form the glycocalyx, which provides cell recognition and cellular identity. Transport across the cell membrane occurs through passive mechanisms (simple diffusion, facilitated diffusion, osmosis, and filtration) that move substances down concentration gradients without ATP, and active mechanisms (active transport pumps like the sodium-potassium pump and secondary active transport via symporters and antiporters) that use ATP to move substances against concentration gradients. Bulk transport processes including endocytosis (phagocytosis, pinocytosis, and receptor-mediated endocytosis) and exocytosis move large materials across the membrane via vesicle formation and fusion. The selective permeability of the hydrophobic lipid bilayer interior allows only small, nonpolar materials to cross unaided, while water-soluble substances require protein assistance to traverse the membrane.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (66, 14, 'The Cytoplasm and Cellular Organelles', 5, '# The Cytoplasm and Cellular Organelles

The cytoplasm comprises cytosol (the jelly-like fluid medium) and cellular organelles that work together to maintain cell function, as exemplified by respiratory epithelial cells where cilia move mucus and the CFTR channel uses ATP-dependent active transport to move Cl⁻ ions, creating an osmotic gradient that draws water into mucus. The endomembrane system—consisting of the endoplasmic reticulum (ER), Golgi apparatus, and vesicles—produces, packages, and exports cellular products; rough ER synthesizes proteins for the cell membrane or export via ribosomal protein synthesis, while smooth ER synthesizes lipids, regulates Ca²⁺, and performs detoxification. The Golgi apparatus sorts and modifies ER-derived proteins and repackages them into vesicles for secretion or delivery to lysosomes, which contain digestive enzymes that break down cellular components and foreign material through autophagy and phagocytosis. Mitochondria, bean-shaped organelles with cristae-folded inner membranes, perform cellular respiration to convert nutrient energy into ATP, while peroxisomes contain enzymes that metabolize lipids and detoxify poisons by converting hydrogen peroxide into water and oxygen. Reactive oxygen species (ROS)—including free radicals and peroxides produced during mitochondrial ATP production—cause oxidative stress and cellular damage implicated in cancer, cardiovascular disease, and neurodegenerative diseases, which antioxidants counteract. The cytoskeleton comprises three protein filaments—microtubules (tubulin-based), microfilaments (actin-based), and intermediate filaments (keratin-based)—that provide structural support, enable cell motility through cilia and flagella, facilitate intracellular transport, and support cell division');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (67, 14, 'The Nucleus and DNA Replication', 6, '# The Nucleus and DNA Replication

The nucleus serves as the cell''s control center, containing DNA that stores the blueprint for all cellular activities and transmitting commands via molecular messengers; most body cells maintain a complete DNA set, though mature red blood cells lack nuclei to accommodate hemoglobin. The nucleus is bounded by a nuclear envelope composed of two lipid bilayers with nuclear pores and pore complexes that regulate passage of proteins, RNA, and solutes between nucleus and cytoplasm, while the nucleoplasm contains nucleic acid building blocks and the nucleolus manufactures ribosomal RNA. DNA is organized within chromatin threads as nucleosomes, where DNA wraps around histone proteins in a beaded-necklace pattern; during cell division, chromatin condenses into chromosomes to safely transport DNA, with humans possessing approximately 22,000 genes distributed across 46 chromosomes. DNA replication, the process of copying DNA before cell division, is essential for organism growth, development, and health, involving a double helix of two antiparallel complementary strands with sugar-phosphate backbones and base pairs (adenine-thymine and cytosine-guanine) that determine the genetic code. DNA replication occurs in two stages: Initiation, in which helicase untwists and separates complementary strands, and Elongation, in which DNA polymerase synthesizes new complementary strands using original strands as templates. Semiconservative replication ensures each new DNA molecule contains one original strand and one newly synthesized strand, proceeding simultaneously at multiple sites until the entire genome is replicated, with DNA proofreading enzymes scanning newly synthesized DNA to ensure precise replication.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (68, 15, 'Chemical Bonds', 1, '# Summary: Chemical Bonds

This section introduces chemical bonds as electrical attractions that hold atoms together to form stable molecules and chemical compounds, establishing the foundation for understanding human physiology. The section covers three major bond types: ionic bonds (formed between oppositely charged ions such as sodium Na+ and chloride Cl–), covalent bonds (formed through electron sharing between atoms, including single, double, and triple covalent bonds), and hydrogen bonds (weak attractions between a partially positive hydrogen atom and an electronegative atom like oxygen). The subsection on covalent bonds explains nonpolar molecules (with equal electron sharing) and polar covalent bonds (with unequal electron sharing), using water as the primary example of a polar molecule with partial charges (δ+ on hydrogen, δ− on oxygen) that enable hydrogen bonding. The hydrogen bonds subsection describes how water molecules interact with electrolytes through dipole-ion bonds (attracting charged ions like Na+ and Cl–) while repelling nonpolar molecules such as fats, lipids, and oils. The ions and ionic bonds subsection establishes that cations (positively charged ions) and anions (negatively charged ions) form ionic bonds through mutual electrical attraction, with dissolved ions in biological fluids called electrolytes generating the electrical activity observed in electrocardiograms (EKG/ECG) and electroencephalograms (EEG). Together, these bond types—particularly hydrogen bonds—are identified as critical for homeostasis, cellular signaling, and energy production in human physiology.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (69, 15, 'Chemical Reactions', 2, 'The Chemical Reactions section of Chapter 2 covers the fundamental principles governing chemical processes in the human body across three subsections. "The Role of Energy in Chemical Reactions" establishes that kinetic energy and potential energy are essential for breaking and forming chemical bonds, distinguishing between exergonic reactions (which release more energy than they absorb) and endergonic reactions (which require energy input), while demonstrating how chemical energy stored in bonds is converted and conserved through catabolism and other metabolic processes. "Characteristics of Chemical Reactions" defines reactants and products governed by the law of conservation of mass, categorizing reactions as synthesis reactions (joining components), decomposition reactions (breaking down compounds), anabolic reactions (energy-requiring), and exchange reactions (involving both bond formation and breaking), with emphasis on chemical equations and reversibility indicated by double arrows. "Factors Influencing the Rate of Chemical Reactions" identifies surface area, physical state, molecular size, and element reactivity as determinants of reaction speed, while explaining how temperature increases kinetic energy to accelerate molecular collisions and how concentration and pressure affect collision frequency. This subsection emphasizes that catalysts—particularly enzymes composed of protein or RNA—increase reaction rates by lowering activation energy without undergoing change themselves, making them essential for facilitating metabolic reactions including food breakdown and energy conversion in the human body.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (70, 15, 'Elements and Atoms: The Building Blocks of Matter', 3, '# Summary: Elements and Atoms: The Building Blocks of Matter

This section establishes foundational concepts of atomic structure and chemical behavior essential to understanding human physiology. The introduction distinguishes mass (the amount of matter in an object, independent of location) from weight (mass affected by gravitational pull, which varies by location). The Elements and Compounds subsection identifies the 92 fundamental elements as pure substances that cannot be created or broken down by ordinary chemical means, with the human body obtaining elements from foods and air; atoms are defined as the smallest unit of an element retaining its chemical properties and are composed of protons (positively charged, nucleus-located), neutrons (uncharged, nucleus-located), and electrons (negatively charged, orbiting in electron shells). The Atoms and Subatomic Particles subsection explains that atomic number is determined by proton number, mass number equals protons plus neutrons, and isotopes are atoms of the same element with different neutron numbers; the periodic table organizes all 92 naturally occurring elements by atomic number, and radioactive isotopes (radioisotopes) with unstable nuclei are characterized by half-life and have medical applications including PET scanning and radioembolization. The Behavior of Electrons subsection introduces electron shells (distinct energy levels around the nucleus), the valence shell (outermost shell determining chemical reactivity), and the octet rule (atoms stabilize with eight valence electrons by giving up, gaining, or sharing electrons), with examples including hydrogen, oxygen forming H₂O through covalent bonds, and carbon forming methane (CH₄).');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (71, 15, 'Inorganic Compounds Essential to Human Functioning', 4, 'This section on inorganic compounds essential to human functioning covers water, salts, acids, and bases as critical substances lacking carbon and hydrogen. Water comprises approximately 70 percent of adult body weight and serves essential functions including lubricating joints via synovial fluid, facilitating lung expansion via pleural fluid, cushioning organs and the brain, and functioning as a heat sink. The subsection explains three types of liquid mixtures—solutions, colloids, and suspensions—and describes solutions as homogeneous mixtures with water as the "universal solvent" and solutes measured in milligrams per deciliter or molarity, distinguishing between hydrophilic compounds that dissolve readily and hydrophobic compounds that do not. Dehydration synthesis and hydrolysis are presented as reversible reactions involving water creation or consumption, while salts are identified as ionic compounds that dissociate into electrolytes, with examples including NaCl, bile salts produced by the liver, and calcium phosphate salts in teeth and bones. Acids and bases are introduced as fundamental inorganic compounds, with strong acids like hydrochloric acid from stomach cells ionizing completely and weak acids like acetic acid ionizing incompletely, while bases release hydroxyl ions or accept hydrogen ions, exemplified by bicarbonate neutralizing stomach acid in the small intestine. The pH scale (0–14) measures hydrogen ion concentration logarithmically with pH 7 as neutral, and human blood maintains pH 7.35–7.45 through homeostatic mechanisms involving breathing, urine excretion, and buffers—solutions of weak acids and conjugate bases that neutralize excess acids or bases. Pathological conditions include acidosis from reduced breathing effectiveness or bicarbonate loss and alkalosis from low carbon dioxide levels or hydrogen/chloride ion loss from vomiting.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (72, 15, 'Organic Compounds Essential to Human Functioning', 5, '# Summary: Organic Compounds Essential to Human Functioning

This section establishes carbon chemistry as the foundation for all organic compounds in human physiology, with carbon atoms completing their valence shells through covalent bonding to form carbon skeletons and hydrocarbons. Five critical functional groups—hydroxyl, carboxyl, amino, methyl, and phosphate—determine organic compound structure and reactivity, with hydroxyl groups participating in dehydration synthesis and hydrolysis, carboxyl groups appearing in fatty acids and amino acids, amino groups found in amino acids, methyl groups occurring in amino acids, and phosphate groups present in phospholipids and nucleotides. Monomers link via dehydration synthesis to form polymers, which are cleaved by hydrolysis. The section surveys four major organic compound classes: carbohydrates (monosaccharides, disaccharides, and polysaccharides including glucose, glycogen, and cellulose); lipids (triglycerides with saturated, monounsaturated, and polyunsaturated fatty acids; phospholipids with hydrophobic tails and hydrophilic heads; cholesterol; and prostaglandins); proteins (polymers of 20 amino acids linked by peptide bonds with primary, secondary, tertiary, and quaternary structures, functioning as enzymes with active sites and substrate specificity); and nucleotides (DNA containing deoxyribose and thymine; RNA containing ribose and uracil; and ATP composed of ribose, adenine, and three phosphate groups that release energy upon hydrolysis).');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (73, 16, 'Accessory Organs in Digestion: The Liver, Pancreas, and Gallbladder', 1, '# Summary: Accessory Organs in Digestion: The Liver, Pancreas, and Gallbladder

The liver, the body''s largest gland located in the right upper quadrant, is suspended by five peritoneal ligaments and receives oxygenated blood via the hepatic artery and nutrient-rich blood via the hepatic portal vein, with all alimentary canal blood passing through hepatic portal circulation before draining into the inferior vena cava. The liver''s functional unit consists of hepatocytes (comprising ~80% of liver volume), bile canaliculi that accumulate bile between adjacent hepatocytes, and hepatic sinusoids (fenestrated capillaries where hepatocytes process nutrients and toxins), organized around portal triads containing a bile duct, hepatic artery branch, and hepatic portal vein branch. Hepatocytes secrete approximately one liter of bile daily—a yellow-brown alkaline mixture containing bile salts, bile pigments, phospholipids, cholesterol, and triglycerides—which flows through bile canaliculi, bile ducts, and the common hepatic duct before joining the cystic duct to form the common bile duct. The pancreas is a retroperitoneal gland positioned transversely behind the stomach with its head in the duodenum''s c-shaped curvature, functioning as both an exocrine gland (acini secreting pancreatic juice into pancreatic ducts) and endocrine gland (islets of Langerhans producing insulin, glucagon, pancreatic polypeptide, and somatostatin). Pancreatic juice (>1 liter daily) contains sodium bicarbonate and digestive enzymes including amylase, lipase, nuclease, and protein-digesting enzymes (trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, procarboxypeptidase');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (74, 16, 'Chemical Digestion and Absorption: A Closer Look', 2, 'This section on Chemical Digestion and Absorption: A Closer Look covers the enzymatic breakdown of macronutrients through hydrolysis and their subsequent absorption in the small intestine. Key salivary enzymes include lingual lipase and salivary amylase, while gastric digestion involves gastric lipase and pepsin; brush border enzymes (α-dextrinase, sucrase, maltase, lactase, peptidases, nucleosidases, phosphatases) and pancreatic enzymes (pancreatic amylase, pancreatic lipase, trypsin, chymotrypsin, elastase, carboxypeptidase, nucleases) complete chemical digestion. Carbohydrate digestion yields monosaccharides (glucose, galactose, fructose); protein digestion produces amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides; lipid digestion generates monoacylglycerides, glycerol, and free fatty acids; and nucleic acid digestion releases pentose sugars, phosphates, and nitrogenous bases. Absorption occurs through five mechanisms: active transport, passive diffusion, facilitated diffusion, co-transport (secondary active transport), and endocytosis. Water-soluble nutrients (glucose, galactose, amino acids, nucleic acid products) are absorbed into capillary blood via the hepatic portal vein, while lipid-soluble nutrients (long-chain fatty acids, monoacylglycerides) are packaged into chylomicrons and transported via lacteals and lymphatic vessels through the thoracic duct to systemic circulation. Specialized absorption mechanisms include glucose and galactose via co-transport with sodium ions, fructose via facilitated diffusion, short-chain fatty acids via simple diffusion, and iron via active transport.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (75, 16, 'Digestive System Processes and Regulation', 3, '# SECTION SUMMARY: Digestive System Processes and Regulation

This section comprehensively covers the major organs, accessory structures, and functional processes of the digestive system. The introduction establishes key anatomical components—mouth, pharynx, esophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine, liver, gallbladder, and pancreas—and their roles in food processing and nutrient absorption through processes including ingestion, mastication, chemical breakdown, propulsion, chyme formation, emulsification, enzymatic activity, and defecation. The Digestive Processes subsection details six sequential processes: ingestion (mastication and bolus formation in the mouth via lingual lipase), propulsion (swallowing and peristalsis through the alimentary canal), mechanical digestion (mastication, tongue movements, stomach churning, and segmentation in the small intestine), chemical digestion (enzymatic breakdown of complex molecules into building blocks), absorption (primarily in the small intestine via epithelial cells and lacteals), and defecation (removal of feces). The regulatory mechanisms subsection explains how mechanoreceptors, chemoreceptors, and osmoreceptors detect food-induced changes and trigger long reflexes (involving central and autonomic nervous systems) and short reflexes (orchestrated by intrinsic nerve plexuses), which activate digestive secretions and alimentary muscle contractions. Key hormonal regulators include gastrin (stimulating gastric acid secretion by parietal cells), secretin (stimulating pancreatic bicarbonate), cholecystokinin or CCK (stimulating pancreatic enzymes and bile secretion), and gastric inhibitory peptide (inhibiting gastric secretion and motility). The section concludes by addressing age-related pathologies affecting digestive function, including hiatal hernia, gastritis, peptic ulcer disease, maldigestion, malabsorption, divert');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (76, 16, 'Overview of the Digestive System', 4, '# Overview of the Digestive System

The digestive system comprises the alimentary canal (a 7.62-meter tube including the mouth, pharynx, esophagus, stomach, small intestine, and large intestine) and accessory digestive organs (salivary glands, gallbladder, liver, and pancreas) that work together to nourish the body through nutrient absorption. The alimentary canal''s four concentric tissue layers—mucosa, submucosa, muscularis, and serosa—vary structurally by region; the mucosa contains non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium in the mouth through anal canal and simple columnar epithelium in the stomach and intestines, while the muscularis consists of smooth muscle (with skeletal muscle in the mouth, pharynx, anterior esophagus, and external anal sphincter) that enables mechanical digestion and propulsion of food. The enteric nervous system (~100 million neurons) provides intrinsic innervation through the myenteric plexus (controlling motility) and submucosal plexus (regulating secretions), while extrinsic autonomic innervation allows sympathetic inhibition and parasympathetic stimulation of digestive functions. Blood supply originates from the aortic arch, thoracic aorta, and abdominal aorta, with the celiac trunk servicing the liver, stomach, and duodenum, and superior and inferior mesenteric arteries supplying remaining intestines; nutrient-rich blood from the small intestine enters the hepatic portal system for processing in the liver before returning to the heart. The peritoneum, a serous membrane composed of parietal peritoneum (lining the abdominal wall) and visceral peritoneum (enveloping organs), contains five major peritoneal folds—greater omentum, falciform ligament, lesser');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (77, 16, 'The Mouth, Pharynx, and Esophagus', 5, '# Summary: The Mouth, Pharynx, and Esophagus

This section covers the anatomical structures and physiological functions of the upper digestive tract. The mouth (oral cavity/buccal cavity) is framed by the lips, cheeks, tongue, and palate, with key structures including the oral vestibule, fauces, hard palate, soft palate, uvula, palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal arches, and palatine and lingual tonsils. The tongue is a muscular organ with intrinsic muscles (longitudinalis inferior, longitudinalis superior, transversus linguae, verticalis linguae) and extrinsic muscles (palatoglossus, hyoglossus, styloglossus, genioglossus, mylohyoid) that facilitate ingestion, mechanical and chemical digestion via lingual lipase, taste sensation, swallowing, and vocalization; its surface features fungiform and filiform papillae, and the lingual frenulum tethers it to the mouth floor. Salivary glands—including three major pairs (parotid, submandibular, and sublingual glands) and minor glands in the oral mucosa—secrete saliva composed of 98–99.5% water with salivary amylase, bicarbonate and phosphate ions, mucus, immunoglobulin A, lysozyme, and epidermal growth factor; parasympathetic stimulation via the glossopharyngeal and facial nerves regulates salivation. Teeth (dentes) mechanically break down food and consist of a crown and root with pulp cavity, dentin, cementum, and enamel; humans develop 20 deciduous teeth replaced by 32 permanent teeth. The pharynx (throat) is a muscular tube subdivided into nasopharynx, orophar');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (78, 16, 'The Small and Large Intestines', 6, '# Summary: The Small and Large Intestines

The small intestine is the primary digestive organ subdivided into the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum, where the hepatopancreatic ampulla (ampulla of Vater) receives bile and pancreatic secretions at the major duodenal papilla, regulated by the hepatopancreatic sphincter (sphincter of Oddi). Three mucosal features—circular folds (plicae circulares), villi containing capillary beds and lacteals, and microvilli forming the brush border—increase absorptive surface area over 600-fold, while intestinal glands (crypts of Lieberkühn) and duodenal glands (Brunner''s glands) secrete intestinal juice and bicarbonate-rich mucus respectively. Specialized mucosal cell types including absorptive cells, goblet cells, Paneth cells, and enteroendocrine cells (G cells, I cells, K cells, M cells, and S cells) perform distinct functions, with Peyer''s patches in the distal ileum providing immune function via MALT. The large intestine is the terminal portion of the alimentary canal subdivided into the cecum, colon, rectum, and anus, with the ileocecal valve controlling chyme flow from the ileum; the cecum receives contents from the ileum while the appendix (vermiform appendix), a lymphoid-containing tube attached via the mesoappendix, may serve as a bacterial reservoir. The colon is organized into the ascending colon (right side), transverse colon, descending colon (left side), and sigmoid colon, with the ascending, descending, and rectum located in the retroperitoneum while the transverse and sigmoid colon are tethered by the mesocolon. The rectum (final 20.3 cm of');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (79, 16, 'The Stomach', 7, 'The Stomach section of Chapter 23 covers the organ''s anatomy, secretory regulation, and digestive functions across three subsections. The introductory subsection describes the stomach''s four regions—cardia, fundus, body, and pylorus—and its four-layered wall with a unique oblique smooth muscle layer enabling churning; it identifies key cell types in gastric glands including parietal cells (producing hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor), chief cells (secreting pepsinogen), mucous neck cells, and enteroendocrine G cells (secreting gastrin), and establishes functional relationships between HCl, pepsin, intrinsic factor, and gastrin in regulating gastric and intestinal functions. The Gastric Secretion subsection explains three control phases—the cephalic phase (triggered by sensory stimuli via conditioned reflex), the gastric phase (lasting 3–4 hours, involving stretch receptors and parasympathetic acetylcholine release), and the intestinal phase (regulated by the duodenum through intestinal gastrin and the enterogastric reflex)—and describes additional regulatory hormones including ghrelin, histamine, serotonin, and somatostatin. The Digestive Functions subsection details the stomach''s roles in mixing (via mixing waves at ~20-second intervals), chemical digestion (involving salivary amylase, lingual lipase, HCl, and pepsin), limited absorption, and storage of chyme, while emphasizing that intrinsic factor production is the stomach''s only function necessary to life and that gastric emptying timing varies by meal composition, with carbohydrates emptying fastest and high-triglyceride meals remaining longest.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (80, 17, 'An Overview of the Endocrine System', 1, '# An Overview of the Endocrine System

The endocrine system comprises ductless glands—including the pituitary, thyroid, parathyroid, adrenal, and pineal glands—along with organs possessing endocrine function such as the hypothalamus, thymus, heart, kidneys, stomach, small intestine, liver, skin, ovaries, testes, adipose tissue, and bone tissue, all of which secrete hormones directly into interstitial fluid and blood vessels for systemic transport. The pancreas exemplifies a dual-function organ with exocrine cells secreting pancreatic juice through ducts to the small intestine and endocrine cells secreting insulin and glucagon to regulate blood glucose levels. Neural and endocrine signaling represent two distinct intercellular communication systems: the nervous system employs electrical signaling via action potentials and rapid chemical signaling through neurotransmitters (serotonin, norepinephrine) that diffuse across the synaptic cleft and bind post-synaptic receptors within milliseconds, while the endocrine system uses only chemical signaling through hormones transported via bloodstream to target cells with response times ranging from seconds to 48 hours. Endocrine signaling is typically less specific than neural signaling, as the same hormone produces varied effects depending on target cell type, and secondary messengers such as cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP) transduce hormone-receptor binding into cellular responses. Beyond endocrine signaling, autocrine signaling occurs when a chemical elicits a response in the same cell that secreted it (exemplified by Interleukin-1), and paracrine signaling occurs when a chemical induces a response in neighboring cells (exemplified by histamine released by immune cells causing bronchial smooth muscle constriction), though paracrines maintain insufficient bloodstream concentration to affect distant tissues. The');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (81, 17, 'Hormones', 2, '# Summary: Hormones

The Hormones section of Chapter 17 establishes hormones as critical regulators of physiological processes including reproduction, growth and development, metabolism, fluid and electrolyte balance, and sleep regulation. The section classifies hormones into two major chemical groups: amino acid–derived hormones (amine, peptide, and protein hormones) and lipid-derived hormones (steroids), with specific examples including melatonin, thyroid hormones, catecholamines, growth hormone, follicle-stimulating hormone, testosterone, estrogens, aldosterone, and cortisol. Target cell response to hormones is modulated by receptor presence and specificity, downregulation and upregulation of receptors, and three primary hormone interactions: permissive effects (exemplified by thyroid hormones and reproductive hormones), synergistic effects (producing amplified responses), and antagonistic effects (illustrated by insulin and glucagon). Hormone action occurs through two major pathways: steroid and thyroid hormones binding intracellular receptors to trigger gene transcription, and water-soluble amino acid–derived hormones binding cell membrane receptors to initiate second messenger systems, including the cAMP system (involving G proteins, adenylyl cyclase, and protein kinases) and the phospholipase C system (involving diacylglycerol, inositol triphosphate, and calcium ions). Hormone secretion is regulated through feedback loops and multiple stimulus types—positive feedback (exemplified by oxytocin and uterine contractions), negative feedback (exemplified by glucocorticoids), and chemical, neural, and humoral stimuli—to maintain homeostasis, with key regulatory relationships including osmoreceptor detection of blood osmolarity triggering ADH release, high blood glucose triggering insulin secretion, and sympathetic nervous system signaling triggering catecholamine release from the adre');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (82, 17, 'Organs with Secondary Endocrine Functions', 3, '# Section Summary: Organs with Secondary Endocrine Functions

This section examines endocrine functions of organs whose primary roles are non-endocrine, beginning with the heart''s production of atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) by specialized atrial wall cells, which responds to increased blood volume or pressure by reducing sodium and water reabsorption in the kidneys, inhibiting the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, and causing vasodilation to decrease blood pressure and volume. The gastrointestinal tract secretes peptide hormones including gastrin (stimulates hydrochloric acid release), secretin (stimulates pancreatic bicarbonate release and inhibits stomach acid), and cholecystokinin/CCK (promotes pancreatic enzyme and bile secretion), while intestinal hormones enhance glucose metabolism by modulating insulin and glucagon secretion and improving cellular insulin sensitivity. The kidneys produce renin (triggers the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system), calcitriol (regulates blood calcium in response to parathyroid hormone), and erythropoietin/EPO (stimulates red blood cell production in response to low oxygen). Bone produces fibroblast growth factor 23/FGF23 (inhibits calcitriol formation and increases phosphorus excretion) and osteocalcin (enhances insulin production and peripheral tissue insulin sensitivity). Adipose tissue secretes leptin (regulates satiety and bone metabolism) and adiponectin (reduces insulin resistance and protects blood vessels), while skin produces cholecalciferol/vitamin D through ultraviolet radiation exposure, which is converted to active calcitriol in the liver and kidneys for calcium absorption and immune function. The thymus produces thymosins for T lymphocyte development, and the liver produces insulin-like growth factor-1/somatomedin (stimulates growth), angioten');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (83, 17, 'The Adrenal Glands', 4, 'The adrenal glands consist of an outer cortex divided into three zones (zona glomerulosa, zona fasciculata, and zona reticularis) and an inner medulla, supplied by suprarenal and renal arteries with hormonal drainage via suprarenal veins. The adrenal medulla is composed of chromaffin cells that produce catecholamines—epinephrine and norepinephrine in a 4:1 ratio—via the sympathoadrenal medullary (SAM) pathway in response to acute stress, triggering fight-or-flight responses including glycogenolysis, increased heart rate and blood pressure, airway dilation, and selective vasodilation to vital organs with vasoconstriction to less essential organs. The zona glomerulosa produces mineralocorticoids, primarily aldosterone, which regulates sodium and potassium balance and blood volume through the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS), where renin catalyzes conversion of angiotensinogen to angiotensin I, and angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) converts it to angiotensin II, which stimulates aldosterone secretion. The zona fasciculata produces glucocorticoids—cortisol, cortisone, and corticosterone—released by ACTH in response to long-term stress, promoting catabolism of glycogen, triglycerides, and proteins while downregulating immune function and inflammation. The zona reticularis produces androgens in response to ACTH, which are converted to testosterone or estrogens in tissues and serve as supplementary sources of sex hormones, particularly estrogens in post-menopausal individuals. The adrenal glands function within the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis, where the hypothalamus stimulates ACTH release from the anterior pituitary to');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (84, 17, 'The Endocrine Pancreas', 5, '# The Endocrine Pancreas

The endocrine pancreas regulates blood glucose levels through two primary hormones: insulin and glucagon. Pancreatic alpha cells secrete glucagon during fasting or exercise, which increases blood glucose through glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, and lipolysis, while glucagon secretion is inhibited by rising blood glucose via negative feedback. Beta cells secrete insulin in response to food intake and elevated blood glucose, triggered initially by glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide; insulin facilitates glucose uptake primarily in skeletal muscle and adipose cells by activating tyrosine kinase receptors and translocating glucose transporter vesicles to the cell membrane for facilitated diffusion. Insulin reduces blood glucose by stimulating glycolysis, promoting liver glycogen storage, inhibiting glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis, and promoting triglyceride and protein synthesis, with secretion regulated by negative feedback. The pancreas also produces somatostatin from delta cells, which inhibits insulin and glucagon release, and pancreatic polypeptide from PP cells, which plays a role in appetite regulation. The section further addresses anabolic steroids as a misuse of the endocrine system, defining them as synthetic versions of testosterone used for performance enhancement; these substances increase muscle mass but cause severe adverse effects including elevated cholesterol and blood pressure, liver and cardiac damage, cardiac arrhythmias, sex-specific effects such as testicular atrophy in males and clitoral enlargement in females, increased aggression, depression, sleep disturbances, acne, and infertility, leading major collegiate and professional sports organizations to ban performance-enhancing drugs.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (85, 17, 'The Pituitary Gland and Hypothalamus', 6, 'This section on the pituitary gland and hypothalamus covers the anatomical relationship between the hypothalamus (a diencephalon structure anterior and inferior to the thalamus) and the pituitary gland (hypophysis), which is suspended by the infundibulum and cradled in the sella turcica of the sphenoid bone. The pituitary consists of two embryologically distinct lobes: the posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis, neural tissue) and anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis, glandular tissue), which collectively secrete nine hormones including growth hormone, prolactin, thyroid-stimulating hormone, adrenocorticotropic hormone, follicle-stimulating hormone, luteinizing hormone, antidiuretic hormone, oxytocin, and melanocyte-stimulating hormone. The posterior pituitary receives axons from hypothalamic neurons in the paraventricular and supraoptic nuclei via the hypothalamic–hypophyseal tract; it stores and releases oxytocin (which stimulates uterine contractions and the milk ejection reflex) and antidiuretic hormone (which increases water reabsorption in kidney tubular cells and is regulated by blood osmolarity). The anterior pituitary contains three regions (pars distalis, pars intermedia, and pars tuberalis) and manufactures hormones regulated by hypothalamic releasing and inhibiting hormones that travel through the hypophyseal portal system originating from the superior hypophyseal artery. Growth hormone promotes protein synthesis and tissue building through direct mechanisms and indirectly via insulin-like growth factors from the liver, while tropic hormones (TSH, ACTH, FSH, and LH) regulate other endocrine glands through negative feedback mechanisms. The section also addresses bisphenol A as an endocrine disruptor that mimics estrogen and antagon');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (86, 17, 'The Thyroid Gland', 7, '# The Thyroid Gland

The thyroid gland section covers three major topics: synthesis and release of thyroid hormones, functions of thyroid hormones, and thyroid dysfunction. Thyroid hormone synthesis involves TSH-stimulated active transport of iodide ions into follicle cells, oxidation to iodine in the colloid, and linkage by peroxidase enzymes to tyrosine residues in thyroglobulin to form triiodothyronine (T₃) and tetraiodothyronine/thyroxine (T₄); these hormones are released via endocytosis and lysosomal digestion, with 99 percent binding to thyroxine-binding globulins and plasma proteins in the bloodstream. Thyroid hormones function as metabolic hormones that bind to intracellular mitochondrial receptors to increase nutrient breakdown, oxygen consumption, and ATP production (the calorigenic effect), and they are essential for protein synthesis, fetal and childhood development, nervous system maturation, and reproductive function while increasing sensitivity to catecholamines from the adrenal medulla. The section also describes calcitonin, produced by parafollicular (C) cells in response to elevated blood calcium, which reduces serum calcium by inhibiting osteoclasts and promoting osteoblastic activity. Thyroid dysfunction results from iodine deficiency, which impairs T₃ and T₄ synthesis and causes goiter formation and cretinism (neonatal hypothyroidism with cognitive deficits and growth retardation), or from pituitary/thyroid tumors and autoimmune disease causing hyperthyroidism with elevated metabolic rate, sweating, tremors, and exophthalmos. Hypothyroidism presents with decreased metabolic rate, weight gain, cold intolerance, and reduced mental activity, while');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (87, 18, 'Accessory Structures of the Skin', 1, 'This section on accessory structures of the skin covers sweat glands, hair, and nails as key epidermal and dermal derivatives. Sweat glands (sudoriferous glands) are merocrine glands that develop from epidermal projections into the dermis; eccrine sweat glands are abundant on palms, soles, and forehead and secrete hypotonic sweat composed of water, salt, antibodies, and metabolic waste, while apocrine sweat glands are associated with hair follicles in armpits and genital regions and secrete thicker sweat with organic compounds subject to bacterial decomposition. Hair structures include the hair follicle, hair shaft, hair root, hair bulb (containing the hair matrix of mitotically active basal cells), and hair papilla (connective tissue with blood capillaries and nerve endings), with the hair shaft composed of three layers—medulla, cortex, and cuticle—whose arrangement determines hair texture. Hair growth involves basal cells of the hair bulb producing keratinocytes that are pushed outward through the three-layered follicle wall (internal root sheath, external root sheath, and glassy membrane), and the hair growth cycle consists of anagen phase (2–7 years), catagen phase (2–3 weeks), and telogen phase (2–4 months), with approximately 50 hairs lost and replaced daily. Hair functions include protection, sensory input via the hair root plexus, thermoregulation through the arrector pili smooth muscle innervated by the sympathetic nervous system, and communication, while hair color derives from melanin produced by melanocytes in the hair papilla. Nails are specialized epidermal structures at fingertips and toes composed of dead keratinocytes, with key components including the nail bed, nail body, nail root (matrix of stratum basale cells enabling continuous growth');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (88, 18, 'Diseases, Disorders, and Injuries of the Integumentary System', 2, 'This section on diseases, disorders, and injuries of the integumentary system covers skin cancer and common dermatological conditions. The subsection on skin cancer identifies three major types—basal cell carcinoma (originating in the stratum basale), squamous cell carcinoma (affecting keratinocytes in the stratum spinosum), and melanoma (arising from melanocytes)—all caused by UV radiation-induced DNA mutations, with basal cell carcinoma being most common and melanoma most fatal due to metastatic ability. The ABCDE diagnostic mnemonic (Asymmetry, Borders, Color, Diameter, Evolving) and additional signs for nodular melanoma aid early detection. The skin disorders subsection addresses inflammatory conditions like eczema (managed with moisturizers and corticosteroids) and acne (resulting from sebaceous gland overactivity, sebum and keratin blockage of hair follicles, and bacterial infection by Propionibacterium and Staphylococcus). Skin injury healing involves sequential processes of blood clot formation, fibroblast collagen deposition creating granulation tissue, capillary proliferation, and macrophage immune response. Burns are classified by depth—first-degree (epidermis only), second-degree (epidermis and partial dermis), third-degree (full thickness with nerve damage), and fourth-degree (involving muscle and bone)—with full-thickness burns requiring debridement and skin grafting. Additional injuries covered include scarring (from aberrant collagen deposition in basket-weave pattern), keloids (from collagen overproduction), atrophic scars (sunken appearance), bedsores or decubitus ulcers (from unrelieved pressure causing necrosis), stretch marks (from dermis stretching beyond elasticity), and calluses (from basal stem cell division responding to constant abrasion).');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (89, 18, 'Layers of the Skin', 3, '# Summary: Layers of the Skin

The Layers of the Skin section examines the structural organization and function of the epidermis and hypodermis. The epidermis is composed of keratinized, stratified squamous epithelium organized into four layers in thin skin (stratum basale, stratum spinosum, stratum granulosum, stratum corneum) and five layers in thick skin (with an additional stratum lucidum on palms and soles). The stratum basale contains basal cells (cuboidal stem cells), Merkel cells (touch receptors), and melanocytes (which produce melanin for UV protection), and attaches to the basal lamina via the basement membrane. The stratum spinosum consists of 8–10 keratinocyte layers joined by desmosomes and contains Langerhans cells (dendritic macrophages), while the stratum granulosum (3–5 layers) accumulates keratin and keratohyalin as cells flatten and die. Keratinocytes throughout the epidermis manufacture and store keratin, an intracellular fibrous protein providing hardness and water resistance, with dead keratinocytes in the stratum corneum shed and replaced over approximately 4 weeks. The hypodermis contains adipose tissue composed of adipocytes that function as energy reserves, thermal insulation, and trauma cushioning, with fat distribution regulated by hormones (testosterone, estrogen, insulin, glucagon, leptin) and genetic factors showing sex-based differences. The section also addresses skin pigmentation disorders including albinism (genetic inability of melanocytes to produce melanin) and vitiligo (patchy loss of melanin production), emphasizing the importance of skin examination in physical assessment and UV protection strategies.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (90, 19, 'Anatomy of the Lymphatic and Immune Systems', 1, '# Summary: Anatomy of the Lymphatic and Immune Systems

This section comprehensively covers the structural and functional organization of the lymphatic and immune systems, beginning with the three temporal phases of immune defense: barrier defenses, the innate immune response, and the adaptive immune response mediated by lymphocytes arising from hematopoietic stem cells in bone marrow. The lymphatic system drains approximately 3 liters of excess interstitial fluid daily through lymphatic capillaries, vessels, trunks, and ducts (the right lymphatic duct and thoracic duct) back into the bloodstream, while also transporting immune cells and facilitating absorption of dietary lipids via specialized lacteals in the small intestine. Primary lymphoid organs—the bone marrow (site of B cell maturation and hematopoiesis) and thymus gland (a bilobed organ with cortex and medulla where T cells/thymocytes mature)—are where lymphocytes develop and undergo selection; thymic involution occurs at ~3% tissue loss annually until age 35–45, then ~1% yearly, driven by decreased FOXN1 gene expression and influenced by sex hormones. Secondary lymphoid organs including lymph nodes (bean-shaped structures with afferent and efferent lymphatic vessels, subcapsular sinuses, cortical lymphoid follicles with germinal centers, and medullary cords), the spleen (divided into red pulp and white pulp, attached via the gastrosplenic ligament), and lymphoid nodules (including tonsils with tonsillar crypts, Peyer''s patches with M cells in the small intestine, and bronchus-associated lymphoid tissue along bronchial bifurcations) serve as staging areas where naïve lymphocytes mount active immune responses. Lymphocytes comprise four main types—B cells (producing antibodies that bind anti');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (91, 19, 'Barrier Defenses and the Innate Immune Response', 2, '# Summary: Barrier Defenses and the Innate Immune Response

This section covers the first and second lines of innate immune defense, beginning with barrier defenses—the skin (with keratinized epithelium, Langerhans cells, sweat and sebaceous glands), mucous membranes (nonkeratinized epithelium with ciliated cells), and secretions (lysozyme, low pH environments, normal flora)—that prevent pathogen entry and growth. The cellular components of innate immunity include phagocytes (macrophages, neutrophils, and dendritic cells) that engulf pathogens via phagocytosis into phagosomes that fuse with lysosomes, and natural killer (NK) cells that induce apoptosis through fas ligand binding or release of perforins and granzymes. Soluble mediators include cytokines and chemokines for cell communication and chemotaxis, early induced proteins such as interferons and C-reactive protein, and the complement system—a cascade of liver-made plasma proteins activated via the alternate pathway (spontaneous) or classical pathway (antibody-dependent)—that opsonizes pathogens and forms the membrane-attack complex (MAC) to disrupt osmotic balance. The inflammatory response, characterized by heat, redness, pain, and swelling, involves mast cell degranulation releasing histamine and leukotrienes, vasodilation and increased vascular permeability, and recruitment of phagocytes to destroy pathogens and remove debris, with chronic inflammation persisting in cases of foreign bodies or autoimmune diseases. Finally, pattern recognition receptors (PRRs) on phagocytes and NK cells recognize pathogen-specific molecules such as bacterial cell wall components and flagellar proteins, initiating phagocytosis or apoptosis to eliminate microbes.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (92, 19, 'Diseases Associated with Depressed or Overactive Immune Responses', 3, 'This section on diseases associated with depressed or overactive immune responses covers immunodeficiencies and hypersensitivity disorders resulting from immune system dysfunction. Immunodeficiencies include inherited conditions caused by gene mutations (such as severe combined immunodeficiency disease affecting both B cell and T cell arms of adaptive immunity) and acquired immunodeficiency from HIV infection, which uses CD4 receptors and co-receptors CCR5 or CXCR4 to infect cells and progress to AIDS; treatments include bone marrow transplants with hematopoietic stem cells, gene therapy, and antiretroviral drugs. The four types of hypersensitivity reactions are described: Type I involves allergen-specific IgE binding to mast cells causing degranulation and anaphylactic shock (treated with epinephrine); Type II involves IgG-mediated complement lysis in mismatched blood transfusions and erythroblastosis fetalis; Type III involves immune complex deposition in blood vessels as in systemic lupus erythematosus; and Type IV (delayed hypersensitivity) is a cellular immune response mediated by Th1 cells and cytotoxic T cells. Autoimmune responses occur when immune tolerance breaks down and the immune system attacks self-antigens through mechanisms including cross-reactivity between bacterial antigens and self-antigens, with genetic factors associated with MHC genes and environmental triggers influencing susceptibility. Over 80 autoimmune diseases are described with specific targets: celiac disease targets tissue transglutaminase; type I diabetes mellitus targets pancreatic beta cells; Graves'' disease and Hashimoto''s thyroiditis target thyroid-stimulating hormone receptor; lupus erythematosus targets nuclear DNA and proteins; myasthenia gravis targets acetylcholine receptors; and rheumatoid arthritis targets joint capsule antigens');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (93, 19, 'The Adaptive Immune Response: B-lymphocytes and Antibodies', 4, '# Summary: The Adaptive Immune Response: B-lymphocytes and Antibodies

This section covers the molecular and cellular basis of B-lymphocyte adaptive immunity, beginning with antibodies (immunoglobulins) as secreted forms of B cell receptors that mediate antigen recognition and immune responses through five classes (IgM, IgD, IgG, IgA, IgE). B cell differentiation in the bone marrow generates up to 100 trillion clones with diverse antigen receptors, while central tolerance mechanisms—clonal deletion and clonal anergy—and peripheral tolerance prevent self-reactive B cells from maturing; upon antigen activation, B cells differentiate into plasma cells that secrete antibodies or memory B cells that generate stronger secondary responses. Antibody structure consists of heavy and light chains linked by disulfide bonds, with an Fc region binding to effector cells and two identical antigen-binding sites; IgM functions as a pentameric B cell receptor activating complement, IgG predominates in secondary responses and crosses the placenta, dimeric IgA protects mucous membranes, and IgE binds mast cell Fc receptors triggering degranulation. Primary and secondary antibody responses differ in kinetics and magnitude, with memory B cells generated during clonal expansion enabling faster, stronger secondary responses. Active immunity develops through adaptive immune responses (naturally via pathogen exposure or artificially via vaccines), while passive immunity involves antibody transfer without immunological memory development, with naturally acquired passive immunity occurring through placental IgG transfer and breastfeeding IgA transfer. T cell-dependent antigens require B and T cell cooperation, with B cells recognizing native antigen, processing and presenting it on class II MHC molecules to Th2 cells that secrete activating cytokines, whereas T cell-independent antigens are repeated carbohydrate structures that directly crosslink surface antibodies');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (94, 19, 'The Immune Response against Pathogens', 5, 'This section on the immune response against pathogens covers the adaptive immune response''s role in controlling infections through mechanisms including seroconversion, antibody production, and clonal expansion, with HIV disease illustrating how anti-HIV antibodies correlate with decreased virus levels yet remain insufficient for complete viral clearance. The subsection on antibody-mediated responses describes neutralization in blood, lymph, and body fluids; IgE and IgA antibody responses; and the roles of Peyer''s patches, microfold cells, and dendritic cells in mounting mucosal immune responses, while introducing the hygiene theory to explain rising allergy incidence in developed countries. Defenses against viruses are mediated primarily by NK cells, interferons, and cytotoxic T cells, with complementary activities between NK cells and cytotoxic T cells targeting virally infected cells that downregulate MHC class I molecules, though pathogens like Mycobacterium tuberculosis evade these responses through structural adaptations. The section concludes by detailing pathogen evasion mechanisms including multiple bacterial strains (exemplified by Staphylococcus aureus and MRSA), viral mutation of surface molecules, genetic recombination between different viruses, and production of immunosuppressive molecules that impair immune function. Together, these subsections illustrate the dynamic interplay between adaptive immune mechanisms and pathogen evasion strategies in determining infection outcomes.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (95, 19, 'Transplantation and Cancer Immunology', 6, 'This section on Transplantation and Cancer Immunology covers four major topics in immune system function and clinical applications. The Rh Factor subsection explains blood typing based on surface antigens, describing how Rh-negative individuals develop anti-Rh antibody responses to Rh-positive fetal blood, resulting in erythroblastosis fetalis in subsequent pregnancies, and how anti-Rh antibody administration prevents this hemolytic disease. Tissue Transplantation discusses how matching MHC molecules (histocompatibility antigens) between donor and recipient is critical for transplant success, with cytotoxic T cell responses causing rejection when MHC molecules differ; the subsection details the six most polymorphic human MHC molecules, the role of immunosuppressive drugs like cyclosporine A, and the complication of graft-versus-host disease in bone marrow transplants. Immune Responses Against Cancer describes the three-stage immune response—elimination, equilibrium, and escape—in which cancer cells bearing tumor-specific antigens may mutate to evade immune recognition, and explains how cancer vaccines targeting the body''s own cancer cells represent a therapeutic approach to enhance anti-cancer immunity. Finally, Psychoneuroimmunology examines interactions between the immune system, central nervous system, and endocrine system, establishing that white blood cells have receptors for neurotransmitters, that hormones like cortisol suppress T cell mechanisms, and that short-term stress enhances innate immunity while chronic stress inhibits both innate and adaptive immune responses.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (96, 20, 'Appendicular Muscles of the Pelvic Girdle and Lower Limbs', 1, '# Summary: Appendicular Muscles of the Pelvic Girdle and Lower Limbs

This section covers the muscles of the lower limbs, organized by anatomical compartment and function. The thigh muscles include the iliopsoas group (psoas major and iliacus), gluteal muscles (gluteus maximus, gluteus medius, gluteus minimus), and deep hip rotators (piriformis, obturator internus, obturator externus, superior gemellus, inferior gemellus, quadratus femoris); the medial compartment muscles (adductor longus, adductor brevis, adductor magnus, pectineus, gracilis); the anterior compartment quadriceps femoris group (rectus femoris, vastus lateralis, vastus medialis, vastus intermedius) and sartorius; and the posterior compartment hamstring group (biceps femoris, semitendinosus, semimembranosus). Most thigh muscles originate on the pelvic girdle and insert on the femur, with the quadriceps tendon (patellar tendon) inserting into the patella and continuing as the patellar ligament to the tibial tuberosity, while hamstring tendons form the popliteal fossa. The leg contains three compartments divided by deep fascia: the anterior compartment (tibialis anterior, extensor hallucis longus, extensor digitorum longus, fibularis tertius) for dorsiflexion; the lateral compartment (fibularis longus, fibularis brevis); and the posterior compartment (gastrocnemius, soleus, plantaris, popliteus, flexor digitorum longus, flexor hallucis longus, tibialis posterior), whose superficial muscles insert onto the calcaneal tendon (Achilles tendon). The foot contains intr');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (97, 20, 'Axial Muscles of the Abdominal Wall, and Thorax', 2, 'This section covers the axial muscles of the abdominal wall and thorax, which function in respiration, trunk movement, and support of internal organs. The **Muscles of the Thorax** subsection describes the **diaphragm**, **external intercostal muscles**, **internal intercostal muscles**, and **innermost intercostal muscles**, which alter thoracic cavity volume during breathing; the diaphragm, the prime mover for inhalation and exhalation, originates at the sternum, ribs 6–12, and lumbar vertebrae and inserts at the central tendon, and contains three openings (caval opening, esophageal hiatus, and aortic hiatus) while participating in the Valsalva maneuver. The **Muscles of the Abdomen** subsection introduces the four pairs of anterolateral abdominal muscles—**external obliques**, **internal obliques**, **transversus abdominis**, and **rectus abdominis**—arranged in three layers oriented in different directions to enable trunk movements and protect internal organs, along with the posterior wall muscles including the **quadratus lumborum**; the **linea alba**, a white fibrous band formed by bilateral rectus sheaths, joins at the anterior midline and encloses the segmented rectus abdominis. The **Muscles of the Pelvic Floor and Perineum** subsection describes the **pelvic diaphragm**, composed of the **levator ani** (pubococcygeus and iliococcygeus) and **ischiococcygeus**, which support pelvic viscera and create skeletal muscle sphincters; the **perineum**, a diamond-shaped space divided into the urogenital triangle and anal triangle, contains sex-specific muscles including the **compressor urethrae** and **sphincter urethrovaginalis** in females and the **');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (98, 20, 'Axial Muscles of the Head, Neck, and Back', 3, 'This section on axial muscles of the head, neck, and back covers muscles organized by functional region and movement capability. Muscles of facial expression originate on the skull and insert into skin rather than bone, including the orbicularis oris, orbicularis oculi, occipitofrontalis (with frontal and occipital bellies connected by the epicranial aponeurosis), buccinator, and corrugator supercilii. Extrinsic eye muscles controlling eyeball movement include six extraocular muscles—four rectus muscles (superior, inferior, medial, lateral) and two oblique muscles (superior, inferior)—all originating from the common tendinous ring and inserting on the eyeball, along with the levator palpebrae superioris and orbicularis oculi for eyelid control. The four muscles of mastication (masseter, temporalis, medial pterygoid, lateral pterygoid) originate from skull bones and insert on the mandible to control jaw movement and chewing. Anterior neck muscles include suprahyoid muscles (digastric, stylohyoid, mylohyoid, geniohyoid) that elevate the hyoid bone and larynx during swallowing, and infrahyoid muscles (omohyoid, sternohyoid, thyrohyoid, sternothyroid) that depress these structures and control voice tone. Head movement is controlled by the sternocleidomastoid and posterior muscles including semispinalis capitis, splenius capitis, and longissimus capitis, which produce rotation, flexion, extension, and lateral flexion. The posterior neck and back contain major muscle groups—splenius muscles, erector spinae (iliocostalis, longissimus, spinalis), transversospinales (semispinalis, multifidus), segmental muscles (interspinales, in');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (99, 20, 'Muscles of the Pectoral Girdle and Upper Limbs', 4, 'This section on muscles of the pectoral girdle and upper limbs covers three major functional groups. The muscles that position the pectoral girdle include anterior thorax muscles (subclavius, pectoralis minor, serratus anterior) and posterior thorax muscles (trapezius, rhomboid major, rhomboid minor), which stabilize and move the scapula through depression, protraction, retraction, elevation, and rotation. The muscles that move the humerus are divided into axial muscles (pectoralis major, latissimus dorsi) and scapular muscles (deltoid, subscapularis, supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres major, teres minor, coracobrachialis), with the subscapularis, supraspinatus, infraspinatus, and teres minor forming the rotator cuff around the shoulder joint. The muscles that move the wrist, hand, and fingers include extrinsic muscles originating in the forearm, organized into anterior compartment muscles (flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus, flexor carpi ulnaris, flexor digitorum superficialis, flexor pollicis longus, flexor digitorum profundus) and posterior compartment muscles (extensor carpi radialis longus, extensor carpi radialis brevis, extensor digitorum, extensor digiti minimi, extensor carpi ulnaris, abductor pollicis longus, extensor pollicis brevis, extensor pollicis longus, extensor indicis). Retinacula on the palmar and dorsal surfaces sheath these tendons at the wrist, and irritation of the flexor digitorum superficialis tendon can cause Carpal Tunnel Syndrome. Intrinsic muscles originating and inserting within the hand include thenar muscles forming the thenar eminence and acting on the thumb, and');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (100, 21, 'Basic Structure and Function of the Nervous System', 1, '# Basic Structure and Function of the Nervous System

This section establishes the foundational anatomy and functional organization of the nervous system through two major subsections. The Central and Peripheral Nervous Systems subsection describes the anatomical divisions: the central nervous system (CNS) comprising the brain and spinal cord, and the peripheral nervous system (PNS) containing all nervous tissue beyond these cavities. Nervous tissue consists of neurons (responsible for communication) and glial cells (providing structural support), with neurons containing a soma, axon, and dendrites. Gray matter contains neuron cell bodies and dendrites, while white matter comprises myelinated axons; in the CNS these structures are called nuclei and tracts respectively, whereas in the PNS they are termed ganglia and nerves, as exemplified by the optic nerve becoming the optic tract at the optic chiasm. The Functional Divisions of the Nervous System subsection explains that functional divisions differ from anatomical divisions because structures can serve multiple functions, and identifies three basic nervous system functions: sensation (receiving external and internal stimuli including taste, smell, touch, sight, and hearing), integration (combining sensory perceptions with memories and emotion), and response (motor responses and glandular activation). The somatic nervous system governs conscious perception and voluntary skeletal muscle contraction, while the autonomic nervous system controls involuntary responses of smooth and cardiac muscle and glandular tissue for homeostasis; the enteric nervous system, a large independent PNS component, controls digestive system smooth muscle and glandular tissue.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (101, 21, 'Communication Between Neurons', 2, '# Summary: Communication Between Neurons

The section on Communication Between Neurons covers neurodegenerative diseases caused by protein misfolding and accumulation (proteopathy), including Alzheimer''s disease (characterized by beta-amyloid plaques in the cerebral cortex), Parkinson''s disease (linked to alpha-synuclein toxicity in the substantia nigra nucleus), and Creutzfeldt-Jacob disease (human variant of prion disease). Graded potentials are local changes in membrane potential determined by stimulus size and classified as depolarizing (from Na+ or Ca2+ entry) or hyperpolarizing (from K+ exit or Cl− entry). Three functional types of graded potentials are distinguished: generator potentials in sensory neuron dendrites, receptor potentials in sensory receptor cells, and postsynaptic potentials (PSPs)—subdivided into excitatory postsynaptic potentials (EPSPs) and inhibitory postsynaptic potentials (IPSPs). Graded potentials summate at the initial segment or axon hillock through spatial summation (multiple inputs at different locations) or temporal summation (multiple signals separated in time), where high-density voltage-gated Na+ channels initiate action potentials. Chemical synapses involve neurotransmitter release from presynaptic elements across the synaptic cleft to postsynaptic receptors, exemplified by the neuromuscular junction (NMJ) with its characteristic components: motor neuron axon terminals, acetylcholine, synaptic cleft, nicotinic acetylcholine receptors, sarcolemma, and acetylcholinesterase. Electrical synapses represent an alternative connection type to chemical synapses in neuronal communication.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (102, 21, 'Nervous Tissue', 3, '# Nervous Tissue Summary

Nervous tissue consists of neurons and glial cells organized to transmit information throughout the body. Neurons are polarized cells with dendrites that receive information at synapses, a cell body (soma) containing the nucleus, an axon hillock (initial segment), and an axon that propagates nerve impulses to target cells via synaptic end bulbs; key structural features include myelin sheaths, nodes of Ranvier, and axon terminals. Neurons are classified by process number into four types: unipolar (pseudo-unipolar) sensory neurons with cell bodies in ganglia, bipolar neurons found in the olfactory epithelium and retina, multipolar neurons comprising all other neuron types, and anaxonic neurons where processes cannot be distinguished at standard magnification. Glial cells are supporting cells that assist neuronal communication; the CNS contains four types—astrocytes (star-shaped cells maintaining extracellular chemistry and contributing to the blood-brain barrier), oligodendrocytes (providing myelin insulation to multiple CNS axon segments), microglia (performing immune surveillance and phagocytosis), and ependymal cells (lining ventricles and producing cerebrospinal fluid at the choroid plexus). The PNS contains two glial types: satellite cells in ganglia providing support similar to astrocytes, and Schwann cells wrapping around single axon segments to provide myelin insulation. Myelin is a lipid-rich sheath composed of glial cell membrane phospholipids and integral proteins that facilitates electrical signal transmission along axons.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (103, 21, 'The Action Potential', 4, '# Summary: The Action Potential

This section covers the electrical properties and mechanisms underlying action potentials in excitable cells. The subsection on Electrically Active Cell Membranes establishes the structural basis for electrical signaling, including the phospholipid bilayer, ion channels (ligand-gated, mechanically-gated, voltage-gated, and leakage channels), and the sodium/potassium pump—an ATPase that maintains ion concentration gradients using ATP energy. Ion channel selectivity operates through electrochemical exclusion and size exclusion mechanisms, allowing selective passage of Na+ and K+ across the hydrophobic core of the membrane. The Potassium Concentration subsection explains how K+ concentration gradients are maintained normally and disrupted during pathological events like stroke or ischemia, with astrocytes playing a critical role in K+ buffering and restoration of ion balance through support of the Na+/K+ pump. The Membrane Potential subsection defines the resting membrane potential (-70 mV) maintained by ion concentration gradients and leakage channels, then describes the complete action potential cycle: depolarization triggered by Na+ influx through opened channels, reaching threshold (-55 mV) and peak (+30 mV); repolarization driven by voltage-gated K+ channel opening at -50 mV; and hyperpolarization overshoot. The subsection details voltage-gated Na+ channel structure (activation and inactivation gates) and voltage-gated K+ channel kinetics, establishing the absolute and relative refractory periods that govern action potential frequency and neuronal excitability.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (104, 22, 'Overview of the Neurological Exam', 1, '# Summary: Overview of the Neurological Exam

The neurological exam is a rapid assessment tool organized into five major sections based on the principle of localization of function—the concept that circumscribed CNS locations are responsible for specific functions. The mental status exam evaluates cerebrum and cerebral cortex functions, including language localized to the left hemisphere between the superior temporal lobe and posterior frontal lobe with connections through the inferior parietal lobe. The cranial nerve exam tests the function of the 12 cranial nerves and their associated structures connecting to the diencephalon and brain stem by assessing sensory and motor functions. The motor and sensory exams are associated with the spinal cord and spinal nerves, with sensory deficits suggesting problems in sensory systems between the spinal cord and brain. The coordination exam and gait exam assess cerebellum function and the ability to perform complex coordinated movements, with gait exam specifically evaluating motor function of walking. Major causes of neurological deficits include circulatory damage (stroke/CVA and transient ischemic attack/TIA), infectious diseases affecting astrocytes and microglia, traumatic injury, and neurodegenerative diseases (Alzheimer''s disease, Parkinson''s disease, Huntington''s disease, amyotrophic lateral sclerosis/ALS, Creutzfeldt–Jacob disease, and multiple sclerosis/MS). The neurological exam helps differentiate acute events from chronic or global conditions and clarifies whether deficits reflect discrete or widespread neurological loss.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (105, 22, 'The Coordination and Gait Exams', 2, 'This section on the Coordination and Gait Exams covers the cerebellum''s critical role in motor learning and coordination assessment. The cerebellum, comprising 10 percent of brain mass, supports procedural memory and motor learning through classical conditioning mechanisms. Anatomically, the cerebellum connects to the brain stem via three white matter bundles—the middle cerebellar peduncle (MCP) linking to the pons, the superior cerebellar peduncle (SCP) connecting to the midbrain and forebrain, and the inferior cerebellar peduncle (ICP) connecting to the medulla—with the cerebrocerebellum planning appendicular movements while the spinocerebellum and vestibulocerebellum coordinate axial musculature for balance and gait. The coordination exam assesses cerebellar function through subtests including tremor checks, rebound response, alternating movements (finger-to-nose, rapid hand movements, heel-to-shin sliding), and speech tasks like repeating "lah-kah-pah" to evaluate biceps/triceps switching and tongue/lip/palate coordination. Posture and gait assessment includes station testing, the Romberg test (distinguishing proprioceptive from cerebellar deficits), tandem gait, and heel-or-toe walking, with ataxia—loss of coordination from cerebellar damage, sensory deficits, or vestibular dysfunction—presenting as wide stance to compensate for impaired balance. The Field Sobriety Test applies coordination exam principles to assess alcohol intoxication by evaluating the cerebellum''s sensitivity to ethanol through straight-line walking, fingertip-to-nose testing with eyes closed, alphabet recitation, and rapid alternating speech movements.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (106, 22, 'The Cranial Nerve Exam', 3, '# The Cranial Nerve Exam

This section examines the clinical assessment of cranial nerves and their associated neural pathways, beginning with hemispheric specialization and the corpus callosum''s role in integrating sensory information between left and right cerebral hemispheres, with language functions localized to the left hemisphere in 95 percent of the population. The sensory cranial nerves (CN I, II, VIII, V, VII, IX) are tested through examination of the olfactory epithelium and olfactory bulb, optic nerve and optic chiasm, vestibulocochlear nerve and inner ear structures, and trigeminal system nuclei, with clinical procedures including olfactory discrimination, Snellen chart visual acuity testing, ophthalmoscopic inspection, Romberg test and vestibulo-ocular reflex for equilibrium, Rinne and Weber tests for hearing, and cotton-tipped applicator testing for trigeminal sensation. Gaze control is mediated by the oculomotor (CN III), trochlear (CN IV), and abducens (CN VI) nerves innervating the extraocular muscles, coordinated through the medial longitudinal fasciculus connecting the superior colliculus and paramedian pontine reticular formation, with clinical testing assessing conjugate movements and detecting internuclear ophthalmoplegia or vestibulo-ocular reflex deficits. The facial (CN VII), glossopharyngeal (CN IX), vagus (CN X), and hypoglossal (CN XII) nerves control oral cavity and facial functions, including gustation via salty, sour, bitter, and sweet stimuli; the gag reflex; pharyngeal and laryngeal muscle contraction for swallowing and speech; salivation through parasympathetic fibers synapsing in the pterygopalatine and otic ganglia');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (107, 22, 'The Mental Status Exam', 4, '# Summary: The Mental Status Exam

The Mental Status Exam section establishes that the cerebrum controls the CNS, PNS, and musculature, with failures in cerebral connections manifesting as tremors, incoordination, or unilateral neglect; cerebral function is assessed through its control of the nervous system and musculature via simple behavioral elicitation such as asking the patient to state their name. The cerebral cortex, a thin layer of gray matter approximately 2.55 mm thick, is the seat of higher mental functions including memory, learning, language, and conscious perception; Brodmann''s cytoarchitectural study identified approximately 50 distinct cortical regions that correlate with functional localization, including primary cortical areas (Brodmann areas 1, 2, 3, 4, 17, and 22) and association areas. Primary somatosensory cortex (Brodmann areas 1–3 in the postcentral gyrus) processes touch, vibration, pain, temperature, and proprioception organized by the sensory homunculus, while primary motor cortex (area 4) and premotor cortex (area 6) control movement; integrative areas such as the posterior parietal cortex coordinate multisensory information and motor functions. The mental status exam assesses four groups of cognitive abilities: orientation and memory (tested via date, location, and identity questions, plus three-word recall and reverse recitation tasks), language and speech (comprehension and fluency subtests), sensorium (conscious sensory perception and praxis), and judgment and abstract reasoning (localized to prefrontal cortex). Memory consolidation involves the hippocampus and medial temporal lobe structures converting short-term memories from the prefrontal lobe into long-term episodic memory stored in multimodal cortical integration areas, as demonstrated by patient Henry Molaison''s anterograde');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (108, 22, 'The Sensory and Motor Exams', 5, '# Summary: The Sensory and Motor Exams

This section examines the structural and functional organization of the spinal cord and the clinical assessment of sensory and motor function. The spinal cord''s gray matter—organized into dorsal horns (sensory), ventral horns (motor), and lateral horns—and white matter columns—dorsal, anterior, and lateral—establish the anatomical basis for understanding neurological deficits. Spinal cord hemisection injuries produce characteristic dissociated sensory loss patterns: the dorsal column pathway (conveying fine touch and proprioception) ascends ipsilaterally and is damaged on the side of injury, while the spinothalamic pathway (conveying pain and temperature) decussates immediately upon entry and ascends contralaterally, thus bypassing hemisection damage. The sensory examination tests somatic senses organized by dermatomes that project to the postcentral gyrus, using modalities including light touch, pain perception, vibration, tactile movement, and proprioception, with discrimination subtests such as two-point discrimination, double simultaneous stimulation, and the Romberg test assessing dorsal column and posterior parietal lobe function. The motor exam evaluates the two-neuron motor pathway: upper motor neurons originating from the precentral gyrus via the corticospinal tract (which decussates in the medulla) and lower motor neurons in the ventral horn projecting to skeletal muscle, with assessment of muscle structure, tone, and strength through inspection, palpation, and resistance testing. Reflex testing—including deep tendon reflexes mediated by the muscle spindle and ventral horn motor neuron, and superficial reflexes like the plantar reflex modified by the descending corticospinal tract—evaluates lower motor neuron function and helps localize lesions to the upper or lower motor neuron.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (109, 23, 'Anatomy and Physiology of the Ovarian Reproductive System', 1, '# Summary: Anatomy and Physiology of the Ovarian Reproductive System

This section comprehensively covers the anatomy and physiological processes of the female reproductive system. The external and internal female genitalia include the vulva (mons pubis, labia majora, labia minora, clitoris, hymen), vagina with its protective Lactobacillus flora, and ovaries supported by the mesovarium, suspensory ligament, and ovarian ligament, with internal anatomy consisting of the ovarian surface epithelium, tunica albuginea, cortex, and medulla. The ovarian cycle encompasses oogenesis (production of gametes from oogonia through primary and secondary oocytes) and folliculogenesis (development of primordial, primary, secondary, and tertiary follicles), regulated by GnRH, FSH, and LH, culminating in ovulation of the secondary oocyte and formation of the corpus luteum. The uterine tubes (fallopian tubes) transport the oocyte via smooth muscle contractions and ciliated cell action, with fertilization typically occurring in the ampulla; bacterial ascension through patent tubes can cause pelvic inflammatory disease. The uterus consists of the perimetrium, myometrium, and endometrium (stratum basalis and stratum functionalis), with position maintained by the broad, round, and uterosacral ligaments. The menstrual cycle comprises three phases—menses, proliferative, and secretory—controlled by ovarian hormones and characterized by endometrial shedding, rebuilding, and preparation for implantation. Additional topics include HPV infection and cervical cancer risk factors, hormonal birth control mechanisms that inhibit FSH and LH to prevent ovulation, and breast anatomy including mammary glands, lactiferous ducts, and alveoli involved in lactation.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (110, 23, 'Anatomy and Physiology of the Testicular Reproductive System', 2, '# Summary: Anatomy and Physiology of the Testicular Reproductive System

This section comprehensively covers male reproductive anatomy and physiology, beginning with the structure of the testes, seminiferous tubules, and the cellular basis of spermatogenesis. The testes, housed in the scrotum and regulated by the dartos and cremaster muscles for thermoregulation, contain seminiferous tubules lined with Sertoli cells (sustentacular cells forming the blood–testis barrier) and spermatogonia (stem cells) that undergo spermatogenesis over approximately 64 days to produce haploid spermatozoa. Formed sperm consist of three regions—head (containing the acrosome with lysosomal enzymes), mid-piece (packed with mitochondria for ATP production), and tail (containing the flagellum for motility)—with approximately 100 to 300 million sperm produced daily. Sperm transport occurs through the epididymis (where maturation and motility acquisition occur over ~12 days), ductus deferens, and ejaculatory duct, with contributions from three accessory glands: seminal vesicles (60% of semen volume, providing fructose), prostate gland (secreting alkaline fluid), and bulbourethral glands (releasing lubricating pre-ejaculate). The penis, composed of three erectile tissue chambers (corpora cavernosa and corpus spongiosum), achieves erection through vasocongestion mediated by nitric oxide-induced smooth muscle relaxation of penile arteries. Endocrine regulation involves the hypothalamic-pituitary-testicular axis: pulsatile GnRH from the hypothalamus stimulates LH and FSH secretion from the anterior pituitary, with LH promoting testosterone synthesis by Leydig cells and FSH promoting sper');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (111, 23, 'Development of the Male and Female Reproductive Systems', 3, '# Development of the Male and Female Reproductive Systems

This section covers embryonic sex determination and differentiation, puberty, and menopause in humans. Embryonic development is initiated by the Y chromosome and SRY gene, which recruit genes to develop testes from bipotential gonads; in the absence of SRY, oogonia and primordial follicles form in the primitive ovary. Testosterone secreted by Leydig cells and anti-Müllerian hormone secreted by sustentacular cells direct bipotential tissues toward male structures (glans penis, epididymis, ductus deferens, seminal vesicles), while their absence allows development of female structures (glans clitoris, uterus, uterine tubes, vagina). Chromosomal intersex conditions such as XXY, XYY, and XO can result from nondisjunction during meiosis. Puberty is controlled by coordinated hormonal release from the hypothalamus (GnRH), anterior pituitary (LH and FSH), and gonads (testosterone or estrogen), initiating spermatogenesis and folliculogenesis along with secondary sex characteristics including voice deepening and facial hair in males and breast development and fat deposition in females. Puberty onset is influenced by genetics, environment, nutrition, and leptin secretion by adipose cells, with menarche occurring progressively earlier in recent history. Menopause, defined as complete cessation of menstruation for one year typically between ages 50–52, results from ovarian follicle depletion causing decreased inhibin and estrogen levels; the peri-menopause is characterized by irregular cycles, endometrial hyperplasia, and symptoms including hot flashes and vaginal dryness. Post-menopausal consequences of low estrogen include increased cardiovascular disease and osteoporosis, which may be managed with hormone therapy');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (112, 24, 'Embryonic Development of the Respiratory System', 1, '# Summary: Embryonic Development of the Respiratory System

Respiratory system development begins at week 4 of gestation when ectodermal tissue from the anterior head region invaginates posteriorly to form olfactory pits, which fuse with endodermal tissue of the developing pharynx to form the nasal cavity. The lung bud, a dome-shaped structure extending from the foregut, develops into the laryngotracheal bud, with the proximal portion forming the trachea and the distal end forming bronchial buds that progressively branch until all segmental bronchi form by week 16. By week 16, respiratory bronchioles form, marking completion of major airway structures, followed by vascularization and alveolar precursor development; type I and type II pneumocytes differentiate around week 19, with type II cells secreting pulmonary surfactant. From week 24 to term, major growth and maturation occur as alveolar precursors develop further, pulmonary surfactant production increases to adequate levels by month 8, pulmonary capillaries expand to create gas exchange surface area, and the respiratory membrane develops. Fetal breathing movements, observable from weeks 20–21, involve muscle contractions that inhale and exhale amniotic fluid, pulmonary surfactant, and mucus; these movements are influenced by maternal factors including hyperglycemia, hypoglycemia, and tobacco use, and may promote alveoli formation and maturation. At birth, thoracic cavity compression during passage through the birth canal expels amniotic fluid, and the first inhalation occurs within 10 seconds, with pulmonary surfactant critical for reducing alveolar surface tension and enabling lung inflation. Respiratory distress syndrome (RDS) affects premature infants born before sufficient pulmonary surfactant production, occurring in up to 50% of infants');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (113, 24, 'Gas Exchange', 2, 'This section on Gas Exchange covers fundamental principles including **pressure**, **partial pressure (Px)**, **atmospheric pressure**, **Dalton''s law**, and **Henry''s law** that govern gas movement. The textbook explains **external respiration** (gas exchange at the **respiratory membrane** in lung **alveoli**) and **internal respiration** (gas exchange in tissues), both occurring via **simple diffusion** along **partial pressure gradients**. Oxygen diffuses from **alveoli** (~104 mm Hg) into **pulmonary capillaries** (~40 mm Hg) due to a 64 mm Hg gradient, while carbon dioxide diffuses from capillaries (~45 mm Hg) into **alveolar air** (~40 mm Hg) in the opposite direction. Key anatomical structures include the **respiratory membrane**, **alveoli**, **pulmonary capillaries**, **pulmonary artery** (carrying deoxygenated blood), **pulmonary veins** (returning oxygenated blood), **bronchioles**, **pulmonary arterioles**, **erythrocytes**, and **hemoglobin**. The textbook emphasizes that **ventilation** (air movement into/out of lungs) and **perfusion** (blood flow in pulmonary capillaries) must be compatible for efficient gas exchange. Additionally, **greater partial pressure of carbon dioxide in alveoli causes bronchioles to increase diameter**, and **greater partial pressure of oxygen in alveoli** influences local vascular responses to optimize the ventilation-perfusion relationship.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (114, 24, 'Modifications in Respiratory Functions', 3, '# Modifications in Respiratory Functions Summary

This section examines alterations to normal respiratory function, beginning with **hyperpnea** and **hyperventilation**—increased ventilation that meets metabolic oxygen demand versus increased ventilation independent of cellular needs, respectively. Exercise-induced hyperpnea is driven by neural mechanisms including **psychological stimuli**, **motor neuron activation**, and **proprioceptors** (in muscles, joints, and tendons) signaling the **respiratory centers of the brain**, rather than solely by chemical factors like blood oxygen or carbon dioxide levels. At high altitudes, decreased atmospheric pressure and partial pressure of oxygen reduce **hemoglobin saturation** (67% at 19,000 feet versus 98% at sea level) and limit oxygen diffusion across the **respiratory membrane** into **pulmonary capillaries**. Tissues at altitude maintain adequate oxygen at rest through increased proportional oxygen release from hemoglobin and elevated **BPG production by erythrocytes**, but physical exertion depletes the small venous oxygen reserve, causing **acute mountain sickness (AMS)** with symptoms including nausea, vomiting, fatigue, lightheadedness, and potentially pulmonary or cerebral edema. **Acclimatization**—the respiratory system''s chronic adjustment to high altitude—occurs when low tissue oxygen stimulates the kidneys to produce **erythropoietin (EPO)**, which stimulates erythrocyte production and increases circulating hemoglobin to transport available oxygen, enabling physical exertion without altitude sickness.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (115, 24, 'Organs and Structures of the Respiratory System', 4, '# Summary: Organs and Structures of the Respiratory System

The respiratory system is organized into conducting and respiratory zones, each with distinct anatomical structures and functions. The conducting zone routes air while filtering debris and pathogens through structures including the external nose, nasal cavity (separated by the nasal septum), nasal conchae, paranasal sinuses, pharynx, larynx (containing the thyroid cartilage, cricoid cartilage, epiglottis, and vocal cords), trachea (supported by 16–20 C-shaped hyaline cartilage rings), primary bronchi, and terminal bronchioles. The nasal epithelium contains pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium with goblet cells and capillaries that warm and humidify incoming air, while serous and mucus-producing cells secrete antimicrobial lysozyme and defensins. The respiratory zone begins where terminal bronchioles join respiratory bronchioles and continues through alveolar ducts and alveolar sacs composed of approximately 200 μm diameter alveoli with elastic walls. Alveoli contain three major cell types: type I alveolar cells (squamous epithelial cells covering 97% of alveolar surface area), type II alveolar cells (which secrete pulmonary surfactant to reduce surface tension), and alveolar macrophages (phagocytic immune cells); alveolar pores connect adjacent alveoli to maintain equal air pressure. The respiratory membrane—approximately 0.5 μm thick and formed by type I alveolar cell epithelium, elastic basement membrane, and capillary endothelium—enables gas exchange through simple diffusion. The section also addresses asthma, a chronic inflammatory disease characterized by bronchospasms and edema triggered by environmental factors, infections, exercise, or stress.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (116, 24, 'The Lungs', 5, 'The Lungs section covers the structural and functional anatomy of the respiratory organs. The Gross Anatomy of the Lungs subsection describes the pyramid-shaped, paired lungs connected to the trachea by the right and left bronchi, with the right lung containing three lobes and the left lung containing two lobes separated by fissures; each lobe subdivides into bronchopulmonary segments supplied by tertiary bronchi and individual arteries, and further into pulmonary lobules separated by interlobular septa. The Pleura of the Lungs subsection explains that the visceral pleura lines the lung surface while the parietal pleura connects to the thoracic wall, mediastinum, and diaphragm, with pleural fluid secreted between these layers lubricating surfaces and creating surface tension that maintains lung position during ventilation. The Blood Supply and Nervous Innervation of the Lungs subsection details how the pulmonary artery carries deoxygenated blood that branches into the pulmonary capillary network at the alveoli to form the respiratory membrane, where oxygenated blood then drains via pulmonary veins through the hilum. This subsection also describes parasympathetic innervation via the vagus nerve causing bronchoconstriction and sympathetic innervation from thoracic ganglia (T2-T5) causing bronchodilation, with sensory fibers converging at the pulmonary plexus to enable autonomic reflexes and gas regulation.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (117, 24, 'The Process of Breathing', 6, '# The Process of Breathing

This section covers pulmonary ventilation, the physiological mechanisms controlling breathing, and clinical applications of respiratory measurements. Pulmonary ventilation comprises inspiration and expiration, driven by pressure gradients where the diaphragm and external intercostal muscles contract during normal inspiration to expand the thoracic cavity and decrease intra-alveolar pressure, while passive expiration relies on lung tissue elasticity and increased intrapulmonary pressure. The textbook distinguishes four breathing modes—eupnea, diaphragmatic breathing, costal breathing, and hyperpnea—and identifies accessory muscles (scalenes, obliques, internal intercostals) that participate during forced breathing. Intrapleural pressure remains negative due to competing elastic forces and pleural fluid surface tension, while pulmonary surfactant secreted by type II alveolar cells reduces surface tension and prevents alveolar collapse. Respiratory volumes and capacities—including tidal volume, expiratory reserve volume, inspiratory reserve volume, residual volume, total lung capacity, vital capacity, inspiratory capacity, and functional residual capacity—are measured by spirometry to diagnose respiratory diseases, with anatomical and alveolar dead space representing non-gas-exchanging airway regions. Neural control of ventilation involves the medulla oblongata (dorsal and ventral respiratory groups), pontine respiratory group (apneustic and pneumotaxic centers), and central and peripheral chemoreceptors that respond to carbon dioxide concentration, hydrogen ion concentration, and blood oxygen levels to regulate respiratory rate and depth. The hypothalamus and limbic system modulate respiration in response to emotions, pain, and body temperature, while second-hand smoke exposure causes significant health effects including increased lung cancer risk and respiratory infections through toxic and carcinogenic compounds.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (118, 24, 'Transport of Gases', 7, 'The Transport of Gases section covers oxygen and carbon dioxide transport mechanisms in blood. Oxygen transport relies on hemoglobin, a metalloprotein in erythrocytes composed of four subunits, each containing heme that binds oxygen to form oxyhemoglobin (HbO₂), with each hemoglobin molecule carrying up to four oxygen molecules through cooperative binding. The oxygen–hemoglobin saturation/dissociation curve demonstrates how partial pressure of oxygen determines hemoglobin saturation (95–99% in healthy individuals) at the respiratory membrane and dissociation at tissues, regulated by factors including temperature, 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate (BPG), and the Bohr effect (pH-dependent oxygen release). Fetal hemoglobin exhibits greater oxygen affinity than maternal hemoglobin due to structural differences, compensating for lower partial pressure gradients at the placenta. Carbon dioxide transport occurs through three mechanisms: dissolved CO₂ in plasma (7–10%), bicarbonate (HCO₃⁻) in plasma (70%), and carbaminohemoglobin binding to hemoglobin''s globin portions (20%), with carbonic anhydrase catalyzing carbonic acid formation and the chloride shift facilitating bicarbonate movement into plasma. The Haldane effect describes how oxygen saturation influences hemoglobin''s affinity for carbon dioxide, with deoxygenated hemoglobin readily binding CO₂ while oxygenated hemoglobin does not. At pulmonary capillaries, dissolved CO₂ and carbaminohemoglobin release CO₂ across the respiratory membrane for exhalation, while at tissue capillaries, lower partial pressure of CO₂ causes diffusion from tissues into blood.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (119, 25, 'Central Processing', 1, '# Central Processing

This section describes how the central nervous system processes somatic sensory information through ascending pathways and cortical integration. The **dorsal column system** (composed of **fasciculus gracilis** and **fasciculus cuneatus**) and **spinothalamic tract** relay sensory information from **dorsal root ganglion neurons** through the **spinal cord** to the **thalamus** and **postcentral gyrus**, with the dorsal column system mediating **touch** and **proprioception** while the spinothalamic tract mediates **pain** and **temperature**. The **trigeminal pathway** carries somatosensory information from the face and head via the **trigeminal ganglion**, **spinal trigeminal nucleus**, and **chief sensory nucleus**, with second neurons decussating along the **trigeminothalamic tract** to reach the **thalamus** and **primary somatosensory cortex**. Cortical processing maintains **topographic mapping**, where the **sensory homunculus** in the **somatosensory cortex** preserves positional correspondence from receptor cells, with lower body represented medially and upper body laterally, while sensitive areas like fingers are exaggerated and less sensitive areas compressed. The visual pathway maintains topography from the **retina** through the **optic chiasm** to the **primary visual cortex**, with the right visual field processed in the left visual cortex and superior visual information inverted in the cortex. Visual processing occurs in two streams: the **ventral stream** (temporal lobe) identifies visual stimuli and their significance for memory, while the **dorsal stream** (parietal lobe) locates objects in space and guides body movements. Depth perception relies on **monocular depth cues** and **binocular depth cues**, with disparate retinal images processed to extract three-dimensional information, while **pros');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (120, 25, 'Motor Responses', 2, '# Motor Responses Summary

Section 14 on Motor Responses covers the neural pathways and structures controlling voluntary and reflex skeletal muscle movement. Cortical responses initiate in the prefrontal cortex, secondary motor cortices (premotor cortex and supplemental motor area), and primary motor cortex in the precentral gyrus, which organize movement through a motor homunculus with topographical representation favoring fine motor control. Descending pathways—the corticobulbar tract (ipsilateral) and corticospinal tract (largely contralateral, crossing at the pyramidal decussation)—originate from Betz cells and synapse with lower motor neurons; the lateral corticospinal tract controls appendicular muscles while the anterior corticospinal tract controls axial trunk muscles with bilateral innervation. The extrapyramidal system comprises the tectospinal, reticulospinal, and vestibulospinal tracts that modulate posture, balance, and muscle tone through input from the basal nuclei and cerebellum, which compares motor commands with proprioceptive feedback via the red nucleus and rubrospinal tract. Ventral horn lower motor neurons form motor units and synapse at the neuromuscular junction, where acetylcholine depolarizes the muscle fiber sarcolemma to produce contraction. Multiple reflex types—withdrawal reflexes, stretch reflexes mediated by muscle spindle receptors, and specialized reflexes including the corneal reflex and Babinski reflex—utilize interneurons in the dorsal horn to coordinate agonist and antagonist muscle activity.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (121, 25, 'Sensory Perception', 3, '# SECTION SUMMARY: Sensory Perception

This section covers the foundational mechanisms and organization of sensory perception in the somatic nervous system. The introduction establishes core concepts: sensation (activation of sensory receptor cells), perception (central processing into meaningful patterns), and the role of receptors in detecting stimuli and generating action potentials relayed to the central nervous system (CNS) for central integration. Sensory receptors are classified by three criteria—cell type and structural organization (free nerve endings, encapsulated nerve endings, specialized receptor cells), location relative to stimuli (exteroceptors, interoceptors, proprioceptors), and functional type based on stimulus transduction (chemoreceptors, osmoreceptors, nociceptors, mechanoreceptors, thermoreceptors). Sensory modalities distinguish between general senses (distributed throughout the body) and special senses (dedicated organs), with detailed coverage of gustation (taste buds in papillae with five recognized tastes mediated by ion channels and G protein–coupled receptors, innervated by facial, glossopharyngeal, and vagus nerves) and olfaction (olfactory receptor neurons in the olfactory epithelium binding odorant molecules via G protein–coupled receptors, projecting through the cribriform plate to the olfactory bulb and primary olfactory cortex without thalamic relay). Sensory nerve organization describes the structural routing of afferent axons through spinal nerves, dorsal roots, dorsal root ganglia, and posterior horn, with topographical arrangement in special senses and contralateral spinal pathways versus mostly ipsilateral cranial nerve systems. The section concludes with anosmia (loss of smell from trauma or neuronal damage) and introduces auditory and vestibular systems, including ear anatomy (auricle, auditory canal, tympanic membrane, ossicles, Eust');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (122, 26, 'Connective Tissue Supports and Protects', 1, 'Connective tissue is characterized by three essential components—cells, ground substance, and protein fibers—whose amounts and organization correlate directly with tissue function. Connective tissues are classified into three broad categories: connective tissue proper (loose and dense types), supportive connective tissue (cartilage and bone), and fluid connective tissue (blood and lymph), each distinguished by ground substance characteristics and fiber organization. Connective tissue proper contains fibroblasts, fibrocytes, adipocytes, and immune cells that produce an extracellular matrix of ground substance (hyaluronic acid and proteoglycans) combined with three fiber types—collagen fibers (tensile strength), elastic fibers (elasticity), and reticular fibers (anchoring)—organized as loose connective tissue (areolar, adipose, reticular) or dense connective tissue (regular, elastic, and irregular). Supportive connective tissues include cartilage, which features chondrocytes in lacunae surrounded by perichondrium and exists in three types (hyaline, fibrocartilage, elastic), and bone, the hardest connective tissue with osteocytes in lacunae, a mineralized matrix containing hydroxyapatite, and two structural forms (cancellous and compact). Tendinitis, inflammation of tendons (fibrous connective tissue attaching muscle to bone), results from repetitive motion and microtrauma, causing pain and limited healing due to tendons'' poor blood supply.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (123, 26, 'Epithelial Tissue', 2, 'Epithelial tissue is a highly cellular, avascular tissue with little extracellular material that lines body surfaces and cavities, characterized by an apical surface exposed to the environment and a basal surface adjacent to the basement membrane (formed by the basal lamina and reticular lamina). Epithelial cells are held together by three types of cell-to-cell junctions: tight junctions that block intercellular substance movement and create selective barriers; anchoring junctions (desmosomes, hemidesmosomes, and adherens junctions) that stabilize tissues using adhesion molecules like cadherin and integrins; and gap junctions that facilitate intercellular communication through small molecule and ion passage. Epithelial tissues are classified by cell shape (squamous, cuboidal, columnar) and number of layers (simple, stratified, pseudostratified, transitional), with simple epithelia performing absorption and secretion in the digestive and reproductive systems, while stratified epithelia provide protection against wear and tear in the skin and mouth. Specialized epithelial types include ciliated columnar epithelium in the respiratory tract, pseudostratified columnar epithelium containing goblet cells in airways, and transitional epithelium in the urinary bladder that changes shape as it fills. Glandular epithelium comprises endocrine glands (ductless structures releasing hormones into the bloodstream) and exocrine glands (duct-bearing structures classified structurally as unicellular or multicellular and functionally by secretion mode: merocrine, apocrine, or holocrine). Exocrine glands are further classified by product type—serous glands producing enzyme-rich secretions, mucous glands producing viscous mucin, and mixed glands containing both components—with examples including sweat glands, sebaceous glands, and salivary glands.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (124, 26, 'Tissue Injury and Aging', 3, '# Tissue Injury and Aging Summary

This section addresses three major topics affecting tissue integrity across the lifespan. The subsection on Tissue and Aging describes senescence and its effects on key structures including skin, hair follicles, elastic and collagen fibers, bones, fibrous cartilage disks, joints, muscles, blood vessels, airways, brain, and spinal cord, with outward signs including skin thinning, graying hair, loss of muscle tone, decreased height, and joint stiffening, while cellular-level changes involve telomere shortening and altered cell membranes reducing efficient transport. The subsection on Tissue Injury and Repair explains inflammation as the body''s initial response to biological, chemical, physical, or radiation injury, involving the four cardinal signs (redness, swelling, pain, local heat) and inflammatory mediators including histamine from mast cells, prostaglandins from injured cells, and recruited neutrophils and macrophages; the inflammatory cascade includes local vasodilation, increased blood vessel permeability causing edema, and tissue repair phases of clotting, fibroblast collagen replacement, angiogenesis producing granulation tissue, and clot dissolution, with outcomes of primary or secondary union and potential scar formation. The subsection on Tissues and Cancer defines cancer as uncontrolled cell growth with invasion and metastasis, involving mutations and epigenetic modifications affecting regulatory signals, with classification by tissue origin as carcinomas, myelomas, leukemias, and sarcomas, and treatment approaches including surgery, radiation, chemotherapy, and hormonal therapy targeting rapidly dividing cells.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (125, 26, 'Types of Tissues', 4, '# Types of Tissues Summary

This section covers the four broad categories of human tissues—epithelial, connective, muscle, and nervous—which are characterized by shared morphological features, orderly cellular arrangement, and specific functions that maintain overall body health. Tissues are organized structures found in complex multicellular eukaryotes, and disruptions to tissue structure indicate injury or disease, detectable through histology, the microscopic study of tissue appearance, organization, and function. Tissue membranes are thin layers of cells covering the body exterior, organs, internal passageways, and moveable joint cavities, divided into connective tissue membranes and epithelial membranes. Connective tissue membranes form solely from connective tissue and encapsulate organs; synovial membranes, a connective tissue membrane type, line freely movable joints (shoulder, elbow, knee) where fibroblasts release hyaluronan to form synovial fluid, enabling frictionless bone movement and nutrient exchange with blood. Epithelial membranes consist of epithelium attached to connective tissue and include mucous membranes (mucosae) lining digestive, respiratory, excretory, and reproductive tracts with mucus and lamina propria support; serous membranes composed of mesodermally derived mesothelium lining coelomic cavities and covering internal organs, including the pleura (lungs), pericardium (heart), and peritoneum (abdominal cavity with mesenteries); and the cutaneous membrane (skin), a stratified squamous epithelial membrane with keratinized cells protecting against desiccation and pathogens. Serous fluid secreted by mesothelium lubricates serous membranes to reduce friction between organs.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (126, 27, 'Endocrine Regulation of Kidney Function', 1, '# Endocrine Regulation of Kidney Function

This section covers the endocrine and paracrine regulation of kidney function through multiple hormone systems. The renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system begins when juxtaglomerular (JG) cells of the afferent arteriole release renin in response to decreased extracellular fluid volume and blood pressure; renin converts angiotensinogen (produced by the liver) into angiotensin I, which is converted to angiotensin II by ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) in the lungs, and angiotensin II acts as a potent vasoconstrictor of afferent and efferent arterioles while stimulating aldosterone release from the adrenal cortex to promote Na+ reabsorption and water retention. Antidiuretic hormone (ADH), a 9-amino acid peptide from the posterior pituitary, maintains plasma osmolarity and blood pressure by stimulating aquaporin protein movement into principal cells of the collecting ducts to enable water recovery by osmosis. Endothelins, 21-amino acid peptide vasoconstrictors produced by endothelial cells and mesangial cells, are stimulated by angiotensin II, bradykinin, and epinephrine; in diabetic kidney disease, chronically elevated endothelin damages podocytes and reduces GFR. Natriuretic hormones—atrial natriuretic hormone (ANH) from heart atria and B-type natriuretic peptide (BNP) from heart ventricles—inhibit aldosterone and ADH release to promote sodium and water excretion while ANH increases GFR through afferent arteriole vasodilation and efferent arteriole vasoconstriction.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (127, 27, 'Gross Anatomy of the Kidney', 2, 'The Gross Anatomy of the Kidney section establishes that kidneys are highly vascularized organs receiving approximately 25 percent of cardiac output at rest, reflecting their critical role in filtration and homeostasis. The renal hilum serves as the entry and exit site for renal arteries, renal veins, nerves, lymphatics, and ureters, with the renal pelvis emerging from the hilum and formed from major and minor calyxes that use smooth muscle peristalsis to funnel urine into the ureter. Renal arteries branch sequentially from the descending aorta into segmental arteries, interlobar arteries, arcuate arteries, cortical radiate arteries, and afferent arterioles that service approximately 1.3 million nephrons per kidney. Nephrons, the functional units of the kidney, consist of a renal corpuscle containing a glomerulus and Bowman''s capsule where afferent arterioles form high-pressure capillaries (~200 µm diameter) for blood filtration, followed by an efferent arteriole draining into peritubular capillaries and vasa recta for solute and water recovery. The kidney''s vascular arrangement constitutes a portal system—the only one where an arteriole connects two capillary beds—with renal veins returning blood directly to the inferior vena cava. The cortex contains all renal corpuscles, proximal convoluted tubules, and distal convoluted tubules, while cortical nephrons have short loops of Henle confined to the cortex and juxtamedullary nephrons (~15%) have long loops of Henle extending into the medulla.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (128, 27, 'Microscopic Anatomy of the Kidney', 3, '# Microscopic Anatomy of the Kidney

Nephrons are the functional units of the kidney that perform filtration, reabsorption, and secretion to modify blood filtrate into urine, while also regulating blood pressure through renin production, red blood cell production via erythropoietin (EPO), and calcium absorption through vitamin D conversion. The renal corpuscle consists of the glomerulus—a high-pressure capillary bed between afferent and efferent arterioles—surrounded by Bowman''s capsule, which captures 10–20% of plasma filtrate and directs it to the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT); the filtration membrane permits passage of substances smaller than 4–8 nm while excluding blood cells and large proteins through fenestrations, podocytes with filtration slits, basement membrane, and negatively charged proteins. The juxtaglomerular apparatus (JGA), located where afferent and efferent arterioles contact the distal convoluted tubule (DCT), contains macula densa cells that monitor Na+ concentration and fluid flow rate, regulating glomerular filtration rate (GFR) and renin release in response to blood osmolarity changes. Renin initiates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system by cleaving angiotensinogen to angiotensin I, which is converted by ACE (from lungs) to angiotensin II, a systemic vasoconstrictor that stimulates aldosterone release from the adrenal cortex to increase Na+ reabsorption and water retention. The proximal convoluted tubule, lined with simple cuboidal epithelium featuring brush border microvilli, actively transports solutes including Na+, Cl–, and glucose. The loop of Henle contains descending thick (simple cuboidal) and thin (simple squamous) segments that');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (129, 27, 'Physiology of Urine Formation', 4, 'This section on the physiology of urine formation covers the mechanisms by which the kidneys modify plasma composition to produce urine. The introductory subsection establishes that nephrons, collecting ducts, and aquaporins (water channel proteins) are essential structures for urine formation, with aquaporins enabling water movement across cell membranes; renal failure—sudden or gradual dysfunction of renal anatomy and/or physiology—presents with symptoms including weakness, lethargy, shortness of breath, edema, anemia, metabolic acidosis or alkalosis, arrhythmias, uremia, and oliguria. The glomerular filtration rate (GFR) subsection defines GFR as the volume of filtrate formed per minute (approximately 125 mL/min in males and 105 mL/min in females, yielding 180 L/day and 150 L/day respectively, with 99% reabsorbed to produce 1–2 liters of daily urine), and describes the filtration membrane composed of the glomerulus, Bowman''s capsule, podocyte processes, and capillary endothelial fenestrations that function as a semipermeable barrier preventing passage of blood cells, platelets, and large proteins while allowing smaller solutes. The net filtration pressure (NFP) subsection explains that NFP (approximately 10 mm Hg) is calculated from glomerular blood hydrostatic pressure, capsular hydrostatic pressure, and blood colloid osmotic pressure, and that afferent capillary smooth muscle autoregulates to maintain steady GFR despite systemic blood pressure changes, with mean arterial pressure requiring maintenance above 60 mm Hg for adequate glomerular filtration; GFR is clinically assessed using inulin or creatinine measurements.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (130, 27, 'Regulation of Fluid Volume and Composition', 5, '# Regulation of Fluid Volume and Composition

This section explains the integrated mechanisms by which the body maintains blood volume, electrolyte composition, and acid-base balance through renal regulation. Volume-sensing mechanisms involve baroreceptors in the aorta and carotid sinuses, granular cells in the juxtaglomerular apparatus, and atrial cardiomyocytes that detect blood pressure and volume changes and trigger coordinated hormonal responses including renin release, angiotensin II and aldosterone production, ANH secretion, and ADH release to regulate GFR, vasoconstriction, and water and sodium reabsorption in the DCT and collecting ducts via aquaporin channels. Diuretics including caffeine, alcohol, hydrochlorothiazide, and mannitol increase urine volume by inhibiting ADH, blocking Na+/Cl– symporters, or promoting osmotic water loss. Electrolyte regulation involves the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system controlling sodium reabsorption in the DCT and collecting ducts, and PTH stimulating calcium reabsorption in the DCT while regulating phosphate excretion. Acid-base homeostasis is maintained through active H+ secretion by the PCT and intercalated cells of the collecting ducts via proton pumps, with H+ buffered by bicarbonate, dihydrogen phosphate ion, and ammonia to regulate urine pH.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (131, 27, 'Regulation of Renal Blood Flow', 6, '# Summary: Regulation of Renal Blood Flow — Sympathetic Nerves

The kidneys receive sympathetic innervation via the celiac plexus and splanchnic nerves, with reduced sympathetic stimulation causing vasodilation and increased renal blood flow at rest, while increased sympathetic activity causes vasoconstriction of afferent arterioles through norepinephrine and stimulates epinephrine release from the adrenal medulla to redirect blood away from the kidneys during stress. When blood pressure decreases, sympathetic nerves trigger renin release, initiating angiotensin II production (a potent vasoconstrictor) and aldosterone production to enhance sodium and water retention. The kidneys maintain a stable glomerular filtration rate (GFR) across varying blood pressures through two autoregulatory mechanisms: the myogenic mechanism, wherein afferent arteriolar smooth muscle cells contract in response to increased pressure and relax in response to decreased pressure; and the tubuloglomerular feedback mechanism, which involves the juxtaglomerular apparatus (JGA) and macula densa cells of the distal convoluted tubule (DCT) that sense fluid flow rate and sodium concentration. Macula densa cells release ATP and adenosine as paracrine factors to stimulate juxtaglomerular cells to constrict the afferent arteriole when GFR increases, while nitric oxide (NO) relaxes the afferent arteriole to fine-tune this response. Only a 10 mm Hg pressure differential across the glomerulus is required for normal GFR, demonstrating that renal blood flow is highly sensitive to small changes in afferent arterial pressure.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (132, 27, 'The Urinary System and Homeostasis', 7, 'The Urinary System and Homeostasis section covers three major homeostatic functions of the kidneys. The Vitamin D Synthesis subsection explains how the kidney activates vitamin D through hydroxylation, converting calcidiol to calcitriol (1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol) to regulate calcium and phosphate homeostasis, and describes erythropoietin (EPO) production—a 193-amino acid protein synthesized 85% by the kidney and 15% by the liver—which stimulates red blood cell formation in response to hypoxia, exercise, and blood loss; renal failure results in anemia and impaired oxygen delivery. The Blood Pressure Regulation subsection details how the kidneys maintain blood pressure through water and sodium reabsorption via ADH-stimulated aquaporin channels in the collecting ducts and the renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system, which involves the kidneys releasing renin to convert hepatic angiotensinogen to angiotensin I, pulmonary ACE converting it to angiotensin II for immediate vasoconstriction, and adrenal cortical aldosterone release for sustained sodium and water reabsorption; loss of renal function impairs vascular volume control, causing hypotension or hypertension with serious cardiovascular consequences. The Stem Cells and Repair of Kidney Damage subsection introduces mesenchymal stem cells as unspecialized cells capable of self-reproduction and differentiation that show therapeutic promise for acute kidney injury, which affects 7–10 percent of hospitalized patients; mesenchymal stem cells demonstrate protective effects by reducing post-surgical kidney damage through growth factor release via endocrine and paracrine signaling, though stem cell therapy remains in early stages with limited human studies.');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO sections(section_id, chapter_id, title, section_order, summary) VALUES (133, 27, 'Tubular Reabsorption', 8, 'Tubular reabsorption in the nephron involves the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT), loop of Henle, distal convoluted tubule (DCT), and collecting ducts working together to recover filtered water and solutes through five membrane transport mechanisms: active transport, diffusion, facilitated diffusion, secondary active transport, and osmosis. In the PCT, approximately 67% of water, sodium, and potassium; nearly 100% of glucose, amino acids, and vitamins; and 50% of chloride are reabsorbed via Na+/glucose symport, Na+/K+ ATPase active exchange, and obligatory water reabsorption, while bicarbonate recovery depends on carbonic anhydrase catalyzing CO₂ and water formation in the lumen. The loop of Henle''s countercurrent multiplier system enables sodium and water recovery through the descending limb''s aquaporin channel proteins (permitting 15% water reabsorption) and the thick ascending limb''s Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase pumps and Na⁺/Cl⁻ symporters (creating a hyperosmotic medullary interstitium), while vasa recta preserve the osmolar gradient. The DCT recovers 10–15% of filtered water and reabsorbs sodium via aldosterone-stimulated Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase, with calcium reabsorption enhanced by parathyroid hormone (PTH) receptors inducing luminal calcium channels and calcitriol inducing calcium-binding proteins. The collecting ducts contain principal cells regulated by ADH and aldosterone to control water reabsorption via aquaporin insertion and sodium recovery via Na⁺/K⁺ channels and pumps, and intercalated cells that secrete or reabsorb H⁺');

-- subsections: 355 rows
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (1, 1, 'Body Cavities and Serous Membranes', 'Body Cavities and Serous Membranes', 'This subsection introduces the major body cavities and serous membranes that organize and protect internal organs. Key structures include the dorsal (posterior) cavity—subdivided into the cranial cavity (housing the brain) and spinal cavity/vertebral cavity (enclosing the spinal cord)—and the ventral (anterior) cavity, which subdivides into the thoracic cavity and abdominopelvic cavity. The thoracic cavity contains the lungs and heart (located in the mediastinum) and is enclosed by the rib cage; the diaphragm separates it from the abdominopelvic cavity below. The abdominopelvic cavity is further distinguished into the abdominal cavity (housing digestive organs) and pelvic cavity (housing reproductive organs), and is clinically divided into nine regions or four quadrants. Serous membranes (serosa) cover cavity walls and organs in the thoracic and abdominopelvic cavities; they consist of parietal layers (lining cavity walls) and visceral layers (covering organs), with a fluid-filled serous space between them. The three serous cavities are: the pleural cavity (enclosed by pleura, surrounding the lungs), the pericardial cavity (enclosed by pericardium, surrounding the heart), and the peritoneal cavity (enclosed by peritoneum, surrounding abdominal and pelvic organs); all secrete serous fluid to reduce friction and protect viscera during organ movement.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (2, 1, 'Body Planes', 'Body Planes', 'This subsection introduces body planes and sections as fundamental tools for anatomical interpretation. A section is a two-dimensional surface created by cutting through a three-dimensional structure; modern medical imaging devices produce "virtual sections" called scans. A plane is an imaginary two-dimensional surface passing through the body, and correct interpretation of body sections and scans requires understanding the plane along which the section was made. The three planes commonly used in anatomy and medicine are: the sagittal plane (divides body or organ vertically into right and left sides), which includes the midsagittal or median plane (runs down the middle) and the parasagittal plane or longitudinal section (divides into unequal sides); the frontal plane or coronal plane (divides body or organ into anterior and posterior portions); and the transverse plane (divides body or organ horizontally into upper and lower portions). Students should search for: section, scan, plane, sagittal plane, midsagittal plane, median plane, parasagittal plane, longitudinal section, frontal plane, coronal plane, transverse plane.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (3, 1, 'Directional Terms', 'Directional Terms', 'This subsection introduces ten directional anatomical terms essential for describing relative locations of body structures: anterior (ventral), posterior (dorsal), superior (cranial), inferior (caudal), lateral, medial, proximal, distal, superficial, and deep. The textbook defines anterior as the front of the body (toes are anterior to the foot), posterior as the back (popliteus is posterior to the patella), superior as above (orbits are superior to the oris), and inferior as below or toward the coccyx (pelvis is inferior to the abdomen). Lateral describes the side (pollex is lateral to the digits), while medial describes toward the middle (hallux is the medial toe). For limbs, proximal indicates nearer to the point of attachment or trunk (brachium is proximal to the antebrachium), and distal indicates farther from attachment (crus is distal to the femur). Superficial describes closer to the body surface (skin is superficial to the bones), while deep describes farther from the surface (brain is deep to the skull). Students should search for: anterior, ventral, posterior, dorsal, superior, cranial, inferior, caudal, lateral, medial, proximal, distal, superficial, deep, directional terms, anatomical terminology.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (4, 2, 'Negative Feedback', 'Negative Feedback', 'Negative feedback systems maintain homeostasis through three components: a sensor (receptor) that monitors physiological values, a control center that compares values to the set point and activates effectors when deviation occurs, and an effector that reverses the change to restore normal range. In blood glucose control, pancreatic beta cells detect excess glucose (stimulus) and release insulin, which signals skeletal muscle fibers, adipocytes, and liver cells to take up glucose; pancreatic alpha cells then detect the resulting decrease in blood glucose and stop insulin release. Temperature regulation involves the brain''s temperature regulation center, heat-loss center, and heat-gain center: the heat-loss center stimulates blood vessel dilation in the skin, activates sweat glands, and increases respiration depth to promote heat loss; the heat-gain center reduces skin blood flow, diverts blood into deep veins, triggers shivering in skeletal muscles, stimulates the thyroid gland to release thyroid hormone, and signals the adrenal glands to release epinephrine to increase metabolism and heat production. Search terms: negative feedback, sensor, receptor, control center, effector, set point, stimulus, homeostasis, blood glucose, pancreatic beta cells, insulin, pancreatic alpha cells, temperature regulation, heat-loss center, heat-gain center, sweat glands, shivering, thyroid hormone, epinephrine, adrenaline.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (5, 2, 'Positive Feedback', 'Positive Feedback', 'Positive feedback intensifies physiological deviations from the normal range and is normal only when a definite endpoint exists. Key structures and processes include: the cervix (contains stretch-sensitive nerve cells), pituitary gland, oxytocin (hormone), uterine smooth muscles, blood vessel wall, and clotting proteins. In childbirth, labor contractions push the baby toward the cervix, stretch-sensitive nerve cells in the cervix send messages to the brain, the brain causes the pituitary gland to release oxytocin into the bloodstream, and oxytocin causes stronger uterine contractions, creating a cycle that stops only when the baby is born and cervical stretching halts. In blood loss response, reduced blood perfusion to vital organs triggers the release of clotting substances in the injured blood vessel wall; each clotting step stimulates release of more clotting substances, accelerating the cascade and sealing the damaged area. Search terms: positive feedback, homeostasis, childbirth, labor contractions, cervix, oxytocin, pituitary gland, blood clotting, blood loss, perfusion, blood pressure.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (6, 3, 'Modern Medical Imaging', 'Modern Medical Imaging', 'Modern medical imaging encompasses several noninvasive techniques that produce three-dimensional or functional images analyzed by computers. **Key structures and techniques:** Computed tomography (CT) or computerized axial tomography (CAT) scanner, Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), Functional MRI (fMRI), Positron emission tomography (PET), and Ultrasonography. **Explicit relationships:** CT uses cross-sectional X-rays to reveal soft tissue details in the brain and thoracic and abdominal viscera; MRI is based on nuclear physics principles where matter exposed to magnetic fields and radio waves emits radio signals, with malignant tissue giving off different signals than normal body tissue; fMRI detects blood flow concentration in body parts to study brain activity and brain abnormalities and diseases; PET uses radiopharmaceuticals to illustrate physiologic activity including nutrient metabolism and blood flow; Ultrasonography uses high-frequency sound waves converted into real-time images and is used to study heart function, blood flow, and fetal growth and development. **Search terms:** Tomography, cross-sectional X-rays, electromagnets, radiopharmaceuticals, high-frequency sound waves, soft tissue scanning, tumor detection, radiation exposure, open MRI scanning, metallic implants, operator-dependent image quality.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (7, 4, 'Narrow Range of Atmospheric Pressure', 'Narrow Range of Atmospheric Pressure', 'This subsection introduces atmospheric pressure as a critical requirement for human life, defined as pressure exerted by atmospheric gases (primarily nitrogen and oxygen) that constantly presses on the body. Key concepts include: atmospheric pressure keeps gases dissolved in body fluids; sudden exposure to very low pressure causes nitrogen gas in blood to expand and form bubbles that block blood vessels and rupture cells. The subsection establishes that breathing—the exchange of oxygen intake and carbon dioxide release—depends on precise atmospheric pressure. Altitude sickness occurs when high-altitude atmospheric pressure is reduced, impairing gas exchange and causing shortness of breath, confusion, headache, lethargy, and nausea; mountain climbers use supplemental oxygen to counteract effects of both low oxygen levels and low barometric pressure. Search terms: atmospheric pressure, pressure, nitrogen gas, blood gases, breathing, oxygen, carbon dioxide, altitude sickness, barometric pressure, gas exchange.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (8, 4, 'Narrow Range of Temperature', 'Narrow Range of Temperature', 'This subsection establishes that human survival requires body temperature to remain within a narrow range from just below to just above 37°C (98.6°F), as chemical reactions and enzyme function depend on this range; when temperature deviates significantly, enzymes lose their normal structure and ability to function, preventing metabolism from proceeding. The body responds to heat exposure through sweating, wherein sweat evaporation from skin removes thermal energy; this response requires adequate water from extracellular fluid, and becomes ineffective in humid environments where sweat cannot evaporate, allowing internal body temperature to rise dangerously. The body responds to cold exposure through shivering (random muscle movement that generates heat) and increased breakdown of stored energy; when energy reserves deplete and core temperature drops significantly, red blood cells lose their ability to give up oxygen, depriving the brain of oxygen critical for ATP production, causing confusion, lethargy, loss of consciousness, and death. Additionally, the body reduces blood circulation to the extremities (hands and feet) during cold exposure to prevent blood cooling and maintain core warmth, though severe cold exposure to fingers and toes can still cause frostbite, permanent tissue damage, gangrene, and amputation. Key search terms: body temperature, 37°C (98.6°F), enzymes, metabolism, sweating, evaporation, extracellular fluid, shivering, stored energy, red blood cells, oxygen, ATP production, blood circulation, extremities, frostbite, gangrene.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (9, 5, 'The Levels of Organization', 'The Levels of Organization', 'This subsection introduces the hierarchical levels of structural organization in the human body, progressing from the chemical level through the organism level. The chemical level comprises subatomic particles (proton, electron, neutron), atoms, and molecules (water molecules, proteins, sugars), which serve as the chemical building blocks of all body structures. The cellular level includes cells with flexible membranes enclosing cytoplasm and organelles, where all functions of human physiology are performed or initiated. Higher organizational levels include tissues (groups of similar cells performing a specific function), organs (anatomically distinct structures composed of two or more tissue types), and organ systems (groups of organs working together to perform major functions), with the textbook covering eleven distinct organ systems. The organism level represents the highest level of organization—a living being with cellular structure capable of independently performing all physiologic functions necessary for life. In multicellular organisms including humans, all cells, tissues, organs, and organ systems work together to maintain the life and health of the organism. **Search terms:** chemical level of organization, cellular level, tissue, organ, organ system, organism, subatomic particles, atoms, molecules, cytoplasm, organelles, biological sex, XX individuals, XY individuals.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (10, 6, 'Blood Supply to the Brain', 'Blood Supply to the Brain', 'This subsection describes the arterial and venous blood supply to the brain and spinal cord, emphasizing multiple protective routes and regulatory mechanisms. Key structures include the aorta, common carotid arteries, internal carotid arteries (entering the cranium via the carotid canal in the temporal bone), external carotid arteries, vertebral arteries (protected by transverse foramina of cervical vertebrae and entering via the foramen magnum), anterior spinal artery, basilar artery, and the circle of Willis—a confluence of the internal carotid and basilar artery branches that maintains cerebral perfusion despite blockage in one vessel. Venous drainage occurs through dural sinuses and veins: the superior sagittal sinus (which absorbs CSF from the meninges in the longitudinal fissure), occipital sinuses, straight sinus, confluence of sinuses, transverse sinuses, sigmoid sinuses, and jugular veins. The subsection also explains the orthostatic reflex, in which stretch receptors at the bases of the common carotids detect blood pressure drops upon standing, triggering sympathetic nervous system activation to increase heart rate and maintain blood pressure. Search terms: aorta, common carotid arteries, internal carotid artery, external carotid artery, vertebral arteries, anterior spinal artery, basilar artery, circle of Willis, dural sinuses, superior sagittal sinus, transverse sinuses, sigmoid sinuses, jugular veins, orthostatic reflex, carotid canal, foramen magnum, transverse foramina.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (11, 6, 'Protective Coverings of the Brain and Spinal Cord', 'Protective Coverings of the Brain and Spinal Cord', 'The meninges are three protective connective tissue membranes covering the brain and spinal cord: the dura mater (thick fibrous outer layer anchored to the cranium and vertebral cavity), the arachnoid mater (thin middle layer forming a loose sac with spider-web-like trabeculae), and the pia mater (thin innermost membrane following gyri, sulci, and other CNS convolutions). The subarachnoid space between the arachnoid and pia mater contains cerebrospinal fluid (CSF), which circulates and provides a liquid cushion; arachnoid granulations filter CSF back into dural sinuses for drainage. The dura mater encloses major blood vessels entering the cranium and vertebral cavity, has infoldings that separate the cerebrum and cerebellum and surround the pituitary gland, and supports venous sinuses. Blood vessels nourishing CNS tissue are located between the pia mater and nervous tissue. A lumbar puncture is a clinical procedure that withdraws CSF by inserting a needle through the dura and arachnoid layers in the lower lumbar region, where the spinal cord does not extend, avoiding damage to central nervous tissue. Search terms: meninges, dura mater, arachnoid mater, pia mater, arachnoid trabeculae, subarachnoid space, cerebrospinal fluid (CSF), arachnoid granulations, dural sinuses, lumbar puncture.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (12, 6, 'The Ventricular System', 'The Ventricular System', '# The Ventricular System Summary

The ventricular system comprises four interconnected ventricles—the lateral ventricles (in the cerebrum), third ventricle (in the diencephalon), and fourth ventricle (between the pons/upper medulla and cerebellum)—all derived from the neural tube''s central canal, connected sequentially by the interventricular foramina, cerebral aqueduct, and apertures (median and lateral) that open into the subarachnoid space. Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) is produced by choroid plexuses (specialized membranes containing ependymal cells that filter blood) located in all four ventricles and by arachnoid granulations (outpocketings of the arachnoid membrane into dural sinuses), circulating approximately 500 milliliters daily through the ventricles, central canal, and subarachnoid space to remove metabolic wastes and provide cushioning for the CNS before reabsorption into the blood via jugular veins. The subsection also addresses meningitis—inflammation of the meninges (three fibrous layers surrounding the CNS)—caused by bacterial pathogens (Streptococcus, Staphylococcus, tuberculosis) or viral pathogens (enteroviruses, herpes virus, West Nile virus), presenting with fever, headache, neck soreness, and neurological symptoms including hearing loss, diagnosed via lumbar puncture through the dura mater and arachnoid membrane into the subarachnoid space, and treated with antibiotics for bacterial but not viral forms.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (13, 7, 'Brain Stem', 'Brain Stem', 'The brain stem comprises the midbrain and hindbrain (pons and medulla), emerging as a tapering cone from the forebrain to connect the brain to the spinal cord, with the cerebellum attached as a separate region. The midbrain, located between the thalamus and pons, divides into the tectum (roof) and tegmentum (floor) separated by the cerebral aqueduct; the tectum contains four colliculi—the inferior colliculus (part of the auditory pathway projecting to the thalamus) and superior colliculus (integrating visual, auditory, and somatosensory information for eye orientation). The pons serves as the main connection between the cerebellum and brain stem, with tegmental gray matter receiving descending forebrain input to the cerebellum, while the medulla (myelencephalon) contains white matter continuous with the spinal cord and tegmental gray matter processing cranial nerve information. The pons and medulla regulate cardiovascular and respiratory systems; cranial nerves connect through the brain stem providing sensory input and motor output for the head and neck; and major ascending and descending pathways between the spinal cord and cerebrum pass through the brain stem. The reticular formation, a diffuse gray matter region throughout the brain stem, relates to sleep, wakefulness, general brain activity, and attention. Search terms: brain stem, midbrain, pons, medulla, tectum, tegmentum, colliculi, inferior colliculus, superior colliculus, cerebral aqueduct, cranial nerves, reticular formation, myelencephalon.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (14, 7, 'The Cerebellum', 'The Cerebellum', 'The cerebellum is a "little brain" covered in gyri and sulci that compares motor commands from the cerebrum with sensory feedback from the periphery to coordinate movement. Descending fibers from the cerebrum branch to neurons in the pons, which project into the cerebellum carrying copies of motor commands sent to the spinal cord; simultaneously, sensory information from spinal and cranial nerves is relayed to the inferior olive nucleus in the medulla, which sends fibers into the cerebellum for comparison with descending cortical commands. The cerebellum receives proprioceptive information about movement and balance from muscles and joints through the inferior olive, and when discrepancies occur between the original motor command and sensory feedback, the cerebellum sends corrective commands to the midbrain. The midbrain then sends descending input to the spinal cord to correct messages to skeletal muscles, enabling coordinated movement. Search terms: cerebellum, gyri, sulci, cerebrum, pons, inferior olive, medulla, primary motor cortex, frontal lobe, spinal cord, proprioceptive information, midbrain, skeletal muscles, motor coordination.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (15, 7, 'The Cerebrum', 'The Cerebrum', '# Summary: The Cerebrum

The cerebrum, the brain''s iconic gray mantle, comprises the cerebral cortex (wrinkled outer gray matter) and subcortical structures beneath it. The longitudinal fissure separates the cerebrum into right and left cerebral hemispheres; the corpus callosum (white matter) provides the major pathway for interhemispheric communication and enables higher neurological functions including memory, emotion, and consciousness. The cerebral cortex''s gyri (ridges) and sulci (grooves) create anatomical landmarks—the lateral sulcus, central sulcus, and parieto-occipital sulcus—that delineate four lobes: frontal, parietal, temporal, and occipital. Brodmann''s areas (52 cytoarchitecturally distinct regions) correlate with localized functions: Brodmann''s areas 17–18 (occipital lobe) process primary visual perception; areas 41–42 (superior temporal lobe) process primary auditory sensation; Brodmann''s areas 1–3 (postcentral gyrus/primary somatosensory cortex) process tactile senses, proprioception, and kinesthesia; the precentral gyrus (primary motor cortex) contains upper motor neurons that instruct spinal cord cells to move skeletal muscles; the premotor area, frontal eye fields, and Broca''s area (left side, language production) control planned movements; and the prefrontal lobe mediates personality, short-term memory, and consciousness. Subcortical nuclei—including the basal nuclei (caudate, putamen, striatum, globus pallidus external and internal segments), basal forebrain (acetylcholine production), hippocampus, and amygdala—augment cortical processes; the basal nuclei compare cortical processing with', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (16, 7, 'The Spinal Cord', 'The Spinal Cord', '# Summary: The Spinal Cord

The spinal cord is a major CNS organ that maintains the neural tube structure and is divided into cervical, thoracic, lumbar, and sacral regions corresponding to vertebral column levels; spinal nerves pass through intervertebral foramina at respective levels, and the cauda equina forms as nerves grow with the vertebral column after the spinal cord ceases significant growth. Surface landmarks include the anterior median fissure, posterior median sulcus, posterolateral sulcus, dorsal (posterior) nerve root, and ventral (anterior) nerve root; dorsal and ventral terminology are used interchangeably with posterior and anterior. Developmentally, the basal plate (ventral midline) gives rise to motor neurons and becomes the anterior spinal cord, while the alar plate (dorsal side) gives rise to sensory neurons; posterior regions are responsible for sensory functions and anterior regions for motor functions. Gray matter appears H-shaped in cross-section and is subdivided into horns: the posterior horn processes sensory information, the anterior horn sends motor signals to skeletal muscles, and the lateral horn (thoracic, upper lumbar, and sacral regions only) contains autonomic motor neuron cell bodies; multipolar motor neurons in the anterior horn are among the largest cells in the body. White matter is organized into columns—posterior columns (ascending tracts carrying sensory information), anterior columns (between anterior horns), and lateral columns (between posterior and anterior horns)—with anterior and lateral columns containing both ascending and descending tracts that carry motor commands from the brain. The gray matter receives sensory input through dorsal roots and sends motor output through ventral roots, representing CNS interactions with peripheral structures; cervical and lumbar spinal cords have enlargements due to larger neuron populations.

**Search terms:** spinal cord, neural tube, cervical region, thoracic region,', 4);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (17, 8, 'Primary Vesicles', 'Primary Vesicles', 'During early neural tube development (beginning at 25 days), the anterior end undergoes enlargements that produce three sac-like structures called primary vesicles, which form the basic divisions of the developing brain. The three primary vesicles are: the prosencephalon (forebrain), the forward-most vesicle positioned at the anterior end; the mesencephalon (midbrain), located in the middle position; and the rhombencephalon (hindbrain), the posterior vesicle. The naming of these vesicles is based on Greek root words with the prefix enkephalon meaning "brain," and each prefix corresponds to the vesicle''s position along the developing nervous system. The anterior end of the neural tube develops into the brain while the posterior portion becomes the spinal cord, establishing the most basic arrangement of tissue in the nervous system. Students searching for this content should use the terms: primary vesicles, prosencephalon, mesencephalon, rhombencephalon, forebrain, midbrain, hindbrain, neural tube, and embryologic development of the brain.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (18, 8, 'Relating Embryonic Development to the Adult Brain', 'Relating Embryonic Development to the Adult Brain', 'Embryonic development of the neural tube establishes the neuraxis (anterior–posterior dimension) and primary vesicles (forebrain, midbrain, hindbrain) that organize the adult nervous system. The cephalic flexure, a major curve between the brain stem and forebrain, reorients the neuraxis so it ends in an anterior position at the cerebrum rather than superior. Secondary vesicles differentiate into adult structures: telencephalon becomes the cerebrum; diencephalon remains the diencephalon (positioned between cerebrum and rest of nervous system); mesencephalon becomes the midbrain; metencephalon becomes the pons and cerebellum; myelencephalon becomes the medulla; and the posterior neural tube becomes the spinal cord. Key anatomical relationships explained by embryonic origin include: the retina (diencephalon-derived) connects to the diencephalon via the optic tract and thalamus; the cerebellum (metencephalon-derived) has its largest white matter connection to the pons (also metencephalon) and connections to the medulla and midbrain (adjacent secondary vesicles), but no direct connection to the cerebrum; and the ventricles (lateral, third, cerebral aqueduct, fourth) are remnants of the neural tube''s hollow center. Search terms: neural tube, neuraxis, cephalic flexure, primary vesicles, secondary vesicles, telencephalon, diencephalon, mesencephalon, metencephalon, myelencephalon, cerebrum, brain stem, cerebellum, retina, optic tract, thalamus, hypothalamus, pons, medulla, midbrain, ventricles, cerebrospinal fluid.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (19, 8, 'Secondary Vesicles', 'Secondary Vesicles', 'This subsection describes how the three primary brain vesicles (forebrain/prosencephalon, midbrain/mesencephalon, and hindbrain/rhombencephalon) differentiate into five secondary vesicles during embryonic development. The prosencephalon divides into the telencephalon (which becomes the cerebrum) and the diencephalon (which gives rise to the thalamus and hypothalamus); notably, the eye cup develops in the embryonic diencephalon and becomes the retina, representing nervous tissue that originates in the CNS but becomes peripheral. The rhombencephalon differentiates into the metencephalon (which corresponds to the pons and gives rise to the cerebellum) and the myelencephalon (which corresponds to the medulla oblongata); the pons and cerebellum share the most significant connection because they develop from the same vesicle. The midbrain (mesencephalon) remains established at the primary vesicle stage and does not further subdivide. The brain stem is collectively defined as structures from the mesencephalon and rhombencephalon except the cerebellum, specifically including the midbrain, pons, and medulla. Key search terms: primary vesicles, secondary vesicles, prosencephalon, telencephalon, diencephalon, mesencephalon, rhombencephalon, metencephalon, myelencephalon, forebrain, midbrain, hindbrain, cerebrum, thalamus, hypothalamus, eye cup, retina, pons, cerebellum, medulla oblongata, brain stem.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (20, 8, 'The Neural Tube', 'The Neural Tube', 'This subsection describes the embryologic origin of the nervous system from the three germ layers and the formation of the neural tube. The ectoderm (outer tissue) gives rise to both the integumentary system and the nervous system; within the ectoderm, neuroectoderm differentiates into neuroepithelium, which forms a neural plate that buckles and folds to create a neural groove flanked by neural folds. As the neural folds converge, they form the neural tube, a hollow structure positioned just beneath the ectoderm, while cells from the neural folds separate to form the neural crest, which runs lateral to the neural tube. The neural crest migrates away from the nascent central nervous system (CNS) and develops into parts of the peripheral nervous system (PNS), including enteric nervous tissue, as well as non-nervous tissues such as craniofacial cartilage and bone and melanocytes. Beginning at 25 days of development, the anterior end of the neural tube develops into the brain and the posterior portion becomes the spinal cord. Key search terms: zygote, germ layers, endoderm, mesoderm, ectoderm, neuroectoderm, neuroepithelium, neural plate, neural groove, neural fold, neural tube, neural crest, central nervous system (CNS), peripheral nervous system (PNS), enteric nervous tissue, brain, spinal cord.', 4);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (21, 9, 'Development of the Skull', 'Development of the Skull', '# Summary: Development of the Skull

During embryonic development, the notochord forms dorsally in week three, and the overlying neural tube gives rise to the brain and spinal cord; by week four, mesoderm tissue on either side of the notochord differentiates into somites, which split into sclerotomes consisting of mesenchyme that will form fibrous connective tissues, cartilages, and bones. Skull bones develop through two mechanisms: intramembranous ossification produces the brain case bones (top and sides) through direct differentiation of mesenchymal cells into bone-producing cells, while endochondral ossification produces the facial bones and floor of the brain case through conversion of hyaline cartilage models into bone. During fetal development, brain case bones remain separated by fontanelles (soft spots), which are large areas of dense connective tissue located at junctions such as the anterior head at the frontal and parietal bones; fontanelles decrease in size and disappear by age 2, but skull bones remain separated at sutures containing dense fibrous connective tissue that allows continued growth as the brain enlarges. At birth, the brain case and orbits are disproportionally large compared to the underdeveloped maxilla and mandible and small paranasal sinuses and nasal cavity; during early childhood, the mastoid process enlarges, the mandible and frontal bone halves fuse, paranasal sinuses enlarge, and jaws expand as teeth appear, contributing to rapid facial growth.

**Search terms:** notochord, neural tube, somite, sclerotome, mesenchyme, intramembranous ossification, endochondral ossification, fontanelle, suture, brain case, facial bones, maxilla, mandible, paranasal sinuses, mastoid process.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (22, 9, 'Development of the Vertebral Column and Thoracic cage', 'Development of the Vertebral Column and Thoracic cage', '# Summary: Development of the Vertebral Column and Thoracic Cage

This subsection describes the embryonic development of vertebrae, ribs, and sternum from mesenchyme-derived tissues. Vertebral development begins with mesenchyme cell accumulation from sclerotomes around the notochord, which differentiate into hyaline cartilage models that undergo endochondral ossification; as vertebrae grow, the notochord largely disappears except for small persistent areas between adjacent vertebrae that contribute to intervertebral disc formation. Ribs initially develop as part of the vertebral cartilage model but separate from the vertebra by the eighth week in the thorax region; the rib cartilage then ossifies except for the anterior portion, which remains as costal cartilage. The sternum develops from paired hyaline cartilage models on either side of the anterior midline beginning in the fifth week; the rib cartilage models attach to the lateral sides of the developing sternum, the two sternal halves fuse along the midline and ossify, with the manubrium and body converting to bone first while the xiphoid process remains cartilaginous until late in life. Key search terms: sclerotome, notochord, hyaline cartilage model, endochondral ossification, intervertebral disc, costal cartilage, sternum, manubrium, xiphoid process.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (23, 10, 'Anterior View of Skull', 'Anterior View of Skull', 'The anterior skull consists of facial bones that support the eyes and facial structures, with the orbit and nasal cavity as dominant features. Key anatomical structures include: the orbit (bony socket housing the eyeball and associated muscles), supraorbital margin, supraorbital foramen (passage for sensory nerve to forehead skin), infraorbital foramen (emergence point for sensory nerve supplying anterior face below orbit), nasal cavity, nasal septum, upper and lower jaws with teeth, perpendicular plate of ethmoid bone (forms upper nasal septum), vomer bone (forms lower nasal septum), inferior nasal concha (independent bone), middle nasal concha (part of ethmoid bone), and superior nasal concha (part of ethmoid bone). The nasal septum divides the nasal cavity into halves, with the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid bone forming the upper portion and the vomer bone forming the lower portion. The nasal conchae project from the lateral walls of the nasal cavity, with the superior nasal concha located just lateral to the perpendicular plate in the upper nasal cavity. Search terms: anterior skull, facial bones, orbit, supraorbital margin, supraorbital foramen, infraorbital foramen, nasal cavity, nasal septum, perpendicular plate of ethmoid bone, vomer bone, inferior nasal concha, middle nasal concha, superior nasal concha, eyeball, upper eyelid.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (24, 10, 'Bones of the Brain Case', 'Bones of the Brain Case', '# Summary: Bones of the Brain Case

The brain case consists of eight bones—paired parietal and temporal bones, plus unpaired frontal, occipital, sphenoid, and ethmoid bones—that contain and protect the brain within the cranial cavity. The cranial cavity is bounded superiorly by the calvaria (skullcap) and laterally and posteriorly by flat skull bones; its floor, the base of the skull, is subdivided into anterior, middle, and posterior cranial fossae that increase in depth anteroposteriorly and correspond to housed brain regions. The parietal bones form the upper lateral skull and are bounded anteriorly by the frontal bone, inferiorly by the temporal bone, and posteriorly by the occipital bone; the temporal bone forms the lower lateral skull and contains subdivisions (squamous, zygomatic process, mastoid, and petrous portions) with landmarks including the external acoustic meatus, internal acoustic meatus, mandibular fossa, articular tubercle, styloid process, stylomastoid foramen, and carotid canal. The frontal bone forms the forehead and extends posteriorly to form the roof of the orbit and floor of the anterior cranial cavity; the occipital bone forms the posterior skull and contains the foramen magnum for spinal cord passage and occipital condyles that articulate with the first cervical vertebra. The sphenoid bone is a keystone bone joining most skull bones, containing the sella turcica with its hypophyseal fossa housing the pituitary gland, lesser and greater wings, and pterygoid plates. The ethmoid bone forms the roof and lateral walls of the upper nasal cavity, the upper nasal septum, and part of the anterior cranial fossa floor, featuring the crista galli (anterior attachment for brain coverings) and cribriform plates with olfactory foramina for', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (25, 10, 'Cranial Fossae', 'Cranial Fossae', 'The cranial fossae are three subdivisions of the skull''s cranial cavity floor that increase in depth anteriorly to posteriorly and each conform to the brain regions they contain: the anterior cranial fossa (shallowest, contains frontal lobes, bounded by frontal bone and ethmoid bone''s crista galli and cribriform plates), the middle cranial fossa (deeper, contains temporal lobes, bounded by lesser wings of sphenoid bone anteriorly and petrous ridges posteriorly, divided by sella turcica), and the posterior cranial fossa (deepest, bounded by petrous ridges and occipital bone, divided by foramen magnum). Key search terms: anterior cranial fossa, middle cranial fossa, posterior cranial fossa, frontal bone, sphenoid bone, ethmoid bone, temporal bones, petrous ridges, sella turcica, crista galli, cribriform plates, foramen magnum, optic canal, superior orbital fissure, foramen rotundum, foramen ovale, foramen spinosum, carotid canal, foramen lacerum, internal acoustic meatus, hypoglossal canal, jugular foramen. The middle cranial fossa contains multiple foramina and canals: the optic canal (passage for optic nerve), superior orbital fissure (passage for nerves to eyeball and forehead), foramen rotundum (sensory nerve to cheek/nose/upper teeth), foramen ovale (sensory nerve to lateral head/chin/lower teeth), foramen spinosum (entry for artery supplying brain coverings), and carotid canal (passage for major artery to brain). The posterior cranial fossa contains the internal acoustic meatus (passage for hearing/equilibrium and facial nerves), hypoglossal canal (passage for nerve to tongue), and jugular foramen (exit', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (26, 10, 'Facial Bones of the Skull', 'Facial Bones of the Skull', 'The facial bones of the skull comprise 14 bones—six paired (maxilla, palatine, zygomatic, nasal, lacrimal, inferior nasal conchae) and two unpaired (vomer, mandible)—that form the upper and lower jaws, nose, nasal cavity, nasal septum, and orbit; the ethmoid bone also contributes to the nasal septum, nasal cavity walls, and orbit. The maxilla forms the upper jaw, much of the hard palate, the medial floor of the orbit, and the lateral base of the nose; its alveolar process contains the upper teeth anchored in sockets called alveoli, and its palatine process joins at the midline to form the anterior three-quarters of the hard palate. The infraorbital foramen on the anterior maxilla is the exit point for a sensory nerve supplying the nose, upper lip, and anterior cheek. The palatine bone contributes to the lateral walls of the nasal cavity and medial wall of the orbit; its horizontal plate joins with the contralateral palatine bone at the midline to form the posterior quarter of the hard palate. The hard palate forms the roof of the mouth and floor of the nasal cavity, separating the oral and nasal cavities. Skull fractures include linear, comminuted, depressed, and contrecoup types; basilar fractures of the occipital bone can damage the artery in the carotid canal, and fractures around the pterion can damage the major arterial branch supplying the skull and brain, potentially causing a hematoma between the brain and skull interior.', 4);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (27, 10, 'Lateral View of Skull', 'Lateral View of Skull', 'The lateral skull view is dominated by the brain case, upper and lower jaws, and the zygomatic arch, a bony bridge separating these regions. The zygomatic arch is formed by the junction of two processes: the temporal process of the zygomatic bone (anterior) and the zygomatic process of the temporal bone (posterior), which join together to span from the cheek to above the ear canal. A major muscle that pulls the mandible upward during biting and chewing arises from the zygomatic arch. Above the zygomatic arch on the lateral brain case is the temporal fossa, a shallow space; below the zygomatic arch and deep to the mandible is the infratemporal fossa. Both the temporal fossa and infratemporal fossa contain muscles that act on the mandible during chewing. Search terms: zygomatic arch, temporal process of zygomatic bone, zygomatic process of temporal bone, temporal fossa, infratemporal fossa, mandible, lateral skull view.', 5);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (28, 10, 'Paranasal Sinuses', 'Paranasal Sinuses', 'The paranasal sinuses are hollow, air-filled spaces within skull bones that communicate with the nasal cavity and are lined with nasal mucosa; they function to reduce bone mass and lighten the skull while adding voice resonance. The four types of paranasal sinuses are: the frontal sinus (located within the frontal bone just above the eyebrows, the most anterior sinus, may be bilateral or fused); the maxillary sinus (paired, located within the maxillary bones below the orbits, the largest sinus, most commonly involved in sinus infections, difficult to drain due to high medial wall connection to nasal cavity); the sphenoid sinus (single midline sinus within the sphenoid bone body anterior and inferior to the sella turcica, the most posterior sinus); and ethmoid air cells (multiple small spaces in the lateral aspects of the ethmoid bone between the upper nasal cavity and medial orbit, just behind the superior nasal conchae). Sinus congestion causes mucosal swelling and excess mucus production that obstructs the narrow passageways between sinuses and nasal cavity, resulting in voice changes and potential fluid accumulation with associated pain. Search terms: paranasal sinuses, frontal sinus, maxillary sinus, sphenoid sinus, ethmoid air cells, nasal cavity, nasal mucosa, sella turcica, superior nasal conchae.', 6);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (29, 10, 'Sutures of the Skull', 'Sutures of the Skull', 'Sutures are immobile joints between adjacent skull bones filled with dense, fibrous connective tissue; their irregular, tightly twisting paths interlock bones and add strength for brain protection. The major sutures of the skull include: the coronal suture (joins frontal bone to right and left parietal bones, runs side-to-side in the coronal plane), the sagittal suture (unites right and left parietal bones along the midline in the sagittal plane, extends posteriorly from the coronal suture), the lambdoid suture (joins occipital bone to right and left parietal and temporal bones, has an upside-down "V" shape, terminates the sagittal suture posteriorly), and the pterion (capital-H-shaped region at the intersection of frontal bone, parietal bone, squamous portion of temporal bone, and greater wing of sphenoid bone). The pterion is the weakest part of the skull and is located approximately two finger widths above the zygomatic arch and a thumb''s width posterior to the zygomatic bone. Search terms: suture, coronal suture, sagittal suture, lambdoid suture, pterion, frontal bone, parietal bone, occipital bone, temporal bone, sphenoid bone, zygomatic arch.', 7);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (30, 10, 'The Nasal Septum and Nasal Conchae', 'The Nasal Septum and Nasal Conchae', 'The nasal septum consists of the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid bone (upper portion), the vomer bone (lower and posterior portions), and the septal cartilage (anterior portion), which together separate the right and left nostrils. The nasal conchae—superior, middle, and inferior—are bony plates attached to the lateral walls of the nasal cavity; the inferior nasal concha is an independent bone, while the middle and superior conchae are formed by the ethmoid bone. The nasal conchae curve downward into the nasal cavity to swirl incoming air, warming and moisturizing it before it reaches the lungs, and they work with mucus secretion to trap dust, pollen, bacteria, and viruses. Search terms: perpendicular plate of the ethmoid bone, vomer bone, septal cartilage, nasal conchae, superior nasal concha, middle nasal concha, inferior nasal concha, nasal cavity, nasal septum.', 8);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (31, 10, 'The Orbit', 'The Orbit', 'The orbit is a cone-shaped bony socket that houses the eyeball and contains muscles for eyeball movement and upper eyelid opening, with a narrow posterior region widening toward a large anterior opening. Seven skull bones contribute to the orbital walls: the frontal bone forms the roof; the zygomatic bone forms the lateral wall and lateral floor; the maxilla forms the medial floor with a small contribution from the palatine bone; the ethmoid bone and lacrimal bone form much of the medial wall; and the sphenoid bone forms the posterior orbit. The medial walls of the two orbits are parallel to each other, while each lateral wall diverges away from the midline at a 45° angle, providing greater lateral peripheral vision. The optic canal at the posterior apex allows passage of the optic nerve from the retina to the brain, and the superior orbital fissure provides passage for the artery that supplies the eyeball, sensory nerves, and nerves that supply the eye movement muscles. Search terms: orbit, eyeball, orbital muscles, frontal bone, zygomatic bone, maxilla, palatine bone, ethmoid bone, lacrimal bone, sphenoid bone, optic canal, optic nerve, superior orbital fissure.', 9);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (32, 11, 'Curvatures of the Vertebral Column', 'Curvatures of the Vertebral Column', 'The adult vertebral column contains four curvatures—two primary curves (thoracic curve involving thoracic vertebrae, and sacrococcygeal curve formed by sacrum and coccyx) retained from fetal development, and two secondary curves (cervical curve and lumbar curve) that develop after birth as infants learn to sit, stand, and walk. Primary curves are concave anteriorly, while secondary curves are concave posteriorly. The curvatures increase vertebral column strength, flexibility, and shock absorption; they deepen when load increases (e.g., carrying a heavy backpack) and spring back when weight is removed. The lumbar curve is generally deeper in females. Spinal curvature disorders include kyphosis (excessive posterior curvature of thoracic region), lordosis (excessive anterior curvature of lumbar region), and scoliosis (abnormal lateral curvature with vertebral twisting). Search terms: vertebral column curvatures, primary curves, secondary curves, thoracic curve, sacrococcygeal curve, cervical curve, lumbar curve, kyphosis, lordosis, scoliosis.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (33, 11, 'General Structure of a Vertebra', 'General Structure of a Vertebra', 'A typical vertebra consists of a body, a vertebral arch, and seven processes; the body is the anterior weight-bearing portion that progressively increases in size down the vertebral column, while adjacent vertebral bodies are separated and united by intervertebral discs. The vertebral arch forms the posterior portion and comprises four parts: right and left pedicles (anchored to the posterior vertebral body and forming lateral sides) and right and left laminae (forming the posterior roof); the large opening between the arch and body is the vertebral foramen, which contains the spinal cord and aligns with other foramina to form the vertebral (spinal) canal. The seven processes arising from the vertebral arch include paired transverse processes (projecting laterally from the pedicle-lamina junction), a single spinous process or vertebral spine (projecting posteriorly at the midline), and paired superior and inferior articular processes on each side; the transverse and spinous processes serve as muscle attachment sites. Adjacent vertebral pedicles form intervertebral foramina, the openings through which spinal nerves exit the vertebral column. Superior articular processes of one vertebra articulate with inferior articular processes of the next higher vertebra, forming slightly moveable joints; the shape and orientation of these articular processes vary regionally and determine the type and range of motion available in each region of the vertebral column.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (34, 11, 'Intervertebral Discs and Ligaments of the Vertebral Column', 'Intervertebral Discs and Ligaments of the Vertebral Column', 'This subsection introduces intervertebral discs and ligaments as the primary support structures of the vertebral column. **Key structures:** intervertebral disc (fibrocartilaginous pad), anulus fibrosus (tough outer layer), nucleus pulposus (gel-like inner material with high water content), anterior longitudinal ligament, supraspinous ligament, nuchal ligament, posterior longitudinal ligament, and ligamentum flavum. **Search terms:** intervertebral disc, anulus fibrosus, nucleus pulposus, herniated disc, sciatica, anterior longitudinal ligament, supraspinous ligament, nuchal ligament, posterior longitudinal ligament, ligamentum flavum, intervertebral foramen, whiplash injury. **Key relationships:** intervertebral discs anchor adjacent vertebral bodies and provide padding during weight bearing; discs are thinnest in the cervical region and thickest in the lumbar region; nucleus pulposus water content declines with age, reducing disc flexibility; herniated nucleus pulposus protrudes posteriorly through weakened anulus fibrosus and compresses spinal nerves at the intervertebral foramen, causing pain and muscle weakness; anterior longitudinal ligament resists excess backward bending; supraspinous ligament and nuchal ligament support forward bending; posterior longitudinal ligament and ligamentum flavum provide support when bending forward; common herniation sites L4/L5 and L5/S1 cause sciatica, while C5/C6 and C6/C7 injuries cause neck and upper limb pain.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (35, 11, 'Regional Modifications of Vertebrae', 'Regional Modifications of Vertebrae', 'This subsection describes regional modifications of vertebrae across the vertebral column, covering cervical, thoracic, lumbar, sacral, and coccygeal vertebrae. **Key structures and search terms:** cervical vertebrae (C1–C7), atlas, axis, dens (odontoid process), bifid spinous process, transverse foramen, thoracic vertebrae, costal facet, lumbar vertebrae, sacrum, sacral vertebrae, median sacral crest, lateral sacral crest, sacral canal, sacral foramina, sacral promontory, auricular surface, coccyx, coccygeal vertebrae. **Explicit relationships:** cervical vertebrae support the least body weight and have small bodies; C7 has a longer spinous process than C3–C6; transverse foramina allow passage of cervical spinal nerves and an artery supplying the brain; the atlas (C1) supports the skull via articulation with occipital condyles and lacks a body and spinous process; the dens of the axis (C2) joins the anterior arch of the atlas and is held by the transverse ligament; thoracic vertebrae have costal facets for rib articulation; lumbar vertebrae carry the greatest body weight and have large bodies; the sacrum forms from fusion of five sacral vertebrae (beginning after age 20) and articulates with the L5 vertebra; sacral foramina allow sacral spinal nerves to exit; the coccyx derives from fusion of four coccygeal vertebrae.', 4);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (36, 11, 'Regions of the Vertebral Column', 'Regions of the Vertebral Column', 'The vertebral column consists of five regions: cervical (C1–C7), thoracic (T1–T12), lumbar (L1–L5), sacrum (fused from five sacral vertebrae), and coccyx (fused from four coccygeal vertebrae), totaling 24 individual vertebrae plus the sacrum and coccyx in adults, though 33 vertebrae are present during development. The C1 vertebra articulates superiorly with the occipital condyles of the skull and inferiorly with the C2 vertebra. The sacral and coccygeal vertebral fusions do not begin until age 20 and are not completed until middle age. Search terms: cervical vertebrae, thoracic vertebrae, lumbar vertebrae, sacrum, coccyx, vertebral column regions, C1 vertebra, occipital condyles, vertebral articulation.', 5);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (37, 12, 'Bone Remodeling', 'Bone Remodeling', 'This subsection introduces bone remodeling, the process in which osteoblasts deposit new bone on the same surface where resorption of old or damaged bone occurs, distinct from bone modeling (matrix resorbed on one surface, deposited on another) which occurs primarily during growth. Bone remodeling is triggered by injury, exercise, and other activities, with approximately 5–10 percent of the skeleton remodeled annually even without external stimuli. The subsection also covers osteogenesis imperfecta (OI), also called brittle bone disease, a genetic disease present from birth in which a genetic mutation impairs collagen production—a critical component of bone matrix—resulting in fragile bones that break easily. Severity ranges from mild to severe; severe OI causes frequent fractures leading to bone deformities, short stature, bowing of long bones, and spinal curvature that compresses the lungs and impairs breathing. Because collagen is important throughout the body, OI patients may also experience fragile skin, weak muscles, loose joints, easy bruising, frequent nosebleeds, brittle teeth, blue sclera, and hearing loss. There is no known cure for OI; treatment focuses on maximizing independence, minimizing fractures, and maximizing mobility through safe exercise.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (38, 12, 'Endochondral Ossification', 'Endochondral Ossification', 'Endochondral ossification is the process by which bone develops by replacing hyaline cartilage (which serves as a template but does not itself become bone); this process occurs in bones at the base of the skull and long bones. Key structures include mesenchymal cells, chondrocytes, perichondrium, osteogenic cells, osteoblasts, periosteum, periosteal collar, medullary cavity, primary ossification center, epiphyseal plate, and secondary ossification center. The process begins at 6–8 weeks after conception when mesenchymal cells differentiate into chondrocytes that form the cartilaginous skeletal precursor; as matrix is produced and calcifies, chondrocytes in the center die and cartilage disintegrates, allowing blood vessels carrying osteogenic cells to invade the spaces. Capillaries penetrate the cartilage and transform the perichondrium into the periosteum, which produces a periosteal collar of compact bone around the diaphysis; by the second or third month of fetal life, the primary ossification center forms deep in the periosteal collar. Chondrocytes and cartilage continue to grow at the epiphyses (bone ends) while ossification replaces cartilage in the diaphyses; by fetal skeleton completion, cartilage remains only as articular cartilage at joint surfaces and as the epiphyseal plate (responsible for longitudinal bone growth). After birth, secondary ossification centers form in the epiphyseal regions through the same sequence of matrix mineralization, chondrocyte death, blood vessel invasion from the periosteum, and osteoblast seeding.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (39, 12, 'How Bones Grow in Length', 'How Bones Grow in Length', 'The epiphyseal plate, a layer of hyaline cartilage in immature long bones, is the primary site of longitudinal bone growth and consists of four functionally distinct zones: the reserve zone (anchors plate to epiphysis via small chondrocytes), the proliferative zone (generates new chondrocytes via mitosis), the zone of maturation and hypertrophy (contains progressively larger, maturing chondrocytes), and the zone of calcified matrix (connects plate to diaphysis). Longitudinal bone growth results from cellular division in the proliferative zone and maturation of cells in the zone of maturation and hypertrophy; osseous tissue is added to the diaphysis when osteoblasts from the diaphysis penetrate the zone of calcified matrix and secrete bone tissue on calcified cartilage. Capillaries and osteoblasts from the diaphysis invade the zone of calcified matrix, where most chondrocytes are dead due to matrix calcification. Hormones control the rate of growth, and when chondrocytes cease proliferation and bone replaces cartilage, longitudinal growth stops and only the epiphyseal line remains. Search terms: epiphyseal plate, hyaline cartilage, ossification, reserve zone, proliferative zone, zone of maturation and hypertrophy, zone of calcified matrix, chondrocytes, osteoblasts, diaphysis, epiphysis, longitudinal growth, epiphyseal line, secondary ossification center.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (40, 12, 'Intramembranous Ossification', 'Intramembranous Ossification', 'Intramembranous ossification is the process by which compact and spongy bone develop directly from mesenchymal connective tissue sheets, forming the flat bones of the face, most cranial bones, and clavicles. Mesenchymal cells differentiate into capillaries, osteogenic cells, and osteoblasts; osteoblasts cluster at an ossification center and secrete osteoid (uncalcified matrix), which calcifies as mineral salts deposit and entraps the osteoblasts, converting them into osteocytes. Osteoid secreted around capillaries creates a trabecular matrix, while osteoblasts on the spongy bone surface become the periosteum, which then forms a protective layer of compact bone superficial to the trabecular bone. Trabecular bone crowds nearby blood vessels, which condense into red marrow. Intramembranous ossification begins in utero during fetal development and continues into adolescence; at birth the skull and clavicles are not fully ossified and skull sutures remain open to allow deformation during birth, with the flat bones of the face being the last to ossify, reaching adult size at the end of the adolescent growth spurt. **Search terms:** intramembranous ossification, mesenchymal cells, osteogenic cells, osteoblasts, osteocytes, ossification center, osteoid, trabecular bone, periosteum, compact bone, cranial bones, clavicles, flat bones of the face, red marrow, skull sutures.', 4);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (41, 13, 'Blood and Nerve Supply', 'Blood and Nerve Supply', '# Blood and Nerve Supply to Bone

This subsection describes the vascular and neural pathways that nourish and regulate bone tissue. Arteries pass through the compact bone and enter the spongy bone and medullary cavity via the nutrient foramen (small openings in the diaphysis); blood vessels from the periosteum also penetrate spongy bone and circulate through marrow cavities, where veins collect the blood and exit through the foramina. Nerves follow the same pathways as blood vessels into bone and concentrate in metabolically active regions, where they sense pain and appear to regulate blood supply and bone growth. The subsection also introduces Paget''s disease—a condition involving overactive osteoclasts (cause unknown, though hereditary factors and unidentified viruses are suspected)—and its diagnosis via X-rays, bone scans (using radioactive ion-containing dye with affinity for areas of bone resorption), and elevated blood alkaline phosphatase levels. Treatment uses bisphosphonates (drugs that decrease osteoclast activity), which reduce deformities and fractures, though they carry a small risk of increased fracture brittleness in old bone.

**Search terms:** nutrient foramen, diaphysis, periosteum, medullary cavity, marrow cavities, osteocytes, osteoclasts, Paget''s disease, bisphosphonates, alkaline phosphatase, bone resorption, bone scans, X-rays.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (42, 13, 'Bone Cells and Tissue', 'Bone Cells and Tissue', 'Bone tissue consists of cells embedded in an extracellular matrix of collagen fibers and inorganic salts; calcium phosphate and calcium carbonate combine to form hydroxyapatite crystals (incorporating magnesium hydroxide, fluoride, and sulfate), which provide hardness and strength while collagen fibers provide flexibility. Four bone cell types perform distinct functions: osteoblasts synthesize and secrete the collagen matrix and calcium salts in growing portions of bone (periosteum and endosteum) and lack mitotic activity; osteocytes are the primary cells of mature bone, located in lacunae, maintain mineral concentration via enzyme secretion, and communicate through cytoplasmic processes extending through canaliculi; osteogenic cells are undifferentiated cells with high mitotic activity found in deep periosteum and marrow layers that are the only bone cells capable of division and develop into osteoblasts; osteoclasts are multinucleated cells originating from monocytes and macrophages that perform bone resorption on bone surfaces and at sites of old, injured, or unneeded bone. The balance between osteoblast bone formation and osteoclast bone resorption is responsible for constant bone reshaping. **Search terms:** osteoblasts, osteocytes, osteogenic cells, osteoclasts, lacuna, canaliculi, hydroxyapatite, collagen matrix, bone resorption, periosteum, endosteum, bone matrix, calcium salts, mineral concentration.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (43, 13, 'Bone Markings', 'Bone Markings', 'This subsection introduces bone markings, the surface features of bones that vary based on function and location. The three general classes of bone markings are: (1) articulations (where two bone surfaces come together to facilitate joint function, with surfaces conforming such as one rounded and one cupped), (2) projections (areas that project above the bone surface, with size and shape indicating forces exerted through attachments), and (3) holes (openings or grooves allowing blood vessels and nerves to enter bone, with size and shape reflecting the size of penetrating vessels and nerves). Specific articulation types include head, facet, and condyle; projection types include spinous process, protuberance, process, spine, tubercle, tuberosity, line, and crest; hole types include foramen, fossa, fovea, sulcus, canal, fissure, meatus, and sinus. Students would search for: bone markings, articulations, projections, holes, head of femur, occipital condyles, spinous process, transverse process, ischial spine, tubercle of humerus, deltoid tuberosity, iliac crest, foramen magnum, mandibular fossa, fovea capitis, sigmoid sulcus, auditory canal, external auditory meatus, and nasal sinus.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (44, 13, 'Compact and Spongy Bone', 'Compact and Spongy Bone', 'Compact and spongy bone are two types of osseous tissue with distinct histological structures and functions: compact bone is dense and found under the periosteum and in the diaphyses of long bones to withstand compressive forces, while spongy (cancellous) bone has open spaces that support weight distribution shifts and reduce bone weight. The microscopic structural unit of compact bone is the osteon (Haversian system), composed of concentric lamellae surrounding a central canal (Haversian canal) that contains blood vessels, nerves, and lymphatic vessels; these vessels and nerves branch through perforating canals (Volkmann''s canals) to reach the periosteum and endosteum. Osteocytes in compact bone occupy lacunae at lamella borders, and canaliculi connect lacunae to the central canal to transport nutrients and remove wastes. Spongy bone contains osteocytes in lacunae arranged within a lattice-like network of trabeculae rather than concentric circles; trabeculae form along lines of stress to provide strength while the trabeculated spaces reduce overall bone density. The spaces within spongy bone contain red marrow, protected by trabeculae, where hematopoiesis occurs. Search terms: compact bone, spongy bone, cancellous bone, osteon, Haversian system, lamellae, central canal, Haversian canal, perforating canal, Volkmann''s canals, lacunae, canaliculi, trabeculae, red marrow, hematopoiesis.', 4);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (45, 13, 'Gross Anatomy of Bone', 'Gross Anatomy of Bone', 'This subsection describes the gross anatomical organization of long bones and flat bones. Long bones consist of the diaphysis (tubular shaft containing a medullary cavity filled with yellow marrow and bounded by compact bone), the epiphysis (wider end sections filled with spongy bone containing red marrow), and the metaphysis (narrow region containing the epiphyseal plate—a hyaline cartilage layer that ossifies into an epiphyseal line by early adulthood). The medullary cavity is lined by the endosteum, where bone growth, repair, and remodeling occur. The periosteum, a fibrous membrane covering the outer bone surface, contains blood vessels, nerves, and lymphatic vessels that nourish compact bone and serves as an attachment site for tendons and ligaments; it does not cover epiphyses at joint surfaces, which are instead covered by articular cartilage that reduces friction and absorbs shock. Flat bones (such as cranial bones) consist of diploë (spongy bone) sandwiched between two layers of compact bone, which together protect internal organs. Search terms: diaphysis, epiphysis, medullary cavity, yellow marrow, compact bone, spongy bone, red marrow, metaphysis, epiphyseal plate, epiphyseal line, endosteum, periosteum, articular cartilage, diploë, flat bones, cranial bones.', 5);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (46, 14, 'Exercise and Bone Tissue', 'Exercise and Bone Tissue', 'This subsection explains how mechanical stress regulates bone tissue composition and structure through the deposition or loss of mineral salts and collagen fibers. Key concepts include: mechanical stress stimulates deposition of mineral salts and collagen fibers, while lack of mechanical stress causes bones to lose mineral salts and collagen fibers and thus strength; bones undergo remodeling in response to forces or lack of forces placed on them; and bone internal and external structure changes as stress increases or decreases to maintain ideal size and weight for activity level. The textbook demonstrates these principles through examples: astronauts lose 1–2 percent of bone mass per month during space missions due to low gravitational forces; people who exercise regularly have thicker bones and greater bone density than sedentary people; broken bones in casts atrophy while contralateral bones maintain mineral salt and collagen fiber concentration; and resistance training has a greater effect on bone tissue deposition than cardiovascular activities and is especially important for preventing bone loss due to aging and osteoporosis. Search terms: mechanical stress, bone remodeling, mineral salts, collagen fibers, bone density, bone mass, exercise, resistance training, cardiovascular activities, osteoporosis, bone atrophy, gravitational forces.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (47, 14, 'Hormones and Bone Tissue', 'Hormones and Bone Tissue', 'This subsection covers hormonal regulation of bone growth, maintenance, and remodeling through interactions between the endocrine and skeletal systems. Key structures and hormones include: the pituitary gland (secretes growth hormone), thyroid gland (secretes thyroxine), kidneys (produce calcitriol), epiphyseal plates, epiphyseal lines, osteoblasts, and chondrocytes. Growth hormone (GH) triggers chondrocyte proliferation in epiphyseal plates to increase long bone length, increases calcium retention to enhance mineralization, and stimulates osteoblastic activity to improve bone density. Thyroxine promotes osteoblastic activity and bone matrix synthesis. Sex hormones (estrogen and testosterone) promote osteoblastic activity, bone matrix production, the adolescent growth spurt, and conversion of epiphyseal plates to epiphyseal lines, thereby ending longitudinal bone growth. Calcitriol stimulates absorption of calcium and phosphate from the digestive tract. Search terms: growth hormone, thyroxine, estrogen, testosterone, calcitriol, epiphyseal plate, epiphyseal line, osteoblastic activity, bone matrix, chondrocyte proliferation, bone mineralization.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (48, 14, 'Nutrition and Bone Tissue', 'Nutrition and Bone Tissue', 'This subsection identifies key nutrients essential for bone health and their specific physiological roles. **Key structures and concepts:** calcium phosphate, calcium carbonate, hydroxyapatite crystals, fluorapatite, osteoblasts, osseous tissue, bone mineralization, and bone remodeling. **Search terms:** calcium, vitamin D, vitamin K, magnesium, fluoride, omega-3 fatty acids, small intestine absorption, hydroxyl group, bone mineral density. **Explicit relationships:** calcium cannot be absorbed from the small intestine without vitamin D; vitamin D plays a role in calcium absorption and bone remodeling; vitamin K supports bone mineralization and may have a synergistic role with vitamin D in regulating bone growth; fluoride displaces the hydroxyl group in hydroxyapatite crystals to form fluorapatite, which stabilizes and strengthens bone mineral; fluoride also increases hydroxyapatite crystal density; inflammation interferes with osteoblast function, and omega-3 fatty acids reduce this inflammation to enhance production of new osseous tissue. The subsection also notes that calcium, magnesium, and fluoride are structural components of bone, and that magnesium comprises more than 60 percent of the body''s total magnesium content within the skeleton.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (49, 15, 'Bone Repair', 'Bone Repair', '# Bone Repair Summary

This subsection describes the sequential process of bone fracture healing, beginning with formation of a fracture hematoma when blood from torn vessels in the periosteum, osteons, and/or medullary cavity clots within six to eight hours. Within 48 hours, chondrocytes from the endosteum create an internal callus by secreting a fibrocartilaginous matrix, while periosteal chondrocytes and osteoblasts create an external callus of hyaline cartilage and bone respectively, stabilizing the fracture. Over subsequent weeks, osteoclasts resorb dead bone, osteogenic cells differentiate into osteoblasts, and cartilage in the calli is replaced by trabecular bone via endochondral ossification. The internal and external calli eventually unite, compact bone replaces spongy bone at the fracture margins, and healing completes, with potential remodeling eliminating external evidence of the fracture.

**Search terms:** fracture hematoma, internal callus, external callus, fibrocartilaginous matrix, hyaline cartilage, endochondral ossification, trabecular bone, compact bone, osteoclasts, osteoblasts, osteogenic cells, periosteum, endosteum, medullary cavity, bone remodeling, bone repair stages.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (50, 15, 'Types of Fractures', 'Types of Fractures', 'This subsection classifies fractures by complexity, location, and structural features, introducing eight primary fracture types: transverse fractures (straight across the long axis), oblique fractures (at non-90-degree angles), spiral fractures (resulting from twisting motion that pulls bone segments apart), comminuted fractures (multiple breaks producing many small pieces between two large segments), impacted fractures (one fragment driven into another via compression), greenstick fractures (partial fractures affecting only one side), open/compound fractures (bone breaks through skin with high infection risk), and closed/simple fractures (skin remains intact). The textbook notes that fractures may be described using multiple terms simultaneously because a single fracture can exhibit features of more than one type (e.g., an open transverse fracture). Treatment and management approaches mentioned include supportive devices (braces for legs, ankles, knees, and wrists; canes, walkers, wheelchairs), immobilization methods (casts, splints, wraps), and surgical interventions (metal rods implanted into long bones of arms and legs). The subsection also references bisphosphonates as a current research treatment and identifies smoking and being overweight as risk factors that weaken bones or increase stress on skeletal structures.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (51, 16, 'Support, Movement, and Protection', 'Support, Movement, and Protection', '# Summary

This subsection introduces the three primary gross functions of the skeletal system: support, movement, and protection. Bones and cartilage compose a scaffold that supports body weight; bones serve as points of attachment for muscles and act as levers while joints serve as fulcrums, enabling movement when muscles span a joint and contract. Bones protect internal organs by covering or surrounding them: ribs protect the lungs and heart, the vertebral column (spine) protects the spinal cord, and the cranium (skull) protects the brain. The subsection defines an orthopedist as a doctor specializing in diagnosing and treating disorders and injuries related to the musculoskeletal system, and describes common orthopedic conditions including scoliosis (lateral curvature), kyphosis (excessive dorsoventral curvature), spina bifida (neural canal failure to close in the fetus), and age-related spinal curvatures caused by accumulated injuries or osteoporosis. Key search terms: skeletal system functions, support, movement, protection, bones as levers, joints as fulcrums, muscle contraction, ribs, vertebral column, cranium, orthopedist, musculoskeletal system, scoliosis, kyphosis, spina bifida, osteoporosis, sports medicine.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (52, 17, 'Circulatory Adjustments', 'Circulatory Adjustments', 'This subsection describes circulatory adjustments following birth, focusing on the closure and transformation of fetal vascular structures. Key anatomical structures include the umbilical cord (containing Wharton''s jelly and umbilical blood vessels), umbilical arteries, ductus venosus, foramen ovale, and ductus arteriosus. The textbook explains that clamping and cutting the umbilical cord collapses umbilical blood vessels, which naturally occurs within 20 minutes as Wharton''s jelly swells in response to lower external temperature; collapsed vessels atrophy to become fibrotic ligaments of the abdominal wall and liver, while the ductus venosus degenerates to the ligamentum venosum and proximal umbilical arteries remain functional to supply the upper bladder. The newborn''s first breath initiates the transition from fetal to neonatal circulation by inflating the lungs, which decreases blood pressure in the pulmonary system, right atrium, and right ventricle, causing blood flow to reverse through the foramen ovale (from left to right atrium) and block the shunt with tissue flaps that fuse within 1 year to form the fossa ovalis. Increased oxygen concentration causes the ductus arteriosus to constrict and become the ligamentum arteriosum, ensuring all blood to the pulmonary circuit is oxygenated by the newly functional neonatal lungs. Search terms: umbilical cord, umbilical arteries, ductus venosus, ligamentum venosum, foramen ovale, fossa ovalis, ductus arteriosus, ligamentum arteriosum, neonatal circulation, fetal circulation.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (53, 17, 'Respiratory Adjustments', 'Respiratory Adjustments', 'This subsection describes respiratory adjustments in newborns at birth, covering fetal lung development, triggers for the first breath, and immediate postnatal changes. Key structures include the placenta, umbilical blood vessels, lungs, pulmonary alveoli, alveolar capillaries, and the respiratory center in the brain. Search terms: fetus, amniotic fluid, labor contractions, umbilical blood vessels, oxygenated blood, carbon dioxide levels, acidosis, respiratory center, first breath, lung pressure, blood flow, circulatory reconfiguration, pulmonary alveoli, alveolar capillaries, respiration. The textbook establishes that labor contractions constrict umbilical blood vessels, reducing oxygenated blood flow and elevating carbon dioxide levels, which causes acidosis and stimulates the respiratory center in the brain to trigger the first breath (typically within 10 seconds of birth). The first breaths inflate the lungs to nearly full capacity, decreasing lung pressure and resistance to blood flow, causing circulatory reconfiguration; simultaneously, pulmonary alveoli open, alveolar capillaries fill with blood, amniotic fluid drains or is absorbed, and the lungs assume the placenta''s role in exchanging carbon dioxide for oxygen.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (54, 17, 'Thermoregulatory Adjustments', 'Thermoregulatory Adjustments', 'This subsection addresses thermoregulatory adjustments newborns must make after birth, when they transition from the warm amniotic fluid (maintained at approximately 98.6°F) to a cooler external environment. Newborns face multiple physiological challenges to temperature regulation: a higher surface area to volume ratio causes faster heat loss and slower heat production; immature musculature limits heat generation by shivering; underdeveloped nervous systems prevent rapid constriction of superficial blood vessels in response to cold; and minimal subcutaneous fat provides inadequate insulation. Newborns employ nonshivering thermogenesis, which involves the breakdown of brown adipose tissue (brown fat) distributed over the back, chest, and shoulders; brown fat is highly vascularized to enable faster oxygen delivery and faster cellular respiration, and contains specialized mitochondria that produce less ATP and more heat than standard cellular respiration. The breakdown of brown fat occurs automatically upon exposure to cold, and during fetal development the placenta secretes inhibitors that prevent brown adipose fat metabolism while promoting its accumulation in preparation for birth. **Search terms:** thermoregulatory adjustments, newborns, body temperature regulation, surface area to volume ratio, nonshivering thermogenesis, brown adipose tissue, brown fat, white fat, vascularization, cellular respiration, mitochondria, amniotic fluid, subcutaneous fat, superficial blood vessels, placenta.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (55, 18, 'Changes in Organ Systems During Pregnancy', 'Changes in Organ Systems During Pregnancy', '# Summary: Changes in Organ Systems During Pregnancy

This subsection describes physiologic adaptations across multiple organ systems during pregnancy, including gastrointestinal, urinary, cardiovascular, respiratory, integumentary, and metabolic changes. **Key structures and processes:** morning sickness (nausea and vomiting), intestinal peristalsis, gastric reflux (heartburn), urinary bladder compression, blood volume increase (30% or 1–2 liters above preconception), pulse and blood pressure elevation, varicose veins, hemorrhoids, respiratory minute volume (50% increase in second half), diaphragm pressure, dyspnea, lightening, respiratory mucosa swelling, nasal congestion, dermis stretching, striae (stretch marks), linea nigra, chloasma, and weight gain (0.45 kg/week; total 10–16.3 kg). **Search entities:** estrogen, progesterone, hCG, decreased intestinal peristalsis, upward uterine pressure on stomach, downward uterine pressure on urinary bladder, uterine compression of pelvic blood vessels, venous return, fetal oxygen demands, increased maternal metabolic rate, upward diaphragm pressure, pelvic elasticity, melanocyte-stimulating hormone, areolae darkening, umbilicus to pubis pigmentation. **Explicit relationships:** increased circulating estrogen, progesterone, and hCG cause pregnancy nausea; decreased peristalsis contributes to nausea and constipation; growing uterus causes gastric reflux, bladder compression (frequent urination), and upward diaphragm pressure (dyspnea); uterine compression of pelvic blood vessels causes varicose veins and hemorrhoids; fetal oxygen demands and increased maternal metabolic rate increase respiratory minute volume; melanoc', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (56, 18, 'Effects of Hormones', 'Effects of Hormones', 'This subsection explains how pregnancy hormones—estrogens, progesterone, hCG, and relaxin—mediate virtually all physiological changes during pregnancy and labor. The corpus luteum produces these hormones during weeks 7–12 from LMP, then the placenta assumes endocrine control during weeks 12–17 by converting maternal and fetal adrenal androgens to estrogens. Key hormone actions include: estrogens suppress FSH and LH (preventing ovulation), induce fetal tissue growth and maturation of fetal lungs and liver, trigger fetal cortisol synthesis, and stimulate maternal uterine enlargement and mammary duct expansion; progesterone inhibits uterine contractions until late gestation when it decreases to allow labor; relaxin increases elasticity of the symphysis pubis joint and pelvic ligaments and dilates the cervix; and hCG promotes corpus luteum survival and stimulates male fetal gonad testosterone secretion. The anterior pituitary enlarges during pregnancy and increases thyrotropin (which raises maternal metabolic rate), prolactin (which enlarges mammary glands), and ACTH (which stimulates maternal cortisol secretion for fetal protein synthesis), while increased parathyroid hormone mobilizes maternal bone calcium for fetal use. Search terms: estrogens, progesterone, hCG, relaxin, corpus luteum, placenta, FSH, LH, fetal lungs, fetal liver, uterine contractions, symphysis pubis, pelvic ligaments, cervix, testosterone, anterior pituitary, thyrotropin, prolactin, ACTH, parathyroid hormone.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (57, 18, 'Physiology of Labor', 'Physiology of Labor', '# Summary: Physiology of Labor

This subsection describes the hormonal and physiological mechanisms that initiate and maintain labor during parturition. Key structures and processes include: progesterone (inhibits uterine contractions in early pregnancy, then drops in the seventh month), estrogen (rises throughout pregnancy, increasing myometrial sensitivity to contraction stimuli), fetal cortisol (rises in eighth month, boosts placental estrogen secretion), oxytocin (secreted by posterior pituitary and fetal pituitary, stimulates uterine contractions), prostaglandins (secreted by fetal membranes, enhance contractile strength), myometrium (uterine smooth muscle that increases oxytocin receptor expression), cervical canal (contains mucus plug that loosens 1–2 days before labor onset, producing "bloody show"), and the fetus in vertex position (stretching myometrium and cervix stimulates contractions). The textbook explicitly states: progesterone inhibits contractions; the rising estrogen-to-progesterone ratio increases myometrial sensitivity; fetal cortisol boosts placental estrogen; oxytocin stimulates contractions in a positive feedback loop with prostaglandins; the fetal pituitary secretes oxytocin, which increases prostaglandins further; myometrial stretching by a vertex-positioned fetus stimulates contractions; and myometrial hypoxia during contractions causes labor pain. Braxton Hicks contractions (false labor) result from decreasing progesterone levels, while true labor represents the sum of these coordinated changes producing regular, increasingly powerful contractions.

**Search terms:** parturition, progesterone, estrogen, fetal cortisol, oxytocin, prostaglandins, myometrium, posterior pituitary, fetal pituitary, cervical canal, mucus plug, bloody show,', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (58, 18, 'Stages of Childbirth', 'Stages of Childbirth', 'Childbirth occurs in three stages: cervical dilation, expulsion of the newborn, and afterbirth (delivery of placenta and membranes). During cervical dilation, the cervix must dilate to 10 cm diameter; uterine contractions stretch the cervix causing dilation and effacement, cervical stretching induces reflexive uterine contractions, and cervical dilation boosts oxytocin secretion from the pituitary which triggers more powerful contractions—a positive feedback loop. Each uterine contraction sharply reduces oxygenated blood flow to the fetus; fetal distress (sustained decrease or increase in fetal heart rate) can result from severe contractions, potentially requiring emergency delivery by vacuum, forceps, or Caesarian section. The expulsion stage begins when the fetal head enters the birth canal; the occiput anterior vertex presentation (most common) has the fetus facing the maternal spinal cord with the occiput exiting first, while breech presentations (buttocks down) are now typically delivered by Caesarian section. Vaginal birth stretches the vaginal canal, cervix, and perineum; episiotomy (incision in posterior vaginal wall and perineum) or perineal tears require suturing. The afterbirth stage involves myometrial contractions that shear the placenta from the uterine wall; continued contractions reduce blood loss and facilitate involution (return of uterus to pre-pregnancy size); postpartum lochia (vaginal discharge of uterine lining cells, erythrocytes, leukocytes, and debris) progresses through lochia rubra, lochia serosa, and lochia alba over approximately 2–3 weeks.', 4);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (59, 18, 'Weight Gain', 'Weight Gain', 'This subsection describes maternal weight gain during pregnancy, a dramatic anatomical change occurring primarily in the second and third trimesters. Key contributors to total weight gain (10–16.3 kg or 22–36 lb) include the fetus (3.2–3.6 kg), placenta and fetal membranes (0.9–1.8 kg), amniotic fluid (0.9–1.4 kg), breast tissue (0.9–1.4 kg), blood volume (1.4 kg), fat storage (0.9–4.1 kg), and enlarged uterus (0.9–2.3 kg). Fat storage serves as a metabolic reserve for breastfeeding demands and accounts for only approximately 2.3 kg in normal pregnancy. During the first trimester, no additional caloric intake is required; however, during the second and third trimesters, an additional 300 calories per day supports fetal growth, with typical weight gain of 0.45 kg per month in the first trimester and 0.45 kg per week thereafter. The subsection also notes that ACTH stimulates maternal cortisol secretion, which contributes to fetal protein synthesis, and increased parathyroid hormone mobilizes calcium from maternal bones for fetal use.', 5);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (60, 19, 'Development of the Placenta', 'Development of the Placenta', '# Development of the Placenta

This subsection covers placental development and structure, including the maternal portion (decidua basalis) and embryonic portion (syncytiotrophoblast, cytotrophoblast cells, chorionic membrane, and chorionic villi), which form by weeks 14–16. The umbilical cord connects the conceptus to the placenta and contains two umbilical arteries (carrying deoxygenated blood and wastes from fetus) and one umbilical vein (carrying nutrients and oxygen to fetus), surrounded by amnion and Wharton''s jelly. The placenta performs nutrition and excretion, respiration, and endocrine functions: capillaries in chorionic villi filter fetal wastes and return oxygenated blood via the umbilical vein; oxygen, carbon dioxide, and lipid-soluble substances cross by simple diffusion, while amino acids and iron cross by active transport; and the placenta secretes hCG, estrogens, and progesterone. Maternal and fetal blood do not commingle because blood cells cannot cross the placental barrier, preventing cytotoxic T cells from destroying the fetus and preventing fetal red blood cells from triggering maternal antibody development. The placenta is permeable to fetotoxic substances (alcohol, nicotine, barbiturates, antibiotics, pathogens), which can cause fetal alcohol spectrum disorders and other abnormalities.

**Search terms:** placenta, decidua basalis, syncytiotrophoblast, cytotrophoblast cells, chorionic membrane, chorion, chorionic villi, umbilical cord, umbilical arteries, umbilical vein, Wharton''s jelly, amnion, placentation, fetal-maternal exchange, simple diffusion, active transport, fetotoxic substances, fetal alcohol spectrum disorders (FASD).', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (61, 19, 'Embryonic Membranes', 'Embryonic Membranes', 'During the second week of development, the blastocyst organizes into extra-embryonic membranes—the amnion, yolk sac, allantois, and chorion—that support and protect the embryo. The amnion forms from epiblast cells extending around the amniotic cavity and fills with amniotic fluid (initially maternal plasma filtrate, later supplemented by fetal urine from week 8 onward), which protects the embryo from trauma and temperature changes while allowing movement and preparation for swallowing and breathing. The yolk sac, formed from hypoblast cells, supplies nutrients and provides primitive blood circulation during weeks 2–3, then serves as the source of blood cells and germ cells after the placenta assumes nourishment at week 4; the allantois develops as an outpocketing of the yolk sac and becomes part of the urinary bladder, with both yolk sac and allantois stalks forming the umbilical cord structure. Ectopic pregnancies occur when embryos implant outside the uterus (in the abdominal cavity, rectouterine cavity/Douglas'' pouch, mesentery, or greater omentum), often caused by scar tissue from infection blocking the uterine tube; placenta previa results from implantation near the internal os of the uterus and is the leading cause of antepartum hemorrhage. During week 3, gastrulation transforms the two-layered embryonic disc into a three-layered disc (ectoderm, mesoderm, endoderm) through migration of cells via the primitive streak, with each germ layer giving rise to specific structures: ectoderm forms the nervous systems and sensory organs; mesoderm forms skeleton, muscles, heart, and blood vessels; endoderm forms the gastrointestinal tract, liver, pancreas, and lungs.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (62, 19, 'Pre-implantation Embryonic Development', 'Pre-implantation Embryonic Development', 'Pre-implantation embryonic development begins with the conceptus—the zygote and associated membranes—transported toward the uterus by peristalsis and cilia of the Fallopian tube epithelium, undergoing five or six rapid mitotic cell divisions that produce blastomeres without increasing total volume. By approximately 3 days post-fertilization, the 16-cell conceptus reaches the uterus as a morula (compacted cell mass), then continues dividing into approximately 100 cells that consume uterine milk while organizing around a fluid-filled blastocoel to form the blastocyst. The blastocyst contains an inner cell mass (fated to become the embryo) and outer trophoblasts (which develop into the chorionic sac and fetal placenta); inner mass cells are totipotent until their fates are set as specific cell lineage precursors. The trophoblast excretes enzymes that degrade the zona pellucida, allowing the conceptus to "hatch" in preparation for implantation. At the end of the first week, the blastocyst adheres to and embeds in the uterine wall via trophoblast cells (50–75 percent of blastocysts fail to implant); successful implantation occurs typically in the fundus or posterior wall, with superficial trophoblast cells fusing to form the syncytiotrophoblast, which digests endometrial cells and anchors the blastocyst. The trophoblast secretes human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG), which directs the corpus luteum to produce progesterone and estrogen to suppress menses and create a suitable embryonic environment; hCG accumulates in maternal blood and urine, enabling positive pregnancy test results by the middle of the second week. Ectopic pregnancy or placenta previa can occur if the', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (63, 20, 'Contact Between Sperm and Oocyte', 'Contact Between Sperm and Oocyte', '# Summary: Contact Between Sperm and Oocyte

This subsection describes the anatomical structures and cellular processes involved in sperm-oocyte interaction during fertilization in the distal uterine tube. Key structures include the secondary oocyte, corona radiata (outer layer of follicular/granulosa cells), zona pellucida (transparent glycoprotein membrane), acrosome (enzyme-filled sperm cap), cortical granules, and the oocyte plasma membrane. The textbook explains that capacitated sperm are chemically attracted by the corona radiata cells, then must penetrate both protective layers: sperm burrow through corona radiata cells, bind to zona pellucida receptors triggering the acrosomal reaction (which releases digestive enzymes), and finally a single sperm fuses with sperm-binding receptors on the oocyte plasma membrane. To prevent polyspermy (penetration by multiple sperm), the oocyte deploys two mechanisms: the fast block (near-instantaneous sodium ion permeability change that depolarizes the plasma membrane, lasting ~1 minute) and the slow block/cortical reaction (calcium ion influx triggers cortical granules to fuse with the plasma membrane and release zonal inhibiting proteins and mucopolysaccharides into the space between plasma membrane and zona pellucida, forming the fertilization membrane). Search terms: secondary oocyte, corona radiata, zona pellucida, acrosome, acrosomal reaction, cortical granules, fast block, slow block, cortical reaction, polyspermy, zonal inhibiting proteins, fertilization membrane.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (64, 20, 'The Zygote', 'The Zygote', 'This subsection describes the formation of the zygote following fertilization, introducing the key structures and processes: secondary oocyte arrest in metaphase of meiosis II, completion of meiosis upon fertilization, formation of the second polar body, and transformation of the oocyte into an ovum (female haploid gamete). The subsection explains that pronuclei (haploid nuclei from sperm and oocyte) decondense, expand, replicate their DNA, migrate toward each other, and undergo nuclear envelope disintegration, allowing male- and female-derived genetic material to intermingle and form a single-celled diploid zygote. Zonal inhibiting proteins cause release of attached sperm and destroy oocyte sperm receptors; mucopolysaccharides and the hardened zona pellucida together form a fertilization membrane that prevents additional sperm binding. The subsection distinguishes between dizygotic (fraternal) twins, which result from two eggs fertilized by two sperm, and monozygotic (identical) twins, which result from zygote division during early development; zygote splitting at the two-cell stage produces twin embryos with individual placentas, while splitting at the blastocyst stage (70–100 cells) produces twin embryos sharing a placenta and chorionic cavity. Search terms: secondary oocyte, metaphase II, meiosis II, polar body, ovum, pronuclei, zygote, fertilization membrane, zona pellucida, zonal inhibiting proteins, dizygotic twins, monozygotic twins, blastocyst.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (65, 20, 'Transit of Sperm', 'Transit of Sperm', 'This subsection describes the transit of sperm through the female reproductive tract and the barriers they encounter before fertilization. Key structures include the vagina, cervix, uterus, and uterine tubes (the typical site of sperm-oocyte encounter), along with cervical mucus and uterine leukocytes that eliminate sperm. The subsection establishes that sperm survival is limited by vaginal acidity (pH 3.8), cervical mucus blockage, and phagocytic destruction by uterine leukocytes, reducing hundreds of millions of ejaculated sperm to a few thousand contenders. Sperm transit to the uterine tubes takes 30 minutes to 2 hours, facilitated by uterine contractions, and sperm can survive in the uterine tubes for 3–5 days, whereas oocytes survive only approximately 24 hours after ovulation. The subsection introduces capacitation (priming)—a process in which female reproductive tract fluids improve sperm motility, deplete cholesterol from the sperm head membrane, and facilitate lysosomal enzyme release needed to penetrate the oocyte''s outer cell layer; sperm cannot penetrate the oocyte without completing capacitation.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (66, 21, 'Other Organ Systems', 'Other Organ Systems', 'This subsection covers fetal organ system development from weeks 9–40, tracking changes in the brain, skeletal system, hematopoietic system, sensory organs, integumentary system, and excretory system. Key structures and processes include: ossification and brain expansion (weeks 9–12); bone marrow taking over erythrocyte production from the liver; liver secretion of bile; eye development with fused eyelids transitioning to open/closed eyelids (weeks 21–30); nail development on fingers and toes; sensory organ development (weeks 13–16) including eye convergence, blinking motions, sucking motions of lips, and ear repositioning; scalp hair growth; kidney development and meconium accumulation in intestines; sebaceous gland secretion of vernix caseosa; lanugo covering skin (weeks 17–20) and subsequent shedding; spinal cord axon myelination (weeks 21–30); lung surfactant production (weeks 21–30); testis descent into scrotum in male fetuses (weeks 21–30); and subcutaneous fat deposition (weeks 31–birth). Students would search for: fetal development timeline, erythrocyte production, vernix caseosa, lanugo, meconium, quickening, fetal position, surfactant, myelination, and crown-to-rump measurements.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (67, 21, 'The Fetal Circulatory System', 'The Fetal Circulatory System', 'The fetal circulatory system integrates with the placenta via the umbilical cord to deliver oxygen and nutrients to the fetus before birth. Key structures include the umbilical vein (carries oxygenated blood from placenta), umbilical arteries (carry deoxygenated blood and wastes back to placenta), and three shunts that bypass immature organs: the ductus venosus (diverts blood from umbilical vein around the liver to the inferior vena cava), the foramen ovale (connects right and left atria, bypassing the pulmonary trunk), and the ductus arteriosus (diverts blood from the pulmonary artery into the aorta). Blood flow pathway: oxygenated blood from umbilical vein → inferior vena cava → right atrium → splits between foramen ovale (to left atrium) and right ventricle → pulmonary trunk → ductus arteriosus diverts to aorta; blood then flows through coronary arteries and carotid arteries before descending aorta carries it to lower body and internal iliac arteries → umbilical arteries → placenta for gas and waste exchange. The fetal lungs are nonfunctional and the fetal liver is semifunctional, necessitating these circulatory bypasses until childbirth when the umbilical cord is severed and the newborn''s circulatory system reconfigures.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (68, 22, 'Changes in the Composition of Breast Milk', 'Changes in the Composition of Breast Milk', 'This subsection describes the composition and physiological roles of breast milk across lactation stages. Key structures and substances include colostrum (secreted 48–72 hours postpartum, high in protein and immunoglobulins), transitional milk (postpartum day 3), and mature milk (postpartum day 10 onward), which differ in protein, fat, lactose, and mineral content. Foremilk (watery, rich in lactose and protein) and hindmilk (opaque, creamy, rich in fat) represent compositional changes within a single feeding. The textbook establishes that prolactin, oxytocin, growth hormone, cortisol, parathyroid hormone, and insulin facilitate lactation by transporting maternal amino acids, fatty acids, glucose, and calcium to breast milk. Breast milk''s laxative properties help expel meconium and clear bilirubin (a product of erythrocyte breakdown processed by the liver) through bile excretion, preventing hyperbilirubinemia and jaundice in newborns. Cow''s milk is unsuitable for infants because its proteins are difficult for the immature digestive system to metabolize and absorb, and its composition differs significantly from human breast milk.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (69, 22, 'The Process of Lactation', 'The Process of Lactation', '# Summary: The Process of Lactation

This subsection describes the hormonal regulation and anatomical mechanisms of lactation. **Key structures and processes:** prolactin (pituitary hormone), oxytocin (posterior pituitary hormone), lactocytes, alveoli, myoepithelial cells, lactiferous ducts, lactiferous sinuses, nipple pores, areola, and the let-down reflex. **Search terms:** prolactin, oxytocin, lactation, milk production, milk secretion, let-down reflex, latent period, neuroendocrine reflex, breastfeeding. **Explicit relationships:** prolactin establishes and maintains breast milk supply and mobilizes maternal micronutrients; estrogen, progesterone, and placental hormones inhibit prolactin-mediated milk synthesis during pregnancy; placental expulsion lifts this inhibition, allowing milk production to commence; sensory nerve fibers in the areola trigger a neuroendocrine reflex resulting in milk secretion from lactocytes into alveoli; oxytocin stimulates myoepithelial cells to squeeze milk from alveoli, which drains into lactiferous ducts, collects in lactiferous sinuses, and discharges through nipple pores; growth hormone, cortisol, parathyroid hormone, and insulin facilitate transport of maternal amino acids, fatty acids, glucose, and calcium to breast milk. Frequent milk removal maintains high prolactin levels for several months, though baseline prolactin eventually decreases to pre-pregnancy levels.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (70, 23, 'Autosomal Dominant Inheritance', 'Autosomal Dominant Inheritance', 'This subsection introduces autosomal dominant inheritance, the pattern occurring when a dominant allele is located on one of the 22 pairs of autosomes (non-sex chromosomes). Key structures and concepts include: dominant alleles, recessive alleles, heterozygous genotype (Nn), homozygous genotype (nn), and the Punnett square tool (named after British geneticist Reginald Punnett) used to visualize inheritance patterns. The subsection establishes that a heterozygous parent has a 50 percent chance of passing the dominant allele to offspring, while a homozygous normal parent always passes the normal allele, resulting in offspring having a 50 percent chance of inheriting the disorder. Specific autosomal dominant disorders discussed include neurofibromatosis type I (which induces tumor formation within the nervous system leading to skin and skeletal deformities), achondroplastic dwarfism, Marfan syndrome, and Huntington''s disease. The textbook notes that autosomal dominant disorders are expressed by the presence of just one gene and may manifest later in life, after childbearing years, as exemplified by Huntington''s disease.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (71, 23, 'Autosomal Recessive Inheritance', 'Autosomal Recessive Inheritance', 'Autosomal recessive inheritance occurs when a genetic disorder corresponds to the recessive phenotype; heterozygous individuals are carriers who do not display symptoms because their unaffected gene compensates. Key structures and concepts include: carriers, recessive phenotype, heterozygous individuals, and autosomal recessive disorders. Cystic fibrosis (CF)—characterized by chronic accumulation of thick mucus in the lungs and digestive tract—exemplifies this pattern; a child born to two CF carriers has a 25 percent chance of inheriting the disease (3:1 dominant:recessive ratio), while a child born to one CF carrier and one unaffected individual has 0 percent probability of inheriting CF but 50 percent chance of being a carrier. Other autosomal recessive genetic illnesses include sickle-cell anemia, Tay–Sachs disease, and phenylketonuria. Search terms: autosomal recessive inheritance, carrier, recessive phenotype, cystic fibrosis, sickle-cell anemia, Tay–Sachs disease, phenylketonuria, heterozygous individuals, parental genotypes, inheritance probability.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (72, 23, 'From Genotype to Phenotype', 'From Genotype to Phenotype', 'This subsection introduces the fundamental structures and concepts linking genotype to phenotype: 23 chromosome pairs (including sex chromosomes XX/XY and 22 autosomal chromosomes), genes that code for proteins, alleles inherited from each parent, and homologous chromosomes carrying genes for the same characteristics. Key concepts include genotype (complete genetic makeup), phenotype (expressed characteristics—physical, behavioral, or biochemical), homozygous and heterozygous states, and allele interactions: dominant alleles mask recessive alleles, incomplete dominance occurs in some cases, and codominance allows both alleles to be expressed simultaneously. The subsection establishes that single genes can determine single traits, but multiple genes commonly interact (e.g., eight or more genes determine eye color), and multiple alleles exist in populations (e.g., three alleles—I_A, I_B, i—encode ABO blood type). The text explains that understanding genotype-to-phenotype expression enables prediction of offspring traits from parental genotypes or phenotypes, with clinical applications in identifying risk for heritable genetic disorders; however, most diseases show multigenic inheritance patterns and environmental influences, limiting predictive accuracy except for single-gene disorders.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (73, 23, 'Mendel’s Theory of Inheritance', 'Mendel’s Theory of Inheritance', 'This subsection introduces Gregor Mendel''s foundational theory of inheritance, established through crosses of garden pea plants in the mid-1800s, demonstrating that physical characteristics transmit to subsequent generations in discrete and predictable patterns. Key concepts include dominant traits (expressed when present in a purebred parent), recessive traits (masked when a dominant allele is present), alleles (variations of genes encoding traits like height), and the distinction between homozygous (two identical alleles) and heterozygous (one dominant and one recessive allele) genotypes. The subsection explains Mendel''s principle of random segregation—that parents pass one allele from each pair to offspring in haploid gametes with equal probability—which produces characteristic phenotypic ratios: 3:1 in heterozygous crosses (Aa × Aa) and 1:2:1 genotypic ratios. Mendel''s principle of independent assortment states that allele pairs sort independently during gamete formation except when located close together on the same chromosome, generating offspring diversity. The subsection notes two qualifiers when applying Mendelian genetics to humans: not all genes follow dominant–recessive patterns (some exhibit incomplete dominance or codominance), and large sample sizes are necessary to overcome chance variability in predicting inheritance outcomes. Search terms: Gregor Mendel, dominant trait, recessive trait, allele, homozygous, heterozygous, phenotype, genotype, random segregation, principle of independent assortment, 3:1 ratio, cystic fibrosis, incomplete dominance, codominance.', 4);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (74, 23, 'Other Inheritance Patterns: Incomplete Dominance, Codominance, and Lethal Alleles', 'Other Inheritance Patterns: Incomplete Dominance, Codominance, and Lethal Alleles', 'This subsection introduces inheritance patterns beyond simple dominant–recessive inheritance: incomplete dominance, codominance, and lethal alleles. In incomplete dominance, heterozygous offspring express an intermediate phenotype between homozygous dominant and recessive parents (e.g., curly hair allele and straight-hair allele produce wavy hair in humans; red and white snapdragons produce pink). Codominance involves equal, distinct, and simultaneous expression of both alleles, exemplified by ABO blood type: the I^A allele produces surface antigen A on erythrocytes, the I^B allele produces surface antigen B, the i allele produces nonfunctional enzyme, and I^A and I^B are codominant to each other while both are dominant to i, resulting in blood types A (I^A I^A or I^A i), B (I^B I^B or I^B i), AB (I^A I^B), and O (ii). Lethal alleles prevent in utero development or cause shortened lifespan; recessive lethal inheritance (e.g., Tay–Sachs) affects offspring of two heterozygous carrier parents who both transmit the faulty allele, while dominant lethal alleles (e.g., Huntington''s disease) are rare but can persist if they cause shortened lifespan after reproductive age. Mutations—changes in DNA nucleotide sequence—arise spontaneously from DNA replication errors or from environmental insults (radiation, viruses, tobacco smoke, toxic chemicals) and can alter amino acid sequence and protein structure and function; spontaneous mutations during meiosis account for many spontaneous abortions.', 5);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (75, 23, 'X-linked Dominant or Recessive Inheritance', 'X-linked Dominant or Recessive Inheritance', 'This subsection explains X-linked inheritance patterns involving genes on the X chromosome of the 23rd chromosome pair. Key structures and concepts include: X-linked dominant inheritance (exemplified by vitamin D–resistant rickets, where affected males pass the disease gene to all female offspring but no male offspring, while affected females have a 50% transmission rate to all offspring) and X-linked recessive inheritance (more common, allowing females to be phenotypically normal carriers; examples include color blindness, hemophilia, and some forms of muscular dystrophy). The textbook establishes that males with X-linked recessive diseases cannot be carriers—they are either affected or genotypically normal—whereas females can be genotypically normal, phenotypically normal carriers, or affected, with affected status requiring inheritance of the recessive gene from both parents. Search terms: X chromosome, 23rd pair, X-linked dominant, X-linked recessive, vitamin D–resistant rickets, color blindness, hemophilia, muscular dystrophy, carrier, phenotype, genotype, male offspring, female offspring.', 6);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (76, 24, 'Buffer Systems in the Body', 'Buffer Systems in the Body', 'This subsection introduces the three major buffer systems in the body: protein buffers, phosphate buffers, and bicarbonate-carbonic acid buffers, which operate at different rates (seconds for chemical buffers, minutes for respiratory adjustment, hours to days for renal adjustment). Key structures and entities include: plasma proteins, hemoglobin, amino acids (with amino groups and carboxyl groups), sodium dihydrogen phosphate (NaH₂PO₄), sodium monohydrogen phosphate (Na₂HPO₄), sodium bicarbonate (NaHCO₃), carbonic acid (H₂CO₃), and carbonic anhydrase. The textbook explicitly states that protein buffers account for two-thirds of blood buffering power and work predominantly inside cells; hemoglobin buffers hydrogen ions liberated during CO₂ conversion to bicarbonate; the kidneys excrete hydrogen ions and conserve bicarbonate to maintain blood pH; the lungs control carbonic acid levels through CO₂ expiration; and bicarbonate and carbonic acid exist in a 20:1 ratio in normal blood pH, making this system most efficient at buffering acidic changes from metabolic wastes like lactic acid and ketone bodies. Students would search for: buffer systems, chemical buffers, protein buffers, hemoglobin, phosphate buffers, bicarbonate-carbonic acid buffer, acid-base balance, pH regulation, respiratory buffering, renal buffering, and interstitial fluid (IF) buffering.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (77, 24, 'Renal Regulation of Acid-Base Balance', 'Renal Regulation of Acid-Base Balance', 'The renal system regulates acid-base balance by controlling blood bicarbonate levels, complementing respiratory control of carbonic acid and CO2 exhalation. Key structures include the renal tubule cells (with apical membranes containing antiport mechanisms), peritubular capillaries, and the filtrate; key ions are bicarbonate (HCO3−), sodium (Na+), hydrogen (H+), potassium (K+), and chloride (Cl−). The bicarbonate conservation mechanism involves four steps: (1) Na+ reabsorption from filtrate in exchange for H+ via antiport in apical membranes; (2) tubule cells producing HCO3− shunted to peritubular capillaries; (3) CO2 availability driving formation of carbonic acid, which dissociates into HCO3− and H+; (4) HCO3− passing into peritubular capillaries and returning to blood, while H+ is secreted into filtrate. Decreased blood bicarbonate results from carbonic anhydrase inhibition by diuretics, excessive loss via diarrhea, Addison''s disease (reduced aldosterone), chronic nephritis, or elevated ketones in uncontrolled diabetes mellitus. H+ ions compete with K+ for Na+ exchange; elevated K+ reduces H+ secretion and bicarbonate conservation, while low K+ increases both. Chloride loss causes increased bicarbonate reabsorption as the body substitutes HCO3− for lost Cl− ions.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (78, 24, 'Respiratory Regulation of Acid-Base Balance', 'Respiratory Regulation of Acid-Base Balance', 'This subsection explains how the respiratory system regulates acid-base balance by controlling blood levels of carbonic acid through CO2 exhalation. Key structures and processes include: CO2 reacting with water to form carbonic acid in blood equilibrium; the lungs and pulmonary capillaries as sites where CO2 and carbonic acid regulation occurs; chemoreceptors (peripheral sensors in aorta and carotid artery walls, and central sensors in the brain) that signal the brain to adjust respiratory rate in response to CO2 and pH changes; and the respiratory center in the medulla oblongata, which modulates breathing rate based on CSF pH. The textbook establishes that increased respiration rate causes CO2 exhalation, which lowers carbonic acid levels and raises blood pH, while hyperventilation causes excessive CO2 loss leading to alkalosis (remedied by rebreathing), and that hypoventilation or impaired respiration causes hypercapnia. Search terms: respiratory regulation of acid-base balance, carbonic acid, CO2, blood pH, chemoreceptors, peripheral blood sensors, aorta, carotid arteries, medulla oblongata, respiratory center, CSF, hypercapnia, hypocapnia, hyperventilation, hypoventilation, alkalosis.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (79, 25, 'Composition of Body Fluids', 'Composition of Body Fluids', 'This subsection describes the composition and electrolyte distribution across body fluid compartments: extracellular fluid (ECF)—comprising plasma and interstitial fluid (IF)—and intracellular fluid (ICF). Blood plasma contains high concentrations of sodium (Na+), chloride (Cl−), bicarbonate, and protein; IF has high sodium, chloride, and bicarbonate but lower protein; ICF contains elevated potassium (K+), phosphate, magnesium, and protein. The textbook establishes that plasma and IF are compositionally more similar to each other than to ICF, and that ICF has high potassium and phosphate while plasma and ECF have high sodium and chloride. Sodium-potassium pumps in cell membranes maintain the concentration gradients by using ATP energy to pump sodium out of cells and potassium into cells, counteracting sodium and potassium "leak" through membrane pores. The subsection also identifies specialized ECF components—cerebrospinal fluid, lymph, synovial fluid, pleural fluid, pericardial fluid, peritoneal fluid, and aqueous humor—and notes that most body fluids are electrically neutral with balanced cations and anions. Search terms: extracellular fluid (ECF), intracellular fluid (ICF), plasma, interstitial fluid (IF), sodium, chloride, potassium, bicarbonate, protein, phosphate, magnesium, sodium-potassium pumps, ATP, cations, anions.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (80, 25, 'Fluid Compartments', 'Fluid Compartments', 'This subsection introduces the two major body fluid compartments separated by physical barriers: intracellular fluid (ICF) and extracellular fluid (ECF). The ICF is enclosed by plasma membranes, comprises approximately 60 percent of total body water (about 25 liters in an average adult male), and constitutes the principal component of cytosol/cytoplasm; its volume is tightly regulated because excessive water loss causes cytosol solute concentration to impair cellular function, while excessive water entry causes cell lysis. The ECF surrounds all cells and comprises one-third of body water, with two primary constituents: plasma (approximately 20 percent of ECF), which travels through blood vessels and transports blood cells, proteins, electrolytes, nutrients, gases, and wastes; and interstitial fluid (IF), through which gases, nutrients, and waste materials travel between capillaries and cells. A selectively permeable cell membrane separates cells from IF and regulates material passage between them. Additional ECF components include cerebrospinal fluid (bathes brain and spinal cord), lymph, synovial fluid (joints), pleural fluid (pleural cavities), pericardial fluid (cardiac sac), peritoneal fluid (peritoneal cavity), and aqueous humor (eye). An appropriate balance of solutes inside and outside cells must be maintained for normal function.

**Search terms:** intracellular fluid (ICF), extracellular fluid (ECF), plasma, interstitial fluid (IF), cytosol, cytoplasm, plasma membrane, cerebrospinal fluid, lymph, synovial fluid, pleural fluid, pericardial fluid, peritoneal fluid, aqueous humor, selectively permeable cell membrane, fluid compartments.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (81, 25, 'Fluid Movement between Compartments', 'Fluid Movement between Compartments', 'This subsection explains fluid movement between body compartments (plasma, interstitial fluid [IF], and intracellular fluid [ICF]) through two primary mechanisms: hydrostatic pressure and osmotic pressure. Hydrostatic pressure (capillary blood pressure), generated by heart pumping, forces plasma and nutrients out of capillaries at the arteriolar end where it exceeds colloid osmotic pressure (produced by circulating albumin); at the venule end, lower hydrostatic pressure allows fluid and wastes to re-enter capillaries, with excess interstitial fluid drained by the lymphatic system to the subclavian veins. Osmotic gradients—produced by solute concentration differences across semi-permeable membranes—drive water movement by osmosis from plasma to IF and from IF to ICF (and in reverse directions); water moves constantly between compartments as conditions change. In the kidneys, increased hydrostatic pressure increases water leaving capillaries and urine filtrate formation, while decreased hydrostatic pressure (as in dehydration) impairs kidney function and can cause kidney failure. During sweating, water loss increases solute concentration in tissues, creating an osmotic gradient that draws water from blood into sweat glands and skin; if sweating continues, dehydration spreads throughout the body, but rehydration reverses this gradient to replenish tissue water. Sodium-potassium pumps maintain high intracellular potassium and low intracellular sodium by using ATP energy to pump sodium out and potassium into cells, counteracting passive leakage through pores.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (82, 25, 'Solute Movement between Compartments', 'Solute Movement between Compartments', 'This subsection describes solute movement between fluid compartments through active and passive transport mechanisms. Key structures and processes include: active transport (which requires ATP energy and moves substances against concentration gradients), the sodium-potassium pump (which pumps sodium out of cells and potassium into cells against their gradients), passive transport (which moves substances down concentration gradients without energy), and facilitated transport (which moves molecules down concentration gradients through specific protein channels). Molecules that move easily across membranes include gases, lipids, and water (which uses aquaporins as water channels); molecules that require facilitated transport include polar molecules such as glucose, amino acids, and ions. Glucose transporters specifically mediate glucose transfer into cells via facilitated transport. Students would search for: active transport, passive transport, facilitated transport, sodium-potassium pump, concentration gradient, ATP, aquaporins, glucose transporters, and solute movement.', 4);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (83, 26, 'Compensation Mechanisms', 'Compensation Mechanisms', 'This subsection describes compensatory mechanisms that maintain blood pH within the normal range (7.35–7.45) through buffers, respiration, and renal mechanisms. Key structures and processes include: respiratory compensation for metabolic acidosis (increases respiratory rate to drive off CO₂ and readjust the bicarbonate to carbonic acid ratio to 20:1 within minutes); respiratory compensation for metabolic alkalosis (decreases respiratory rate to conserve CO₂, with limited effectiveness); renal compensation for respiratory acidosis (increases conservation of bicarbonate and secretion of H⁺ via exchange mechanism); renal compensation for respiratory alkalosis (decreases bicarbonate production and reabsorbs H⁺ from tubular fluid via K⁺-H⁺ exchange mechanism/antiporter). The subsection defines four acid-base disorders with characteristic lab values: metabolic acidosis (pH ↓, PCO₂ normal then ↓, HCO₃⁻ ↓); respiratory acidosis (pH ↓, PCO₂ ↑, HCO₃⁻ normal then ↑); metabolic alkalosis (pH ↑, PCO₂ normal then ↑, HCO₃⁻ ↑); respiratory alkalosis (pH ↑, PCO₂ ↓, HCO₃⁻ normal then ↓). Lab tests measuring pH, PCO₂, and total HCO₃⁻ identify whether imbalance is respiratory or metabolic and assess compensation effectiveness; metabolic acid-base imbalances typically result from kidney disease, while compensatory mechanisms have limits when kidney failure or respiratory disease impairs function.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (84, 26, 'Metabolic Acidosis: Primary Bicarbonate Deficiency', 'Metabolic Acidosis: Primary Bicarbonate Deficiency', 'Metabolic acidosis is defined as blood pH below 7.35 caused by primary bicarbonate deficiency, disrupting the normal 20:1 bicarbonate-to-carbonic acid buffer ratio at pH 7.40. The most common causes are organic acids or excessive ketone bodies in the blood. Eight specific causes are listed: diarrhea (bicarbonate loss), uremia (phosphoric, sulfuric, and lactic acids), diabetic ketoacidosis (increased ketone bodies), strenuous exercise (lactic acid), methanol (formic acid), paraldehyde (β-hydroxybutyric acid), isopropanol (propionic acid), and ethylene glycol (glycolic, oxalic, and formic acids); salicylate/aspirin produces sulfasalicylic acid and causes acidosis when overdosed. Additional mechanisms include decreased excretion of hydrogen ions (which inhibits bicarbonate conservation) and excessive bicarbonate loss through the gastrointestinal tract. Diabetic ketoacidosis can be initiated by missed insulin doses in insulin-dependent diabetics. **Search terms:** metabolic acidosis, primary bicarbonate deficiency, pH 7.35, bicarbonate-to-carbonic acid ratio, ketone bodies, diabetic ketoacidosis, uremia, lactic acid, sulfasalicylic acid, hydrogen ion excretion.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (85, 26, 'Metabolic Alkalosis: Primary Bicarbonate Excess', 'Metabolic Alkalosis: Primary Bicarbonate Excess', 'Metabolic alkalosis is characterized by blood pH above 7.45 due to primary bicarbonate excess, the opposite of metabolic acidosis. Key causes include ingestion of excessive bicarbonate, citrate, or antacids; Cushing''s disease (chronic ACTH hypersecretion by the anterior pituitary gland), which elevates aldosterone levels and increases potassium loss by urinary excretion; loss of hydrochloric acid through vomiting; potassium depletion from diuretic use for hypertension; and excessive laxative use. The subsection also covers related acid-base disorders: respiratory acidosis (blood overly acidic due to excess carbonic acid and CO2, caused by conditions like pneumonia, emphysema, or congestive heart failure) and respiratory alkalosis (blood overly alkaline due to deficiency in carbonic acid and CO2, typically from hyperventilation caused by emotional upset, fear, fever, infections, hypoxia, elevated catecholamines, or aspirin overdose/salicylate toxicity). Search terms: metabolic alkalosis, primary bicarbonate excess, pH, ACTH, aldosterone, potassium loss, respiratory acidosis, carbonic acid, respiratory alkalosis, hyperventilation, catecholamines, salicylate toxicity.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (86, 27, 'Regulation of Sodium and Potassium', 'Regulation of Sodium and Potassium', 'This subsection covers the hormonal regulation of sodium and potassium balance in the kidneys, focusing on aldosterone and angiotensin II. Key structures include the renal collecting tubule, distal tubule, distal convoluted tubules, collecting ducts, glomerular capillaries, Bowman''s capsule, adrenal cortex, and the sodium-potassium pump. Aldosterone increases sodium reabsorption and potassium excretion in the distal tubule and collecting ducts by stimulating the sodium-potassium pump, causing increased blood sodium levels and blood volume; it is released when blood potassium increases, blood sodium severely decreases, or blood pressure decreases. Angiotensin II causes vasoconstriction, increases systemic blood pressure, increases the glomerular filtration rate, and signals increased aldosterone release from the adrenal cortex. A negative feedback loop operates wherein increased osmolality of the extracellular fluid (ECF) following aldosterone-stimulated sodium absorption inhibits further aldosterone release. Search terms: aldosterone, angiotensin II, sodium reabsorption, potassium excretion, distal tubule, collecting duct, sodium-potassium pump, glomerular filtration rate, extracellular fluid osmolality.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (87, 27, 'Roles of Electrolytes', 'Roles of Electrolytes', 'This subsection introduces six key electrolytes—sodium (Na+), potassium (K+), chloride (Cl−), bicarbonate (HCO3−), calcium (Ca2+), and phosphate—and their physiological roles in nerve excitability, endocrine secretion, membrane permeability, buffering, and fluid compartment regulation. Sodium is the major extracellular cation responsible for one-half of the osmotic pressure gradient between ICF and ECF; it is freely filtered through glomerular capillaries, reabsorbed in the proximal convoluted tubule, and regulated by the kidneys, with hyponatremia and hypernatremia representing abnormal low and high concentrations. Potassium is the major intracellular cation that establishes resting membrane potential in neurons and muscle fibers; sodium-potassium pumps maintain potassium concentration gradients between ICF and ECF, and potassium is excreted through the distal convoluted tubule and collecting ducts under aldosterone influence, with hypokalemia and hyperkalemia as pathological states. Chloride is the predominant extracellular anion contributing to osmotic pressure and electrical neutrality of ECF; its renal reabsorption follows sodium pathways. Bicarbonate, the second most abundant blood anion, is produced from CO2 via carbonic anhydrase in red blood cells, functions in acid-base buffering, and is conserved (not excreted) by the kidneys. Calcium and phosphate are incorporated into bones and teeth (serving as mineral reserves), with calcium essential for muscle contraction, enzyme activity, blood coagulation, neurotransmitter release, and hormone secretion, while phosphate is found in phospholipids, ATP, nucleotides, and buffers. Search terms: sodium, potassium, chloride, bic', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (88, 28, 'Regulation of Water Intake', 'Regulation of Water Intake', '# Summary: Regulation of Water Intake

This subsection introduces **plasma osmolality**, **dehydration**, and the **thirst response** as key mechanisms regulating water intake. **Osmoreceptors** in the hypothalamus''s thirst center monitor blood solute concentration; when plasma osmolality increases above ideal values, the hypothalamus triggers conscious thirst awareness and releases **antidiuretic hormone (ADH)** through the posterior pituitary gland, which signals the kidneys to recover water from urine and dilute blood plasma. The hypothalamus also sends signals via the **sympathetic nervous system** to salivary glands, decreasing watery serous output and increasing mucus output, producing "dry mouth" and reinforcing thirst sensation. When decreased blood volume results from water loss, **baroreceptors** in the aortic arch and carotid arteries detect reduced blood pressure and signal the heart to increase contraction rate and strength; simultaneously, the kidneys'' **renin-angiotensin hormonal system** produces **angiotensin II**, which stimulates thirst and triggers **aldosterone** release from the adrenal glands, causing sodium reabsorption in the distal tubules of nephrons with water following osmotically back into blood. Dehydration—insufficient water in blood and tissues—can result from vomiting, diarrhea, or inadequate fluid consumption and constitutes a medical emergency potentially causing loss of consciousness, coma, or death without rapid rehydration.

**Search terms:** osmolality, plasma osmolality, dehydration, thirst response, osmoreceptors, hypothalamus, antidiuretic hormone (ADH), posterior pituitary gland, sympathetic nervous system, salivary glands, baroreceptors, aortic arch, carotid arteries, renin-angiotensin hormonal system, angio', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (89, 28, 'Role of ADH', 'Role of ADH', 'Antidiuretic hormone (ADH), also known as vasopressin, is produced in the hypothalamus and stored and released by the posterior pituitary to control water reabsorption from the collecting ducts and tubules in the kidney. When osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus detect increased blood plasma concentration, the hypothalamus signals ADH release; ADH constricts arterioles in peripheral circulation and causes epithelial cells lining renal collecting tubules to move aquaporins (water channel proteins) from the cell interior to the apical surface and cell membrane, increasing water permeability and water reabsorption into the bloodstream. When blood plasma becomes less concentrated, ADH levels decrease, aquaporins are removed from collecting tubule cell membranes, and water passage from urine into blood decreases. Diuretics are compounds that increase urine output and decrease water conservation; alcohol acts as a diuretic by inhibiting ADH release, and caffeine at high concentrations acts as a diuretic. Diuretics are used clinically to treat hypertension, congestive heart failure, and fluid retention associated with menstruation.

**Search terms:** antidiuretic hormone, ADH, vasopressin, hypothalamus, posterior pituitary, osmoreceptors, collecting ducts, collecting tubules, aquaporins, water channel proteins, water reabsorption, peripheral circulation, arterioles, diuretic, alcohol, caffeine, hypertension, congestive heart failure, fluid retention.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (90, 29, 'Ankle and Foot Joints', 'Ankle and Foot Joints', 'The talocrural joint forms the ankle through articulations between the talus bone and the distal tibia and fibula; the superior talus articulates with the inferior tibia to bear body weight, while the talus sides articulate with the medial malleolus of the tibia and lateral malleolus of the fibula. The ankle is a uniaxial hinge joint permitting dorsiflexion and plantar flexion, while additional tarsal joints—including the subtalar joint (between talus and calcaneus), talus-navicular joint, and calcaneus-cuboid joint—are plane joints that enable foot inversion and eversion. The talocrural joint is supported by ligaments on its sides: the medial deltoid ligament resists excessive eversion, and three lateral ligaments (anterior talofibular ligament, posterior talofibular ligament, and calcaneofibular ligament) resist excessive inversion. The anterior talofibular ligament and posterior talofibular ligament span between the talus bone and lateral malleolus of the fibula, while the calcaneofibular ligament connects the calcaneus bone and fibula. Search terms: talocrural joint, talus, tibia, fibula, medial malleolus, lateral malleolus, subtalar joint, deltoid ligament, anterior talofibular ligament, posterior talofibular ligament, calcaneofibular ligament, dorsiflexion, plantar flexion, inversion, eversion.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (91, 29, 'Articulations of the Vertebral Column', 'Articulations of the Vertebral Column', 'This subsection describes the synovial joints of the vertebral column, focusing on zygapophysial joints (facet joints)—plane joints formed between superior and inferior articular processes of adjacent vertebrae that provide limited motion. The orientation of articular processes varies by region: cervical vertebrae have flattened, upward- or downward-facing processes enabling flexion, extension, lateral flexion, and rotation; thoracic vertebrae have vertically positioned processes allowing greatest rotation but limited by overlapping spinous processes and the thoracic cage; lumbar vertebrae permit extension, flexion, and lateral flexion but prohibit rotation due to articular process orientation. The atlanto-occipital joint, formed between the superior articular processes of the atlas and occipital condyles, has U-shaped curvature along the anterior-posterior axis enabling head flexion and extension. The atlantoaxial joint consists of three articulations: paired articulations between the axis''s superior articular processes and the atlas''s inferior articular processes (relatively flat, horizontally oriented), plus a pivot joint between the dens and the anterior arch of the atlas, with a posterior ligament stabilizing the dens, allowing atlas rotation on the axis for head rotation. Search terms: zygapophysial joints, facet joints, cervical vertebrae, thoracic vertebrae, lumbar vertebrae, atlanto-occipital joint, atlas, axis, dens, atlantoaxial joint, articular processes, occipital condyles.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (92, 29, 'Elbow Joint', 'Elbow Joint', 'The elbow joint is a uniaxial hinge joint formed by three articulations—the humeroulnar joint (trochlea of humerus with trochlear notch of ulna), the humeroradial joint, and the proximal radioulnar joint—all enclosed within a single articular capsule. The articular capsule is thin anteriorly and posteriorly but thickened along its margins by intrinsic ligaments that prevent side-to-side movements and hyperextension. The ulnar collateral ligament arises from the medial epicondyle of the humerus and attaches to the medial side of the proximal ulna; its anterior portion resists hyperextension and is vulnerable to injury from forceful forearm extension (Tommy John surgery). The radial collateral ligament arises from the lateral epicondyle of the humerus and blends into the annular ligament. The annular ligament encircles the head of the radius and supports its articulation with the radial notch of the ulna at the proximal radioulnar joint, a pivot joint that allows radius rotation during supination and pronation of the forearm. Search terms: elbow joint, humeroulnar joint, humeroradial joint, proximal radioulnar joint, articular capsule, ulnar collateral ligament, radial collateral ligament, annular ligament, trochlea, trochlear notch, medial epicondyle, lateral epicondyle, radial notch, supination, pronation.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (93, 29, 'Hip Joint', 'Hip Joint', 'The hip joint is a multiaxial ball-and-socket joint formed between the head of the femur and the acetabulum of the hip bone, designed for weight-bearing and stability during standing and walking with limited range of motion compared to the shoulder joint. Key anatomical structures include the acetabulum (deepened by the acetabular labrum, a fibrocartilage lip), the articular capsule, and three intrinsic ligaments—the iliofemoral ligament, pubofemoral ligament, and ischiofemoral ligament—which arise from the hip bone margins and attach to the femur at the base of the neck, spiraling around the femoral head and neck. The ligament of the head of the femur (ligamentum teres) is an intracapsular ligament that spans between the acetabulum and femoral head; though normally slack and providing no significant joint support, it provides a pathway for an artery that supplies the head of the femur. Hip extension tightens the ligaments, pulling the femoral head into the acetabulum and stabilizing the joint during upright standing with minimal muscle contraction. Common clinical concerns include femoral neck fractures ("broken hip"), particularly in elderly individuals with osteoporosis, which can disrupt blood supply and lead to necrosis of the femoral neck or head; post-surgical complications from prolonged bed rest include pneumonia, pressure ulcer infection, and thrombophlebitis that may result in pulmonary embolism. Search terms: hip joint, ball-and-socket joint, femur, acetabulum, acetabular labrum, articular capsule, iliofemoral ligament, pubofemoral ligament, ischiofemoral ligament, ligament of the head of the femur, ligamentum teres, femoral neck fracture, osteoarthritis.', 4);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (94, 29, 'Knee Joint', 'Knee Joint', 'The knee joint, the body''s largest joint, consists of three articulations—the femoropatellar joint (patella and distal femur) and medial and lateral tibiofemoral joints (femoral and tibial condyles)—all enclosed within a single articular capsule. The knee functions as a hinge joint through rolling and gliding motions of the femur on the tibia, allowing flexion and extension; rotation is available only when flexed. The patella, a sesamoid bone in the quadriceps femoris tendon, slides vertically in a groove on the distal femur and protects the tendon while the quadriceps femoris acts via the patellar ligament to extend the leg and provide dynamic stabilization. Two C-shaped fibrocartilage menisci (medial and lateral) located between femoral and tibial articulating surfaces provide padding and fill gaps; the medial meniscus is anchored to the articular capsule and tibial collateral ligament, limiting its movement. The knee is supported by extrinsic collateral ligaments (fibular/lateral and tibial/medial collateral ligaments) that stabilize the extended knee, and intrinsic intracapsular cruciate ligaments (anterior and posterior) anchored at the intercondylar eminence; the posterior cruciate ligament supports the flexed, weight-bearing knee and prevents anterior femoral sliding, while the anterior cruciate ligament resists hyperextension when the knee is extended. Search terms: knee joint, femoropatellar joint, tibiofemoral joint, patella, quadriceps femoris, patellar ligament, meniscus, medial meniscus, lateral meniscus, fibular collateral ligament, tibial collateral ligament, anterior cruciate ligament, posterior cruciate ligament, intercondylar eminence, popliteus', 5);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (95, 29, 'Shoulder Joint', 'Shoulder Joint', 'The shoulder joint, or glenohumeral joint, is a ball-and-socket joint formed by articulation between the head of the humerus and the glenoid cavity of the scapula, possessing the largest range of motion of any joint in the body. The glenoid cavity is small and shallow (approximately one-third the size of the humeral head) and is deepened by the glenoid labrum, a fibrocartilage lip extending around the cavity''s outer margin. The articular capsule is thin and loose; weak intrinsic ligaments (coracohumeral ligament and three glenohumeral ligaments) provide some support by strengthening the superior and anterior capsule walls. Primary structural support derives from four rotator cuff muscles (supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and subscapularis) that arise from the scapula, attach to the greater or lesser tubercles of the humerus, and whose tendons fuse to the anterior, superior, and posterior articular capsule walls; these muscles function as "dynamic ligaments" by constantly adjusting contraction to stabilize the humeral head within the glenoid cavity. The subacromial bursa and subscapular bursa prevent friction between rotator cuff tendons and the scapula. Repetitive upper limb abduction (throwing, swimming, racquet sports) may cause bursal or tendon inflammation, glenoid labrum tears, or rotator cuff degeneration; humeral dislocations typically occur inferiorly when force is applied during full abduction, and shoulder injuries may result in adhesive capsulitis ("frozen shoulder").', 6);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (96, 29, 'Temporomandibular Joint', 'Temporomandibular Joint', 'The temporomandibular joint (TMJ) is a synovial joint formed by articulation between the mandibular fossa and articular tubercle of the temporal bone with the condyle of the mandible, enabling mandibular depression, mandibular elevation, and side-to-side and protraction/retraction motions. The joint contains a flexible articular disc positioned between the temporal bone and mandibular condyle that smooths movements; during mouth opening, the mandible undergoes both hinging (downward motion) and anterior gliding of the condyle and articular disc from the mandibular fossa onto the articular tubercle. The TMJ is supported by an extrinsic ligament spanning from the base of the skull to the lingula on the medial mandibular ramus. TMJ dislocation occurs when the mandibular condyle moves anterior to the articular tubercle (from excessive mouth opening or jaw trauma), preventing mouth closure. Temporomandibular joint disorder is a painful condition caused by arthritis, articular cartilage wear, muscle fatigue, articular disc damage, or jaw injury, presenting with headache, difficulty chewing, or lock jaw, and treated with pharmacologic agents or bite guards. Search terms: temporomandibular joint, TMJ, mandibular fossa, articular tubercle, mandibular condyle, articular disc, mandibular depression, mandibular elevation, TMJ dislocation, temporomandibular joint disorder.', 7);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (97, 30, 'Functional Classification of Joints', 'Functional Classification of Joints', 'The functional classification of joints is determined by the amount of mobility between adjacent bones, categorizing joints as synarthrosis (immobile), amphiarthrosis (slightly moveable), or diarthrosis (freely moveable). Fibrous joints and cartilaginous joints are functionally classified as either synarthrosis or amphiarthrosis, while all synovial joints are classified as diarthrosis. Synarthroses provide strong unions for protection, exemplified by skull sutures and the manubriosternal joint uniting the manubrium and body of the sternum. Amphiarthroses permit limited mobility, including cartilaginous joints between vertebral bodies (united by intervertebral discs of fibrocartilage) and the pubic symphysis (a cartilaginous joint anchoring the pubic regions of the hip bones). Diarthroses are freely moveable joints found primarily in the appendicular skeleton and are subdivided by axes of motion: uniaxial joints (e.g., elbow joint) allow movement in one plane, biaxial joints (e.g., metacarpophalangeal joints) allow movement in two planes, and multiaxial joints (e.g., shoulder and hip joints) allow movement in all three anatomical planes (transverse, frontal, sagittal), including anterior-posterior, medial-lateral, and rotational movements around the long axis.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (98, 31, 'Additional Structures Associated with Synovial Joints', 'Additional Structures Associated with Synovial Joints', 'This subsection describes additional structures that support synovial joints: articular discs (small, oval-shaped fibrocartilage) and menisci (larger, C-shaped fibrocartilage) located between articulating bones, which unite bones, provide shock absorption and cushioning, or smooth movements—exemplified at the sternoclavicular joint, radius-ulna joint, knee joint, and temporomandibular joint. Bursae are thin connective tissue sacs filled with lubricating liquid that reduce friction between skin, ligaments, muscles, or tendons and bones; they are classified as subcutaneous (e.g., prepatellar bursa over the kneecap, olecranon bursa at the elbow), submuscular (e.g., trochanteric bursa between the greater trochanter of the femur and gluteus maximus muscle), or subtendinous (e.g., subacromial bursa protecting shoulder muscle tendon under the acromion of the scapula, suprapatellar bursa above the knee). Tendon sheaths are smaller connective tissue sacs surrounding tendons at joints, containing lubricating fluid for smooth tendon motion. Bursitis—inflammation of a bursa causing pain, swelling, tenderness, and joint stiffness—occurs most commonly at the shoulder, hip, knee, or elbow and includes subacromial bursitis, trochanteric bursitis, ischial bursitis, prepatellar bursitis ("housemaid''s knee"), and olecranon bursitis ("student''s elbow"), arising from muscle overuse, trauma, pressure, rheumatoid arthritis, gout, or infection and treated with antibiotics, NSAIDs, corticosteroids, or fluid drainage. Muscles crossing joints also provide dynamic support by increasing contraction strength in response to increased forces.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (99, 31, 'Structural Features of Synovial Joints', 'Structural Features of Synovial Joints', 'Synovial joints are defined by a joint cavity bounded by the articular capsule (fibrous connective tissue attached to bone outside the articulating surface), within which bones articulate. Friction reduction and joint lubrication are achieved through articular cartilage (hyaline cartilage covering bone articulating surfaces) and synovial fluid (secreted by the synovial membrane lining the inner articular capsule surface); synovial fluid also nourishes the avascular articular cartilage. Ligaments—classified as extrinsic (outside articular capsule), intrinsic (fused to articular capsule wall), or intracapsular (inside articular capsule)—connect bones, allow normal movements, and limit excessive motion. Tendons (dense connective tissue attaching muscle to bone) provide dynamic support; muscles and tendons crossing the joint serve as "dynamic ligaments" and are particularly important at joints with relatively weak ligaments, such as the shoulder joint. Synovial joints are functionally classified as diarthroses due to their capacity for smooth, free movement. Search terms: synovial joint, joint cavity, articular capsule, articular cartilage, hyaline cartilage, synovial membrane, synovial fluid, ligament, extrinsic ligament, intrinsic ligament, intracapsular ligament, tendon, diarthrosis.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (100, 31, 'Types of Synovial Joints', 'Types of Synovial Joints', '# Summary: Types of Synovial Joints

This subsection classifies synovial joints into six structural types based on articulating bone surface shapes: pivot, hinge, condyloid, saddle, plane, and ball-and-socket joints. Pivot joints (atlantoaxial joint between C1 and C2 vertebrae; proximal radioulnar joint between radius head and ulnar radial notch) are uniaxial, allowing rotation around a single axis as a rounded bone portion rotates within a ligament-formed ring. Hinge joints (elbow joint with trochlea of humerus articulating with trochlear notch of ulna; knee, ankle, interphalangeal joints) are uniaxial, permitting only bending and straightening along one axis. Condyloid (ellipsoid) joints (metacarpophalangeal joints between metacarpal distal end and proximal phalanx; radiocarpal joint between radius shallow depression and scaphoid, lunate, and triquetrum carpal bones) are biaxial, allowing anterior-posterior and side-to-side movements. Saddle joints (first carpometacarpal joint between trapezium and first metacarpal at thumb base; sternoclavicular joint) are biaxial with concave-convex articulating surfaces, enabling thumb opposition. Plane (gliding) joints (intercarpal joints, intertarsal joints, acromioclavicular joint, zygapophysial joints between superior and inferior articular processes) are multiaxial based on shape but functionally limited by ligaments and neighboring bones. Ball-and-socket joints (hip joint with femur head articulating with acetabulum; glenohumeral shoulder joint with humerus head articulating with glenoid cavity) are multiaxial, permitting anterior-posterior, medial-lateral, and rotational movements; the shallow', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (101, 32, 'Abduction and Adduction', 'Abduction and Adduction', 'This subsection introduces abduction and adduction as medial-lateral movements occurring in the coronal plane at condyloid, saddle, and ball-and-socket joints; abduction moves limbs, fingers, toes, or thumb laterally away from the body''s midline, while adduction brings them toward or across the midline. The text describes circumduction as circular movement involving sequential combination of flexion, adduction, extension, and abduction, occurring at biaxial condyloid and saddle joints and multiaxial ball-and-socket joints. Rotation is presented as occurring within the vertebral column, at pivot joints, or at ball-and-socket joints; at the uniaxial atlantoaxial joint, the atlas rotates around the dens of the axis to allow head rotation, and at the proximal radioulnar joint, the radius rotates during pronation and supination. At multiaxial shoulder and hip joints, the humerus and femur rotate around their long axes, producing medial (internal) rotation when the anterior surface moves toward the midline and lateral (external) rotation when it moves away. Key searchable terms: abduction, adduction, circumduction, rotation, coronal plane, condyloid joints, saddle joints, ball-and-socket joints, pivot joints, atlantoaxial joint, atlas, axis, dens, proximal radioulnar joint, medial rotation, lateral rotation, humerus, femur.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (102, 32, 'Flexion and Extension', 'Flexion and Extension', 'This subsection introduces flexion and extension as primary body movements occurring in the sagittal plane, involving anterior or posterior motions of the neck, trunk, and limbs. Key anatomical structures include the vertebral column (with intervertebral discs forming symphysis joints and plane synovial joints between articular processes), shoulder, elbow, wrist, metacarpophalangeal and interphalangeal joints, hip joint, and knee joint. The textbook defines flexion as anterior motion or angle decrease between bones, while extension is posterior motion or angle increase; lateral flexion of the vertebral column occurs in the coronal plane. In the upper limb, flexion and extension occur at the arm (shoulder), forearm (elbow), hand (wrist), and fingers; thumb flexion brings it against the index finger or palm, while thumb extension moves it away from the palm. In the lower limb, hip flexion brings the thigh forward and upward (limited by hip ligaments in extension), and knee flexion bends the knee while extension straightens it. Hyperextension and hyperflexion represent abnormal excessive movements causing injury, commonly occurring at hinge joints (knee, elbow) and in the cervical region during whiplash injuries.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (103, 32, 'Opposition and Reposition', 'Opposition and Reposition', 'Opposition and reposition are thumb movements occurring at the first carpometacarpal joint, a saddle joint formed between the trapezium carpal bone and the first metacarpal bone. Opposition brings the thumb tip into contact with a finger tip through a combination of flexion and abduction at this joint, while reposition returns the thumb to its anatomical position next to the index finger. The subsection also presents a comprehensive classification of joint types by their structural category and movement capabilities: pivot joints (uniaxial; atlantoaxial joint, proximal radioulnar joint) allow rotational movement; hinge joints (uniaxial; knee, elbow, ankle, interphalangeal joints) allow flexion/extension; condyloid joints (biaxial; metacarpophalangeal joints, radiocarpal joint, metatarsophalangeal joints) allow flexion/extension, abduction/adduction, and circumduction; saddle joints (biaxial; first carpometacarpal joint, sternoclavicular joint) allow flexion/extension, abduction/adduction, and circumduction; plane joints (multiaxial; intertarsal joints, vertebral articular processes) allow inversion/eversion or flexion/extension/lateral flexion; and ball-and-socket joints (multiaxial; shoulder and hip joints) allow flexion/extension, abduction/adduction, circumduction, and medial/lateral rotation. Search terms: opposition, reposition, first carpometacarpal joint, saddle joint, trapezium, first metacarpal bone, thumb flexion, thumb abduction, pivot joint, hinge joint, condyloid joint, plane joint, ball-and-socket joint.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (104, 32, 'Superior Rotation and Inferior Rotation', 'Superior Rotation and Inferior Rotation', 'Superior rotation and inferior rotation are scapular movements defined by glenoid cavity direction and produced by combinations of muscles acting on the scapula; these motions involve rotation around a point inferior to the scapular spine. During superior rotation, the glenoid cavity moves upward while the medial end of the scapular spine moves downward, and this motion is required for full abduction of the upper limb because without it, the greater tubercle of the humerus would hit the acromion of the scapula, preventing arm abduction above shoulder height. Superior rotation also occurs when carrying heavy loads on the shoulder without arm abduction. Inferior rotation occurs during limb adduction and involves downward motion of the glenoid cavity with upward movement of the medial end of the scapular spine. Key searchable terms: superior rotation, inferior rotation, scapula, glenoid cavity, scapular spine, upper limb abduction, greater tubercle of humerus, acromion, limb adduction.', 4);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (105, 32, 'Supination and Pronation', 'Supination and Pronation', 'Supination and pronation are forearm movements that occur between two positions: the anatomical (supinated) position with palm facing forward and radius-ulna parallel, and the pronated position with palm facing backward and radius-ulna forming an X-shape. Pronation is the motion from supinated to pronated position, produced by rotation of the radius at the proximal radioulnar joint (a pivot joint) accompanied by movement at the distal radioulnar joint, causing the distal radius to cross over the distal ulna. Supination is the opposite motion, in which rotation of the radius returns the bones to parallel positions with palm anterior-facing. Search terms: supination, pronation, forearm movements, anatomical position, radius, ulna, proximal radioulnar joint, distal radioulnar joint, pivot joint, rotation of the radius.', 5);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (106, 33, 'Krebs Cycle/Citric Acid Cycle/Tricarboxylic Acid Cycle', 'Krebs Cycle/Citric Acid Cycle/Tricarboxylic Acid Cycle', '# Summary: Krebs Cycle/Citric Acid Cycle/Tricarboxylic Acid Cycle

The Krebs cycle (also called citric acid cycle or tricarboxylic acid cycle) is a metabolic pathway occurring in the inner mitochondrial matrix where pyruvate molecules from glycolysis are metabolized to generate high-energy molecules: ATP, NADH, and FADH2. Pyruvate dehydrogenase converts three-carbon pyruvate into two-carbon acetyl CoA through oxidative decarboxylation, releasing CO2 and producing NADH; acetyl CoA then combines with four-carbon oxaloacetate (catalyzed by citrate synthase) to form six-carbon citrate, initiating the cycle. The cycle proceeds through sequential enzymatic conversions: aconitase converts citrate to isocitrate; isocitrate dehydrogenase converts isocitrate to five-carbon α-ketoglutarate (producing CO2 and NADH); α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase converts α-ketoglutarate to four-carbon succinyl CoA; succinyl CoA dehydrogenase converts succinyl CoA to succinate (producing GTP, which generates ATP); succinate dehydrogenase converts succinate to fumarate (producing FADH2); fumarase converts fumarate to malate; and malate dehydrogenase converts malate back to oxaloacetate (producing NADH). Each cycle turn produces three NADH, one ATP (via GTP), and one FADH2; NADH and FADH2 subsequently enter the electron transport chain and oxidative phosphorylation in the inner mitochondrial membrane. The Krebs cycle also supplies starting materials for protein and fat metabolism, and pyruvate carbons', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (107, 33, 'Oxidative Phosphorylation and the Electron Transport Chain', 'Oxidative Phosphorylation and the Electron Transport Chain', 'The electron transport chain (ETC) consists of four enzyme complexes (Complex I–IV) and two coenzymes (ubiquinone and Cytochrome c) embedded in the inner mitochondrial membrane that transfer electrons from NADH and FADH2 and pump H+ ions across the inner membrane to create a proton gradient. Molecular oxygen (O2) serves as the terminal electron acceptor, combining with electrons and H+ ions to form water. ATP synthase, a protein pore complex in the inner mitochondrial membrane, harnesses the flow of H+ ions down their gradient to phosphorylate ADP and produce ATP; approximately three ATP are generated per oxidized NADH and two ATP per oxidized FADH2, yielding a net total of 36 ATP per glucose molecule in aerobic respiration. Gluconeogenesis synthesizes new glucose from pyruvate, lactate, glycerol, or amino acids (alanine, glutamine) primarily in the liver during fasting and low blood glucose conditions. Key enzymatic differences distinguish gluconeogenesis from glycolysis: pyruvate is converted to oxaloacetate, then phosphoenolpyruvate carboxykinase (PEPCK) converts oxaloacetate to phosphoenolpyruvate (PEP); hexokinase is replaced by glucose-6-phosphatase and phosphofructokinase-1 is replaced by fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase. Glycerol from fat breakdown can be phosphorylated to dihydroxyacetone phosphate (DHAP) and used by the liver as a gluconeogenesis substrate.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (108, 34, 'Mechanisms of Heat Exchange', 'Mechanisms of Heat Exchange', 'This subsection introduces the four mechanisms of heat exchange—conduction, convection, radiation, and evaporation—that the body uses to maintain homeostasis when the environment is not thermoneutral. Conduction is the transfer of heat between two objects in direct contact (e.g., skin contacting a cold or warm object), accounting for approximately 3 percent of body heat loss. Convection is the transfer of heat to surrounding air or water; warmed air or water rises away from the body and is replaced by cooler air or water, accounting for approximately 15 percent of body heat loss, and can result in hyperthermia if convection currents draw heat away faster than the body can replace it. Radiation is the transfer of heat via infrared waves from the body to the environment, accounting for approximately 60 percent of body heat loss. Evaporation is the transfer of heat through the evaporation of water (sweat) from the skin; the rate of evaporation depends on relative humidity, with sweating serving as the primary cooling mechanism during exercise and accounting for approximately 20 percent of heat loss at rest. All four mechanisms rely on the principle that heat flows from higher to lower concentration and vary in rate according to environmental temperature and conditions.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (109, 35, 'Ketone Body Oxidation', 'Ketone Body Oxidation', 'This subsection covers ketone body oxidation and the interconnected processes of ketone metabolism and lipogenesis. Key structures and molecules include β-hydroxybutyrate (the primary ketone body), acetoacetate, acetyl CoA, acetone, and the brain as an organ capable of using ketones as an alternative energy source when glucose is limited. The textbook explains that β-hydroxybutyrate is oxidized to acetoacetate (releasing NADH), then converted to acetoacetyl CoA, which is split into two acetyl CoA molecules that enter the Krebs cycle for energy production. When ketones are produced faster than they can be used, they break down into CO₂ and acetone; acetone is removed by exhalation (causing sweet-smelling breath), while CO₂ can acidify blood and cause diabetic ketoacidosis. The subsection also details lipogenesis—the conversion of excess acetyl CoA into fatty acids, triglycerides, cholesterol, steroids, and bile salts in adipocytes and hepatocytes—and describes the citrate-malate shuttle mechanism: pyruvate carboxylase converts pyruvate to oxaloacetate, pyruvate dehydrogenase converts pyruvate to acetyl CoA, these combine to form citrate (which crosses the mitochondrial membrane), and citrate is reconverted to oxaloacetate and acetyl CoA in the cytoplasm, where acetyl CoA becomes malonyl CoA for fatty acid synthesis. Search terms: ketone body oxidation, β-hydroxybutyrate, acetoacetate, acetyl CoA, lipogenesis, adipocytes, hepatocytes, pyruvate carboxylase, pyruvate dehydrogenase, citrate, malonyl CoA, diabetic ketoacidosis, Krebs cycle.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (110, 36, 'The Absorptive State', 'The Absorptive State', '# Summary: The Absorptive State

The absorptive state (fed state) occurs after a meal when anabolism exceeds catabolism, involving digestion and nutrient absorption through the intestines. Carbohydrate digestion begins in the mouth; protein and fat digestion begin in the stomach and small intestine; sugars and amino acids enter the bloodstream while fats enter the lymphatic system, with both systems transporting nutrients to the liver, adipose tissue, and muscle cells. Rising blood glucose stimulates pancreatic beta cells to release insulin, which initiates glucose absorption by liver hepatocytes, adipose cells, and muscle cells; once inside cells, glucose converts to glucose-6-phosphate, establishing a concentration gradient that promotes continued glucose uptake. Insulin stimulates glycogen storage in liver and muscle cells and promotes protein synthesis in muscle; excess glucose is stored as glycogen or fat in adipose tissue as triglycerides. The subsection also addresses phenylketonuria (PKU), caused by deficient phenylalanine hydroxylase enzyme activity, which prevents adequate breakdown of phenylalanine to tyrosine, resulting in toxic phenylalanine accumulation that damages the central nervous system and brain; symptoms include delayed neurological development, intellectual disability, seizures, and tremors. PKU treatment includes diet modification (avoiding phenylalanine-rich foods like aspartame, certain animal products, and starches), vitamin supplementation, and gene therapy, with early dietary intervention reducing symptom severity.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (111, 36, 'The Postabsorptive State', 'The Postabsorptive State', '# Summary: The Postabsorptive State

The postabsorptive (fasting) state occurs when food has been digested, absorbed, and stored, requiring the body to rely initially on stored glycogen. Key structures and hormones include: alpha cells of the pancreas (which release glucagon), liver cells, peripheral tissues, and the brain. As blood glucose drops (below the normal range of 80–120 mg/dL), insulin levels decrease and glucagon is released from alpha cells of the pancreas; glucagon inhibits glycogen synthesis and stimulates glycogen breakdown in liver cells, releasing glucose for use by peripheral tissues and the brain. Gluconeogenesis begins in the liver to replace glucose used by peripheral tissues, while the liver does not absorb glucose after prolonged fasting but instead converts excess absorbed glucose into triglycerides and fatty acids for long-term storage. During starvation, glycolysis shuts off in cells that can use alternative fuels; muscles switch from glucose to fatty acids, which convert to acetyl CoA for the Krebs cycle and ATP production, while pyruvate, lactate, and alanine from muscle cells are exported to the liver for glucose synthesis. After several days of starvation, ketone bodies become the major fuel source for the heart and other organs, created from fatty acids and triglyceride stores to prevent protein breakdown; once fat and protein stores deplete, muscle proteins are broken down for gluconeogenesis.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (112, 37, 'Food and Metabolism', 'Food and Metabolism', 'This subsection introduces caloric measurement and energy balance in human nutrition, defining the nutritional Calorie as the heat required to raise 1 kg of water by 1 °C, and establishing that daily caloric needs (1500–2000 calories) depend on body mass, age, height, gender, activity level, and exercise frequency. Key relationships include: accumulation of 3500 excess calories causes one pound of weight gain; processing of carbohydrates requires less energy than processing of proteins; calories burned must exceed calories ingested for weight loss; and the type of food ingested affects metabolic rate. The subsection covers MyPlate dietary guidelines, which categorize food into six groups (fruits, vegetables, grains, protein foods, dairy, and oils), and introduces vitamins as organic compounds involved in mineral and bone metabolism, cell and tissue growth, and energy metabolism, with B vitamins playing the largest metabolic role. Fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K) are absorbed with lipids in chylomicrons and can accumulate in body lipid stores, potentially causing hypervitaminosis, while water-soluble vitamins (eight B vitamins and vitamin C) are absorbed with water, move through water-based bodily fluids without storage, and are excreted in urine. Search terms: calories, nutritional Calorie, caloric needs, weight gain, carbohydrates, proteins, MyPlate, food groups, vitamins, fat-soluble vitamins, water-soluble vitamins, B vitamins, vitamin A, vitamin D, vitamin E, vitamin K, vitamin C, β-carotene, chylomicrons, hypervitaminosis, ChooseMyPlate.gov, SuperTracker.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (113, 38, 'Catabolic Reactions', 'Catabolic Reactions', '# Summary: Catabolic Reactions

Catabolic reactions break down large organic molecules into smaller molecules, releasing chemical bond energy with approximately 40% transferred to adenosine triphosphate (ATP) and 60% released as heat. ATP, the energy currency of cells, consists of adenine, ribose, and three phosphate groups; the high-energy bond between the second and third phosphate groups is cleaved by catabolic enzymes to produce adenosine diphosphate (ADP) and phosphate (Pi), with ATP, ADP, and Pi constantly cycled through energy-building and energy-releasing reactions. ATP drives all bodily functions including muscle contraction, maintaining electrical potential of nerve cells, and food absorption in the gastrointestinal tract. The four major macromolecular groups—carbohydrates, lipids, proteins, and nucleic acids—serve as ATP sources, with carbohydrates (polysaccharides like starch and glycogen; monosaccharides like glucose and fructose) being the most common fuel; glucose is the primary monosaccharide for ATP production and is regulated by endocrine control mechanisms, with excess glucose stored as glycogen in the liver and skeletal muscles or converted to triglyceride in adipocytes. Triglycerides undergo β-oxidation for energy, with excess fat stored in adipocytes of subcutaneous tissue and other tissues and organs; proteins are broken down into amino acids for use as building blocks or further catabolized for ATP production, and nucleic acids (DNA and various RNAs) are digested into nucleotides for absorption and use in nucleic acid metabolism.

**Search terms:** catabolic reactions, ATP (adenosine triphosphate), ADP (adenosine diphosphate), high-energy bond, phosphate groups, carbohydrates, glucose, glycogen, lipids, triglycerides', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (114, 38, 'Hormonal Regulation of Metabolism', 'Hormonal Regulation of Metabolism', '# Summary: Hormonal Regulation of Metabolism

This subsection introduces catabolic hormones (cortisol, glucagon, adrenaline/epinephrine, cytokines) that stimulate molecular breakdown and energy production, and anabolic hormones (growth hormone, insulin-like growth factor, insulin, testosterone, estrogen) that promote molecular synthesis. Cortisol is released from the adrenal gland in response to stress and increases blood glucose via gluconeogenesis; glucagon is released from pancreatic alpha cells during starvation and stimulates hepatic glycogen breakdown to raise blood glucose, functioning opposite to insulin in a negative-feedback system. Adrenaline/epinephrine is released via sympathetic nervous system activation and increases heart rate, heart contractility, causes vasoconstriction, acts as a bronchodilator, and stimulates gluconeogenesis. Growth hormone is synthesized and released from the pituitary gland and stimulates cell, tissue, and bone growth; insulin-like growth factor stimulates muscle and bone growth while inhibiting apoptosis; insulin is produced by pancreatic beta cells, controls blood glucose levels, promotes glucose uptake into muscle, adipose, and liver cells, and opposes glucagon in a negative-feedback system. Testosterone (produced by testes and ovaries) stimulates muscle mass, strength, and bone growth; estrogen (produced primarily by ovaries, also by liver and adrenal glands) increases metabolism and fat deposition.

**Search terms:** catabolic hormones, anabolic hormones, cortisol, glucagon, adrenaline, epinephrine, cytokines, growth hormone, insulin-like growth factor, insulin, testosterone, estrogen, gluconeogenesis, glycogen breakdown, negative-feedback system, blood glucose regulation, adrenal gland, pancreas, pituitary gland, sympathetic nervous system, bron', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (115, 39, 'Urea Cycle', 'Urea Cycle', 'The urea cycle is a set of biochemical reactions occurring primarily in the liver and secondarily in the kidney that converts ammonium ions into urea and water to prevent toxic accumulation of ammonium in the body. Ammonium ions are produced from amino acid breakdown through transamination, a process in which an amine group or ammonium ion from an amino acid is exchanged with a keto group on another molecule, generating a Krebs cycle intermediate and ammonium that enters the urea cycle. In the urea cycle, ammonium combines with CO₂ to produce urea, which is eliminated through the kidneys in urine. Amino acids also serve as an energy source through the Krebs cycle, as their processing generates metabolic intermediates including pyruvate, acetyl CoA, acetoacyl CoA, oxaloacetate, and α-ketoglutarate, particularly during starvation. Search terms: urea cycle, ammonium ions, amino acid breakdown, transamination, liver, kidney, urea, Krebs cycle, metabolic intermediates, nitrogenous byproducts.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (116, 40, 'Endurance Exercise', 'Endurance Exercise', 'Endurance exercise primarily utilizes slow-twitch fibers (slow fibers, SO fibers) and involves aerobic metabolism to sustain contractions over long periods with little force but numerous repetitions. Endurance training induces cellular adaptations in slow fibers: increased mitochondria production enabling greater aerobic metabolism and ATP production, increased myoglobin in the sarcoplasm to store oxygen for mitochondria, and angiogenesis (formation of more extensive capillary networks) to supply oxygen and remove metabolic waste. Muscle mass does not greatly increase during endurance training to maintain a smaller diffusion area for nutrients and gases, allowing capillary networks to supply deep muscle portions. The proportion of SO fibers in a muscle determines its suitability for endurance activities; postural muscles contain a large number of SO fibers and relatively few FO and FG fibers to maintain posture, and endurance athletes like marathon-runners benefit from a larger proportion of SO fibers. These cellular changes collectively enable sustained low-level muscle contractions for extended periods without fatigue, though endurance training can result in overuse injuries such as stress fractures and joint and tendon inflammation.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (117, 40, 'Performance-Enhancing Substances', 'Performance-Enhancing Substances', 'This subsection covers performance-enhancing substances used by athletes to boost muscle performance, including anabolic steroids, erythropoietin (EPO), human growth hormone (hGH), and creatine. Anabolic steroids are a form of testosterone that stimulates muscle formation and increase muscle mass and power output; EPO is a hormone normally produced in the kidneys that triggers red blood cell production to increase oxygen availability for aerobic respiration in muscles; hGH facilitates muscle mass building and promotes healing of muscle and other tissues after strenuous exercise, allowing faster recovery and more sustained high-level performance. Creatine phosphate provides quick bursts of ATP to muscles during initial contraction stages, and increasing available creatine is thought to produce more ATP and increase explosive power output. Although these substances often improve performance, most are banned by governing bodies and illegal for nonmedical purposes; anabolic steroid use has been linked to infertility, aggressive behavior, cardiovascular disease, and brain cancer, and performance-enhancing substances generally carry serious, often nonreversible, and sometimes fatal health risks due to physiological strain exceeding what the body can handle. Search terms: anabolic steroids, testosterone, erythropoietin (EPO), human growth hormone (hGH), creatine, creatine phosphate, ATP, muscle mass, power output, aerobic respiration, muscle healing, performance-enhancing substances.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (118, 40, 'Resistance Exercise', 'Resistance Exercise', 'Resistance exercise recruits fast glycolytic (FG) fibers to produce short, powerful movements through high rates of ATP hydrolysis and cross-bridge formation, and causes muscle hypertrophy by increasing myofibril and sarcomere formation, which increases muscle fiber thickness and requires dietary protein supplementation. Unlike endurance training, resistance training does not significantly increase mitochondria or capillary density but does increase connective tissue development and tendon strength to contain powerful contractions and prevent tendon damage as force transfers from muscle to bone. Effective strength training requires progressive intensity increases; continued weight lifting without increasing load does not increase muscle size, and muscles adapt to heavier loads by adding structural proteins. Cellular damage to the sarcolemma and myofibrils occurs after intense resistance exercise, contributing to post-exercise soreness, but muscles gain mass during repair when additional structural proteins replace damaged tissue. Improper resistance training causes overuse injuries to muscle, tendon, or bone if the load is excessive, recovery time is insufficient, or joint alignment is poor. Search terms: FG fibers, SO/FO fibers, muscle hypertrophy, myofibrils, sarcomeres, connective tissue, tendons, ATP hydrolysis, cross-bridge formation, sarcolemma, overuse injuries, stress fractures.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (119, 41, 'ATP and Muscle Contraction', 'ATP and Muscle Contraction', 'This subsection explains the cross-bridge cycle, the repeated sequence of myosin head attachment, power stroke, and detachment that enables thin filaments to slide past thick filaments during muscle contraction. Key structures include myosin heads, actin binding sites, thick filaments, thin filaments, and the M-line; key processes are cross-bridge formation, inorganic phosphate (Pi) release, the power stroke (approximately 10 nm movement toward the M-line), myosin head re-cocking, and ATP hydrolysis by myosin''s intrinsic ATPase activity. The textbook explicitly states that ATP binding causes myosin head detachment from actin; Pi release causes stronger myosin-actin attachment; ATP hydrolysis energy re-cocks the myosin head into a high-energy configuration; and the power stroke expends this energy, leaving myosin in a low-energy position. Continued muscle contraction requires ATP availability for the cross-bridge cycle to recur; absence of ATP prevents myosin head detachment and causes rigor mortis. Search terms: cross-bridge cycle, myosin head, actin, power stroke, ATP, ADP, inorganic phosphate, sarcomere, thick filament, thin filament, myosin, rigor mortis.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (120, 41, 'Sources of ATP', 'Sources of ATP', 'This subsection explains ATP as the energy source for muscle contraction and the three mechanisms of ATP regeneration in muscle cells: creatine phosphate metabolism, anaerobic glycolysis, and aerobic respiration. ATP powers the cross-bridge cycle and active-transport Ca++ pumps in the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR); muscle contraction cannot occur without sufficient ATP. Creatine phosphate stores energy in phosphate bonds and is catalyzed by creatine kinase to rapidly regenerate ATP from ADP, powering the first ~15 seconds of contraction; glycolysis then provides ATP for ~1 minute by breaking down glucose (from blood glucose or stored glycogen) into two ATP and pyruvic acid per glucose molecule, with pyruvic acid converted to lactic acid when oxygen is unavailable. Aerobic respiration, occurring in mitochondria and utilizing glucose, pyruvic acid, and fatty acids, provides ~95% of ATP in resting or moderately active muscles, generating ~36 ATP per glucose molecule and requiring a steady O2 supply; myoglobin stores excess oxygen to support efficient contractions. Muscle fatigue—when a muscle cannot contract in response to nervous system signals—correlates with reduced ATP reserves, lactic acid buildup (lowering intracellular pH), Na+/K+ imbalances disrupting Ca++ flow from the SR, and SR/sarcolemma damage; intense activity creates an oxygen debt requiring O2 to restore ATP, creatine phosphate, and convert lactic acid back to pyruvic acid or glucose/glycogen in the liver.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (121, 41, 'The Sliding Filament Model of Contraction', 'The Sliding Filament Model of Contraction', '# Summary: The Sliding Filament Model of Contraction

The sliding filament model describes how skeletal muscle fibers contract when signaled by a motor neuron: thin filaments slide past thick filaments within sarcomeres, with thin filaments anchored at Z-discs and thick filaments anchored at the M-line. Contraction initiation requires Ca++ entry into the sarcoplasm, which binds to troponin (a component of the troponin-tropomyosin complex); this binding causes tropomyosin to slide away from myosin-binding sites on actin filaments, exposing active sites and allowing myosin heads to bind and form cross-bridges. The myosin heads then pull the thin filaments toward the sarcomere center, but each head can only pull a short distance before requiring ATP-dependent "re-cocking" to pull again. Key structures include actin filaments, myosin filaments, tropomyosin, troponin, myosin heads, Z-discs, M-line, and sarcomeres; key processes include Ca++ binding to troponin, tropomyosin displacement, cross-bridge formation, and myosin head pulling. Search terms: sliding filament model, thin filaments, thick filaments, myosin-binding sites, tropomyosin, troponin, troponin-tropomyosin complex, Ca++ (calcium ions), cross-bridges, myosin heads, actin microfilaments, sarcoplasm, Z-discs, M-line, sarcomeres, ATP.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (122, 42, 'Motor Units', 'Motor Units', 'This subsection introduces motor units—the group of muscle fibers innervated by a single motor neuron—as the fundamental basis for nervous system control of skeletal muscle contraction. Key structures and concepts include: motor neurons and their axon terminals, skeletal muscle fibers, neuromuscular junctions (NMJs), small motor units (exemplified in extraocular eye muscles and hand muscles for fine control), and large motor units (exemplified in thigh and back muscles for gross movements). The textbook establishes that each muscle fiber is innervated by only one motor neuron, and that small motor units contain lower-threshold motor neurons that fire first and generate small contractile forces, while large motor units contain higher-threshold motor neurons that activate larger muscle fibers and generate greater contractile forces. Recruitment—the progressive activation of additional motor units—produces increasing muscle contraction strength, allowing the nervous system to generate forces ranging from minimal (picking up a feather with biceps brachii) to maximal (lifting heavy weights). Motor units are distributed throughout skeletal muscles to provide a wide range of control; to prevent fatigue, motor units alternate activity rather than contracting simultaneously, enabling the nervous system to efficiently utilize muscles for sustained contractions.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (123, 42, 'Muscle Tone', 'Muscle Tone', 'Muscle tone is the small amount of sustained contraction in skeletal muscles maintained through cyclical activation of motor units via complex nervous system-skeletal muscle interaction, enabling joint stabilization and posture maintenance. Key structures and concepts include: skeletal muscles, motor units, contractile proteins, nervous system, central nervous system (CNS), cerebellum, and upper motor neurons. Hypotonia—absence of low-level contractions—results from damage to the cerebellum or loss of innervations (as in poliomyelitis) and presents with flaccid appearance and weak reflexes. Hypertonia—excessive muscle tone—results from damage to upper motor neurons and presents as either muscle rigidity (Parkinson''s disease) or spasticity (some strokes), accompanied by hyperreflexia. The cyclical motor unit activation prevents complete muscle fatigue by allowing some motor units to recover while others remain active.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (124, 42, 'The Frequency of Motor Neuron Stimulation', 'The Frequency of Motor Neuron Stimulation', 'This subsection introduces how motor neuron stimulation frequency controls muscle tension through several mechanisms. Key structures and processes include: the twitch (single contraction from one motor neuron action potential), measured by a myogram; the three phases of a twitch—latent period (action potential propagation and Ca++ release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum), contraction phase (Ca++ binding to troponin, cross-bridge formation, sarcomere shortening), and relaxation phase (Ca++ pumping back into SR, cross-bridge cycling cessation); and graded muscle response (nervous system modification of force production). The textbook establishes that motor neuron firing frequency and the number of active motor neurons both affect skeletal muscle tension, and describes how increased stimulation frequency produces wave summation (successive twitches summed together via additional Ca++ release activating more sarcomeres), incomplete tetanus (tension 3–4 times greater than single twitch), and complete tetanus (continuous contraction when relaxation phase disappears). Additional concepts include treppe (staircase effect—progressive increase in muscle tension from steady motor neuron signaling and elevated sarcoplasmic Ca++ concentration, requiring adequate ATP) and the relationship between thick and thin filament overlap and cross-bridge formation in tension production.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (125, 42, 'The Length-Tension Range of a Sarcomere', 'The Length-Tension Range of a Sarcomere', 'This subsection introduces the **length-tension relationship** of sarcomeres and explains how sarcomere length directly influences the force generated during muscle contraction. Key structures include **myosin heads**, **actin**, **thin filaments**, **thick filaments**, **cross-bridges**, **H zone**, **actin-binding sites**, **accessory proteins**, and **connective tissue**. The subsection establishes that maximal tension occurs when a sarcomere is at 80–120 percent of its resting length (with 100 percent representing optimal overlap of actin-binding sites and myosin heads), and that tension decreases when sarcomeres are stretched beyond 120 percent (insufficient filament overlap) or shortened beyond 80 percent (thin filaments extend past myosin heads, shrinking the H zone). The text states that when thick and thin filaments do not overlap at all, no cross-bridges can form and no tension is produced, though accessory proteins and connective tissue normally prevent this extreme stretching. Students would search for: **length-tension relationship**, **sarcomere length**, **cross-bridge formation**, **filament overlap**, **myosin heads**, **actin**, **H zone**, and **optimal sarcomere length**.', 4);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (126, 43, 'Excitation-Contraction Coupling', 'Excitation-Contraction Coupling', 'Excitation-contraction coupling in skeletal muscle begins with motor neurons (originating in the spinal cord and brainstem) transmitting action potentials along axons to the neuromuscular junction (NMJ), where acetylcholine (ACh) is released into the synaptic cleft and binds to ACh receptors on the motor end-plate of the sarcolemma, causing depolarization and opening of voltage-gated sodium channels that trigger an action potential along the sarcolemma. Acetylcholinesterase (AChE) degrades ACh to prevent unwanted extended excitation, while the propagating action potential travels along the sarcolemma and down T-tubules (transverse invaginations), which form triads with the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR) membranes and surround myofibrils containing actin and myosin. The action potential triggers opening of calcium channels in the SR membrane, releasing Ca²⁺ ions into the sarcoplasm where they interact with shielding proteins (troponin-tropomyosin complex) on the thin filaments, exposing actin-binding sites for myosin head attachment. Myosin then pulls actin filaments toward the center of the sarcomere, shortening the muscle fiber. Key searchable terms: membrane potential, ion channels, action potential, motor neurons, axons, neuromuscular junction (NMJ), acetylcholine (ACh), ACh receptors, motor end-plate, depolarization, voltage-gated sodium channels, acetylcholinesterase (AChE), T-tubules, sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR), triad, myofibril, sarcomere, calcium ions (Ca²⁺), thin filament, thick filament, troponin-tropomyosin complex, actin', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (127, 43, 'The Sarcomere', 'The Sarcomere', 'The sarcomere is the functional unit of skeletal muscle, defined as a packet of actin and myosin microfilaments along with regulatory proteins troponin and tropomyosin. Each sarcomere is approximately 2 μm in length, bordered by Z-discs (Z-lines) to which actin myofilaments are anchored, and organized as a three-dimensional cylinder within myofibrils. The sarcomere contains two primary myofilament types: thin filaments (actin with its troponin-tropomyosin complex projecting from Z-discs toward the center) and thick filaments (myosin with multiple heads projecting from the center toward but not reaching the Z-discs). Sarcomeres are bundled sequentially within myofibrils that run the entire length of the muscle fiber and attach to the sarcolemma; the striated appearance of skeletal muscle results from this sequential arrangement of actin and myosin myofilaments. As myofibrils contract, the entire muscle cell contracts, with hundreds to thousands of myofibrils (each containing thousands of sarcomeres) found within a single muscle fiber.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (128, 44, 'Arches of the Foot', 'Arches of the Foot', '# Summary: Arches of the Foot

This subsection introduces the three arches of the foot—the transverse arch, medial longitudinal arch, and lateral longitudinal arch—and their roles in shock absorption and weight distribution during locomotion. The transverse arch forms the medial-lateral curvature of the mid-foot and is formed by the cuneiform bones and bases of the first to fourth metatarsal bones; it distributes body weight side to side. The longitudinal arches run the length of the foot, formed by tarsal bones posteriorly and metatarsal bones anteriorly, with the talus bone receiving body weight at the top and conveying it to the ground through the calcaneus bone posteriorly and metatarsal heads anteriorly. Ligaments unite adjacent foot bones and provide elasticity that allows the arches to flatten and spread during weight bearing, storing energy; when weight is removed, elastic ligaments recoil to restore the arches and release stored energy. Foot muscles also contribute to energy absorption, and prolonged or excessive stretching of supporting ligaments can result in pes planus (flat foot or fallen arches), particularly affecting the medial longitudinal arch.

**Search terms:** transverse arch, medial longitudinal arch, lateral longitudinal arch, cuneiform bones, metatarsal bones, tarsal bones, talus, calcaneus, pes planus, flat foot, fallen arches, shock absorption, weight distribution, arch rebound, elastic ligaments, foot muscles.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (129, 44, 'Metatarsal Bones', 'Metatarsal Bones', 'The metatarsal bones form the anterior half of the foot, positioned between the tarsal bones (cuboid and cuneiform) posteriorly and the phalanges anteriorly, numbered 1–5 from medial to lateral. Key structures include the base (proximal end) of each metatarsal, the head (expanded distal end) that forms the ball of the foot, and the fifth metatarsal''s lateral expansion at its base that serves as a muscle attachment site. Each metatarsal articulates with the proximal phalanx of a toe to form a metatarsophalangeal joint; the second metatarsal is the longest. The foot contains 14 phalanx bones total: the big toe (hallux, toe 1) has two phalanges (proximal and distal), while toes 2–5 each have three phalanges (proximal, middle, and distal), with interphalangeal joints connecting adjacent phalanges. A bunion is a localized swelling on the medial side of the foot at the base of the big toe, adjacent to the first metatarsophalangeal joint. Search terms: metatarsal bones, metatarsal base, metatarsal head, cuboid bone, cuneiform bones, metatarsophalangeal joint, phalanges, phalanx bones, hallux, interphalangeal joint, bunion.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (130, 44, 'Tarsal Bones', 'Tarsal Bones', 'The tarsal bones form the posterior half of the foot and include seven bones organized into three groups: the proximal group (talus and calcaneus), the intermediate group (navicular and cuboid), and the distal group (medial cuneiform, intermediate cuneiform, and lateral cuneiform). The talus, the most superior tarsal bone, articulates with the tibia and fibula to form the ankle joint through three articulation areas: the superomedial surface of the talus with the medial malleolus of the tibia, the top of the talus with the distal end of the tibia, and the lateral side of the talus with the lateral malleolus of the fibula. Body weight transfers from the tibia to the talus to the calcaneus (the largest tarsal bone), which rests on the ground; the sustentaculum tali, a bony extension of the medial calcaneus, supports the medial side of the talus. The talus articulates inferiorly with the calcaneus and anteriorly with the navicular bone; the navicular articulates anteriorly with the three cuneiform bones; and the cuboid articulates with the anterior end of the calcaneus and with the navicular and lateral cuneiform bones. The three cuneiforms have broad superior surfaces and narrow inferior surfaces that together produce the transverse (medial-lateral) curvature of the foot. Search terms: tarsal bones, talus, calcaneus, navicular, cuboid, medial cuneiform, intermediate cuneiform, lateral cuneiform, ankle joint, sustentaculum tali.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (131, 45, 'Carpal Bones', 'Carpal Bones', 'The wrist contains eight carpal bones arranged in a proximal row (scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform) and distal row (trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, hamate), with the pisiform articulating with the triquetrum and the hamate featuring a hook of the hamate on its anterior surface. The scaphoid and lunate articulate directly with the distal radius, the triquetrum articulates with a fibrocartilaginous pad spanning the radius and ulnar styloid process, and the distal ulna does not articulate with any carpal bones; the radiocarpal joint (formed by proximal carpal bones and radius) and midcarpal joint (between proximal and distal carpal rows) together enable all wrist movements. The distal carpal bones articulate with the metacarpal bones, and the carpal bones form a U-shaped grouping with the flexor retinaculum (attached to trapezium, scaphoid, hamate, and pisiform) creating the carpal tunnel, through which pass tendons of nine anterior forearm muscles and an important nerve. Inflammation or swelling in the carpal tunnel compresses the nerve, causing carpal tunnel syndrome characterized by pain, numbness, and muscle weakness. **Search terms:** carpal bones, scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform, trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, hamate, hook of the hamate, radiocarpal joint, midcarpal joint, flexor retinaculum, carpal tunnel, carpal tunnel syndrome.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (132, 45, 'Metacarpal Bones', 'Metacarpal Bones', 'The subsection introduces the five metacarpal bones of the palm, which lie between the carpal bones and the phalanges. Key anatomical structures include: metacarpal bones (numbered 1–5 from thumb to pinky), carpometacarpal joints (where metacarpal proximal ends articulate with distal carpal bones), metacarpophalangeal joints (where metacarpal distal ends articulate with proximal phalanges), and the knuckles (formed by the distal ends of metacarpals). The first metacarpal bone is separated from the others, allowing independent thumb mobility; the second and third metacarpals are immobile and firmly anchored; and the fourth and fifth metacarpals have limited anterior-posterior mobility, with the fifth showing greater mobility. The anterior movement of the fourth and fifth metacarpals, particularly the fifth, increases grip strength during power gripping. Search terms: metacarpal bones, carpometacarpal joint, metacarpophalangeal joint, thumb mobility, hand gripping, knuckles.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (133, 46, 'Limb Growth', 'Limb Growth', '# Summary: Limb Growth

Limb development begins with the formation of limb buds—small bulges appearing on the lateral embryo near the end of the fourth week (upper limb) and shortly thereafter (lower limb)—each consisting of mesenchyme core covered by ectoderm. The apical ectodermal ridge, formed by thickened ectoderm at the limb bud tip, stimulates underlying mesenchyme proliferation and drives proximal-to-distal limb outgrowth; cells farther from this ridge slow division and differentiate. During the sixth week, distal limb bud regions expand and flatten into paddle shapes (future hand or foot), with constrictions appearing at the wrist/ankle and elbow/knee sites, while mesenchyme differentiates into hyaline cartilage that forms models of future limb bones; programmed cell death within the paddle creates separations between developing fingers and toes. Initial limb positioning has palms/foot soles facing medially with thumb/big toe oriented toward the head; during the seventh week, the upper limb undergoes 90-degree lateral rotation (palm faces anteriorly, thumb points laterally) while the lower limb undergoes 90-degree medial rotation (big toe moves to medial foot side). **Search terms:** limb bud, apical ectodermal ridge, mesenchyme, ectoderm, proximal-to-distal axis, paddle shape, handplate, footplate, hyaline cartilage, limb rotation, upper limb lateral rotation, lower limb medial rotation, fourth week, sixth week, seventh week of development.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (134, 46, 'Ossification of Appendicular Bones', 'Ossification of Appendicular Bones', 'This subsection covers ossification of appendicular bones, the developmental process by which girdle and limb bones form bone tissue. Key structures and processes include: endochondral ossification (the primary mechanism for all girdle and limb bones except the clavicle), hyaline cartilage, cartilage models, primary ossification centers (appearing in the diaphysis by week 12), secondary ossification centers (appearing in epiphyses usually after birth), the epiphyseal plate (a layer of growing hyaline cartilage between diaphysis and epiphysis that drives bone lengthening), and epiphyseal plate fusion (occurring when bones reach adult size). The subsection details femur ossification specifically: primary center appears at week 7; secondary centers develop in the distal end (shortly before birth), femoral head (late first year), greater trochanter (year 4), and lesser trochanter (ages 9–10); fusion occurs in reverse sequence with lesser trochanter fusing first (age 11), greater trochanter (age 12), femoral head (ages 14–17), and distal condyles last (ages 16–19). The clavicle is distinguished as the exception, developing via intramembranous ossification (mesenchymal cells differentiating directly into bone-producing cells without a cartilage model), beginning ossification at week 5 but not completing until age 25. The subsection notes that epiphyseal plates appear dark on radiographs due to lower cartilage density and may be mistaken for fractures, making knowledge of plate disappearance timing clinically important for pediatric radiograph interpretation.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (135, 47, 'Hip Bone', 'Hip Bone', 'The hip bone (coxal bone) forms the pelvic girdle and consists of three fused components—the ilium, ischium, and pubis—each defining distinct regions of the adult hip bone. Key iliac landmarks include the iliac crest, anterior superior iliac spine, anterior inferior iliac spine, posterior superior iliac spine, posterior inferior iliac spine, iliac fossa, arcuate line of the ilium, auricular surface of the ilium, and greater sciatic notch; the ilium is firmly united to the sacrum at the sacroiliac joint. The ischium forms the posteroinferior region and includes the ischial tuberosity (attachment for posterior thigh muscles and weight-bearing during sitting), ischial ramus, lesser sciatic notch, and ischial spine. The pubis forms the anterior portion and includes the pubic body, pubic tubercle, superior pubic ramus, pectineal line of the pubis, and inferior pubic ramus; the pubic body joins the opposite hip bone at the pubic symphysis, and the inferior pubic ramus joins the ischial ramus to form the ischiopubic ramus, creating the subpubic angle. The ilium, pubis, and ischium converge to form the acetabulum (hip joint cavity), and the obturator foramen lies between the ischium and pubis. Ligaments supporting the pelvis include the anterior sacroiliac ligament, posterior sacroiliac ligament, sacrospinous ligament (sacrum to ischial spine), and sacrotuberous ligament (sacrum to ischial tuberosity); these ligaments help define the greater sciatic foramen and lesser sciatic foramen. The pelvic brim (pelvic inlet) is defined by the pubic symphysis, pectineal line of the pubis,', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (136, 48, 'Balance in Competing Autonomic Reflex Arcs', 'Balance in Competing Autonomic Reflex Arcs', 'This subsection explains how the sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions of the autonomic nervous system maintain homeostasis through competing inputs at target effectors via dual innervation. Postganglionic sympathetic fibers release norepinephrine (binding to adrenergic receptors), while postganglionic parasympathetic fibers release ACh (binding to muscarinic receptors); exceptions include postganglionic sympathetic fibers to sweat glands and blood vessels in skeletal muscle, which release ACh instead. The subsection illustrates this competition through two key examples: heart rate control, where norepinephrine causes depolarization and increased heart rate (120–140 bpm during exercise) while ACh causes hyperpolarization and decreased heart rate (60–80 bpm at rest), with an intrinsic rate of 100 bpm without parasympathetic input; and the pupillary light reflex, where low light triggers the sympathetic system (via upper thoracic spinal cord → superior cervical ganglion → norepinephrine on radial iris fibers) to dilate the pupil, while high light triggers the parasympathetic system (via Edinger–Westphal nucleus → oculomotor nerve → ciliary ganglion → ACh on circular iris fibers) to constrict the pupil. The subsection introduces autonomic tone—the tendency of an organ system toward sympathetic or parasympathetic dominance at rest (e.g., the heart exhibits parasympathetic tone)—and describes blood pressure regulation, where adrenergic receptors on blood vessel smooth muscle respond to norepinephrine and adrenal medulla hormones (epinephrine and norepinephrine) to increase pressure, while parasympathetic input is limited to erectile tissue vessels where ACh causes dilation.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (137, 48, 'The Structure of Reflexes', 'The Structure of Reflexes', 'This subsection contrasts somatic and visceral (autonomic) reflexes, establishing that somatic reflexes have a single-step efferent pathway from lower motor neurons in the ventral horn directly to skeletal muscle, while visceral reflexes employ a two-step efferent pathway: preganglionic fibers from lateral horn neurons (spinal cord) or cranial nucleus neurons (brainstem) project to ganglia, then postganglionic fibers project to target effectors. The afferent branch—sensory neurons with cell bodies in sensory ganglia (cranial nerves or dorsal root ganglia) projecting into the CNS—is often similar between both systems. Key visceral sensory structures include baroreceptors in the aorta and carotid sinuses that detect blood volume and pressure changes; baroreceptors from carotid arteries have axons in the glossopharyngeal nerve, while those from the aorta have axons in the vagus nerve. The subsection explains that visceral sensations typically remain unconscious but can reach conscious awareness if sufficiently strong, and describes referred pain—the phenomenon where visceral sensations are perceived in unexpected body locations (e.g., heart pain felt in the left shoulder and arm)—attributed to visceral and somatosensory fibers entering the spinal cord at the same level. Search terms: somatic reflex, visceral reflex, autonomic reflex, efferent branch, preganglionic fiber, postganglionic fiber, afferent branch, baroreceptor, glossopharyngeal nerve, vagus nerve, referred pain, mediastinal region.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (138, 49, 'Forebrain Structures', 'Forebrain Structures', '# Summary: Forebrain Structures in Autonomic Control

The hypothalamus serves as the control center for homeostatic mechanisms and autonomic balance by integrating visceral reflex information from multiple forebrain sources. The optic nerve projects to the hypothalamus to drive pupillary reflexes: high light levels trigger parasympathetic responses, while low light levels activate sympathetic responses. The hypothalamus transmits autonomic output via two main tracts—the dorsal longitudinal fasciculus and the medial forebrain bundle—which connect to the Edinger–Westphal nucleus of the oculomotor complex and the lateral horns of the thoracic spinal cord, thereby influencing parasympathetic nuclei in the brain stem and preganglionic neurons of the thoracolumbar spinal cord. The olfactory cortex, septal nuclei of the basal forebrain, and amygdala project into the hypothalamus through the medial forebrain bundle to inform autonomic regulation. The amygdala (nuclei in the medial temporal lobe, part of the limbic lobe) sends signals to the hypothalamus via the medial forebrain bundle that stimulate the sympathetic fight-or-flight response during anxiety or fear, and the hypothalamus responds by stimulating stress hormone release through endocrine system control.

**Search terms:** hypothalamus, forebrain structures, autonomic control, visceral reflexes, optic nerve, pupillary reflexes, dorsal longitudinal fasciculus, medial forebrain bundle, Edinger–Westphal nucleus, oculomotor complex, thoracic spinal cord, thoracolumbar spinal cord, olfactory cortex, septal nuclei, basal forebrain, amygdala, limbic lobe, temporal lobe, sympathetic response, parasympathetic response', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (139, 49, 'The Medulla', 'The Medulla', 'The medulla contains the cardiovascular center, which controls smooth and cardiac muscle through autonomic connections and coordinates responses to cardiovascular homeostatic shifts such as blood pressure changes. Key structures include the solitary nucleus, which receives sensory input about blood pressure and cardiac function from the glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves and outputs sympathetic stimulation through the upper thoracic lateral horn; the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus nerve, which provides parasympathetic motor output that decreases heart rate, relaxes bronchial tubes, and activates digestive function; and the nucleus ambiguus, which contributes parasympathetic output to the vagus nerve and innervates pharyngeal and laryngeal muscles for swallowing and speech while contributing to parasympathetic cardiac tone. Descending inputs from the hypothalamus stimulate sympathetic activity in response to anxiety or stress through the cardiac accelerator nerves (which increase heart rate) and vasomotor nerves (which constrict blood vessels). Search terms: cardiovascular center, solitary nucleus, dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus nerve, nucleus ambiguus, cardiac accelerator nerves, vasomotor nerves, upper thoracic lateral horn, glossopharyngeal nerve, vagus nerve, enteric nervous system.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (140, 50, 'Chemical Signaling in the Autonomic Nervous System', 'Chemical Signaling in the Autonomic Nervous System', 'This subsection introduces chemical signaling in the autonomic nervous system through synaptic transmission of signaling molecules to receptor proteins. Key structures and concepts include: cholinergic synapses (releasing acetylcholine/ACh) and adrenergic synapses (releasing norepinephrine); cholinergic receptors (nicotinic receptors as ligand-gated cation channels; muscarinic receptors as G protein–coupled receptors); and adrenergic receptors (α-adrenergic receptors with subtypes α1 and α2, and β-adrenergic receptors with subtypes β1, β2, and β3, all G protein–coupled). The textbook establishes that all preganglionic fibers (sympathetic and parasympathetic) release ACh to nicotinic receptors on ganglionic neurons; postganglionic parasympathetic fibers release ACh to muscarinic receptors; and postganglionic sympathetic fibers release norepinephrine to α- or β-adrenergic receptors, except those projecting to sweat glands and blood vessels of skeletal muscles, which release ACh to muscarinic receptors. The subsection distinguishes neurotransmitters (released at synapses) from hormones (released into bloodstream), notes that the adrenal medulla releases epinephrine and norepinephrine as hormones, and describes postganglionic connections as neuroeffector junctions with varicosities rather than typical synaptic end bulbs. Search terms: acetylcholine, norepinephrine, epinephrine, adrenaline, nicotinic receptor, muscarinic receptor, α-adrenergic receptor, β-adrenergic receptor, preganglionic fibers, postganglionic fibers, ganglionic neurons, adrenal medulla,', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (141, 50, 'Parasympathetic Division of the Autonomic Nervous System', 'Parasympathetic Division of the Autonomic Nervous System', 'The parasympathetic division, also called the craniosacral system, originates from preganglionic neurons in brain stem nuclei and the lateral horn of the sacral spinal cord; preganglionic fibers from the cranial region travel in cranial nerves while those from the sacral region travel in spinal nerves. Key anatomical structures include the Edinger–Westphal nucleus (midbrain), salivatory nuclei (upper medulla), dorsal nucleus of the vagus nerve, and nucleus ambiguus, which project through cranial nerves III, VII, IX, and X to terminal ganglia located near or within target organs (intramural ganglia). The parasympathetic system is characterized by long preganglionic fibers and short postganglionic fibers; preganglionic fibers in cranial nerve III terminate in the ciliary ganglion to innervate iris smooth muscle for pupillary control, fibers in the facial nerve activate ganglia controlling the lacrimal gland, and fibers in the vagus nerve project to terminal ganglia innervating the heart, bronchi, esophagus, stomach, liver, pancreas, gallbladder, and small intestine. Postganglionic fibers from vagus-activated ganglia are incorporated into organ structures such as the mesenteric plexus and intramural ganglia. Search terms: parasympathetic division, craniosacral system, terminal ganglia, intramural ganglia, Edinger–Westphal nucleus, salivatory nuclei, ciliary ganglion, dorsal nucleus of the vagus nerve, nucleus ambiguus, mesenteric plexus.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (142, 50, 'Sympathetic Division of the Autonomic Nervous System', 'Sympathetic Division of the Autonomic Nervous System', 'The sympathetic division of the autonomic nervous system is the thoracolumbar system, originating from the thoracic and upper lumbar spinal cord, with central neurons in the lateral horn projecting through ventral spinal roots to ganglia adjacent to the vertebral column. Key anatomical structures include the sympathetic chain ganglia (23 per side: 3 cervical, 12 thoracic, 4 lumbar, 4 sacral), collateral ganglia (celiac ganglion, superior mesenteric ganglion, inferior mesenteric ganglion), and the adrenal medulla; preganglionic fibers are myelinated and short, while postganglionic fibers are unmyelinated and long. Preganglionic fibers project to chain ganglia via white rami communicantes and to postganglionic neurons via gray rami communicantes; some preganglionic fibers bypass chain ganglia and travel through splanchnic nerves (greater splanchnic nerve, lesser splanchnic nerve) to collateral ganglia, while others project directly to chromaffin cells in the adrenal medulla. The sympathetic system causes divergent, coordinated effects across multiple organ systems—increasing heart rate, breathing rate, and blood flow to skeletal muscle while decreasing digestive system activity and increasing sweat gland secretion—achieved through extensive branching where a single preganglionic neuron may innervate 10–20 target effectors. Search terms: sympathetic division, thoracolumbar system, sympathetic chain ganglia, collateral ganglia, prevertebral ganglia, preganglionic fiber, postganglionic fiber, white rami communicantes, gray rami communicantes, splanchnic nerve, adrenal medulla, chromaffin cells, fight-or-flight response.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (143, 51, 'Broad Autonomic Effects', 'Broad Autonomic Effects', 'This subsection examines nicotine''s broad effects on the autonomic nervous system and its cardiovascular consequences. Nicotine acts as an acetylcholine (ACh) analog, binding to nicotinic receptors (ligand-gated ion channels) on ganglionic neurons in both sympathetic and parasympathetic ganglia, causing depolarization of the ganglia equally in both divisions. For most target organs, nicotine''s simultaneous activation of competing sympathetic and parasympathetic inputs results in no net change, as opposing effects cancel out (exemplified in the digestive tract where sympathetic sphincter contraction and parasympathetic muscular contraction produce minimal net movement). However, nicotine is problematic in the cardiovascular system because parasympathetic regulation of blood pressure is minimal and only limited blood vessels receive parasympathetic input, so nicotine preferentially increases vascular tone sympathetically and raises blood pressure. Additionally, cardiac muscle is intrinsically active and generates its own action potentials; the autonomic system modulates heart rate (sympathetic speeds up, parasympathetic slows down) but does not initiate beating, and because these mechanisms are not mutually exclusive, conflicting signals from nicotine can affect cardiac rhythm. The subsection establishes that smoking is a risk factor for cardiovascular disease due to these unbalanced autonomic effects.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (144, 51, 'Parasympathetic Effects', 'Parasympathetic Effects', 'This subsection classifies parasympathetic drugs as muscarinic agonists or antagonists that modulate activity at postganglionic terminals where parasympathetic postganglionic fibers release ACh onto muscarinic receptors (M1–M5). Parasympathomimetic drugs (muscarinic agonists) such as pilocarpine enhance cholinergic effects and increase parasympathetic tone; pilocarpine treats glaucoma by constricting the pupil via iris smooth muscle and contracting the ciliary muscle to open corneal perforations, allowing aqueous humor drainage and reducing intraocular pressure. Anticholinergic drugs (muscarinic antagonists) such as atropine and scopolamine block muscarinic receptors, inhibit parasympathetic function, and increase sympathetic tone; these compounds derive from the Atropa genus (belladonna/deadly nightshade) and systemic suppression of parasympathetic function can be fatal, with pilocarpine serving as the antidote for atropine or scopolamine poisoning. The subsection also notes that nicotinic agonists (nicotine) mimic ACh at preganglionic synapses and cause release of norepinephrine in the sympathetic system and ACh in the parasympathetic system, making the cardiovascular system susceptible to hypertension and arrhythmias. Search terms: parasympathomimetic drugs, anticholinergic drugs, muscarinic agonists, muscarinic antagonists, pilocarpine, atropine, scopolamine, belladonna, nicotinic agonists, sympathomimetic drugs, sympatholytic drugs, β-blockers, α-agonists.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (145, 51, 'Sympathetic Effect', 'Sympathetic Effect', 'This subsection covers drugs that modify sympathetic nervous system function through the adrenergic system, where norepinephrine and epinephrine bind to α-adrenergic or β-adrenergic receptors. Sympathomimetic drugs enhance adrenergic function by binding to adrenergic receptors to mimic norepinephrine, increasing norepinephrine production and release from postganglionic fibers, or blocking neurotransmitter reuptake at synapses in the sympathetic efferent pathway; examples include phenylephrine (an α1-adrenergic agonist used as a decongestant and mydriatic agent), pseudoephedrine, cocaine (which interferes with dopamine uptake), and caffeine (an adenosine receptor antagonist that indirectly increases adrenergic activity by blocking adenosine suppression of norepinephrine release). Sympatholytic (or sympathoplegic) drugs block norepinephrine or epinephrine binding to α-adrenergic or β-adrenergic receptors; β-blockers such as metoprolol (β1-specific) and propranolol (nonspecific) block β-receptors associated with vasoconstriction and cardioacceleration to treat cardiovascular disease, while α-blockers and clonidine (an α-agonist) are used as antianxiety medications and to treat hypertension, pain conditions, and attention deficit hyperactivity disorder. Key search terms include: sympathomimetic drug, sympatholytic drug, sympathoplegic drug, α-adrenergic receptor, β-adrenergic receptor, phenylephrine, pseudoephedrine, cocaine, caffeine, adenosine receptor, β-blocker, metoprolol, propranolol, α-blocker, clonid', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (146, 52, 'Blood Plasma', 'Blood Plasma', 'Blood plasma is approximately 92 percent water with dissolved and suspended substances, primarily proteins, comprising the remaining volume; serum is defined as plasma after clotting factors have been removed. The three major groups of plasma proteins are albumin (54% of total plasma protein, manufactured by the liver, serves as a binding protein for fatty acids and steroid hormones, and is the most significant contributor to osmotic pressure), globulins (38% of total plasma protein, subdivided into alpha, beta, and gamma globulins—where alpha and beta globulins transport iron, lipids, and fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K, while gamma globulins are antibodies/immunoglobulins produced by plasma cells), and fibrinogen (7% of total plasma protein, produced by the liver, essential for blood clotting). Beyond proteins, plasma contains electrolytes (sodium, potassium, calcium ions), dissolved gases (oxygen, carbon dioxide, nitrogen), organic nutrients (vitamins, lipids, glucose, amino acids), and metabolic wastes, which together constitute approximately 1 percent of plasma volume. Key search terms: plasma composition, water content, plasma proteins, albumin, globulins, fibrinogen, serum, osmotic pressure, blood clotting, electrolytes, dissolved gases.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (147, 52, 'Characteristics of Blood', 'Characteristics of Blood', 'This subsection describes the key physical and chemical characteristics of blood. The color of blood depends on hemoglobin oxygen saturation: bright red when oxygenated in the lungs and dusky red when deoxygenated in tissues. Blood viscosity is approximately five times greater than water and is influenced by plasma proteins and formed elements; viscosity impacts blood pressure and flow. Blood temperature averages 38 °C (slightly higher than body temperature of 37 °C) due to friction and resistance as it flows through blood vessels, especially as vessels age and lose elasticity. Blood pH averages 7.4 (range 7.35–7.45 in healthy individuals), making it slightly alkaline compared to pure water (pH 7.0); buffers in blood regulate pH. Blood constitutes approximately 8 percent of adult body weight, with males averaging 5–6 liters and females averaging 4–5 liters; hematocrit reflects erythrocyte percentage (approximately 41 percent in females and 47 percent in males), while mean plasma percentage is approximately 59 percent in females and 53 percent in males. Search terms: hemoglobin, oxygen saturation, viscosity, plasma proteins, formed elements, blood pressure, blood flow, blood temperature, blood pH, buffers, hematocrit, erythrocytes, WBCs, platelets.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (148, 52, 'Composition of Blood', 'Composition of Blood', 'Blood composition consists of formed elements and plasma, which can be separated and analyzed through clinical blood tests. The hematocrit test uses centrifugation to measure the percentage of erythrocytes (RBCs) in a blood sample; erythrocytes settle at the bottom due to their density, with normal values approximately 45% overall (37–47% for females, mean 41%; 42–52% for males, mean 47%), also referred to as packed cell volume (PCV). Above the erythrocytes lies the buffy coat, a pale layer comprising leukocytes (WBCs) and thrombocytes (platelets), which constitute less than 1% of the sample. Blood plasma, a pale straw-colored liquid, comprises the remainder of the sample, with mean plasma percentages of approximately 59% for females and 53% for males. Key searchable entities include: hematocrit, erythrocytes (RBCs), leukocytes (WBCs), thrombocytes (platelets), buffy coat, blood plasma, packed cell volume (PCV), centrifuge, and formed elements.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (149, 52, 'Functions of Blood', 'Functions of Blood', 'This subsection introduces the primary and secondary functions of blood, including oxygen and nutrient delivery, waste removal, defense, heat distribution, and homeostatic maintenance. Key anatomical structures and processes include: oxygen diffuses into blood from the lungs, moves to the heart, which pumps it to body cells; nutrients absorbed in the digestive tract travel via bloodstream to the liver for processing before delivery to body cells; hormones released by endocrine glands are carried by bloodstream to distant target cells; carbon dioxide is transported to the lungs for exhalation, and waste products are transported to the kidneys and liver for excretion as urine or bile. The subsection describes defense mechanisms in which WBCs protect against external threats (disease-causing bacteria) and internal threats (mutated DNA cells, virus-infected cells), and blood platelets and plasma proteins interact to block ruptured blood vessel areas during bleeding. Body temperature regulation occurs via negative-feedback loop, with blood transport from core to body periphery on warm days to dissipate heat through skin vessels, and diversion away from skin on cold days to maintain core warmth. Blood proteins and compounds act as buffers to regulate pH of body tissues and help regulate water content of body cells. Search terms: blood functions, oxygen delivery, nutrient delivery, waste removal, WBCs (white blood cells), hormones, bloodstream, plasma, blood platelets, heat dissipation, homeostasis, pH regulation, buffers.', 4);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (150, 53, 'ABO Transfusion Protocols', 'ABO Transfusion Protocols', '# Summary: ABO Transfusion Protocols

This subsection addresses safe transfusion practices and emergency blood administration protocols, focusing on blood type compatibility and universal donor/recipient designations. Key structures and concepts include: type O − blood (universal donor), type AB + blood (universal recipient), A and B antigens on erythrocyte surfaces, anti-A and anti-B antibodies in blood plasma, Rh antigen, and Rh antibodies. The textbook establishes that type O erythrocytes lack A and B antigens, preventing agglutination when transfused into recipients with anti-A or anti-B antibodies; however, type O blood plasma contains both anti-A and anti-B antibodies that may cause limited adverse effects in type A, B, or AB recipients due to dilution. Type AB + patients can receive any blood type because their erythrocytes display both A and B antigens and produce no anti-A or anti-B antibodies; Rh + patients can receive both Rh + and Rh − blood. Prior Rh antigen exposure in Rh − individuals may result in circulating Rh antibodies that trigger agglutination. In mass casualty situations where type O blood is unavailable, intravenous saline solution or artificial blood substitutes (hemoglobin- and perfluorocarbon-based oxygen carriers) may be administered to replace lost blood volume. RhoGAM administration since 1968 has reduced hemolytic disease of the newborn (HDN) incidence from 13–14 percent to 0.1 percent in the United States.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (151, 53, 'Antigens, Antibodies, and Transfusion Reactions', 'Antigens, Antibodies, and Transfusion Reactions', 'This subsection introduces antigens and antibodies as the molecular basis of transfusion reactions, defining antigens as non-self substances that trigger immune responses from leukocytes. Key structures and processes include: antigens (also called isoantigens or agglutinogens on RBCs; composed of proteins, carbohydrates, lipids, or nucleic acids), antibodies or immunoglobulins (Y-shaped proteins produced by plasma cells that bind to erythrocyte antigens), and agglutination (the clumping of erythrocytes caused by antibodies attaching to multiple RBC surfaces). The subsection explicitly states that agglutination causes erythrocyte clumps to block small blood vessels, depriving tissues of oxygen and nutrients, and that hemolysis (degradation of erythrocyte clumps) releases hemoglobin into the bloodstream, which travels to the kidneys for filtration; excessive hemoglobin can overwhelm kidney capacity and cause kidney failure. The subsection identifies the ABO blood group and Rh blood group as the two most clinically significant erythrocyte antigen classifications among more than 50 identified antigens. Search terms: antigens, antibodies, immunoglobulins, isoantigens, agglutinogens, isoantibodies, agglutinins, agglutination, hemolysis, plasma cells, B lymphocytes, ABO blood group, Rh blood group, transfusion reactions, erythrocytes.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (152, 53, 'Rh Blood Groups', 'Rh Blood Groups', 'The Rh blood group is classified by the presence or absence of the Rh D antigen on erythrocytes; approximately 85% of Americans are Rh positive (Rh+) and those lacking the antigen are Rh negative (Rh−). The Rh group is distinct from the ABO group and is designated by adding "positive" or "negative" to the ABO blood type (e.g., A+, AB−). Unlike preformed ABO antibodies, anti-Rh antibodies are produced only in Rh− individuals after exposure to the Rh antigen through transfusion or, more commonly, birth of an Rh+ baby to an Rh− parent—a process called sensitization. When an Rh− person previously sensitized becomes pregnant with an Rh+ baby, maternal anti-Rh antibodies cross the placenta and destroy fetal red blood cells, causing hemolytic disease of the newborn (HDN) or erythroblastosis fetalis, which can result in anemia, agglutination, hemolysis, and fetal death. RhoGAM (Rh immune globulin) prevents anti-Rh antibody development in Rh− pregnant people by destroying fetal Rh+ erythrocytes that cross the placental barrier; administered during weeks 26−28 of pregnancy and within 72 hours after birth, it has reduced HDN incidence from 13−14% to 0.1% in the United States since its introduction in 1968.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (153, 53, 'The ABO Blood Group', 'The ABO Blood Group', 'The ABO blood group system is defined by the presence or absence of two glycoprotein antigens—A and B—on erythrocyte membranes, determining blood types A, B, AB, or O. ABO blood types are genetically determined; type A individuals have A antigens and naturally-formed anti-B antibodies; type B individuals have B antigens and naturally-formed anti-A antibodies; type AB individuals have both A and B antigens but lack naturally-formed antibodies to either; type O individuals lack both A and B antigens but have both anti-A and anti-B antibodies circulating in blood plasma. Natural antibody formation occurs because A and B antigens are found in foods and microbes throughout nature, exposing the human immune system to these antigens at an early age. Anti-B antibodies in type A blood cause agglutination and hemolysis when encountering erythrocytes with B antigens; similarly, anti-A antibodies in type B blood cause agglutination and hemolysis when encountering erythrocytes with A antigens. The ABO blood group and Rh blood group are the most significant erythrocyte antigen groups in terms of potential patient harm, among more than 50 identified erythrocyte membrane antigens.', 4);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (154, 54, 'Disorders of Erythrocytes', 'Disorders of Erythrocytes', '# Summary: Disorders of Erythrocytes

This subsection covers disorders affecting erythrocyte size, shape, and number, including anemia and polycythemia. **Anemia** (deficient RBCs or hemoglobin) is classified into three major groups: blood loss anemias, anemias caused by faulty or decreased RBC production, and anemias caused by excessive RBC destruction; clinicians use the kinetic approach (evaluating RBC production, destruction, and removal) and morphological approach (examining RBC size via mean corpuscle volume [MCV], classifying cells as normocytic, microcytic, or macrocytic). Specific anemias discussed include: **sickle cell anemia** (genetic disorder producing hemoglobin S, causing erythrocytes to assume sickle shape at low oxygen concentrations, lodging in capillaries and blocking blood flow); **iron deficiency anemia** (insufficient iron for heme production); **megaloblastic anemia** (vitamin B12 and/or folate deficiency); **pernicious anemia** (poor vitamin B12 absorption); **aplastic anemia** (deficient RBC stem cells, triggered by radiation, medication, chemotherapy, or infection); and **thalassemia** (inherited condition with abnormal RBC maturation). Anemia causes reduced oxygen delivery to tissues, producing fatigue, lethargy, infection risk, impaired cognition, headaches, and dyspnea. **Polycythemia** (elevated RBC count, detected by elevated hematocrit) occurs transiently in dehydration and chronically at high altitudes; **polycythemia vera** (bone marrow disease causing excessive immature erythrocyte production) dangerously elevates blood viscosity and blood pressure.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (155, 54, 'Lifecycle of Erythrocytes', 'Lifecycle of Erythrocytes', 'This subsection covers erythrocyte production and degradation, detailing the essential nutrients and trace elements required for red blood cell synthesis and the fate of hemoglobin components after erythrocyte destruction. Key structures and processes include: erythrocyte production in the bone marrow at >2 million cells/second; iron absorption and storage in ferritin and hemosiderin; ferroportin transport of iron across intestinal cell plasma membranes; transferrin binding and transport of iron to erythrocyte precursors when EPO stimulates production; copper''s role as a component of hephaestin and ceruloplasmin, which enable iron oxidation from Fe²⁺ to Fe³⁺; zinc as a co-enzyme for heme synthesis; folate and vitamin B₁₂ as co-enzymes for DNA synthesis; and the 120-day erythrocyte lifespan followed by macrophage phagocytosis in bone marrow, liver, and spleen. After erythrocyte degradation, globin is broken into amino acids for recycling; iron is stored or transported by transferrin to red bone marrow; and the non-iron heme portion is degraded into biliverdin (green pigment) and bilirubin (yellow pigment), which binds to albumin, travels to the liver for bile manufacture, and is further converted to urobilinogen and stercobilin for fecal elimination, with urobilins removed by the kidneys. Search terms: erythrocytes, hemoglobin, iron, ferritin, hemosiderin, ferroportin, transferrin, copper, hephaestin, ceruloplasmin, zinc, folate, vitamin B₁₂, macrophage, biliverdin, bilirubin, albumin, bile, urobilinogen, stercobilin, urobilins,', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (156, 54, 'Shape and Structure of Erythrocytes', 'Shape and Structure of Erythrocytes', 'This subsection covers erythrocyte maturation, structure, and function in oxygen and carbon dioxide transport. Key anatomical structures include reticulocytes (immature erythrocytes containing ribosomal remnants), mature erythrocytes (biconcave disks lacking nucleus, mitochondria, and endoplasmic reticulum), spectrin (cytoskeletal protein enabling flexibility), hemoglobin (composed of four globin chains—alpha 1 and 2, beta 1 and 2—each bound to heme containing Fe2+ iron ions), oxyhemoglobin (bright red oxygenated form), deoxyhemoglobin/reduced hemoglobin (darker red deoxygenated form), and carbaminohemoglobin (CO2-bound hemoglobin). The textbook explicitly states that erythrocytes lack mitochondria and rely on anaerobic respiration, allowing them to deliver all transported oxygen to tissues; that the biconcave shape provides greater surface area-to-volume ratio for gas exchange; that spectrin enables erythrocytes to flex through narrow capillaries and form rouleaux in wider vessels; and that each hemoglobin molecule transports four oxygen molecules while approximately 76% of CO2 dissolves in plasma and 23–24% binds to hemoglobin as carbaminohemoglobin. The subsection also addresses clinical monitoring via pulse oximeter (measuring percent saturation at 95–100% normal range) and partial pressure of oxygen (PO2 in mm Hg), defines hypoxemia and hypoxia, and describes the negative-feedback loop in which interstitial fibroblasts in the kidney secrete EPO in response to hypoxia, thereby increasing erythrocyte production to restore oxygen saturation.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (157, 55, 'Disorders of Clotting', 'Disorders of Clotting', 'This subsection covers clotting disorders divided into two categories: coagulation failure and excessive clot formation. **Coagulation failure disorders** include thrombocytopenia (insufficient platelets causing inability to form clots) and hemophilia (inadequate production of clotting factors)—specifically hemophilia A (factor VIII deficiency; ~80% of cases) and hemophilia B (factor IX deficiency; ~20% of cases), both X-linked genetic disorders typically inherited from carrier females to male offspring, and the rare hemophilia C (factor XI nonfunctional; autosomal, not sex-linked). **Excessive clot formation disorders** include thrombocytosis (excessive platelets increasing thrombosis risk) and thrombophilia/hypercoagulation (tendency to form thrombi), which may be familial or acquired (lupus, heparin reactions, polycythemia vera, sickle cell disease, pregnancy, obesity). The subsection defines a **thrombus** as an aggregation of platelets, erythrocytes, and WBCs trapped in fibrin strands; distinguishes **mural thrombus** (adheres to vessel wall, decreases blood flow) from **occlusive thrombus** (blocks blood flow in small vessels); and explains that thrombi are caused by endothelial lining damage or venous stasis, potentially leading to deep vein thrombosis. When a thrombus fragment enters circulation, it becomes an **embolus**; when trapped, an **embolism**, which may cause heart attack, stroke, or pulmonary embolism. **Treatment options** include clotting factor infusions, aspirin (anticoagulant inhibiting platelet aggregation), and thrombolytic agents (including tissue plasminogen activator, which catalyzes plasminogen-to-plasmin conversion to degrade clots).', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (158, 55, 'Formation of the Platelet Plug', 'Formation of the Platelet Plug', 'This subsection describes hemostasis through platelet plug formation and the coagulation cascade. Key structures and substances include platelets, von Willebrand factor, collagen, and endothelial lining; platelet-released chemicals (adenosine diphosphate, serotonin, prostaglandins, phospholipids); and the 12 clotting factors (I–XIII, with VI as a placeholder). The textbook establishes that platelets bind to exposed collagen and endothelial lining assisted by von Willebrand factor; ADP helps additional platelets adhere; serotonin and prostaglandins maintain vasoconstriction; and the platelet plug temporarily seals vessel openings while coagulation proceeds. Coagulation involves three pathways—the extrinsic pathway (triggered by trauma, completed in seconds), the intrinsic pathway (triggered by internal vessel damage or foreign materials, completed in minutes), and the common pathway—all dependent on clotting factors secreted primarily by the liver and platelets, with vitamin K and calcium ion (factor IV) required. The extrinsic pathway involves factor III release from damaged cells, activation of factor VII by factor III, and activation of factor X; the intrinsic pathway involves factor XII activation by negatively charged molecules, sequential activation of factors XI and IX, and factor VIII combining with factor IX to activate factor X. In the common pathway, prothrombinase converts prothrombin (factor II) to thrombin, which converts fibrinogen (factor I) to fibrin; factor XIII stabilizes the fibrin clot. Fibrinolysis—the gradual degradation of the clot—involves conversion of plasminogen to plasmin and release of bradykinin, a vasodilator that reverses vasoconstriction and restores circulation.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (159, 56, 'Characteristics of Leukocytes', 'Characteristics of Leukocytes', 'Leukocytes are formed elements that originate from hematopoietic stem cells in the bone marrow and differ fundamentally from erythrocytes: they are larger, far less numerous (5000–10,000 per µL), possess a nucleus and organelles, exist in many types rather than one, and have shorter lifespans ranging from hours to minutes during acute infection. Unlike erythrocytes that circulate continuously within blood vessels, leukocytes routinely leave the bloodstream to perform defensive functions in body tissues by exiting capillaries and small vessels through emigration (diapedesis)—a process in which they squeeze through adjacent cells in the vessel wall. Once in tissues, leukocytes take up fixed positions in lymphatic tissue, bone marrow, the spleen, the thymus, or other organs, or move amoeba-like through tissue spaces, sometimes wandering freely and sometimes responding to positive chemotaxis, in which injured or infected cells and nearby leukocytes emit chemical signals that attract additional leukocytes to the site. When leukocytes arrive at their destination tissues, they are given distinct names such as macrophage or microglia depending on their function. Key search terms: leukocytes, erythrocytes, hematopoietic stem cells, emigration, diapedesis, capillaries, lymphatic tissue, spleen, thymus, bone marrow, positive chemotaxis, macrophage, microglia, differential counts.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (160, 56, 'Classification of Leukocytes', 'Classification of Leukocytes', 'This subsection classifies leukocytes into two major groups—granular leukocytes (neutrophils, eosinophils, basophils) and agranular leukocytes (monocytes, lymphocytes)—based on cytoplasmic granule visibility. Granular leukocytes are produced in red bone marrow, have lobed nuclei, and possess short lifespans of hours to days; neutrophils comprise 50–70% of total leukocyte count, contain lysozyme and defensins in granules, and are rapid responders to bacterial infection, while eosinophils (2–4% of count) contain antihistamine molecules and toxins targeting parasitic worms, and basophils (less than 1% of count) release histamines and heparin to intensify inflammation. Agranular leukocytes include lymphocytes (20–30% of count), which arise from lymphoid stem cells and develop in lymphatic tissues, comprising three major groups—natural killer cells, B cells (which produce antibodies via humoral immunity), and T cells (which provide cellular-level immunity)—plus monocytes (2–8% of count) originating from myeloid stem cells that mature into macrophages capable of phagocytosis and chemotactic signaling. Search terms: granular leukocytes, agranular leukocytes, neutrophils, eosinophils, basophils, monocytes, macrophages, lymphocytes, natural killer cells, B cells, T cells, lysozyme, defensins, histamines, heparin, antibodies, immunoglobulins, humoral immunity, cellular-level immunity, plasma cells, memory cells.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (161, 56, 'Disorders of Leukocytes', 'Disorders of Leukocytes', 'This subsection covers disorders of leukocytes and the structure and function of platelets. Key leukocyte disorders include leukopenia (too few leukocytes produced, impairing disease resistance), leukocytosis (excessive leukocyte proliferation with nonfunctional cells), leukemia (cancer involving myelocytic leukemia or lymphocytic leukemia; chronic leukemia involves accumulation of mature leukocytes that fail to die, while acute leukemia involves overproduction of immature leukocytes), and lymphoma (cancer in which malignant T and/or B lymphocytes collect in lymph nodes, spleen, liver, and other tissues). Platelets are cytoplasmic fragments derived from megakaryocytes (descended from myeloid stem cells), not cells themselves; thrombopoietin secreted by the kidneys and liver stimulates megakaryoblast proliferation and maturation into megakaryocytes, which release 2000–3000 platelets each through extensions into bone marrow capillaries. Platelets (2–4 µm diameter, 150,000–160,000 per µL blood) circulate for approximately 10 days, with one-third migrating to the spleen for storage; they are critical to hemostasis (stoppage of blood flow following vessel damage) and secrete growth factors for tissue repair. Search terms: leukopenia, leukocytosis, leukemia, myelocytic leukemia, lymphocytic leukemia, chronic leukemia, acute leukemia, lymphoma, T lymphocytes, B lymphocytes, platelets, thrombocytes, megakaryocytes, megakaryoblasts, myeloid stem cells, thrombopoietin, bone marrow capillaries, hemostasis, growth factors.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (162, 56, 'Disorders of Platelets', 'Disorders of Platelets', 'This subsection introduces two key platelet disorders: thrombocytosis, a condition of too many platelets that may trigger unwanted blood clots (thrombosis), and thrombocytopenia, an insufficient number of platelets that results in improper blood clotting and excessive bleeding. Students searching for this content should use the terms "thrombocytosis," "thrombocytopenia," "platelets," "blood clots," and "thrombosis." The subsection establishes that thrombocytosis causes formation of unwanted blood clots (a potentially fatal disorder) and that thrombocytopenia causes improper blood clotting and excessive bleeding. The material also includes interactive Webscope resources for examining blood smears and identifying formed elements, including individual leukocyte types, with guidance on systematic cell classification and comparison to normal blood smear patterns.', 4);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (163, 57, 'Differentiation of Formed Elements from Stem Cells', 'Differentiation of Formed Elements from Stem Cells', 'All formed elements of blood originate from hematopoietic stem cells (hemocytoblasts) located in red bone marrow, which undergo mitosis and cytokinesis in response to hemopoietic growth factors to produce daughter cells that either remain stem cells or differentiate into specialized cell types. Stem cells occupy a hierarchal system of decreasing diversification potential: totipotent stem cells (zygote) give rise to all body cells; pluripotent stem cells give rise to multiple cell types and fetal membranes; mesenchymal cells develop into connective tissue types (fibrous connective tissue, bone, cartilage, blood); and hematopoietic stem cells represent the lowest level in this hierarchy. Hematopoietic stem cells differentiate into two lineages: lymphoid stem cells, which give rise to lymphocytes (T cells, B cells, natural killer cells) that migrate to lymphatic tissues (lymph nodes, spleen, thymus) for maturation; and myeloid stem cells, which give rise to erythrocytes, megakaryocytes (producing platelets), monocytes, and granular leukocytes (neutrophils, eosinophils, basophils). Differentiation proceeds through intermediate precursor cells identified by the suffix -blast (megakaryoblasts, proerythroblasts), with proerythroblasts becoming reticulocytes before maturing into erythrocytes. Search terms: formed elements, hematopoietic stem cell, hemocytoblast, hemopoiesis, hemopoietic growth factors, lymphoid stem cells, myeloid stem cells, lymphocytes, T cells, B cells, natural killer cells, erythrocytes, megakaryocytes, platelets, monocytes, neutrophils, eosinophils, basophils, -blast precursor cells', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (164, 57, 'Hemopoietic Growth Factors', 'Hemopoietic Growth Factors', '# Summary: Hemopoietic Growth Factors

Hemopoiesis—the development of formed elements from stem cells through precursor cells (identified by the suffix -blast, such as megakaryoblasts and proerythroblasts) to mature cells—is initiated by hemopoietic growth factors. Erythropoietin (EPO), a glycoprotein hormone secreted by interstitial fibroblast cells of the kidneys in response to low oxygen levels, prompts the production of erythrocytes and is used medically to treat certain anemias and disorders requiring increased erythrocyte counts. Thrombopoietin, another glycoprotein hormone produced by the liver and kidneys, triggers the development of megakaryocytes into platelets. Cytokines are glycoproteins secreted by red bone marrow, leukocytes, macrophages, fibroblasts, and endothelial cells that act locally as autocrine or paracrine factors to stimulate proliferation of progenitor cells and resistance to disease; the two major subtypes are colony-stimulating factors (CSFs)—including granulocyte CSF, monocyte CSF, GM-CSF, and multi-CSF—and interleukins (IL-1, IL-2, IL-3, etc.), which are produced by bone marrow and endothelium and are suspected to play roles in cell differentiation, maturation, immunity, and inflammation. Search terms: erythropoietin, thrombopoietin, cytokines, colony-stimulating factors, interleukins, hemopoiesis, megakaryocytes, platelets, erythrocytes, leukocytes.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (165, 58, 'Heart Sounds', 'Heart Sounds', '# Heart Sounds Summary

This subsection introduces the diagnostic technique of auscultation using a stethoscope to assess cardiac function through audible heart sounds. The key structures and sounds are: S1 (first heart sound), produced by closing of the atrioventricular valves during ventricular contraction; S2 (second heart sound), produced by closing of the semilunar valves during ventricular diastole; S3 (third heart sound), rarely heard in healthy individuals but may indicate congestive heart failure if present in later life; and S4 (fourth heart sound), resulting from atrial contraction pushing blood into a stiff or hypertrophic ventricle and indicating left ventricular failure. The subsection defines murmurs as unusual heart sounds caused by turbulent blood flow, graded on a scale of 1 to 6, and describes how phonocardiograms or auscultograms record these sounds using specialized electronic stethoscopes. Clinical auscultation technique involves deep breathing: inhalation increases blood flow into the right side of the heart and amplifies right-sided heart murmurs, while expiration partially restricts blood flow into the left side of the heart and amplifies left-sided heart murmurs. Search terms: auscultation, stethoscope, S1, S2, S3, S4, atrioventricular valves, semilunar valves, ventricular contraction, ventricular diastole, murmur, phonocardiogram, auscultogram, congestive heart failure, left ventricular failure.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (166, 58, 'Phases of the Cardiac Cycle', 'Phases of the Cardiac Cycle', '# Summary: Phases of the Cardiac Cycle

This subsection describes the sequential phases of the cardiac cycle, including atrial systole, ventricular systole (isovolumic contraction and ventricular ejection phases), and ventricular diastole (isovolumic ventricular relaxation and late ventricular diastole phases). Key structures include the atria, ventricles, atrioventricular valves (tricuspid and mitral valves), semilunar valves (pulmonary and aortic valves), superior and inferior venae cavae, coronary sinus, pulmonary veins, pulmonary trunk, and aorta. The subsection establishes that during atrial systole (P wave on ECG), the atrial kick contributes 20–30% of ventricular filling to reach end diastolic volume (EDV) of approximately 130 mL; during ventricular systole (QRS complex, 270 ms total), isovolumic contraction closes the atrioventricular valves while pressure rises, followed by the ventricular ejection phase when semilunar valves open and stroke volume (70–80 mL) is pumped out, leaving end systolic volume (ESV) of 50–60 mL. During ventricular diastole (T wave, 430 ms total), isovolumic ventricular relaxation closes the semilunar valves and produces the dicrotic notch as pressure falls, followed by late ventricular diastole when atrioventricular valves reopen and blood flows from the major veins into the relaxed atria and ventricles. The subsection explicitly relates ECG waves to cardiac events and valve opening/closing to pressure changes.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (167, 59, 'Conduction System of the Heart', 'Conduction System of the Heart', '# Summary: Conduction System of the Heart

This subsection introduces the cardiac conduction system components and their sequential electrical activity: the sinoatrial (SA) node, atrioventricular (AV) node, atrioventricular bundle (bundle of His), left and right bundle branches, and Purkinje fibers. The SA node, located in the superior and posterior walls of the right atrium near the superior vena cava orifice, functions as the pacemaker with the highest inherent depolarization rate and initiates sinus rhythm; the impulse spreads through internodal pathways (anterior, middle, posterior bands) and Bachmann''s bundle to reach the AV node in ~50 ms. The AV node, located in the inferior right atrium within the atrioventricular septum, delays impulse transmission (~100 ms) to allow atrial contraction before ventricular activation; the cardiac skeleton prevents direct impulse spread to ventricles except through the AV node. The atrioventricular bundle proceeds through the interventricular septum and divides into left and right bundle branches, which supply the left and right ventricles respectively; the right bundle branch portions in the moderator band supply right papillary muscles. Both bundle branches connect to Purkinje fibers at the apex, which conduct the impulse to ventricular myocardial contractile cells in ~75 ms, initiating contraction from apex toward base. Cardiac conductive cells lack stable resting potentials and exhibit spontaneous depolarization (prepotential) via slow Na⁺ influx followed by Ca²⁺ influx; contractile cells demonstrate stable resting potentials (~−80 to −90 mV) and exhibit rapid depolarization, prolonged plateau phase (due to slow Ca²⁺ channels), and repolarization with extended absolute (~200', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (168, 59, 'Structure of Cardiac Muscle', 'Structure of Cardiac Muscle', 'Cardiac muscle cells (cardiomyocytes) are shorter than skeletal muscle cells and contain striations formed by sarcomeres with A bands and I bands; their contractile elements are virtually identical to skeletal muscle. T tubules penetrate the sarcolemma to reach the cell interior and are located only at Z discs (unlike skeletal muscle where they occur at A-I band junctions), resulting in one-half as many T tubules in cardiac muscle; the sarcoplasmic reticulum stores few calcium ions, requiring most calcium to come from outside the cell and causing slower contraction onset. Cardiomyocytes typically have a single central nucleus, branch freely, and contain abundant mitochondria for energy production; they store myoglobin, lipids, and glycogen in the cytoplasm and undergo aerobic respiration metabolizing lipids and carbohydrates. Intercalated discs mark junctions between adjacent cardiomyocytes and consist of desmosomes, specialized linking proteoglycans, tight junctions, and gap junctions that allow ion passage and synchronize contraction; intercellular connective tissue also binds cells together to withstand contraction forces. Cardiac muscle cells undergo twitch-type contractions with long refractory periods followed by brief relaxation periods; the extended refractory period prevents tetany, which would be incompatible with life by preventing blood pumping.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (169, 60, 'Cardiovascular Centers', 'Cardiovascular Centers', '# Summary: Cardiovascular Centers and Nervous Control of Heart Rate

Nervous control of heart rate is centralized in the two paired cardiovascular centers of the medulla oblongata, which consist of cardioaccelerator regions and cardioinhibitory centers. The cardioaccelerator regions stimulate activity via sympathetic stimulation of the cardioaccelerator nerves and cardiac nerves (which travel through cervical ganglia and superior thoracic ganglia T1–T4 to the SA node, AV node, and ventricular myocardium), while cardioinhibitory centers decrease heart activity via parasympathetic stimulation through the vagus nerve (cranial nerve X). Both sympathetic and parasympathetic stimulations flow through the cardiac plexus near the base of the heart; sympathetic stimulation releases norepinephrine (NE) at beta-1 receptors, which shortens repolarization and increases heart rate, while parasympathetic stimulation releases acetylcholine (ACh) via the vagus nerve to the SA node, AV node, atria, and ventricles, opening potassium ion channels to slow spontaneous depolarization and decrease heart rate. Without nervous stimulation, the SA node establishes a sinus rhythm of approximately 100 bpm; normally vagal stimulation predominates to maintain resting rates below this, and any increases beyond 100 bpm require sympathetic stimulation. The subsection also addresses bradycardia (caused by inherent factors such as SA or AV node abnormalities, or external causes including metabolic disorders, thyroid pathologies, electrolyte imbalances, beta blocker overprescription, and prolonged bed rest) and tachycardia (correlated with fever, anemia, hypoxia, hyperthyroidism, catecholamine hypersecretion, and cardiomyopathies),', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (170, 60, 'Correlation Between Heart Rates and Cardiac Output', 'Correlation Between Heart Rates and Cardiac Output', 'This subsection examines the relationship between heart rate (HR), stroke volume (SV), and cardiac output (CO) during varying physiological conditions. Key structures and processes include: diastole, ventricular filling, ejection fraction (normally 55–70%, mean 58%), and the compensatory mechanisms between HR and SV. The textbook establishes that increased HR initially increases SV and CO; however, as HR rises, decreased diastolic filling time reduces ventricular filling and subsequently SV. In healthy young individuals, CO rises from resting to ~120 bpm, remains stable from 120–160 bpm (as increasing HR offsets decreasing SV), and decreases above 160 bpm when SV declines faster than HR increases. During exercise, the rate of blood returning to the heart increases, initially maintaining high SV despite reduced filling time. The subsection identifies the target heart rate range of 120–160 bpm as optimal for aerobic exercise, where CO is maintained and both heart and lungs receive maximum benefit.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (171, 60, 'Input to the Cardiovascular Center', 'Input to the Cardiovascular Center', 'The cardiovascular center receives input from visceral receptors—proprioreceptors (in muscles, joint capsules, and tendons), baroreceptors (in the aortic sinus, carotid bodies, venae cavae, pulmonary vessels, and right heart), and chemoreceptors (innervated by glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves)—via visceral sensory fibers in the vagus and sympathetic nerves through the cardiac plexus, plus stimuli from the limbic system. Proprioreceptor firing increases with physical activity, prompting the cardiac centers to suppress parasympathetic stimulation and increase sympathetic stimulation to increase blood flow; the baroreceptor reflex maintains cardiac homeostasis by decreasing sympathetic and increasing parasympathetic stimulation when baroreceptor firing increases (indicating elevated pressure and stretch), and reversing this response when firing decreases. The atrial reflex (Bainbridge reflex) occurs when increased venous return stretches atrial walls and activates specialized baroreceptors, causing the cardiac center to increase sympathetic stimulation and inhibit parasympathetic stimulation to increase heart rate. Chemoreceptors detect metabolic byproducts (carbon dioxide, hydrogen ions, lactic acid) and falling oxygen levels, providing feedback to cardiovascular centers about blood flow needs. The limbic system impacts heart rate in response to emotional state and stress, with stress typically elevating heart rate and cortisol levels. Search terms: cardiac reflexes, baroreceptor reflex, atrial reflex, Bainbridge reflex, proprioreceptors, baroreceptors, chemoreceptors, cardiac plexus, cardiovascular center, sympathetic stimulation, parasympathetic stimulation, limbic system.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (172, 60, 'Resting Cardiac Output', 'Resting Cardiac Output', 'Resting cardiac output (CO) is calculated as the product of stroke volume (SV) and heart rate (HR) using the equation CO = HR × SV, where SV represents blood pumped per ventricle and is measured via echocardiogram by calculating the difference between end-diastolic volume (EDV) and end-systolic volume (ESV): SV = EDV – ESV. Mean resting SV is approximately 70 mL (normal range 55–100 mL) and mean resting HR is approximately 75 bpm (range 60–100 bpm), yielding a mean CO of 5.25 L/min (range 4.0–8.0 L/min) per ventricle. Variables influencing CO include size of the heart, physical and mental condition, sex, contractility, duration of contraction, preload (EDV), and afterload (resistance). Ejection fraction, calculated by dividing SV by EDV and expressed as a percentage, represents the portion of blood ejected with each contraction and ranges from approximately 55–70 percent with a mean of 58 percent. Search terms: cardiac output, stroke volume, heart rate, end-diastolic volume, end-systolic volume, echocardiogram, ejection fraction, preload, afterload, contractility, resting cardiac output.', 4);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (173, 60, 'Stroke Volume', 'Stroke Volume', '# Stroke Volume Summary

Stroke volume (SV) is determined by the difference between end-diastolic volume (EDV) and end-systolic volume (ESV), and is regulated by three primary factors: preload, contractility, and afterload. Preload, synonymous with EDV, represents ventricular stretch prior to contraction; the Frank-Starling mechanism (Starling''s Law of the Heart) states that within physiological limits, the force of heart contraction is directly proportional to initial muscle fiber length, with optimal sarcomere length enabling maximal myosin-actin cross-bridge formation and increased SV. Contractility—the force of heart muscle contraction—is controlled by positive inotropic factors (sympathetic stimulation, epinephrine, norepinephrine, thyroid hormones, glucagon, dopamine, isoproterenol, digitalis, and hypercalcemia) and negative inotropic factors (parasympathetic stimulation, hypoxia, acidosis, hyperkalemia, beta blockers, and calcium channel blockers); sympathetic stimulation releases norepinephrine from cardiac nerves and triggers adrenal epinephrine/norepinephrine secretion, binding alpha and beta receptors to increase contractility and decrease ESV, while parasympathetic stimulation via the vagus nerve releases acetylcholine to decrease contractility and increase ESV. Afterload is the tension ventricles must develop to pump blood against vascular resistance; increased resistance or valve stenosis increases afterload. Baroreceptors (aorta, carotid arteries, venae cavae, atria) and chemoreceptors respond to changes in stretch and blood gases to modulate parasympathetic and sympathetic stimulation, thereby adjusting heart rate and stroke volume to maintain homeostasis.', 5);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (174, 61, 'Chambers and Circulation through the Heart', 'Chambers and Circulation through the Heart', '# Summary: Chambers and Circulation through the Heart

The human heart contains four chambers—the right atrium, left atrium, right ventricle, and left ventricle—where the atria function as receiving chambers and the ventricles serve as the primary pumping chambers. Blood circulates through two distinct but linked circuits: the pulmonary circuit transports blood to and from the lungs for gas exchange (picking up oxygen and delivering carbon dioxide), while the systemic circuit transports oxygenated blood to body tissues and returns deoxygenated blood to the heart. The right ventricle pumps deoxygenated blood into the pulmonary trunk, which bifurcates into the left and right pulmonary arteries leading to pulmonary capillaries where gas exchange occurs; the pulmonary trunk arteries are the only post-natal arteries carrying relatively deoxygenated blood. Highly oxygenated blood returns from the lungs via the pulmonary veins—the only post-natal veins carrying highly oxygenated blood—into the left atrium, which pumps blood into the left ventricle, which pumps oxygenated blood into the aorta and systemic circuit branches to systemic capillaries. Deoxygenated blood from systemic capillaries flows through venules into the superior vena cava and inferior vena cava, which return blood to the right atrium, completing the continuous circulation cycle.

**Search terms:** right atrium, left atrium, right ventricle, left ventricle, pulmonary circuit, systemic circuit, pulmonary trunk, left and right pulmonary arteries, pulmonary capillaries, pulmonary veins, aorta, systemic capillaries, superior vena cava, inferior vena cava, gas exchange, oxygen, carbon dioxide', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (175, 61, 'Coronary Circulation', 'Coronary Circulation', '# Coronary Circulation Summary

This subsection introduces the anatomy and physiology of coronary circulation, the dedicated blood supply to cardiac muscle cells (cardiomyocytes) and heart structures. Key anatomical structures include: the left coronary artery (distributing to left atrium, left ventricle, and interventricular septum), the circumflex artery and anterior interventricular artery/left anterior descending artery (LAD) as branches of the left coronary artery, the right coronary artery (distributing to right atrium, portions of both ventricles, and heart conduction system), marginal arteries, and the posterior interventricular artery/posterior descending artery; coronary veins including the great cardiac vein, posterior cardiac vein, middle cardiac vein, small cardiac vein, anterior cardiac veins, and coronary sinus; and aortic sinuses (left posterior aortic sinus, anterior aortic sinus, right posterior aortic sinus) from which coronary arteries arise. The subsection explains that coronary circulation cycles with cardiac activity (peaks during relaxation, nearly ceases during contraction), that anastomoses form interconnections between vessel branches allowing collateral circulation, and that coronary artery blockage causes myocardial infarction (MI)—death of cardiac muscle cells resulting from ischemia and hypoxia. The text details MI pathophysiology (atherosclerotic plaque buildup or plaque embolism), clinical presentation (angina pectoris, dyspnea, palpitations, diaphoresis, syncope), diagnostic methods (ECG alterations in ST and Q components, echocardiography, cardiac magnetic resonance imaging, elevated creatine kinase MB and cardiac troponin), and treatments (supplemental oxygen, aspirin, nitroglycerin, thrombolytic agents, heparin, balloon an', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (176, 61, 'Internal Structure of the Heart', 'Internal Structure of the Heart', 'This subsection describes the internal partitioning structures of the heart and their functional significance. Key anatomical structures include the septa (walls dividing heart chambers): the interatrial septum, which contains the fossa ovalis (remnant of the fetal foramen ovale closed by the septum primum after birth); the interventricular septum, which is substantially thicker than the interatrial septum due to greater ventricular pressure; and the atrioventricular septum, which contains four openings for blood flow between atria and ventricles and into the pulmonary trunk and aorta. The subsection establishes that atrioventricular valves (including the bicuspid and tricuspid valves) are located in openings between atria and ventricles to ensure one-way blood flow, while semilunar valves are located at openings leading to the pulmonary trunk and aorta. The cardiac skeleton (skeleton of the heart)—composed of dense connective tissue forming four rings—reinforces the atrioventricular septum, serves as the attachment point for heart valves, and provides a boundary for the heart electrical conduction system. Search terms: septum, septa, interatrial septum, fossa ovalis, foramen ovale, septum primum, interventricular septum, atrioventricular septum, atrioventricular valves, bicuspid valve, tricuspid valve, semilunar valves, cardiac skeleton, skeleton of the heart.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (177, 61, 'Location of the Heart', 'Location of the Heart', 'The heart is located within the thoracic cavity in the mediastinum, medially between the lungs, and is enclosed by the pericardium (pericardial sac) within the pericardial cavity. The dorsal surface lies near vertebral bodies while the anterior surface sits deep to the sternum and costal cartilages; the base (superior surface) is located at the level of the third costal cartilage and has the superior and inferior venae cavae, aorta, and pulmonary trunk attached to it. The apex (inferior tip) lies just left of the sternum between the fourth and fifth ribs, with the right side deflected anteriorly and left side posteriorly; this leftward deviation creates the cardiac notch in the left lung''s inferior lobe. The heart''s position between the vertebrae and sternum enables cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR), performed by chest compression at T4–T9 at least 5 cm deep at 100 compressions per minute, which manually compresses blood into the pulmonary and systemic circuits. Search terms: mediastinum, pericardium, pericardial sac, pericardial cavity, heart base, heart apex, superior venae cava, inferior venae cava, aorta, pulmonary trunk, cardiac notch, cardiopulmonary resuscitation, CPR, chest compression.', 4);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (178, 61, 'Membranes, Surface Features, and Layers', 'Membranes, Surface Features, and Layers', 'This subsection introduces the pericardium (pericardial sac), the membrane surrounding the heart, which consists of two sublayers: the fibrous pericardium (outer layer of dense connective tissue that protects the heart and maintains its position in the thorax) and the serous pericardium (inner layer with two components—the parietal pericardium fused to the fibrous pericardium, and the visceral pericardium or epicardium fused to the heart wall). The pericardial cavity, located between the epicardium and pericardium, contains lubricating serous fluid. The epicardium is a macroscopic layer (unlike visceral serous membranes in most organs) composed of a simple squamous epithelium called mesothelium reinforced with loose, irregular, or areolar connective tissue; this mesothelium secretes the serous fluid that reduces friction during heart contraction. Key search terms: pericardium, pericardial sac, fibrous pericardium, serous pericardium, parietal pericardium, visceral pericardium, epicardium, pericardial cavity, mesothelium, serous fluid.', 5);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (179, 61, 'Shape and Size of the Heart', 'Shape and Size of the Heart', 'The subsection describes the gross anatomy of the heart, including its pinecone shape (broad superiorly, tapering to the apex) and typical dimensions (12 cm length, 8 cm width, 6 cm thickness; approximately fist-sized). Normal heart weight differs by sex: female hearts weigh approximately 250–300 grams, while male hearts weigh approximately 300–350 grams. The subsection explains that cardiac muscle undergoes hypertrophy in response to exercise, whereby protein myofilaments increase individual cell size without increasing cell number, enabling athletes'' hearts to pump blood more effectively at lower rates than nonathletes'' hearts. The subsection also distinguishes exercise-induced cardiac enlargement from pathological enlargement caused by hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, a condition of unknown etiology that can cause sudden death in young people. Key search terms: heart shape, heart size, heart weight, cardiac hypertrophy, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, athlete''s heart.', 6);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (180, 62, 'Cell Cycle Control', 'Cell Cycle Control', '# Cell Cycle Control Summary

This subsection introduces the regulatory system governing cell cycle progression through checkpoints, cyclins, and cyclin-dependent kinases (CDKs). Key structures and processes include: checkpoints (G1 checkpoint, G2 checkpoint, metaphase checkpoint), cyclins, cyclin-dependent kinases (CDKs), sister chromatids, microtubules, and the metaphase plate. The textbook establishes that cyclins and CDKs work together to determine progression past cell checkpoints by providing "stop" and "go" signals; the metaphase checkpoint ensures sister chromatids are properly attached to microtubules and lined up at the metaphase plate before anaphase separation occurs. Failures of cell cycle control—caused by inherited genetic abnormalities or combinations of genetic predisposition and environmental factors—lead to abnormal cell division and cancer, while immune system cells normally recognize and destroy cancerous cells; tumors are classified as benign if they do not damage surrounding tissues or malignant if they are capable of damage. Students would search for: cell cycle control, checkpoints, cyclin, cyclin-dependent kinase, G1 checkpoint, G2 checkpoint, metaphase checkpoint, sister chromatids, metaphase plate, cancer, benign tumor, malignant tumor.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (181, 62, 'The Cell Cycle', 'The Cell Cycle', '# Summary: The Cell Cycle

The cell cycle consists of two general phases—interphase (G1, S, and G2 phases) and mitosis followed by cytokinesis—with cells spending most time in interphase. During G1 phase, cells grow and perform metabolic functions; during S phase (synthesis phase), DNA replication occurs, doubling the amount of DNA and creating sister chromatids attached at the centromere; during G2 phase, cells prepare for mitosis; and G0 phase represents a resting state for cells that have temporarily or permanently stopped dividing. Mitosis is divided into four sequential stages—prophase (during which chromatin condenses into chromosomes, the nucleolus and nuclear envelope disintegrate, centrioles form centrosomes that extend microtubules to create the mitotic spindle, and kinetochores on centromeres attach to spindle microtubules), metaphase (sister chromatids align on the metaphase plate), anaphase (sister chromatids separate and are pulled to opposite cell ends by kinetochores as microtubules shorten), and telophase (two daughter nuclei form, chromosomes uncoil to chromatin, nucleoli reappear, and the mitotic spindle breaks apart)—followed by cytokinesis, during which a cleavage furrow composed of actin microfilaments divides the cytoplasm to form two new cells. Failure to undergo cytokinesis after mitosis results in multinucleated cells, which can indicate cancerous cells.

**Search terms:** cell cycle, interphase, G1 phase, S phase, G2 phase, G0 phase, mitosis, cytokinesis, sister chromatid, centromere, prophase, metaphase, anaphase, telophase, centrosome, centriole, mitotic spindle, kinetochore', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (182, 63, 'Stem Cells', 'Stem Cells', 'This subsection introduces stem cells as unspecialized cells capable of unlimited division and differentiation into specialized cell types, categorized by differentiation potential: totipotent stem cells (arise from zygote division, can differentiate into any cell type), pluripotent stem cells (develop from totipotent cells, differentiate into any human tissue type but cannot support full organism development), multipotent stem cells (differentiate into different cell types within a given lineage, such as red blood cells or white blood cells), oligopotent stem cells (limited to a few cell types), and unipotent cells (fully specialized, reproduce only their own type). The subsection describes stem cells present at different life stages: embryonic stem cells, fetal stem cells, and adult stem cells, including epithelial stem cells (give rise to keratinocytes in epidermis), hematopoietic stem cells in bone marrow (give rise to red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets), endothelial stem cells (give rise to endothelial cells lining blood and lymph vessels), and mesenchymal stem cells (give rise to muscle cell types). Differentiation involves changes in cell size, shape, metabolic activity, and function, and occurs through unique genetic expression—the selective "reading" of relevant DNA portions controlled by transcription factors, proteins that bind to specific genes and promote or inhibit their transcription. Search terms: stem cell, totipotent, pluripotent, multipotent, oligopotent, unipotent, embryonic stem cell, fetal stem cell, adult stem cell, epithelial stem cell, hematopoietic stem cell, endothelial stem cell, mesenchymal stem cell, differentiation, genetic expression, transcription factor.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (183, 64, 'From DNA to RNA: Transcription', 'From DNA to RNA: Transcription', 'This subsection introduces transcription, the process by which DNA is transcribed into messenger RNA (mRNA), a single-stranded intermediate that carries genetic code from the nucleus to the cytoplasm for protein synthesis. Key structures and concepts include: mRNA (single-stranded nucleic acid containing uracil instead of thymine, with ribose sugar containing an additional oxygen atom compared to DNA); codons (three-base mRNA sequences that encode amino acids); the promoter (nucleotide sequence triggering transcription initiation); the coding strand (DNA template strand); RNA polymerase (enzyme that unwinds DNA and adds nucleotides to growing RNA); and the terminator sequence (nucleotide sequence causing RNA to separate from DNA and polymerase, ending transcription). Transcription occurs in three stages: initiation (promoter triggers start), elongation (RNA polymerase unwinds DNA segment and aligns complementary nucleotides), and termination (terminator sequence causes RNA to fold and separate). Post-transcriptional modification converts pre-mRNA to mature mRNA through splicing, a process in which a spliceosome removes introns (non-coding regions) and pastes together exons (remaining coding segments), allowing different protein variations to result from a single gene. The mature mRNA then travels from the nucleus to the cytoplasm.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (184, 64, 'From RNA to Protein: Translation', 'From RNA to Protein: Translation', 'Translation is the process of synthesizing a polypeptide (chain of amino acids) from mRNA codons, requiring two major components: the ribosome (substrate) and transfer RNA or tRNA (translator molecules). The ribosome, composed of ribosomal RNA (rRNA) and proteins, exists as small and large subunits in the cytoplasm; many ribosomes associate with rough ER to synthesize proteins destined for the Golgi apparatus. Transfer RNA (tRNA) ferries amino acids to the ribosome and contains an anticodon (three-base sequence) that recognizes and binds to complementary mRNA codons, attaching each amino acid to the growing polypeptide chain. Translation occurs in three stages—initiation (ribosomal subunits bind to mRNA), elongation (tRNA anticodons recognize mRNA codons, amino acids attach with enzymatic assistance and energy), and termination (stop codon signals release of completed protein)—with the mRNA strand shifting one codon over after each tRNA release. Multiple ribosomes can simultaneously translate a single mRNA strand, forming a polyribosome, which increases protein synthesis efficiency. Search terms: translation, polypeptide, ribosome, rRNA, tRNA, anticodon, codon, initiation, elongation, termination, polyribosome, rough ER, Golgi apparatus.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (185, 65, 'Membrane Proteins', 'Membrane Proteins', 'This subsection introduces the two major types of membrane proteins embedded in or associated with the phospholipid bilayer: integral proteins (including channel proteins and cell recognition proteins) and peripheral proteins. Key structures and concepts include channel proteins, which selectively allow ions to pass into or out of the cell; receptors, which are recognition proteins that bind specific ligands and induce chemical reactions within the cell; and glycoproteins, which have attached carbohydrates that extend into the extracellular matrix and form the glycocalyx—a fuzzy coating that aids in cell recognition and provides cellular identity. The subsection explicitly states that when a ligand (such as dopamine) binds to a receptor on nerve cells, a channel within the transmembrane protein opens to allow ions to flow into the cell, demonstrating that some integral proteins serve dual roles as both receptors and ion channels. Peripheral proteins are typically located on the inner or outer surface of the lipid bilayer or attached to integral proteins, and some act as digestive enzymes on intestinal cell surfaces. Search terms: integral proteins, peripheral proteins, channel proteins, cell recognition proteins, receptor, ligand, glycoprotein, glycocalyx, dopamine receptor, transmembrane protein, extracellular matrix.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (186, 65, 'Structure and Composition of the Cell Membrane', 'Structure and Composition of the Cell Membrane', 'The cell membrane is composed of a phospholipid bilayer with embedded proteins and cholesterol, where phospholipids are amphipathic molecules containing a polar, hydrophilic phosphate head and nonpolar, hydrophobic lipid tails (saturated and unsaturated fatty acids). The phospholipid heads face outward toward both the intracellular fluid (ICF) and extracellular fluid (ECF)—including interstitial fluid (IF)—while the hydrophobic lipid tails meet at the inner interface, excluding water from this region. Cholesterol contributes to membrane fluidity, and the membrane maintains a fluid state with lipids and proteins that are not rigidly fixed in place. Key search terms: cell membrane, phospholipid bilayer, amphipathic molecules, hydrophilic, hydrophobic, phosphate head, lipid tails, cholesterol, intracellular fluid, extracellular fluid, interstitial fluid, membrane proteins, membrane fluidity.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (187, 65, 'Transport across the Cell Membrane', 'Transport across the Cell Membrane', '# Summary: Transport across the Cell Membrane

This subsection introduces membrane transport mechanisms that regulate cellular concentrations of ions (Ca++, Na+, K+, Cl–), nutrients (glucose, amino acids, fatty acids), and wastes (CO2). The cell membrane''s selective permeability—enabled by its hydrophobic lipid bilayer interior—allows only small, nonpolar materials (lipids, O2, CO2, alcohol) to cross unaided; water-soluble substances require protein assistance. Transport occurs via two categories: passive transport (no ATP required) and active transport (ATP-dependent). Key concepts include concentration gradients, diffusion (particle movement from high to low concentration), and osmosis (water diffusion through semipermeable membranes). Simple diffusion moves O2 into cells and CO2 out via the lipid bilayer; facilitated diffusion uses carrier proteins (glucose transporter) and channels (sodium channels) to transport large, polar, or charged molecules (glucose, Na+, amino acids) down their concentration gradients without energy. Osmosis creates isotonic, hypertonic, and hypotonic conditions affecting cell shape and function. Filtration uses hydrostatic pressure gradients in the circulatory and kidney systems. Active transport uses ATP and membrane pumps to move substances against concentration gradients: the sodium-potassium pump (Na+/K+ ATPase) moves three Na+ out and two K+ in per ATP, maintaining electrical gradients in nerve cells; secondary active transport includes symporters (sodium-glucose symporter) and antiporters (sodium-hydrogen antiporter). Endocytosis (phagocytosis, pinocytosis, receptor-mediated endocytosis) internalizes materials into vesicles; exocytosis exports vesicle contents via membrane fusion. The cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator (CFTR) is a', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (188, 66, 'Organelles for Energy Production and Detoxification', 'Organelles for Energy Production and Detoxification', 'This subsection introduces mitochondria and peroxisomes as organelles for energy production and detoxification. Mitochondria are bean-shaped organelles with an outer lipid bilayer membrane and an inner lipid bilayer membrane folded into cristae; proteins, enzymes, and molecules on the inner membrane perform cellular respiration, converting energy from nutrient molecules (such as glucose) into adenosine triphosphate (ATP), which provides usable cellular energy. Oxygen is required during cellular respiration; the muscular system uses large amounts of ATP to sustain muscle contraction, and nerve cells require large quantities of ATP to run sodium-potassium pumps, with individual neurons containing over a thousand mitochondria. Peroxisomes are membrane-bound organelles containing enzymes that perform lipid metabolism and chemical detoxification; their enzymes transfer hydrogen atoms from various molecules to oxygen, producing hydrogen peroxide (H₂O₂), which peroxisomes then convert into water and oxygen, and they neutralize poisons such as alcohol and free radicals. Reactive oxygen species (ROS)—including peroxides, free radicals (hydroxyl radical OH, H₂O₂, superoxide)—are produced by normal cellular processes including mitochondrial ATP production and oxygen metabolism; free radicals contain unpaired electrons and can oxidize other molecules, causing cellular damage, and are implicated in cancer, coronary artery disease, Alzheimer''s disease, cardiovascular diseases, diabetes, Parkinson''s disease, arthritis, Huntington''s disease, and schizophrenia. Oxidative stress describes damage to cellular lipids, proteins, carbohydrates, and nucleic acids caused by ROS accumulation beyond cellular defense capacity; antioxidants (found in fruits and vegetables) work by being oxidized themselves to halt destructive reaction cascades initiated by free radicals.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (189, 66, 'Organelles of the Endomembrane System', 'Organelles of the Endomembrane System', 'The endomembrane system comprises three major organelles—the endoplasmic reticulum (ER), Golgi apparatus, and vesicles—that work together to produce, package, and export cellular products. The ER is a continuous system of channels composed of lipid bilayer material that is continuous with the nuclear membrane; rough ER (RER) contains embedded ribosomes (the sites of protein synthesis composed of ribosomal RNA subunits and mRNA) and synthesizes proteins destined for the cell membrane or export, while smooth ER (SER) lacks ribosomes and synthesizes lipids (phospholipids and steroid hormones), sequesters and regulates cellular Ca++, metabolizes carbohydrates, and performs detoxification. Proteins synthesized in rough ER undergo glycosylation and are transported via vesicles to the Golgi apparatus, which sorts, modifies, and repackages products into new vesicles for secretion or delivery to lysosomes. Lysosomes are organelles containing digestive enzymes that break down unneeded cellular components, foreign material, and damaged organelles; enzyme-containing vesicles from the Golgi form new lysosomes or fuse with existing ones. Autophagy is the process of cells digesting their own structures; autolysis occurs when lysosomes release digestive enzymes into the cytoplasm to kill the cell in a controlled manner called apoptosis, and phagocytic white blood cells use lysosomes to digest phagocytized bacteria.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (190, 66, 'The Cytoskeleton', 'The Cytoskeleton', 'The cytoskeleton is a network of three protein-based filaments—microtubules, microfilaments, and intermediate filaments—that provide structural support, enable cell motility, support cell reproduction, and facilitate intracellular transport. Microtubules, composed of tubulin subunits, maintain cell shape, resist compression, position organelles, and form cilia and flagella; cilia beat rhythmically to move materials in the respiratory airways and move egg cells in the fallopian tubes, while flagella (found only on sperm cells) enable cell locomotion; microtubules also guide genetic material separation during cell division via paths extending from centrioles. Microfilaments, the thinnest filaments composed of actin protein chains, work with myosin to produce muscle contraction and, during cell division, create a cleavage furrow that splits the cell into two daughter cells. Intermediate filaments, composed of keratin subunits wound together, are intermediate in thickness between microtubules and microfilaments; they resist tension forces, maintain cell shape and structure in concert with microtubules, anchor organelles together, and link cells to other cells through special cell-to-cell junctions. Search terms: cytoskeleton, microtubules, tubulin, centrioles, cilia, flagella, microfilaments, actin, myosin, muscle contraction, cleavage furrow, intermediate filaments, keratin, cell shape, cell division.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (191, 67, 'DNA Replication', 'DNA Replication', 'This subsection introduces DNA replication, the process of copying DNA before cell division, which is essential for organism growth, development, and health. Key structures include the DNA double helix composed of two antiparallel complementary strands with sugar-phosphate backbones and base pairs (adenine-thymine and cytosine-guanine), where base sequence determines the genetic code. The subsection describes two stages of DNA replication: Initiation, in which helicase untwists and separates the complementary strands, and Elongation, in which DNA polymerase synthesizes new complementary strands using the original strands as templates. DNA replication is semiconservative—each new DNA molecule contains one original strand and one newly synthesized strand—and proceeds simultaneously at multiple sites until the entire genome is replicated. The subsection emphasizes that DNA proofreading enzymes scan newly synthesized DNA for mistakes to ensure precise replication, and notes that cell types including nerve cells, skeletal muscle fibers, and cardiac muscle cells do not divide. Search terms: DNA replication, double helix, complementary strands, antiparallel, DNA bases (adenine, thymine, cytosine, guanine), genetic code, helicase, DNA polymerase, semiconservative replication, genome, DNA proofreading, nucleotides, initiation, elongation.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (192, 67, 'Organization of the Nucleus and Its DNA', 'Organization of the Nucleus and Its DNA', 'The nucleus is bounded by a nuclear envelope consisting of two lipid bilayers with nuclear pores that allow passage of proteins, RNA, and solutes between nucleus and cytoplasm; pore complexes regulate this passage. The nucleoplasm contains solutes including nucleic acid building blocks, and the nucleolus manufactures ribosomal RNA; newly synthesized ribosomal subunits exit through nuclear pores. DNA is organized within chromatin threads as nucleosomes, where DNA wraps around histone proteins in a beaded-necklace pattern; during cell division, chromatin condenses into chromosomes (the condensed form of chromatin) to safely transport DNA to daughter cells. Humans have approximately 22,000 genes distributed across 46 chromosomes. Key search terms: nuclear envelope, nuclear pores, pore complexes, nucleoplasm, nucleolus, chromatin, DNA, histone proteins, nucleosome, chromosome, genes.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (193, 68, 'Covalent Bonds', 'Covalent Bonds', 'This subsection introduces covalent bonds, contrasting them with ionic bonds and explaining how atoms share electron pairs to stabilize valence shells. Key structures and concepts include: single covalent bonds (one electron pair shared), double covalent bonds (two electron pairs shared), triple covalent bonds (three electron pairs shared), nonpolar molecules (electrically balanced covalent bonds with equal electron sharing), and polar covalent bonds (unequal electron sharing creating regions of opposite electrical charge). The subsection uses water as the primary example of a polar molecule, explaining that the oxygen nucleus (eight protons) attracts electrons more strongly than hydrogen nuclei (one proton each), causing electrons to migrate toward oxygen and creating partial charges (δ+ on hydrogen regions, δ− on oxygen region). The water molecule''s dipole structure—with positive charges at the hydrogen end and negative charge at the oxygen end—enables interactions between charged regions of polar molecules, forming hydrogen bonds, described as one of the most important bonds in human physiology. Search terms: covalent bonds, ionic bonds, electron sharing, valence shells, single covalent bond, double covalent bond, triple covalent bond, nonpolar molecules, polar molecules, polar covalent bonds, water molecule, partial charges, delta notation, dipole, hydrogen bond.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (194, 68, 'Hydrogen Bonds', 'Hydrogen Bonds', 'This subsection introduces hydrogen bonds, which form when a weakly positive hydrogen atom already bonded to one electronegative atom (such as oxygen in water) is attracted to another electronegative atom in a different molecule. The textbook explains that hydrogen bonds always involve hydrogen that is part of a polar molecule, with water molecules serving as the primary natural example—specifically, the weakly negative oxygen atom in one water molecule attracts the weakly positive hydrogen atoms of two other water molecules. The subsection also describes how water molecules interact with other substances: water strongly attracts charged molecules and ions (electrolytes) through dipole-ion bonds, as exemplified by table salt (Na+ and Cl–) dissolving in water, while water repels molecules with nonpolar covalent bonds such as fats, lipids, and oils. Key search terms: hydrogen bonds, water molecules, electronegative atoms, polar molecules, dipole-ion bonds, electrolytes, sodium (Na+), chloride (Cl–), nonpolar covalent bonds, fats, lipids, oils.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (195, 68, 'Ions and Ionic Bonds', 'Ions and Ionic Bonds', '# Summary: Ions and Ionic Bonds

This subsection introduces ions, cations, anions, and ionic bonds as fundamental chemical structures in human physiology. Key entities include: ion (an atom with electrical charge from electron donation or acceptance), cation (positively charged ion), anion (negatively charged ion), ionic bond (ongoing close association between oppositely charged ions), and specific biological examples: potassium (K/K+), fluorine (F/fluoride F–), magnesium (Mg/Mg2+), selenium (Se/Se2–), sodium (Na+), and chloride (Cl–). The textbook explicitly states that atoms achieve full valence shells by either gaining electrons (if shell is more than half-full) or donating electrons (if shell is less than half-full); potassium donates one electron to become K+ (cation); fluorine accepts one electron to become fluoride F– (anion); opposite charges of cations and anions exert mutual attraction forming ionic bonds; sodium donates an electron to chlorine, which accepts it to become Cl– (anion); and water breaks ionic bonds in salts to free ions. The subsection establishes that dissolved ions in biological fluids produce electrical charges and are called electrolytes, generating the electrical activity observed in electrocardiograms (EKG/ECG) and electroencephalograms (EEG).', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (196, 69, 'Characteristics of Chemical Reactions', 'Characteristics of Chemical Reactions', 'This subsection introduces the fundamental components and types of chemical reactions: reactants (substances entering a reaction) and products (substances produced), governed by the law of conservation of mass, which states that matter cannot be created or destroyed in a chemical reaction. Key reaction types include synthesis reactions (joining of separate components, requiring energy storage in bonds), decomposition reactions (breaking down larger compounds into constituent parts, releasing stored potential energy), anabolic reactions (energy-requiring), and exchange reactions (involving both bond formation and breaking, with absorption, storage, and release of chemical energy). The subsection explains that chemical equations use arrows to indicate reaction direction and that reversibility—indicated by double arrows—is theoretically possible for any chemical reaction, though in the human body many reactions proceed predictably in one direction as the "path of least resistance" because the alternate direction requires more energy. Students searching for this content would use: reactant, product, law of conservation of mass, chemical equation, synthesis reaction, decomposition reaction, anabolic reaction, exchange reaction, reversibility, double arrow, endergonic reaction, and exergonic reaction.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (197, 69, 'Factors Influencing the Rate of Chemical Reactions', 'Factors Influencing the Rate of Chemical Reactions', 'This subsection identifies key factors that influence the rate of chemical reactions: surface area of reactants, physical state (gases react faster than liquids or solids), molecular size (smaller molecules react faster), and element reactivity (highly reactive elements like hydrogen react faster than stable elements like helium). Temperature increases the kinetic energy of subatomic particles, causing atoms, ions, and molecules to move faster and collide more frequently, thereby accelerating reactions; however, extremely high heat damages body cells while normal body temperature alone is insufficient to sustain life. Concentration and pressure affect reaction rates by increasing the likelihood of random collisions between particles. Catalysts—substances that increase reaction rates without undergoing change—are essential because they lower activation energy (the threshold energy needed to break bonds in reactants); enzymes, the most important catalysts in the human body, are composed of protein or RNA and facilitate most chemical reactions including food breakdown and energy conversion. Search terms: surface area, reactants, gases, liquids, solids, molecular size, element reactivity, hydrogen, helium, temperature, kinetic energy, thermal energy, random collisions, concentration, pressure, catalyst, activation energy, enzyme, protein, ribonucleic acid (RNA), valence shell electrons.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (198, 69, 'The Role of Energy in Chemical Reactions', 'The Role of Energy in Chemical Reactions', 'This subsection introduces the fundamental role of energy in enabling chemical reactions, establishing that sufficient kinetic energy is required for matter to collide with enough precision and force to break old chemical bonds and form new ones. Key concepts include kinetic energy (energy of matter in motion), potential energy (energy of position or structural arrangement), and chemical energy (potential energy stored in chemical bonds between atoms and molecules). The textbook explains that in the human body, potential energy is stored in bonds between atoms and molecules, and that chemical energy is invested when bonds form and released when bonds break. Two major reaction types are defined: exergonic reactions release more energy than they absorb (exemplified by catabolism of foods in an energy bar, which releases energy as heat and fuel molecules), while endergonic reactions absorb more energy than they release and require energy input. The subsection emphasizes energy conservation—chemical energy is neither created nor destroyed but converted from one form to another, and in many cases, energy from exergonic reactions powers endergonic reactions. Search terms: kinetic energy, potential energy, chemical energy, chemical bonds, exergonic reactions, endergonic reactions, catabolism, energy conversion.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (199, 70, 'Atoms and Subatomic Particles', 'Atoms and Subatomic Particles', '# Summary: Atoms and Subatomic Particles

An atom is the smallest quantity of an element that retains the unique properties of that element, composed of three important subatomic particles: protons (positively charged, designated p+), neutrons (electrically neutral), and electrons (negatively charged, designated e–). The number of protons determines the element and defines the atomic number; the number of protons and neutrons combined determines the mass number; electrons orbit the nucleus in an electron cloud rather than fixed orbits, and in a neutral atom the number of electrons equals the number of protons, creating electrostatic attraction that provides structural stability. The periodic table of the elements organizes the 92 naturally occurring elements by atomic number and identifies the chemical symbol, atomic number, and mass number for each element; elements in the same column share the same number of valence electrons that participate in chemical reactions. An isotope is one of the different forms of an element distinguished by different numbers of neutrons (examples: 12C, 13C, 14C); heavy isotopes contain more neutrons than usual and tend to be unstable and radioactive. A radioactive isotope (radioisotope) is an isotope whose nucleus readily decays, emitting subatomic particles and electromagnetic energy, with different radioisotopes characterized by their half-life (the time for half a sample to decay); controlled medical uses of radioisotopes include radioembolization for liver tumors and positron emission tomography (PET) scanning to detect metabolically active tissues.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (200, 70, 'Elements and Compounds', 'Elements and Compounds', 'This subsection introduces elements as the 92 fundamental substances composing all matter, defined as pure substances that cannot be created or broken down by ordinary chemical means. Key anatomical and physiological entities include: elements (oxygen, carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen, calcium, sodium, iron), compounds (glucose), and atoms as the smallest unit of an element retaining its chemical properties. The textbook establishes that the human body derives all its elements from foods eaten and air breathed, and that calcium consumed in dairy products is absorbed by the digestive system and incorporated into bones. Atoms are composed of subatomic particles—protons (positively charged, located in nucleus), neutrons (uncharged, located in nucleus), and electrons (negatively charged, orbiting nucleus in electron shells)—with the nucleus defined as the dense central core containing protons and neutrons. The atomic number is determined by the number of protons and identifies the element; the mass number equals total protons and neutrons; and isotopes are atoms of the same element with different numbers of neutrons. Compounds are substances composed of two or more elements joined by chemical bonds, such as glucose, which always contains the same elements (carbon, hydrogen, oxygen) in the same relative amounts (six carbon, six oxygen, twelve hydrogen units).', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (201, 70, 'The Behavior of Electrons', 'The Behavior of Electrons', 'This subsection introduces electron shells, valence shells, and the octet rule as fundamental concepts governing atomic behavior and chemical reactivity in the human body. Key structures include electron shells (layers of electrons at distinct energy levels around the nucleus), with the first shell holding maximum two electrons and all others holding maximum eight electrons; the valence shell (outermost electron shell) determines an atom''s chemical reactivity. The subsection establishes that a full valence shell makes an atom stable, while an incomplete valence shell makes an atom reactive. The octet rule states that atoms will give up, gain, or share electrons so that the valence shell contains exactly eight electrons (except hydrogen and helium, which stabilize with two). Specific examples demonstrate these principles: hydrogen''s single valence electron is drawn into relationships with other atoms; oxygen (six valence electrons) gains two electrons through covalent bonds with hydrogen atoms to form H₂O; carbon (four valence electrons) shares electrons with four hydrogen atoms to form methane (CH₄). Search terms: electron shells, valence shell, octet rule, covalent bonds, atomic stability, atomic reactivity, hydrogen, oxygen, carbon, neon, lithium, helium, H₂O, methane.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (202, 71, 'Acids and Bases', 'Acids and Bases', 'This subsection introduces acids, bases, and pH regulation as fundamental inorganic compounds affecting human physiology. Key structures and concepts include: acids (substances releasing hydrogen ions/H+), strong acids (ionizing completely, exemplified by hydrochloric acid/HCl from stomach cells), weak acids (incompletely ionizing, exemplified by acetic acid/vinegar), bases (substances releasing hydroxyl ions/OH– or accepting H+), and bicarbonate (HCO3–, a weak base that neutralizes stomach acid in the small intestine). The pH scale (0–14) measures hydrogen ion concentration logarithmically, with pH 7 as neutral; human blood maintains pH 7.35–7.45 through homeostatic mechanisms involving breathing, urine excretion, and buffers (solutions of weak acids and conjugate bases). Buffers neutralize excess acids or bases by binding hydrogen ions when pH drops below 7.35 or releasing them when pH rises above 7.45. Pathological conditions include acidosis (excessive blood acidity caused by reduced breathing effectiveness, bicarbonate loss, or ketone production in diabetes) and alkalosis (excessive alkalinity caused by low carbon dioxide levels or loss of hydrogen/chloride ions from vomiting). Search terms: acid, base, hydrogen ion, hydroxyl ion, pH, strong acid, weak acid, strong base, weak base, hydrochloric acid, acetic acid, bicarbonate, buffer, acidosis, alkalosis, diabetic ketoacidosis, respiratory acidosis, metabolic acidosis, respiratory alkalosis, metabolic alkalosis.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (203, 72, 'The Chemistry of Carbon', 'The Chemistry of Carbon', '# Summary: The Chemistry of Carbon and Organic Compounds

This subsection establishes carbon chemistry as the foundation for all organic compounds in human physiology. Carbon atoms complete their valence shells through covalent bonding rather than electron transfer, forming carbon skeletons and hydrocarbons with hydrogen and other elements. Five functional groups—hydroxyl, carboxyl, amino, methyl, and phosphate—are critical to organic compound structure and reactivity; hydroxyl groups participate in dehydration synthesis and hydrolysis, carboxyl groups appear in fatty acids and amino acids, amino groups are found in amino acids, methyl groups occur in amino acids, and phosphate groups are present in phospholipids and nucleotides. Monomers link via dehydration synthesis to form polymers, and polymers are cleaved by hydrolysis. The subsection then surveys four major organic compound classes: carbohydrates (monosaccharides, disaccharides, and polysaccharides including glucose, glycogen, and cellulose); lipids (triglycerides with saturated, monounsaturated, and polyunsaturated fatty acids; phospholipids with hydrophobic tails and hydrophilic heads; cholesterol; and prostaglandins); proteins (polymers of 20 amino acids linked by peptide bonds, with primary, secondary, tertiary, and quaternary structures, functioning as enzymes with active sites and substrate specificity); and nucleotides (DNA containing deoxyribose and thymine; RNA containing ribose and uracil; and ATP composed of ribose, adenine, and three phosphate groups that release energy upon hydrolysis).

**Search terms:** carbon skeleton, hydrocarbons, functional groups, hydroxyl group, carboxyl group, amino group, methyl group, phosphate group, dehydration synthesis, hydrolysis, macromolecule, monomer, polymer', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (204, 73, 'The Liver', 'The Liver', '# Summary: The Liver

The liver, the body''s largest gland, is located inferior to the diaphragm in the right upper quadrant and is divided into a large right lobe and smaller left lobe; it is connected to the abdominal wall and diaphragm by five peritoneal ligaments (falciform ligament, coronary ligament, two lateral ligaments, and ligamentum teres hepatis) and to the stomach by the lesser omentum. The hepatic artery delivers oxygenated blood to the liver while the hepatic portal vein delivers nutrient-rich blood from the small intestine; all blood from the alimentary canal passes through the liver via hepatic portal circulation before draining through the central vein, hepatic vein, and into the inferior vena cava. The liver''s three main histological components are hepatocytes (the primary cell type accounting for ~80% of liver volume), bile canaliculi (small ducts that accumulate bile between adjacent hepatocytes), and hepatic sinusoids (fenestrated capillary spaces formed from hepatic portal veins and hepatic arteries where hepatocytes process nutrients, toxins, and waste); the portal triad at the hepatic lobule perimeter consists of a bile duct, hepatic artery branch, and hepatic portal vein branch. Hepatocytes secrete approximately one liter of bile daily—a yellow-brown alkaline mixture containing water, bile salts, bile pigments, phospholipids (lecithin), electrolytes, cholesterol, and triglycerides—which flows through bile canaliculi, bile ductules, bile ducts, right and left hepatic ducts, and the common hepatic duct; the common hepatic duct joins the cystic duct from the gallbladder to form the common bile duct. Bile salts and phospholipids (poss', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (205, 73, 'The Pancreas', 'The Pancreas', 'The pancreas is a retroperitoneal gland with dual exocrine and endocrine functions, positioned transversely behind the stomach with its head nestled in the duodenum''s c-shaped curvature, body extending left approximately 15.2 cm, and tail ending in the spleen''s hilum. Key anatomical structures include acini (exocrine cell clusters) that secrete pancreatic juice into pancreatic ducts; the main pancreatic duct fuses with the common bile duct and enters the duodenum via the hepatopancreatic ampulla (controlled by its sphincter); and the accessory duct (duct of Santorini) enters the duodenum separately. The endocrine component consists of islets of Langerhans scattered among acini, producing insulin, glucagon, pancreatic polypeptide, and somatostatin. Pancreatic juice (>1 liter daily) contains water, salts, sodium bicarbonate (pH 7.1–8.2), and digestive enzymes (amylase, lipase, nuclease, trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, procarboxypeptidase); protein-digesting enzymes are secreted inactive and activated in the duodenum by enteropeptidase, while starch-, fat-, and nucleic acid-digesting enzymes are secreted active. Regulation involves hormonal control (secretin stimulates bicarbonate-rich juice release in response to acidic chyme; CCK stimulates enzyme-rich juice secretion in response to proteins and fats) and parasympathetic innervation (vagal stimulation during cephalic and gastric phases), with bicarbonate secretion maintaining venous blood pH homeostasis between pancreatic and gastric circulation.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (206, 74, 'Chemical Digestion', 'Chemical Digestion', '# Chemical Digestion and Absorption: A Closer Look

This subsection covers the enzymatic breakdown of large food molecules (proteins, lipids, nucleic acids, starches) into absorbable subunits via hydrolysis, and the subsequent absorption mechanisms in the small intestine. Key digestive enzymes include: salivary enzymes (lingual lipase, salivary amylase), gastric enzymes (gastric lipase, pepsin), brush border enzymes (α-dextrinase, sucrase, maltase, lactase, peptidases, nucleosidases, phosphatases), and pancreatic enzymes (pancreatic amylase, pancreatic lipase, trypsin, chymotrypsin, elastase, carboxypeptidase, nucleases). Carbohydrate digestion produces monosaccharides (glucose, galactose, fructose); protein digestion produces amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides; lipid digestion produces monoacylglycerides, glycerol, and free fatty acids; nucleic acid digestion produces pentose sugars, phosphates, and nitrogenous bases. Absorption occurs via five mechanisms: active transport, passive diffusion, facilitated diffusion, co-transport (secondary active transport), and endocytosis. Water-soluble nutrients (glucose, galactose, amino acids, nucleic acid products) are absorbed into capillary blood via the hepatic portal vein; lipid-soluble nutrients (long-chain fatty acids, monoacylglycerides) are packaged into chylomicrons and transported via lacteals and lymphatic vessels to systemic circulation through the thoracic duct. Specialized absorption includes: glucose and galactose via co-transport with sodium ions; fructose via facilitated diffusion; short-chain fatty acids via simple diffusion; iron via active', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (207, 75, 'Digestive Processes', 'Digestive Processes', '# Summary: Digestive Processes

This subsection introduces the six digestive processes—ingestion, propulsion, mechanical digestion, chemical digestion, absorption, and defecation—and their key anatomical structures and mechanisms. **Ingestion** occurs in the mouth, where food is chewed (mastication) and mixed with saliva containing enzymes that break down carbohydrates and lipids via lingual lipase, producing a bolus. **Propulsion** includes swallowing (voluntary) and peristalsis (involuntary sequential contractions and relaxation of alimentary wall smooth muscles), which propels food through the digestive tract and mixes it with digestive juices. **Mechanical digestion** (mastication, tongue movements, stomach churning, and segmentation) physically breaks food into smaller pieces and increases surface area without changing chemical composition; segmentation—localized contractions of circular muscle in the muscularis layer of the small intestine—moves contents back and forth to mix food with digestive juices and facilitate absorption. **Chemical digestion**, beginning in the mouth, uses digestive secretions (water, enzymes, acids, salts) to break complex molecules into building blocks (e.g., proteins into amino acids). **Absorption** occurs primarily in the small intestine, where nutrients pass through epithelial cells of the mucosa into the bloodstream; lipids are absorbed into lacteals and transported via lymphatic vessels to the subclavian veins. **Defecation** removes undigested materials as feces from the anus. The textbook notes that some organs control single processes (mouth for ingestion, anus for defecation), while most digestive processes involve multiple organs and occur gradually throughout the alimentary canal; some absorption occurs in the mouth and stomach (alcohol, aspirin).', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (208, 76, 'Blood Supply', 'Blood Supply', '# Blood Supply to the Digestive System

This subsection introduces the arterial and venous blood supply to the alimentary canal and explains how the cardiovascular system supports digestive function. Key structures include: the aortic arch, thoracic aorta, and abdominal aorta (which supply anterior and posterior alimentary canal regions); the celiac trunk (services liver, stomach, duodenum); superior and inferior mesenteric arteries (supply remaining small and large intestines); hepatic portal system (collects nutrient-rich blood from small intestine); and lacteals (lymphatic structures that absorb lipids). The textbook explicitly states that blood vessels serve two functions: transporting protein and carbohydrate nutrients absorbed by mucosal cells, and supplying organs with nutrients and oxygen; that the celiac trunk services the liver, stomach, and duodenum; that superior and inferior mesenteric arteries supply the remaining small and large intestines; and that veins from the small intestine empty into the hepatic portal system, which processes or stores nutrients in the liver before blood returns to the heart. Students would search for: alimentary canal blood supply, aortic arch, thoracic aorta, abdominal aorta, celiac trunk, superior mesenteric artery, inferior mesenteric artery, hepatic portal system, and lacteals.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (209, 76, 'Digestive System Organs', 'Digestive System Organs', 'The digestive system divides into two organ categories: the alimentary canal (also called the gastrointestinal tract or gut) and accessory digestive organs. The alimentary canal is a one-way tube approximately 7.62 meters in length during life, consisting of the mouth, pharynx, esophagus, stomach, small intestine, and large intestine, with the anus as the terminal opening; its main function is to nourish the body through absorption of nutrients. Within the mouth, the teeth and tongue perform mechanical digestion while the salivary glands perform chemical digestion. The accessory digestive organs—salivary glands, gallbladder, liver, and pancreas—sprout from the developing gut mucosa and maintain connections to the gut via ducts; they release secretions (bile and enzymes) essential for food breakdown and are critical to digestive system function. Nutrients enter the body''s "inner space" only through the process of absorption, as food and wastes within the alimentary canal are technically outside the body until absorbed.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (210, 76, 'Histology of the Alimentary Canal', 'Histology of the Alimentary Canal', 'The alimentary canal consists of four concentric tissue layers—mucosa, submucosa, muscularis, and serosa (continuous with mesentery)—whose structural details vary by region to support specific functions. The mucosa comprises epithelium (non-keratinized stratified squamous in mouth/pharynx/esophagus/anal canal; simple columnar in stomach/intestines), lamina propria (containing blood/lymphatic vessels and mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue including Peyer''s patches in the distal ileum), and muscularis mucosae (smooth muscle that creates undulating folds in stomach and small intestine). The submucosa is dense connective tissue connecting mucosa to muscularis, containing blood/lymphatic vessels, submucosal glands, and the submucosal plexus of nerves. The muscularis (muscularis externa) consists of inner circular and outer longitudinal smooth muscle layers in the small intestine—contractions promote mechanical digestion and propel food—but is skeletal muscle in the mouth, pharynx, anterior esophagus, and external anal sphincter (enabling voluntary swallowing and defecation); the stomach adds an oblique muscle layer, and the colon''s longitudinal layer forms three bands called tenia coli. The serosa (visceral peritoneum with loose connective tissue) is present only in the abdominal cavity, while the mouth, pharynx, and esophagus have adventitia (dense collagen sheath) instead, anchoring the canal near the vertebral column.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (211, 76, 'Nerve Supply', 'Nerve Supply', 'This subsection covers the nerve supply to the digestive system, including both intrinsic and extrinsic innervation of the alimentary canal. Key structures include the enteric nervous system (running from esophagus to anus with ~100 million neurons), the myenteric plexus (plexus of Auerbach) located in the muscularis layer responsible for motility and muscle contractions, and the submucosal plexus (plexus of Meissner) located in the submucosal layer responsible for regulating digestive secretions and food detection. Cranial nerves provide sensory and motor innervation to the mouth for taste and chewing. Extrinsic innervation is provided by the autonomic nervous system: sympathetic activation decreases GI secretion and motility by restricting enteric neuron activity, while parasympathetic activation increases GI secretion and motility by stimulating enteric neurons. Search terms: enteric nervous system, myenteric plexus, plexus of Auerbach, submucosal plexus, plexus of Meissner, cranial nerves, sympathetic nervous system, parasympathetic nervous system, alimentary canal innervation, GI motility, digestive secretions.', 4);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (212, 76, 'The Peritoneum', 'The Peritoneum', 'The peritoneum is a serous membranous sac composed of squamous epithelial tissue and connective tissue that holds digestive organs in place within the abdominal cavity. It consists of two regions: the parietal peritoneum, which lines the abdominal wall, and the visceral peritoneum, which envelopes the abdominal organs; the peritoneal cavity is the space bounded by these visceral and parietal peritoneal surfaces. The visceral peritoneum contains multiple large folds that envelope abdominal organs and hold them to the dorsal surface of the body wall; within these folds are blood vessels, lymphatic vessels, and nerves that innervate and supply the adjacent organs. A watery lubricating fluid in the peritoneal cavity minimizes friction between the serosal surfaces. During fetal development, certain digestive structures—the duodenum, pancreas, and portions of the large intestine (ascending colon, descending colon, and rectum)—remain completely or partially posterior to the peritoneum, a location described as retroperitoneal. Search terms: peritoneum, parietal peritoneum, visceral peritoneum, peritoneal cavity, peritoneal folds, retroperitoneal, duodenum, pancreas, ascending colon, descending colon, rectum.', 5);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (213, 77, 'The Esophagus', 'The Esophagus', 'The esophagus is a muscular tube approximately 25.4 cm in length that connects the pharynx to the stomach, located posterior to the trachea and running through the mediastinum of the thorax. Key anatomical structures include the upper esophageal sphincter (continuous with the inferior pharyngeal constrictor), the lower esophageal sphincter (also called gastroesophageal or cardiac sphincter), and the esophageal hiatus through which the esophagus penetrates the diaphragm. The esophageal wall consists of mucosa (non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium with basal and parabasal cells; lamina propria containing mucus-secreting glands), muscularis (skeletal muscle in upper third, mixed skeletal and smooth muscle in middle third, smooth muscle in lower third), and adventitia (loose connective tissue not covered by visceral peritoneum). Deglutition (swallowing) occurs in three phases controlled by the medulla oblongata: the voluntary phase (tongue moves bolus to oropharynx), the pharyngeal phase (receptors in oropharynx send impulses to deglutition center; uvula and soft palate close nasopharynx; laryngeal muscles constrict; pharyngeal constrictor muscles move bolus; upper esophageal sphincter relaxes), and the esophageal phase (peristalsis propels bolus via circular and longitudinal muscularis contractions; distention triggers lower esophageal sphincter relaxation; esophageal glands secrete lubricating mucus). Search terms: esophagus, upper esophageal sphincter, lower esophageal sphincter, gastroesophageal sphincter, cardiac sphincter, esophageal hiatus, peristalsis, bolus, de', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (214, 77, 'The Mouth', 'The Mouth', 'This subsection introduces the anatomical structures and functional organization of the mouth (oral cavity/buccal cavity), which is framed by the cheeks, tongue, and palate. Key structures include: the lips (labia) with the labial frenulum attaching to the gum; the orbicularis oris muscle regulating mouth opening/closing and the buccinator muscles in the cheeks; the oral vestibule (pocket framed by gums, teeth, cheeks, and lips); the fauces (opening between oral cavity and oropharynx); and the oral cavity proper (extending from gums and teeth to fauces). The palate, an arched roof structure, enables simultaneous digestion and respiration; it consists of the hard palate (anterior region formed by maxillary and palatine bones, serving as a septum between oral and nasal cavities) and the soft palate (posterior region composed of skeletal muscle). The uvula, a fleshy tissue extending from the soft palate''s posterior edge, moves upward during swallowing to prevent food and liquid from entering the nasal cavity. Two muscular folds—the palatoglossal arch (anterior, next to tongue base) and palatopharyngeal arch (posterior, forming superior and lateral fauces margins)—extend from the soft palate; between them lie the palatine tonsils (lymphoid tissue protecting the pharynx), with lingual tonsils located at the tongue base. Search terms: oral cavity, buccal cavity, lips, labia, labial frenulum, orbicularis oris muscle, buccinator muscles, oral vestibule, fauces, oral cavity proper, palate, hard palate, soft palate, uvula, palatoglossal arch, palatopharyngeal arch, palatine tonsils, lingual tonsils.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (215, 77, 'The Pharynx', 'The Pharynx', 'The pharynx (throat) is a skeletal muscle tube lined with mucous membrane that functions in both digestion and respiration, receiving food from the mouth and air from the mouth and nasal cavities. It comprises three subdivisions: the nasopharynx (breathing and speech only), the oropharynx (breathing and digestion), and the laryngopharynx (breathing and digestion), with the oropharynx continuous below with the laryngopharynx, the laryngopharynx''s inferior border connecting to the esophagus, and its anterior portion connecting to the larynx. The pharyngeal mucosa contains stratified squamous epithelium with mucus-producing glands. During swallowing, elevator skeletal muscles of the pharynx contract to raise and expand it to receive the bolus, then relax while constrictor muscles contract to force the bolus into the esophagus and initiate peristalsis; simultaneously, the soft palate and uvula rise to close the nasopharynx entrance, the larynx is pulled superiorly, and the epiglottis folds inferiorly to cover the glottis, blocking access to the trachea and bronchi. When food enters the trachea, coughing typically forces it back into the pharynx. Search terms: pharynx, nasopharynx, oropharynx, laryngopharynx, elevator muscles, constrictor muscles, epiglottis, glottis, soft palate, uvula, bolus, peristalsis, swallowing.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (216, 77, 'The Salivary Glands', 'The Salivary Glands', 'This subsection introduces the salivary glands and their secretory products, covering both minor salivary glands (labial glands, buccal glands, palatal glands, lingual glands) housed in oral mucosa and three pairs of major salivary glands located outside the oral cavity: the parotid glands (between skin and masseter muscle, near ears; secrete through parotid duct near second upper molar), submandibular glands (floor of mouth; secrete through submandibular ducts), and sublingual glands (below tongue; secrete through lesser sublingual ducts). Saliva composition is 98–99.5% water with 4.5% solutes including ions, glycoproteins, enzymes, and growth factors; key functional components include salivary amylase (initiates carbohydrate breakdown until inactivated by stomach acids), bicarbonate and phosphate ions (maintain pH 6.35–6.85), salivary mucus (lubricates food and facilitates bolus formation and swallowing), immunoglobulin A (prevents microbial epithelial penetration), lysozyme (antimicrobial), and epidermal growth factor. Each major salivary gland secretes a unique saliva formulation: parotid glands secrete watery saliva with salivary amylase; submandibular glands secrete amylase-containing saliva thickened with mucus; sublingual glands secrete the thickest saliva with the least salivary amylase content. Students should search for: salivary glands, parotid glands, submandibular glands, sublingual glands, salivary amylase, saliva composition, and saliva pH.', 4);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (217, 77, 'The Teeth', 'The Teeth', 'Teeth (dentes) are bone-like organs that mechanically break down food through tearing, grinding, and crushing; humans develop two dentitions: 20 deciduous teeth (appearing around 6 months) replaced by 32 permanent teeth (between ages 6–12), consisting of incisors, cuspids (canines), premolars (bicuspids), and molars, with wisdom teeth (third molars) erupting in early adulthood and sometimes remaining impacted. Teeth are secured in alveolar processes of the maxilla and mandible by the periodontal ligament, and surrounded by gingivae (gums). Each tooth has a crown (projecting above the gum line) and root (embedded in bone), both containing a pulp cavity with nerves and blood vessels; the root canal runs through the root, dentin surrounds the pulp cavity, cementum covers the dentin in the root, and enamel (the hardest body substance) covers the dentin in the crown. Enamel is susceptible to tooth decay and dental caries (cavities), which develop when bacteria feeding on sugars release acids that degrade enamel''s calcium crystals and cause soft tissue inflammation. Teeth function in digestion by shredding and crushing food into smaller particles for deglutition, working with lips and cheeks (confining food), salivary glands (secreting saliva and salivary amylase), tongue muscles (manipulating food into a bolus), and lingual glands (secreting lingual lipase).', 5);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (218, 77, 'The Tongue', 'The Tongue', 'The tongue is a muscular organ attached to the mandible, styloid processes of the temporal bones, and hyoid bone, and is positioned over the floor of the oral cavity. It facilitates ingestion, mechanical digestion, chemical digestion via lingual lipase, taste and texture sensation, swallowing, and vocalization. The tongue contains intrinsic muscles (longitudinalis inferior, longitudinalis superior, transversus linguae, verticalis linguae) that change tongue size and shape, and extrinsic muscles (palatoglossus, hyoglossus, styloglossus, genioglossus, mylohyoid) that position food for chewing, gather it into a bolus, and enable swallowing. The tongue surface features fungiform papillae (mushroom-shaped, containing taste buds) and filiform papillae (long and thin, with touch receptors for food manipulation), both extensions of the lamina propria covered in stratified squamous epithelium. Lingual glands in the lamina propria secrete mucus and serous fluid containing lingual lipase, which breaks down triglycerides after activation in the stomach. The lingual frenulum, a fold of mucous membrane on the tongue''s underside, tethers the tongue to the floor of the mouth; ankyloglossia (tongue tie) occurs when this structure is too short or malformed, potentially impairing speech.', 6);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (219, 78, 'The Large Intestine', 'The Large Intestine', 'The large intestine is the terminal portion of the alimentary canal subdivided into four main regions: the cecum, colon, rectum, and anus; it extends from the appendix to the anus and frames the small intestine on three sides. The ileocecal valve, located at the opening between the ileum and large intestine, controls chyme flow from the small intestine to the large intestine. The cecum, the first part of the large intestine, is a sac-like structure suspended inferior to the ileocecal valve that receives contents from the ileum and continues absorption of water and salts. The appendix (vermiform appendix) is a winding tube approximately 7.6 cm long that attaches to the cecum and contains lymphoid tissue; though generally considered vestigial, it may serve as a bacterial reservoir in diarrheal illness and its twisted anatomy provides a haven for enteric bacteria accumulation and multiplication. The mesoappendix, the mesentery of the appendix, tethers the appendix to the mesentery of the ileum. Primary functions of the large intestine include finishing nutrient and water absorption, synthesizing certain vitamins, forming feces, and eliminating feces from the body.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (220, 78, 'The Small Intestine', 'The Small Intestine', 'The small intestine is the primary digestive organ where most digestion and practically all absorption occur, subdivided into three regions—duodenum, jejunum, and ileum—that receive chyme from the stomach via the pyloric sphincter. Key anatomical structures include the hepatopancreatic ampulla (ampulla of Vater) where the bile duct and main pancreatic duct join, opening into the duodenum at the major duodenal papilla and regulated by the hepatopancreatic sphincter (sphincter of Oddi); the ileocecal sphincter connecting the ileum to the cecum; and the mesentery tethering the jejunum and ileum to the posterior abdominal wall. Three mucosal features—circular folds (plicae circulares), villi (containing capillary beds and lacteals), and microvilli (forming the brush border)—increase absorptive surface area over 600-fold, with intestinal glands (crypts of Lieberkühn) and duodenal glands (Brunner''s glands) secreting intestinal juice and bicarbonate-rich mucus respectively. Extrinsic innervation derives from parasympathetic vagus nerve fibers and sympathetic thoracic splanchnic nerve fibers; the superior mesenteric artery provides main arterial supply, with venous drainage to the superior mesenteric vein and hepatic portal vein to the liver. Mucosal cell types include absorptive cells, goblet cells, Paneth cells, G cells (secreting intestinal gastrin), I cells (secreting cholecystokinin), K cells (secreting glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide), M cells (secreting motilin), and S cells (secreting secretin); Peyer''s patches in the distal ileum provide immune function via MALT. Mot', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (221, 79, 'Digestive Functions of the Stomach', 'Digestive Functions of the Stomach', 'The stomach performs multiple digestive functions including mixing, chemical digestion, limited absorption, and storage, with the exception of ingestion and defecation. Key structures and processes include: mixing waves (a unique type of peristalsis occurring at ~20-second intervals), chyme (the mixture of food and gastric juices), the pylorus (which filters and permits only liquids and small particles through the pyloric sphincter), gastric emptying (rhythmic waves forcing ~3 mL of chyme into the duodenum), and the fundus (which stores undigested food and gases). Chemical digestion involves salivary amylase (inactivated by acidic chyme), lingual lipase (activated by acidity and breaks down triglycerides into free fatty acids and mono- and diglycerides), HCl, and pepsin (which initiate protein breakdown). Gastric emptying is regulated by both the stomach and duodenum; chyme in the duodenum activates receptors that inhibit gastric secretion. The stomach''s only function necessary to life is production of intrinsic factor, which is required for intestinal absorption of vitamin B12 (necessary for mature red blood cell production and normal neurological functioning). Gastric emptying timing varies by meal composition: carbohydrate-heavy foods empty fastest, followed by high-protein foods, while high-triglyceride meals remain longest (up to 6+ hours).', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (222, 79, 'Gastric Secretion', 'Gastric Secretion', 'Gastric secretion is controlled by neural and hormonal mechanisms organized into three phases: the cephalic phase (triggered by smell, taste, sight, or thought of food via conditioned reflex), the gastric phase (lasting 3–4 hours, initiated by food entry and involving stretch receptors, parasympathetic acetylcholine release, and gastrin secretion from enteroendocrine G cells), and the intestinal phase (regulated by the duodenum through intestinal gastrin and the enterogastric reflex). Key structures and processes include: stretch receptors activating parasympathetic neurons; gastrin stimulating parietal cells to produce HCl and promoting smooth muscle contractions; pepsinogen conversion to pepsin in acidic conditions; and negative feedback where low pH suspends HCl secretion and increases mucous secretion. The duodenum regulates gastric emptying via intestinal gastrin (excitatory) and the enterogastric reflex (inhibitory, closing the pyloric sphincter). Additional hormones produced by stomach mucosa include ghrelin (regulating food intake via the hypothalamus), histamine (stimulating parietal cell HCl release), serotonin (contracting stomach muscle), and somatostatin (restricting gastric secretions, motility, emptying, and pancreatic secretions). Search terms: cephalic phase, gastric phase, intestinal phase, gastrin, G cells, parietal cells, HCl, pepsinogen, pepsin, stretch receptors, acetylcholine, enterogastric reflex, pyloric sphincter, ghrelin, histamine, serotonin, somatostatin, enteroendocrine cells.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (223, 80, 'Neural and Endocrine Signaling', 'Neural and Endocrine Signaling', 'This subsection introduces neural and endocrine signaling as two distinct intercellular communication systems. The nervous system employs electrical signaling (action potentials) and chemical signaling via neurotransmitters (serotonin, norepinephrine), which diffuse across the synaptic cleft and bind to post-synaptic receptors, producing responses within milliseconds; the endocrine system uses only chemical signaling through hormones secreted by endocrine organs into extracellular fluid and transported via bloodstream to bind receptors on target cells, with response times ranging from seconds (adrenal hormones epinephrine and norepinephrine in fight-or-flight response) to 48 hours (reproductive hormones). Key entities and relationships include: neurotransmitters act locally and rapidly; action potentials trigger neurotransmitter diffusion across synaptic cleft; receptor binding transduces signals into cellular responses; adenylyl cyclase produces cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP) as secondary messenger during epinephrine activation of liver cells; oxytocin promotes uterine contractions, supports breastfeeding, and may influence sexual response and emotional attachment. The subsection emphasizes that endocrine signaling is typically less specific than neural signaling (same hormone produces varied effects depending on target cell type), that the nervous system enables quick responses to external environment changes while the endocrine system maintains internal homeostasis and controls reproduction, and that the two systems are connected—nervous system stimulation of adrenal glands enables rapid endocrine responses.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (224, 80, 'Other Types of Chemical Signaling', 'Other Types of Chemical Signaling', '# Summary: Other Types of Chemical Signaling

This subsection distinguishes three modes of chemical signaling: endocrine signaling, in which hormones secreted into extracellular fluid diffuse into blood or lymph and travel throughout the body; autocrine signaling, in which a chemical elicits a response in the same cell that secreted it; and paracrine signaling, in which a chemical induces a response in neighboring cells. Key entities include Interleukin-1 (IL-1), a signaling molecule important in inflammatory response that undergoes autocrine signaling when it binds to receptors on the surface of cells that secreted it; histamine, a paracrine released by immune cells in the bronchial tree that causes smooth muscle cells of the bronchi to constrict; and neurotransmitters, which act locally within the synaptic cleft. The textbook emphasizes that although paracrines may enter the bloodstream, their concentration is too low to elicit responses from distant tissues, distinguishing them from endocrine hormones. The subsection also introduces endocrinology as a medical specialty and endocrinologists as doctors who treat endocrine system disorders, thyroid disease, and diabetes mellitus through blood testing, hormone replacement, and other interventions.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (225, 80, 'Structures of the Endocrine System', 'Structures of the Endocrine System', 'The endocrine system comprises cells, tissues, and organs that secrete hormones directly into interstitial fluid and blood vessels for transport throughout the body; the major structural components are endocrine glands (ductless glands), including the pituitary, thyroid, parathyroid, adrenal, and pineal glands. Additional organs with endocrine function include the hypothalamus, thymus, heart, kidneys, stomach, small intestine, liver, skin, ovaries, testes, adipose tissue, and bone tissue. The pancreas exemplifies a dual-function organ: its exocrine cells secrete pancreatic juice through the pancreatic and accessory ducts to the small intestine lumen, while its endocrine cells secrete insulin and glucagon to regulate blood glucose levels. The endocrine system differs from the exocrine system, whose glands (such as sebaceous and sweat glands) release secretions through ducts. Search terms: endocrine gland, ductless gland, pituitary gland, thyroid gland, parathyroid gland, adrenal gland, pineal gland, pancreas, insulin, glucagon, hypothalamus, thymus, exocrine gland, exocrine system, interstitial fluid, blood vessels.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (226, 81, 'Factors Affecting Target Cell Response', 'Factors Affecting Target Cell Response', 'This subsection explains that target cell response to hormones depends on receptor presence and specificity, and is modulated by downregulation (decreased receptor number in response to high hormone levels) and upregulation (increased receptor number in response to chronically low hormone levels). The textbook identifies three primary types of hormone interactions affecting target cell response: the permissive effect, in which one hormone enables another to act (exemplified by thyroid hormones and reproductive hormones, where iodine deficiency impairs both); the synergistic effect, in which two hormones with similar effects produce amplified responses; and the antagonistic effect, in which two hormones have opposing effects (illustrated by insulin, which increases liver glucose storage and decreases blood glucose, and glucagon, which stimulates glycogen breakdown and increases blood glucose). Key search terms include: downregulation, upregulation, receptor sensitivity, permissive effect, synergistic effect, antagonistic effect, thyroid hormones, reproductive hormones, FSH, estrogens, female ova maturation, insulin, glucagon, calmodulin, angiotensin II, and growth hormone–releasing hormone (GHRH).', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (227, 81, 'Pathways of Hormone Action', 'Pathways of Hormone Action', 'This subsection introduces hormone receptors and the major pathways by which hormones trigger cellular responses. Key structures and processes include: hormone receptors (proteins located intracellularly or on the cell membrane), steroid hormones and thyroid hormones (which cross the cell membrane and bind intracellular receptors to trigger gene transcription), and water-soluble amino acid–derived hormones (which bind cell membrane receptors and initiate second messenger systems). The subsection details two primary second messenger pathways: the cAMP system, in which a water-soluble hormone binds a cell membrane receptor associated with a G protein, activating adenylyl cyclase to convert ATP to cAMP, which then activates protein kinases that initiate a phosphorylation cascade; and the phospholipase C (PLC) system, in which PLC cleaves a membrane phospholipid into diacylglycerol (DAG) and inositol triphosphate (IP3), with DAG activating protein kinases and IP3 causing calcium ion release from the smooth endoplasmic reticulum, allowing calcium ions to act as second messengers either directly or by binding calmodulin. Specific hormones mentioned include calcitonin, glucagon, thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH), growth hormone–inhibiting hormone (GHIH/somatostatin), angiotensin II, and growth hormone–releasing hormone (GHRH). The subsection emphasizes that phosphodiesterase (PDE) deactivates cAMP and that the target cell''s response depends on both the hormone and the target cell type.

**Search terms:** hormone receptor, steroid hormones, thyroid hormones, water-soluble hormones, amino acid–derived hormones, second messenger, cAMP, cyclic adenosine monophosphate, G protein, adenylyl cyclase, adenylate cyclase', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (228, 81, 'Regulation of Hormone Secretion', 'Regulation of Hormone Secretion', 'This subsection explains how hormone secretion is regulated through feedback loops and multiple stimulus types to maintain homeostasis and prevent disease. **Key structures and concepts:** positive feedback loops, negative feedback loops, oxytocin, uterine muscles, pituitary gland, glucocorticoid hormones, adrenal glands, hypothalamus, chemical and neural stimuli, humoral stimuli, osmoreceptors, blood osmolarity, ADH (antidiuretic hormone), kidneys, insulin, pancreas, glucagon, glycogen, releasing and inhibiting hormones, anterior pituitary hormones, sympathetic nervous system, norepinephrine, and epinephrine. **Key relationships:** oxytocin release signals uterine muscles to contract, cervical stretch signals the pituitary gland to release more oxytocin (positive feedback); rising glucocorticoid concentrations cause the hypothalamus and pituitary gland to reduce signaling to the adrenal glands (negative feedback); osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus detect blood osmolarity changes and signal the hypothalamus to release ADH, which causes the kidneys to reabsorb water and reduce urine volume; high blood glucose causes the pancreas to release insulin, which increases glucose uptake and liver storage as glycogen; the hypothalamus produces releasing and inhibiting hormones that control pituitary hormone secretion; sympathetic neurons signal the adrenal glands to secrete norepinephrine and epinephrine in response to perceived danger. **Search terms:** regulation of hormone secretion, feedback loops, positive feedback, negative feedback, humoral stimuli, neural stimuli, hormonal stimuli, blood osmolarity, ADH, insulin, glucagon, glucocorticoids.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (229, 81, 'Types of Hormones', 'Types of Hormones', '# Summary: Types of Hormones

Hormones are classified into two major chemical groups: amino acid–derived hormones (amine, peptide, and protein hormones) and lipid-derived hormones (steroids). Amine hormones are synthesized from single amino acids (tryptophan or tyrosine) with the carboxyl group removed; examples include melatonin (secreted by the pineal gland, regulates circadian rhythm), thyroid hormones (regulate metabolism), and catecholamines—epinephrine and norepinephrine (secreted by the adrenal medulla, involved in fight-or-flight response) and dopamine (secreted by the hypothalamus, inhibits anterior pituitary hormone release). Peptide hormones consist of short amino acid chains (e.g., antidiuretic hormone from the pituitary for fluid balance; atrial-natriuretic peptide from the heart to decrease blood pressure), while protein hormones are longer polypeptides synthesized via DNA transcription to mRNA to amino acid chain (e.g., growth hormone from the pituitary; follicle-stimulating hormone, a glycoprotein from the pituitary that stimulates egg maturation in ovaries and sperm maturation in testes). Steroid hormones derive from cholesterol and are hydrophobic; examples include testosterone and estrogens (produced by gonads), aldosterone (produced by adrenal glands, involved in osmoregulation), and cortisol (produced by adrenal glands, involved in metabolism). Because steroid hormones are not water-soluble, they must bind to transport proteins in blood, which extends their half-life (cortisol: 60–90 minutes) compared to amino acid–derived hormones (epinephrine: ~1 minute).

**Search terms:** amine hormones, peptide horm', 4);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (230, 82, 'Adipose Tissue', 'Adipose Tissue', 'This subsection covers organs with secondary endocrine functions, including adipose tissue, skin, thymus, liver, heart, gastrointestinal tract, kidneys, and skeleton. Key hormones and their functions include: leptin (adipose tissue; binds to brain neurons to produce satiety and regulate bone metabolism), adiponectin (adipose tissue; reduces insulin resistance and protects blood vessels), cholecalciferol/vitamin D (skin; produced when cholesterol in epidermis is exposed to ultraviolet radiation; converted in liver to intermediate form, then in kidneys to active calcitriol for calcium absorption and immune function), thymosins (thymus; contribute to T lymphocyte development and immune response), insulin-like growth factor-1/somatomedin (liver; stimulates bodily growth especially of bones), angiotensinogen (liver; precursor to angiotensin which increases blood pressure), thrombopoietin (liver; stimulates platelet production), and hepcidin (liver; blocks iron release to regulate iron homeostasis). Additional hormones include atrial natriuretic peptide from heart, gastrin/secretin/cholecystokinin/GIP/GLP-1 from gastrointestinal tract, renin/calcitriol/erythropoietin from kidneys, and FGF23/osteocalcin from skeleton. Search terms: leptin, adiponectin, cholecalciferol, vitamin D, calcitriol, thymosins, T lymphocytes, insulin-like growth factor-1, angiotensinogen, thrombopoietin, hepcidin, atrial natriuretic peptide, gastrin, secretin, cholecystokinin, GIP, GLP-1, renin, erythropoietin, FGF23, osteocalcin', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (231, 82, 'Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Gastrointestinal Tract', 'This subsection covers secondary endocrine functions of the gastrointestinal tract, kidneys, and bone. In the GI tract, endocrine cells in the mucosa of the stomach and small intestine secrete peptide hormones including gastrin (secreted in response to stomach distention; stimulates hydrochloric acid release), secretin (secreted by small intestine in response to acidic chyme; stimulates pancreatic bicarbonate release and inhibits stomach hydrochloric acid secretion), and cholecystokinin/CCK (released from small intestine; promotes pancreatic enzyme secretion and bile release from gallbladder). Intestinal hormones also aid glucose metabolism by stimulating pancreatic beta cells to secrete insulin, reducing glucagon secretion from alpha cells, and enhancing cellular insulin sensitivity. The kidneys produce renin (released in response to declined blood flow; triggers the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system and stimulates sodium and water reabsorption), calcitriol (produced from vitamin D3 in response to parathyroid hormone secretion; regulates blood calcium), and erythropoietin/EPO (produced in response to low oxygen levels; stimulates red blood cell production in bone marrow). Bone produces fibroblast growth factor 23/FGF23 (secreted by bone cells in response to increased blood vitamin D3 or phosphate; triggers kidneys to inhibit calcitriol formation and increase phosphorus excretion) and osteocalcin (produced by osteoblasts; stimulates pancreatic beta cells to increase insulin production and enhances peripheral tissue insulin sensitivity and glucose utilization). **Search terms:** gastrin, secretin, cholecystokinin, CCK, renin, renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, RAAS, calcitriol, vitamin D3, parathyroid', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (232, 83, 'Adrenal Cortex', 'Adrenal Cortex', 'The adrenal cortex contains three structurally distinct regions—zona glomerulosa, zona fasciculata, and zona reticularis—each producing different hormone classes. The zona glomerulosa produces mineralocorticoids, primarily aldosterone, which regulates sodium and potassium concentration and is released in response to elevated blood K+, low blood Na+, low blood pressure, or low blood volume; aldosterone increases K+ excretion and Na+ retention, thereby increasing blood volume and blood pressure, and is controlled by CRH from the hypothalamus triggering ACTH release from the anterior pituitary. Aldosterone is a key component of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS), in which kidney cells secrete renin in response to low blood volume or low blood pressure, renin catalyzes conversion of angiotensinogen (produced by the liver) to angiotensin I, and angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) in the lungs converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II, which initiates vasoconstriction, stimulates kidney tubule reabsorption of NaCl and water, and signals the adrenal cortex to secrete aldosterone. The zona fasciculata produces glucocorticoids—cortisol (the most important), cortisone (converted from cortisol by the liver), and corticosterone—which are released by ACTH in response to long-term stressors and promote catabolism of glycogen, triglycerides, and muscle proteins; cortisol also downregulates the immune system and inhibits the inflammatory response. The zona reticularis produces androgens in response to ACTH, which are converted in tissues to testosterone or estrogens and supplement gonadal androgens; in post-menopausal people, zona reticularis androgens become the main source of estrogens.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (233, 83, 'Adrenal Medulla', 'Adrenal Medulla', 'The adrenal medulla is composed of chromaffin cells, which are postganglionic sympathetic nervous system (SNS) neurons that produce the catecholamines epinephrine (adrenaline) and norepinephrine (noradrenaline) in a 4:1 ratio. The adrenal medulla releases these hormones in response to acute, short-term stress via the SAM pathway (sympathoadrenal medullary pathway), which originates from the hypothalamus. Epinephrine and norepinephrine signal the liver and skeletal muscle to convert glycogen into glucose, increase heart rate, pulse, and blood pressure, dilate airways, and cause vasodilation of blood vessels to vital organs (lungs, brain, heart, skeletal muscle) while triggering vasoconstriction to less essential organs (gastrointestinal tract, kidneys, skin). These hormones also downregulate immune system components and produce additional effects including pupil dilation, dry mouth, loss of appetite, and loss of peripheral vision. Search terms: adrenal medulla, chromaffin cells, epinephrine, norepinephrine, catecholamines, SAM pathway, sympathetic nervous system, fight-or-flight response, glycogenolysis, vasodilation, vasoconstriction.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (234, 84, 'Regulation of Blood Glucose Levels by Insulin and Glucagon', 'Regulation of Blood Glucose Levels by Insulin and Glucagon', 'This subsection introduces the endocrine pancreas''s role in blood glucose regulation through two primary hormones: insulin and glucagon. Pancreatic receptors sense blood glucose levels and trigger alpha cells to secrete glucagon during fasting or exercise, which increases blood glucose through glycogenolysis (liver glycogen conversion to glucose), gluconeogenesis (amino acid conversion to glucose), and lipolysis (triglyceride breakdown); glucagon secretion is inhibited by rising blood glucose via negative feedback. Beta cells secrete insulin in response to food intake and elevated blood glucose, with glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide serving as the initial trigger; insulin facilitates glucose uptake primarily in skeletal muscle and adipose cells (not in red blood cells, brain, liver, kidneys, or small intestine lining) by activating tyrosine kinase receptors and translocating glucose transporter vesicles to the cell membrane for facilitated diffusion. Insulin also reduces blood glucose by stimulating glycolysis, promoting liver glycogen storage, inhibiting glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis, and promoting triglyceride and protein synthesis; insulin secretion is regulated by negative feedback. The subsection also identifies four pancreatic hormones: insulin (beta cells, protein, reduces blood glucose), glucagon (alpha cells, protein, increases blood glucose), somatostatin (delta cells, protein, inhibits insulin and glucagon release), and pancreatic polypeptide (PP cells, protein, role in appetite).', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (235, 85, 'Anterior Pituitary', 'Anterior Pituitary', '# Summary: Anterior Pituitary

The anterior pituitary originates from the digestive tract and contains three regions—the pars distalis, pars intermedia, and pars tuberalis—and manufactures seven hormones: growth hormone (GH/somatotropin), thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH/thyrotropin), adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH/corticotropin), follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH), luteinizing hormone (LH), beta endorphin, and prolactin. Anterior pituitary hormone secretion is regulated by hypothalamic releasing hormones and inhibiting hormones that travel through the hypophyseal portal system (originating from the superior hypophyseal artery, branching from the carotid arteries, forming a primary capillary plexus and portal veins) to stimulate or inhibit hormone release. TSH, ACTH, FSH, and LH are classified as tropic hormones because they regulate the function of other endocrine glands. GH promotes protein synthesis and tissue building through direct mechanisms and indirectly by stimulating insulin-like growth factors (IGFs) from the liver and other tissues; GH levels are controlled by GHRH and GHIH (somatostatin) from the hypothalamus, and GH stimulates lipolysis (glucose-sparing effect) and glycogen breakdown (diabetogenic effect), with skeletal muscle and cartilage cells particularly sensitive to IGF stimulation. TSH is released in response to thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH) and regulates thyroid gland activity through negative feedback with thyroid hormones. ACTH derives from pro-opiomelanocortin (POMC) and stimulates the adrenal cortex to secrete corticosteroids such as cortisol, with release regulated by corticotrop', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (236, 85, 'Posterior Pituitary', 'Posterior Pituitary', 'The posterior pituitary is an extension of hypothalamic neurons from the paraventricular and supraoptic nuclei; their axons descend via the hypothalamic–hypophyseal tract within the infundibulum and terminate in the posterior pituitary, which stores and secretes—but does not produce—hormones. The paraventricular nuclei produce oxytocin and the supraoptic nuclei produce antidiuretic hormone (ADH); both hormones travel along axons to storage sites in axon terminals. Oxytocin stimulates uterine contractions and cervical dilation during childbirth through a positive feedback mechanism (cervical stretching stimulates additional oxytocin synthesis and release), and is necessary for the milk ejection reflex and parent–newborn bonding (attachment). ADH is released by osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus in response to high blood osmolarity; it targets tubular cells of the kidneys to increase epithelial permeability to water, promoting water reabsorption and reducing urine excretion, and is controlled by negative feedback as blood osmolarity decreases. ADH (also called vasopressin) causes blood vessel constriction at high concentrations, increasing blood pressure; alcohol inhibits ADH release causing increased urine production, while diabetes insipidus involves chronic ADH underproduction leading to chronic dehydration and potential electrolyte imbalances.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (237, 86, 'Functions of Thyroid Hormones', 'Functions of Thyroid Hormones', 'This subsection describes the functions of thyroid hormones T3 and T4, which are metabolic hormones that influence basal metabolic rate. T3 and T4 bind to intracellular receptors on the mitochondria, causing increased nutrient breakdown and oxygen use to produce ATP, and they initiate transcription of genes involved in glucose oxidation; this metabolic activity produces the calorigenic effect, which raises body temperature. Thyroid hormones are required for protein synthesis, fetal and childhood tissue development and growth, normal nervous system development in utero and early childhood, and continued neurological function in adults. T3 and T4 have a complex interrelationship with reproductive hormones, and deficiencies can influence libido, fertility, and reproductive function; additionally, thyroid hormones increase the body''s sensitivity to catecholamines (epinephrine and norepinephrine) from the adrenal medulla by upregulation of receptors in blood vessels, and excessive T3 and T4 levels accelerate heart rate, strengthen the heartbeat, and increase blood pressure. The subsection also introduces calcitonin, a hormone produced by parafollicular cells (C cells) in the thyroid tissue between follicles, which is released in response to a rise in blood calcium. Search terms: T3, T4, metabolic hormones, basal metabolic rate, intracellular receptors, mitochondria, calorigenic effect, protein synthesis, nervous system development, reproductive hormones, catecholamines, epinephrine, norepinephrine, adrenal medulla, calcitonin, parafollicular cells, C cells, blood calcium.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (238, 86, 'Synthesis and Release of Thyroid Hormones', 'Synthesis and Release of Thyroid Hormones', 'This subsection describes the synthesis and release of thyroid hormones, focusing on the key structures and processes involved in hormone production. TSH binding to receptors in follicle cells triggers active transport of iodide ions (I–) from the bloodstream into the cytosol, where they are concentrated at levels higher than in blood; these ions then move to the follicle lumen, undergo oxidation to form iodine (I₂), and pass into the colloid. In the colloid, peroxidase enzymes link iodine to tyrosine amino acids in thyroglobulin to form intermediaries that combine to produce triiodothyronine (T₃, containing three iodines) and tetraiodothyronine/thyroxine (T₄, containing four iodines). TSH stimulates endocytosis of colloid back into follicle cells, where lysosomal enzymes release free T₃ and T₄, which diffuse across the follicle cell membrane into the bloodstream; less than one percent circulates unbound while 99 percent binds to thyroxine-binding globulins (TBGs), albumin, or other plasma proteins, preventing free diffusion into body cells. When blood T₃ and T₄ levels decline, bound hormones are released from plasma proteins and cross target cell membranes; T₃ is more potent than T₄, and many cells convert T₄ to T₃ by removing an iodine atom. **Search terms:** TSH, follicle cells, iodide ions, thyroglobulin, colloid, peroxidase enzymes, tyrosine, triiodothyronine (T₃), tetraiodothyronine, thyroxine (T₄), endocytosis, lysosomal enzymes,', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (239, 87, 'Sweat Glands', 'Sweat Glands', 'Sweat glands (sudoriferous glands) are merocrine glands that develop from epidermal projections into the dermis and secrete sweat via exocytosis through ducts to cool the body during thermoregulation. The two types are eccrine sweat glands—found abundantly on palms, soles, and forehead, lying coiled deep in the dermis with ducts rising to skin surface pores, secreting hypotonic sweat composed of water, salt, antibodies, metabolic waste traces, and dermicidin—and apocrine sweat glands—associated with hair follicles in armpits and genital regions, lying deeper in the dermis or hypodermis with ducts emptying into hair follicles, secreting thicker sweat with organic compounds subject to bacterial decomposition. Sweat release is controlled by both nervous and hormonal regulation and plays a role in human pheromone response. Aluminum-based antiperspirants block sweat by precipitating in the sweat gland duct due to pH change, forming a physical obstruction at the pore. The composition of sweat determines whether body odor results from sweating, with apocrine sweat''s organic compounds being particularly subject to bacterial decomposition and odor production.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (240, 88, 'Skin Disorders', 'Skin Disorders', 'This subsection covers common skin disorders (eczema, acne) and injuries (burns, wounds, scars, bedsores, stretch marks, calluses). Eczema is an inflammatory allergic reaction manifesting as dry, itchy patches, swelling, and flaking, managed with moisturizers, corticosteroid creams, and immunosuppressants; acne occurs in sebaceous gland-rich areas where androgens stimulate sebum release, and overproduction of sebum and keratin blocks hair follicles, with acne-causing bacteria (Propionibacterium and Staphylococcus) causing infection and scarring. Skin injury healing involves blood clot formation, fibroblast collagen deposition forming granulation tissue, blood capillary proliferation, and macrophage immune response. Burns result from intense heat, radiation, electricity, or chemicals causing skin cell death, dehydration, and infection risk; classified as first-degree (epidermis only), second-degree (epidermis and partial dermis), third-degree (full epidermis and dermis with nerve damage), or fourth-degree (muscle and bone involvement), with full-thickness burns requiring debridement and skin grafting. Scarring occurs when fibroblasts produce collagen in a basket-weave pattern that fails to regenerate original skin structure or accessory structures; keloids result from collagen overproduction while atrophic scars appear sunken. Additional injuries include bedsores (decubitus ulcers) from unrelieved pressure causing necrosis, stretch marks from dermis stretching beyond elasticity limits, and calluses from basal stem cell division in response to constant abrasion.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (241, 89, 'The Epidermis', 'The Epidermis', 'The epidermis is composed of keratinized, stratified squamous epithelium organized into four or five layers depending on body location: thin skin (four layers—stratum basale, stratum spinosum, stratum granulosum, stratum corneum) found on most of the body, and thick skin (five layers with an additional stratum lucidum) found only on palms and soles. Keratinocytes in all layers except the stratum basale manufacture and store keratin, an intracellular fibrous protein that provides hardness and water resistance; dead keratinocytes in the stratum corneum are regularly shed and replaced over approximately 4 weeks. The stratum basale (also called stratum germinativum) is the deepest epidermal layer composed primarily of basal cells (cuboidal stem cells that produce all keratinocytes via mitosis) and contains two additional cell types: Merkel cells (receptors that stimulate sensory nerves for touch perception) and melanocytes (which produce melanin for skin color and UV protection); this layer attaches to the basal lamina via the basement membrane (intertwining collagen fibers) and connects to dermal papillae that increase epidermal-dermal adhesion strength. The stratum spinosum (8–10 keratinocyte layers) has a spiny appearance due to desmosomes joining cell processes; keratinocytes here begin keratin synthesis and release water-repelling glycolipid; Langerhans cells (dendritic macrophages) are interspersed throughout. The stratum granulosum (3–5 layers) appears grainy due to accumulation of keratin and keratohyalin (as lamellar granules) as cells flatten and die; the stratum lucidum (thick skin only) is a translucent layer of dead, flattened ker', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (242, 90, 'Functions of the Lymphatic System', 'Functions of the Lymphatic System', 'The lymphatic system performs multiple critical functions: draining excess interstitial fluid back into the bloodstream, transporting immune system cells, and facilitating absorption of dietary lipids and fat-soluble vitamins from the gut. Blood pressure causes capillary filtration, releasing approximately 20 liters of plasma daily into the interstitial space; while 17 liters is reabsorbed directly by blood vessels, the remaining 3 liters is drained by lymphatic vessels, trunks, and ducts. Interstitial fluid becomes lymph once it enters the lymphatic system; when the lymphatic system is damaged (blocked by cancer cells or destroyed by injury), protein-rich interstitial fluid accumulates in tissue spaces, causing lymphedema with serious medical consequences. Immune system cells use lymphatic vessels to travel from interstitial spaces back into circulation and use lymph nodes—small, bean-shaped organs distributed throughout the lymphatic system—as major staging areas for developing critical immune responses. Search terms: lymphatic system, interstitial fluid, lymph, capillary filtration, lymphatic vessels, lymph nodes, lymphedema, immune system cells, dietary lipids, fat-soluble vitamins.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (243, 90, 'Lymphocytes: B Cells, T Cells, Plasma Cells, and Natural Killer Cells', 'Lymphocytes: B Cells, T Cells, Plasma Cells, and Natural Killer Cells', 'Lymphocytes are the primary cells of adaptive immune responses, comprising four main types: B cells, T cells, plasma cells, and natural killer (NK) cells. B cells and T cells are morphologically identical with a large central nucleus and thin cytoplasm layer, but are distinguished by surface protein markers and secreted molecules; both initially develop in bone marrow, with B cells maturing in red bone marrow and T cells migrating to and maturing in the thymus gland. B cells function by producing antibodies, which are proteins that bind specifically to antigens (chemical structures on pathogen surfaces that bind to T or B lymphocyte antigen receptors); upon antigen activation, B cells differentiate into plasma cells that secrete soluble antibodies. Plasma cells differ morphologically from standard B and T cells by containing extensive cytoplasm packed with rough endoplasmic reticulum for protein synthesis. T cells do not secrete antibodies but perform various adaptive immune functions, including secreting soluble factors that communicate with other immune cells or destroying cells infected with intracellular pathogens. Natural killer cells are circulating blood cells containing cytotoxic granules in extensive cytoplasm, sharing this mechanism with cytotoxic T cells, and serve as first-line defenses against viruses and certain cancers. B cells, T cells, and NK cells circulate in bloodstream and lymph and reside in secondary lymphoid organs including the spleen and lymph nodes; the human body contains approximately 10^12 lymphocytes.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (244, 90, 'Primary Lymphoid Organs and Lymphocyte Development', 'Primary Lymphoid Organs and Lymphocyte Development', '# Summary: Primary Lymphoid Organs and Lymphocyte Development

The primary lymphoid organs—bone marrow and thymus gland—are sites where lymphocytes mature, proliferate, and undergo selection to enable attack on pathogens while preserving the body''s own cells. B cells undergo nearly all development in red bone marrow, while immature T cells (thymocytes) leave the bone marrow and mature largely in the thymus gland, a bilobed organ located between the sternum and aorta. The thymus is structurally organized by connective tissue into a capsule, lobes, and trabeculae-divided lobules, with an outer cortex (densely packed with thymocytes, epithelial cells, macrophages, and dendritic cells) and an inner medulla (less dense collection of thymocytes, epithelial cells, and dendritic cells where thymocytes migrate before leaving). Hematopoiesis occurs in red bone marrow, while yellow bone marrow serves as energy storage; embryonic blood cell production in the yolk sac is later taken over by the spleen, lymph nodes, and liver before bone marrow assumes most hematopoietic functions.

**Search terms:** primary lymphoid organs, bone marrow, thymus gland, lymphocyte development, B cell, T cell, thymocyte, red bone marrow, yellow bone marrow, hematopoiesis, thymus cortex, thymus medulla, trabeculae, epithelial cells, macrophages, dendritic cells', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (245, 90, 'Secondary Lymphoid Organs and their Roles in Active Immune Responses', 'Secondary Lymphoid Organs and their Roles in Active Immune Responses', 'Secondary lymphoid organs—lymph nodes, spleen, and lymphoid nodules—are sites where naïve lymphocytes (fully functional but antigen-inexperienced cells that have left primary lymphoid organs) mount active immune responses. These organs share common structural features: lymphoid follicles containing B cell-rich and T cell-rich areas where lymphocytes form; reticular fibers with associated fixed macrophages; germinal centers where B lymphocytes rapidly divide and differentiate; and high endothelial venules (specialized post-capillary vessels with thicker, columnar endothelial cells) that allow blood cells to directly enter the tissues. Lymph nodes specifically function as "filters of the lymph," removing debris and pathogens; bacteria from infected interstitial fluid are taken up by lymphatic capillaries and transported to regional lymph nodes, where dendritic cells and macrophages internalize and kill pathogens. Search terms: secondary lymphoid organs, lymph nodes, spleen, lymphoid nodules, naïve lymphocytes, lymphoid follicles, germinal centers, high endothelial venules, reticular fibers, dendritic cells, macrophages, antigen response.', 4);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (246, 90, 'Structure of the Lymphatic System', 'Structure of the Lymphatic System', 'This subsection describes the structural organization of the lymphatic system, introducing lymphatic capillaries (terminal lymphatics), lymphatic vessels, lymph nodes, and lymphatic ducts as key components. Lymphatic capillaries, formed by one cell-thick endothelial layers with overlapping cells and anchored by collagen filaments, allow interstitial fluid to enter and become lymph; they are absent from the central nervous system, bone marrow, bones, teeth, and cornea. Lymph flows from lymphatic capillaries through progressively larger lymphatic vessels (which have three-tunic structure and one-way semi-lunar valves) and lymphatic trunks, eventually draining into the bloodstream via the right lymphatic duct (draining the right head, thorax, and upper limb into the right subclavian vein) and the thoracic duct (draining the remaining body into the left subclavian vein); the thoracic duct originates at the cisterna chyli beneath the diaphragm and receives lymph from the lower abdomen, pelvis, and lower limbs via the left and right lumbar trunks and intestinal trunk. In the small intestine, specialized lymphatic capillaries called lacteals transport dietary triglycerides, lipids, and proteins as chyle into the lymphatic system and eventually the bloodstream. Lymph movement is driven by skeletal muscle contraction and breathing rather than heart pumping, with one-way valves preventing backflow; approximately 500–600 lymph nodes are distributed throughout the body, commonly located near the groin, armpits, neck, chest, and abdomen. Search terms: lymphatic capillaries, terminal lymphatics, lymphatic vessels, lymphatic ducts, lymph nodes, right lymphatic duct, thoracic duct, right subclavian vein, left subclavian vein, cisterna chyli', 5);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (247, 90, 'The Organization of Immune Function', 'The Organization of Immune Function', 'The immune system comprises barriers, cells, and soluble proteins organized into three temporal phases: barrier defenses (skin and mucous membranes) that act instantaneously; the rapid, nonspecific innate immune response; and the slower, specific adaptive immune response primarily controlled by lymphocytes. All immune cells arise in the bone marrow through differentiation pathways from hematopoietic stem cells, which persist throughout adulthood and continuously replace blood cells. Immune cells are classified into three functional groups: phagocytic cells that ingest pathogens; lymphocytes that coordinate adaptive immunity; and cells containing cytoplasmic granules that mediate responses against parasites and intracellular pathogens. Key search terms: barrier defenses, innate immune response, adaptive immune response, lymphocytes, leukocytes, hematopoietic stem cells, bone marrow, phagocytic cells, cytoplasmic granules.', 6);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (248, 91, 'Cells of the Innate Immune Response', 'Cells of the Innate Immune Response', 'This subsection introduces the major cellular components of innate immune defense: phagocytes (macrophages, neutrophils, dendritic cells) and natural killer (NK) cells. Key structures and processes include phagocytosis, in which phagocytes engulf pathogens as phagosomes that fuse with lysosomes; macrophages (agranulocytes found in tissues as Kupffer cells in liver, histiocytes in connective tissue, and alveolar macrophages in lungs); neutrophils (granulocytes containing vasoactive mediators like histamine, attracted via chemotaxis from blood); monocytes (circulating agranulocytes that differentiate into macrophages or dendritic cells); and NK cells (lymphocytes that induce apoptosis in infected cells). The subsection explicitly states that NK cells kill infected cells through two mechanisms: fas ligand binding to fas molecules on infected cell surfaces, or release of perforins (which form membrane pores) and granzymes (protein-digesting enzymes entering via perforin pores) that trigger intracellular apoptosis. Students would search for: phagocyte, phagocytosis, phagosome, lysosome, macrophage, neutrophil, dendritic cell, granulocyte, agranulocyte, monocyte, NK cell, apoptosis, fas ligand, fas molecule, perforin, and granzyme.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (249, 91, 'Inflammatory Response', 'Inflammatory Response', 'The inflammatory response is the hallmark of the innate immune response, characterized by four primary features—heat, redness, pain, and swelling—that can result from tissue injury or infection. Key anatomical structures and mediators include mast cells, histamine, leukotrienes, prostaglandins, neutrophils, macrophages, and dendritic cells. The textbook identifies four important parts to the inflammatory response: tissue injury (which stimulates release of mast cell granules containing inflammatory mediators), vasodilation (histamine and prostaglandins increase local blood vessel diameter, causing increased blood flow), increased vascular permeability (inflammatory mediators cause plasma leakage and edema formation), and recruitment of phagocytes (leukotrienes attract neutrophils by chemotaxis, and macrophage cytokines recruit additional macrophages). Acute inflammation is a short-term response, while chronic inflammation—caused by foreign bodies, persistent pathogens, or autoimmune diseases such as rheumatoid arthritis—is ongoing and associated with tissue destruction and fibrosis. The inflammatory process brings fluid and cells to destroy pathogens and remove debris, isolates the infection site, encourages clotting factor entry for wound repair, and facilitates antigen transport to lymph nodes by dendritic cells for adaptive immune response development.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (250, 91, 'Recognition of Pathogens', 'Recognition of Pathogens', 'This subsection introduces pattern recognition receptors (PRRs), membrane-bound receptors present on phagocytic cells and cytotoxic NK cells that recognize pathogen-specific molecules such as bacterial cell wall components and bacterial flagellar proteins, as well as molecules released by stressed or damaged cells. PRRs evolved prior to the adaptive immune response and are constitutively expressed on cell surfaces regardless of pathogen exposure. The innate immune system employs a limited number of PRRs with broad pathogenic specificity, constrained by finite cell membrane surface area and the genetic cost of encoding each receptor type, contrasting sharply with the adaptive immune system''s strategy of using large numbers of highly specific receptors. When innate immune cells recognize and bind a pathogen via PRRs, they initiate phagocytosis or, in the case of intracellular pathogens, cellular apoptosis to destroy the microbe and prevent further infection. Receptor types vary by cell type but typically include receptors for bacterial components and for complement. Search terms: pattern recognition receptors (PRRs), phagocytic cells, cytotoxic NK cells, bacterial cell wall components, bacterial flagellar proteins, innate immune response, adaptive immune response, phagocytosis, cellular apoptosis, complement receptors.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (251, 91, 'Soluble Mediators of the Innate Immune Response', 'Soluble Mediators of the Innate Immune Response', 'This subsection introduces soluble mediators of innate immunity: cytokines (signaling molecules enabling short-distance cell communication), chemokines (soluble mediators that attract cells over longer distances via chemotaxis), and early induced proteins (non-constitutive proteins synthesized during innate immune response). Key early induced proteins include interferons (secreted by virus-infected cells to induce antiviral proteins in adjacent cells), mannose-binding protein, and C-reactive protein (both liver-made, bind bacterial cell wall polysaccharides). The subsection explains opsonization—tagging pathogens for phagocytosis through antibody or antimicrobial protein binding—and details the complement system, a constitutive cascade of blood plasma proteins made in the liver. The complement system operates via two pathways: the alternate pathway (activated spontaneously without antibodies) and the classical pathway (requires antibody-bound antigen). Both pathways converge at C3 protein splitting; in the alternate pathway, C3 activation by factor P, factor B, and factor D produces C3b (binds pathogen surface for opsonization) and C3a (diffuses as chemotactic agent). Proteins C5–C9 form the membrane-attack complex (MAC), which kills pathogens by disrupting osmotic balance. Complement fragments attract macrophages and neutrophils to infection sites, and surface-bound C3b receptors on phagocytes enhance pathogen destruction.', 4);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (252, 92, 'Autoimmune Responses', 'Autoimmune Responses', 'Autoimmune responses occur when immune tolerance breaks down and the immune system attacks self-antigens, causing significant damage; key structures and processes include tolerance breakdown, cross-reactivity between bacterial antigens and self-antigens, MHC gene expression, and complement protein activation. One mechanism of autoimmune disease initiation is cross-reactivity following bacterial infection, exemplified by rheumatic fever, where antibodies to Streptococcus M protein cross-react with heart myosin, activate complement proteins, and damage heart valves. Genetic factors associated with MHC genes influence autoimmune disease susceptibility, while environmental triggers and the hygiene hypothesis (autoimmune diseases are rare in countries with high infectious disease incidence) also play roles in autoimmune responses. The textbook describes over 80 autoimmune diseases with specific targets and effects: celiac disease targets tissue transglutaminase (small intestine damage); diabetes mellitus type I targets pancreatic beta cells (low insulin production); Graves'' disease targets thyroid-stimulating hormone receptor via blocking antibodies (hyperthyroidism); Hashimoto''s thyroiditis targets thyroid-stimulating hormone receptor via mimicking antibodies (hypothyroidism); lupus erythematosus targets nuclear DNA and proteins (multi-system damage); myasthenia gravis targets acetylcholine receptors in neuromuscular junctions (muscle weakness); rheumatoid arthritis targets joint capsule antigens (joint inflammation). Treatment typically uses immunosuppressive and anti-inflammatory drugs such as steroids to manage symptoms.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (253, 93, 'Active versus Passive Immunity', 'Active versus Passive Immunity', 'This subsection distinguishes between active and passive immunity as two mechanisms for acquiring resistance to pathogens. Active immunity develops through an adaptive immune response within an individual and includes naturally acquired active immunity (response to pathogen exposure) and artificially acquired active immunity (via vaccines—killed or weakened pathogens that generate immunological memory without severe symptoms). Passive immunity arises from transfer of antibodies without requiring the recipient to mount their own immune response; naturally acquired passive immunity occurs when IgG transfers from maternal circulation to fetus via the placenta and when IgA transfers to newborns via breastfeeding, while artificially acquired passive immunity involves injections of immunoglobulins from animals previously exposed to specific pathogens. The subsection establishes that passive immunity provides fast-acting but temporary protection (antibodies degrade over time) and lacks development of immunological memory, whereas active immunity generates lasting immunological memory. Key search terms: active immunity, passive immunity, naturally acquired active immunity, artificially acquired active immunity, vaccine, immunological memory, passive transfer of antibodies, IgG, IgA, placenta, breastfeeding, immunoglobulin injections, pathogen exposure.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (254, 93, 'Antibody Structure', 'Antibody Structure', 'This subsection introduces antibody structure and the five antibody classes (IgM, IgD, IgG, IgA, IgE), explaining that antibodies are glycoproteins composed of heavy chains and light chains linked by disulfide bonds, with an Fc region that binds to Fc receptors on effector cells and two identical antigen-binding sites. Key entities include: heavy chain, light chain, Fc region, antigen-binding site, IgM, IgD, IgG, IgA, IgE, class switching, complement proteins, complement cascade, mast cells, plasma cells, memory B cells, clonal selection, clonal expansion, primary response, and secondary response. The textbook establishes that IgM and IgD function as B cell antigen receptors on naïve B cells; IgM is the largest antibody (pentameric, 10 antigen-binding sites) and first produced during primary response, activating complement cascade and promoting chemotaxis, opsonization, and cell lysis; class switching changes antibody class while maintaining antigen specificity and antigen-binding sites; IgG is monomeric and predominates in late primary and secondary responses, crosses the placenta, and activates complement less effectively than IgM; dimeric IgA is the only antibody leaving the body interior to protect mucous membranes and is present in breast milk; and IgE binds strongly to mast cell Fc receptors, making mast cell degranulation antigen-specific and potentially causing anaphylaxis. The subsection also describes primary and secondary antibody responses: primary response is delayed several days (time for B cell clones to expand and differentiate into plasma cells) with low antibody levels, while secondary response shows no time delay and much higher antibody production, with memory B cells generated simultaneously during clonal expansion enabling the faster, stronger secondary response', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (255, 93, 'B Cell Differentiation and Activation', 'B Cell Differentiation and Activation', 'B cell differentiation occurs in the bone marrow, generating up to 100 trillion different clones with diverse antigen receptors. Central tolerance mechanisms—clonal deletion (apoptosis of immature B cells binding strongly to self-antigens) and clonal anergy (functional inactivation of B cells exposed to soluble self-antigen)—prevent self-reactive B cells from maturing. Peripheral tolerance involves mature B cells leaving the bone marrow; when these cells bind self-antigen without signals from helper T cells (Th2), they undergo apoptosis, demonstrating T cell control over the adaptive immune response. Upon antigen activation, B cells differentiate into plasma cells, which migrate to the bone marrow and secrete antibodies until death (terminally differentiated), or into memory B cells that result from clonal expansion and generate stronger, faster secondary immune responses compared to primary responses.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (256, 93, 'T cell-dependent versus T cell-independent Antigens', 'T cell-dependent versus T cell-independent Antigens', 'This subsection distinguishes T cell-dependent and T cell-independent antigens in B cell activation. T cell-independent antigens are repeated carbohydrate moieties on bacterial cell walls that crosslink surface antibodies (which have two binding sites per antibody) on B cells, providing sufficient activation without T cell cytokines. T cell-dependent antigens are complex protein-based antigens that lack sufficient repetition to efficiently crosslink surface antibodies and require cooperation between B and T cells: the B cell''s surface immunoglobulin recognizes native antigen, which is internalized, processed, and presented on class II MHC molecules to Th2 cells; the Th2 cell binds via its antigen receptor and secretes cytokines that diffuse to and activate the B cell. The B cell thus receives dual signals from its surface antibody and T cell cytokines, and functions as a professional antigen-presenting cell. Key search terms: T cell-dependent antigen, T cell-independent antigen, Th2 cells, B cell activation, surface antibody crosslinking, class II MHC molecule, antigen receptor, cytokines, surface immunoglobulin, antigen-presenting cell.', 4);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (257, 94, 'Defenses against Viruses', 'Defenses against Viruses', '# Summary: Defenses against Viruses

The primary mechanisms against viruses are NK cells, interferons, and cytotoxic T cells, with antibodies effective only during the protection phase before viral entry into cells. Many virally infected cells downregulate MHC class I molecule expression, which prevents cytotoxic T cell recognition but allows NK cells to identify and destroy these class I-negative infected cells, creating complementary activities between NK and cytotoxic T cells. Interferons slow viral replication and are used to treat hepatitis B and C but cannot completely eliminate viruses, whereas cytotoxic T cells are key to the antiviral response through clonal expansion and their ability to kill multiple target cells before viral replication completes. Dendritic cells sample antigens from the intestinal lumen and transport them to regional lymph nodes to mount an immune response. Pathogens have evolved evasion mechanisms—exemplified by Mycobacterium tuberculosis, which possesses a complex cell wall resistant to macrophage digestive enzymes, allowing persistent infection and chronic disease. Search terms: NK cells, interferons, cytotoxic T cells, antibodies, MHC class I molecules, viral infection, clonal expansion, dendritic cells, hepatitis B, hepatitis C, Mycobacterium tuberculosis, tuberculosis, pathogen evasion.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (258, 94, 'Evasion of the Immune System by Pathogens', 'Evasion of the Immune System by Pathogens', 'This subsection describes mechanisms by which pathogens evade immune responses, including: multiple bacterial strains (exemplified by Staphylococcus aureus and methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus), viral mutation, genetic recombination, and immunosuppressive molecules. Key entities and search terms include: Staphylococcus aureus, methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA), boils, influenza virus, H1N1 swine flu, surface antigens, surface molecules, vaccine formulations, and immunosuppressive molecules. The textbook explicitly states that different bacterial strains differ in surface antigens and that immune response against one strain does not affect other strains; that viruses'' surface molecules mutate continuously, causing annual flu vaccines to become ineffective; that genetic recombination (combining gene segments from two different pathogens) occurs when two viruses infect the same cell, as exemplified by recombination between human and pig influenza viruses leading to the 2010 H1N1 outbreak; and that pathogens produce immunosuppressive molecules that impair immune function, with viruses being especially effective at this form of evasion.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (259, 95, 'Immune Responses Against Cancer', 'Immune Responses Against Cancer', 'This subsection describes the three-stage immune response to cancer—elimination, equilibrium, and escape—wherein the immune system initially targets tumor-specific antigens and kills most cancer cells, but cancer cells may mutate to evade immune recognition and escape control. Key structures and processes include tumor-specific antigens, cancer cell mutation and division, and the immune system''s capability to detect and proliferate against cancer cells. Cancer vaccines, which differ from viral and bacterial vaccines by targeting the body''s own cancer cells, represent a therapeutic approach to enhance the early anti-cancer immune response; vaccines have been developed for malignant melanoma and renal cell carcinoma and show clinical promise. The subsection also notes that hepatitis B virus causes liver cancer and human papilloma virus causes cervical cancer, and that vaccines against these viruses can prevent these cancers by stimulating immune response. Search terms include: immune response to cancer, tumor-specific antigens, elimination-equilibrium-escape stages, cancer cell mutation, cancer vaccines, malignant melanoma, renal cell carcinoma, hepatitis B virus, human papilloma virus, and HIV as a pathogen that escapes immune response.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (260, 95, 'The Rh Factor', 'The Rh Factor', 'Red blood cells are typed based on surface antigens, including the Rh antigen system; individuals are classified as Rh-positive (presence of Rh antigen) or Rh-negative (absence of Rh antigen). Erythroblastosis fetalis, a hemolytic disease of the newborn, occurs when an Rh-negative birth parent has multiple Rh-positive children. During the first Rh-positive birth, the parent mounts a primary anti-Rh antibody response to fetal blood cells entering the bloodstream; in subsequent Rh-positive pregnancies, IgG antibodies against Rh-positive blood from the secondary response cross the placenta and attack fetal blood, causing anemia. This immune response occurs because the fetus is not genetically identical to the birth parent. Treatment involves administering antibodies specific for Rh factor to the pregnant person during the first and subsequent births to destroy fetal blood entering the maternal system and prevent the immune response. **Search terms:** Rh antigen, Rh-positive, Rh-negative, ABO blood type, erythroblastosis fetalis, hemolytic disease of the newborn, anti-Rh antibody response, IgG antibodies, placenta, fetal blood.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (261, 95, 'Tissue Transplantation', 'Tissue Transplantation', 'Tissue transplantation success depends primarily on matching MHC molecules (histocompatibility antigens) between donor and recipient, as MHC polymorphism and polygeny create extensive variation in the human population. When donor organ MHC molecules differ from the recipient''s, a cytotoxic T cell response causes transplant rejection, evidenced histologically by massive T lymphocyte infiltration within weeks post-transplant. The six most polymorphic human MHC molecules—three class I (A, B, C) and three class II (DP, DQ, DR)—require matching of at least 3–4 molecules for successful transplantation, with immunosuppressive drugs like cyclosporine A improving outcomes. Family members are more likely to share MHC molecules than unrelated individuals; unrelated donors are identified through worldwide databases, though supply remains insufficient. Bone marrow transplants carry the additional risk of graft-versus-host disease, in which donor lymphocytes attack recipient tissues, causing rash and damage to liver and mucosa; this is mitigated by removing mature T cells from donor marrow before transplantation. Certain virus-caused cancers (Kaposi''s sarcoma from human herpesvirus, liver cancer from hepatitis B virus, cervical cancer from human papilloma virus) are controlled by a healthy immune system, and vaccines against hepatitis B and human papilloma viruses can prevent their associated cancers.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (262, 96, 'Muscles That Move the Feet and Toes', 'Muscles That Move the Feet and Toes', 'This subsection covers the muscles that move the feet and toes, organized by anatomical compartments and regions. The leg contains three compartments divided by deep fascia: the anterior compartment (tibialis anterior, extensor hallucis longus, extensor digitorum longus, fibularis tertius) whose muscles raise the front of the foot during dorsiflexion; the lateral compartment (fibularis longus/peroneus longus, fibularis brevis/peroneus brevis); and the posterior compartment (gastrocnemius, soleus, plantaris, popliteus, flexor digitorum longus, flexor hallucis longus, tibialis posterior), whose superficial muscles insert onto the calcaneal tendon (Achilles tendon) and keep humans upright. The superior extensor retinaculum (transverse ligament of the ankle) and inferior extensor retinaculum hold anterior compartment tendons in place during dorsiflexion. The foot contains intrinsic muscles organized into a dorsal group (extensor digitorum brevis) and a plantar group (four layers), which provide arch support and contribute to toe movements; the plantar aponeurosis, running from the calcaneus bone to the toes, provides principal support for the longitudinal arch and inflammation of this structure causes plantar fasciitis. Search terms: anterior/lateral/posterior compartments of leg, tibialis anterior, extensor hallucis longus, extensor digitorum longus, fibularis tertius, fibularis longus, fibularis brevis, gastrocnemius, soleus, plantaris, popliteus, flexor digitorum longus, flexor hallucis longus, tibialis posterior, calcaneal tendon, Achilles tendon, extensor retinaculum, intrinsic muscles of foot, plantar aponeurosis, plantar fasciitis.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (263, 96, 'Muscles of the Thigh', 'Muscles of the Thigh', 'This subsection introduces the muscles of the thigh, organized by anatomical compartment and function. Key structures include the iliopsoas group (psoas major and iliacus), gluteal muscles (gluteus maximus, gluteus medius, gluteus minimus), and deep hip rotators (piriformis, obturator internus, obturator externus, superior gemellus, inferior gemellus, quadratus femoris); the medial compartment muscles (adductor longus, adductor brevis, adductor magnus, pectineus, gracilis); the anterior compartment quadriceps femoris group (rectus femoris, vastus lateralis, vastus medialis, vastus intermedius) and sartorius; and the posterior compartment hamstring group (biceps femoris, semitendinosus, semimembranosus). The subsection establishes that most thigh muscles originate on the pelvic girdle and insert on the femur, and describes specific actions: the tensor fascia latae acts as a synergist of the gluteus medius and iliopsoas in flexing and abducting the thigh; the adductor longus flexes the thigh while adductor magnus extends it; the quadriceps tendon (patellar tendon) inserts into the patella and continues as the patellar ligament to the tibial tuberosity; and the hamstring tendons form the popliteal fossa. The pectineus is located within the femoral triangle, which also contains the femoral nerve, femoral artery, femoral vein, and deep inguinal lymph nodes.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (264, 97, 'Muscles of the Abdomen', 'Muscles of the Abdomen', 'This subsection introduces the four pairs of abdominal muscles that form the anterolateral abdominal wall: external obliques, internal obliques, transversus abdominis, and rectus abdominis, along with the quadratus lumborum of the posterior wall. The external obliques originate from ribs 5–12 and ilium, insert on ribs 7–10, linea alba, and ilium, and produce twisting at waist and lateral flexion of the vertebral column; the internal obliques have similar origins and insertions and produce the same movements; the transversus abdominis originates from ilium and ribs 5–10, inserts on sternum, linea alba, and pubis, and produces compression of the abdominal cavity during forceful exhalations, defecation, urination, and childbirth; the rectus abdominis originates from pubis, inserts on sternum and ribs 5 and 7, and produces flexion of the vertebral column for sitting up. The subsection describes the three-layer arrangement of flat skeletal muscles (external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis) oriented in different directions to allow trunk movements and protect internal abdominal organs, with the linea alba—a white fibrous band made of bilateral rectus sheaths—joining at the anterior midline and enclosing the rectus abdominis, which is segmented by tendinous intersections. The posterior abdominal wall is formed by lumbar vertebrae, ilia, psoas major, iliacus, and quadratus lumborum, which stabilize the body and maintain posture. Search terms: external obliques, internal obliques, transversus abdominis, rectus abdominis, quadratus lumborum, linea alba, rectus sheaths, tendinous intersections, anterolateral abdominal wall, posterior', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (265, 97, 'Muscles of the Pelvic Floor and Perineum', 'Muscles of the Pelvic Floor and Perineum', 'The pelvic floor comprises the pelvic diaphragm, which spans from the pubis to the coccyx and consists of the levator ani (composed of the pubococcygeus and iliococcygeus) and the ischiococcygeus. The levator ani supports the pelvic viscera, resists abdominal muscle pressure to aid defecation and childbirth, and creates skeletal muscle sphincters at the urethra and anus; the ischiococcygeus assists by pulling the coccyx anteriorly. The perineum is a diamond-shaped space bounded by the pubic symphysis (anteriorly), coccyx (posteriorly), and ischial tuberosities (laterally), lying inferior to the pelvic diaphragm and divided into the urogenital triangle (anterior, containing external genitals) and anal triangle (posterior, containing the anus), with superficial and deep layers. Sex-specific perineal muscles include the compressor urethrae and sphincter urethrovaginalis in females (which close the vagina) and the deep transverse perineal muscle in males (which plays a role in ejaculation). Search terms: pelvic floor, pelvic diaphragm, levator ani, pubococcygeus, iliococcygeus, ischiococcygeus, perineum, urogenital triangle, anal triangle, compressor urethrae, sphincter urethrovaginalis, deep transverse perineal muscle.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (266, 97, 'Muscles of the Thorax', 'Muscles of the Thorax', 'This subsection introduces the thoracic muscles that facilitate breathing by altering thoracic cavity volume: the **diaphragm**, **external intercostal muscles**, **internal intercostal muscles**, and **innermost intercostal muscles**. The diaphragm, the prime mover for inhalation and exhalation, originates at the sternum, ribs 6–12, and lumbar vertebrae and inserts at the central tendon; it separates the thoracic and abdominal cavities and is dome-shaped at rest. The external intercostals (11 pairs) elevate the ribs during inhalation, originating at the superior rib and inserting at the inferior rib of each intercostal space; the internal intercostals (11 pairs) draw ribs together during forced exhalation with opposite origin-insertion points. The innermost intercostal muscles act as synergists for the internal intercostals. The diaphragm contains three openings—the caval opening (inferior vena cava), esophageal hiatus (esophagus and nerves), and aortic hiatus (aorta, thoracic duct, azygous vein)—and participates in the Valsalva maneuver with abdominal muscles during defecation, urination, and childbirth. Search terms: diaphragm, external intercostal muscles, internal intercostal muscles, innermost intercostal muscles, thoracic cavity, central tendon, caval opening, esophageal hiatus, aortic hiatus, Valsalva maneuver, inhalation, exhalation.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (267, 98, 'Muscles That Create Facial Expression', 'Muscles That Create Facial Expression', 'Muscles of facial expression originate on the skull surface and insert into the dermis and connective tissue of the skin; because they insert in skin rather than bone, their contraction moves the skin to create facial expressions. Key structures include: orbicularis oris (circular muscle that moves the lips), orbicularis oculi (circular muscle that closes the eye), occipitofrontalis (moves scalp and eyebrows), buccinator (compresses cheek, enables whistling/blowing/sucking and contributes to chewing), and corrugator supercilii (prime mover of eyebrows, raises them as if surprised and lowers them as if frowning). The occipitofrontalis consists of a frontal belly (frontalis) on the forehead and an occipital belly (occipitalis) on the back of the head, connected by the epicranial aponeurosis (galea aponeurosis) rather than muscle across the top of the head. Search terms: muscles of facial expression, orbicularis oris, orbicularis oculi, occipitofrontalis, frontalis, occipitalis, epicranial aponeurosis, galea aponeurosis, buccinator, corrugator supercilii.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (268, 98, 'Muscles That Move the Eyes', 'Muscles That Move the Eyes', '# Summary: Muscles That Move the Eyes

Eyeball movement is controlled by extrinsic eye muscles, which originate outside the eye and insert onto the outer surface of the eyeball within the eye socket. The six extraocular muscles include four rectus muscles (superior rectus, inferior rectus, medial rectus, lateral rectus) and two oblique muscles (superior oblique, inferior oblique); all rectus and oblique muscles originate from the common tendinous ring attached to the optic foramen and sphenoid bone, and insert on various surfaces of the eyeball to produce elevation, depression, abduction, adduction, and rotational movements. Additional eyelid muscles include the levator palpebrae superioris, which originates from the roof of the orbit (sphenoid bone) and elevates the upper eyelid, and the orbicularis oculi, which originates from medial orbital bones and closes the eyelids through compression. Clinical examination of coordinated eye muscle function involves following a target through vertical and horizontal directions. Search terms: extrinsic eye muscles, superior rectus, inferior rectus, medial rectus, lateral rectus, superior oblique, inferior oblique, levator palpebrae superioris, orbicularis oculi, common tendinous ring, optic foramen, eyeball movement, eye socket.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (269, 98, 'Muscles That Move the Head', 'Muscles That Move the Head', '# Summary: Muscles That Move the Head

The neck muscles balance, move, and rotate the head, which is attached to the vertebral column; unilateral contraction produces head rotation while bilateral contraction produces flexion or extension. The **sternocleidomastoid** originates from the sternum and clavicle, inserts on the temporal bone (mastoid process) and occipital bone, and divides the neck into anterior and posterior triangles; it laterally flexes and rotates the head, rotating to the opposite side when contracting individually and flexing the head when contracting bilaterally. The **semispinalis capitis**, **splenius capitis**, and **longissimus capitis** all originate from transverse, articular, or spinous processes of cervical and thoracic vertebrae and insert on the occipital bone and/or temporal bone (mastoid process); when contracting individually, each laterally flexes and rotates the head to the same side, and when contracting bilaterally, each extends the head. Search terms: sternocleidomastoid, semispinalis capitis, splenius capitis, longissimus capitis, head rotation, head flexion, head extension, lateral flexion, neck muscles, anterior triangle, posterior triangle.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (270, 98, 'Muscles That Move the Lower Jaw', 'Muscles That Move the Lower Jaw', 'This subsection introduces the four primary muscles of mastication (chewing): the masseter, temporalis, medial pterygoid, and lateral pterygoid. The masseter is the main chewing muscle, originating from the maxilla arch and zygomatic arch and inserting on the mandible to elevate and close the lower jaw. The temporalis originates from the temporal bone, inserts on the mandible, and elevates and retracts the mandible to close the mouth and pull the lower jaw under the upper jaw. The lateral pterygoid originates from the pterygoid process of the sphenoid bone, inserts on the mandible, and depresses, protracts, abducts, and adducts the mandible to open the mouth and move the lower jaw side-to-side. The medial pterygoid originates from the sphenoid bone and maxilla, inserts on the mandible and temporomandibular joint, and elevates, protracts, abducts, and adducts the mandible to close the mouth and move the lower jaw side-to-side. Search terms: mastication, mandible, masseter, temporalis, lateral pterygoid, medial pterygoid, zygomatic arch, temporal bone, pterygoid process, sphenoid bone, maxilla, temporomandibular joint.', 4);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (271, 98, 'Muscles of the Anterior Neck', 'Muscles of the Anterior Neck', 'The anterior neck muscles assist in deglutition and speech by controlling the larynx and hyoid bone, a horseshoe-shaped bone that serves as a foundation for tongue movement. Suprahyoid muscles (digastric, stylohyoid, mylohyoid, geniohyoid) are located superior to the hyoid bone and raise it, the floor of the mouth, and the larynx during swallowing; the digastric also depresses the mandible, the stylohyoid moves the hyoid bone posteriorly, the mylohyoid lifts the hyoid bone and presses the tongue upward, and the geniohyoid raises and pulls the hyoid bone anteriorly while depressing the mandible. Infrahyoid muscles (omohyoid, sternohyoid, thyrohyoid, sternothyroid) are located inferiorly and depress the hyoid bone and control laryngeal position; the thyrohyoid elevates the thyroid cartilage of the larynx while the sternothyroid depresses it to create different voice tones. During general anesthesia, relaxation of muscles necessary for breathing and tongue movement can cause airway obstruction, which is prevented by endotracheal intubation—placement of a tube into the trachea to maintain an open airway to the lungs and seal it from the oropharynx. Search terms: suprahyoid muscles, infrahyoid muscles, digastric muscle, stylohyoid muscle, mylohyoid muscle, geniohyoid muscle, omohyoid muscle, sternohyoid muscle, thyrohyoid muscle, sternothyroid muscle, hyoid bone, larynx, deglutition, endotracheal intubation, thyroid cartilage.', 5);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (272, 98, 'Muscles of the Posterior Neck and the Back', 'Muscles of the Posterior Neck and the Back', 'This subsection introduces the major muscle groups of the posterior neck and back, including the splenius muscles (splenius capitis, splenius cervicis), erector spinae group (iliocostalis, longissimus, spinalis subdivisions), transversospinales muscles (semispinalis capitis, semispinalis cervicis, semispinalis thoracis, multifidus), segmental muscles (interspinales, intertransversarii), and scalene muscles (anterior scalene, middle scalene, posterior scalene). The splenius capitis inserts on the head region and performs head extension, lateral flexion, and rotation; the splenius cervicis extends onto the cervical region with similar functions. The erector spinae comprises three laterally-to-medially arranged groups—iliocostalis (cervicis, thoracis, lumborum), longissimus (capitis, cervicis, thoracis), and spinalis (capitis, cervicis, thoracis)—and functions as the primary extensor of the vertebral column while controlling flexion, lateral flexion, rotation, and lumbar curve maintenance. The transversospinales muscles run from transverse processes to spinous processes; semispinalis muscles extend and laterally flex the vertebral column, while multifidus of the lumbar region assists in these movements. The segmental muscles (interspinales and intertransversarii) stabilize the vertebral column by connecting consecutive vertebrae. The scalene muscles flex, laterally flex, and rotate the head while contributing to deep inhalation.', 6);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (273, 99, 'Muscles That Move the Humerus', 'Muscles That Move the Humerus', 'This subsection introduces muscles that move the humerus, divided into axial muscles (pectoralis major, latissimus dorsi) and scapular muscles (deltoid, subscapularis, supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres major, teres minor, coracobrachialis). Key anatomical relationships include: the pectoralis major covers the anterior thorax; the latissimus dorsi inserts into an aponeurosis on the inferior back; the deltoid is the major abductor of the arm and also facilitates flexion, medial rotation, extension, and lateral rotation; the subscapularis originates on the anterior scapula and medially rotates the arm; the supraspinatus and infraspinatus abduct and laterally rotate the arm respectively; the teres major extends the arm and assists in adduction and medial rotation; the teres minor laterally rotates and extends the arm; and the coracobrachialis flexes and adducts the arm. The tendons of the subscapularis, supraspinatus, infraspinatus, and teres minor form the rotator cuff (musculotendinous cuff), a circle of tendons connecting the scapula to the humerus around the shoulder joint. Search terms: pectoralis major, latissimus dorsi, deltoid, subscapularis, supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres major, teres minor, coracobrachialis, rotator cuff, musculotendinous cuff, humerus, shoulder joint.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (274, 99, 'Muscles That Move the Wrist, Hand, and Fingers', 'Muscles That Move the Wrist, Hand, and Fingers', 'This subsection covers muscles that move the wrist, hand, and fingers, organized into extrinsic muscles (originating in the forearm) and intrinsic muscles (originating and inserting within the hand). The extrinsic muscles include the anterior compartment muscles—superficial (flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus, flexor carpi ulnaris, flexor digitorum superficialis) and deep (flexor pollicis longus, flexor digitorum profundus)—which originate on the humerus and insert on hand structures; and the posterior compartment muscles—superficial (extensor carpi radialis longus, extensor carpi radialis brevis, extensor digitorum, extensor digiti minimi, extensor carpi ulnaris) and deep (abductor pollicis longus, extensor pollicis brevis, extensor pollicis longus, extensor indicis)—which originate on the humerus, radius, and ulna. The flexor digitorum superficialis flexes the hand and digits at the knuckles; poor ergonomics can irritate its tendons in the carpal tunnel and pinch the median nerve, causing Carpal Tunnel Syndrome. Retinacula (flexor retinaculum on the palmar surface; extensor retinaculum on the dorsal surface) sheath the forearm muscle tendons at the wrist. The intrinsic muscles include thenar muscles (abductor pollicis brevis, opponens pollicis, flexor pollicis brevis, adductor pollicis) on the radial palm forming the thenar eminence and acting on the thumb; hypothenar muscles (abductor digiti minimi, flexor digiti minimi brevis, opponens digiti minimi) on the medial palm forming the hypothenar eminence and acting on', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (275, 99, 'Muscles That Position the Pectoral Girdle', 'Muscles That Position the Pectoral Girdle', 'This subsection introduces muscles that position the pectoral girdle, divided into anterior thorax muscles (subclavius, pectoralis minor, serratus anterior) and posterior thorax muscles (trapezius, rhomboid major, rhomboid minor). The subclavius originates from the first rib, inserts on the inferior surface of the clavicle, and stabilizes the clavicle by depressing it. The pectoralis minor originates from ribs 2–4 or 3–5, inserts on the coracoid process of the scapula, and causes scapula depression and rib elevation while rotating the shoulder anteriorly and assisting with inhalation. The serratus anterior originates from ribs 1–8 or 1–9, inserts on the anterior surface of the vertebral border of the scapula, and causes scapula protraction and rib elevation. The trapezius originates from the skull and vertebral column, inserts on the acromion, spine of scapula, and clavicle, and causes scapula rotation inferiorly, retraction, elevation, depression, and spine extension. The rhomboid major and rhomboid minor both originate from thoracic and cervical vertebrae, insert on the medial border of the scapula, stabilize the scapula during pectoral girdle movement, and cause scapula retraction and inferior rotation; when contracted, the rhomboids move the scapula medially to pull the shoulder and upper limb posteriorly.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (276, 100, 'Functional Divisions of the Nervous System', 'Functional Divisions of the Nervous System', 'This subsection introduces the functional divisions of the nervous system, which differ from anatomical divisions (CNS/PNS) because the same structure can serve multiple functions—exemplified by the optic nerve, which carries signals to the cerebral cortex for conscious visual perception and to the hypothalamus for reflexive smooth muscle responses. The three basic nervous system functions are sensation (receiving environmental stimuli including the "big five" senses: taste, smell, touch, sight, and hearing, plus internal stimuli), integration (combining sensory perceptions with memories, learning, and emotion in integration or association areas), and response (motor responses involving skeletal, cardiac, and smooth muscle contraction, plus glandular activation). The somatic nervous system (SNS) governs conscious perception and voluntary motor responses (skeletal muscle contraction), including reflexes and procedurally learned automatic responses, while the autonomic nervous system (ANS) controls involuntary responses of smooth and cardiac muscle and glandular tissue for homeostasis regulation. The enteric nervous system (ENS), a large part of the PNS independent of the CNS, controls smooth muscle and glandular tissue in the digestive system and is sometimes considered part of autonomic control related to gastrointestinal functions. Search terms: sensation, stimulus, integration, motor response, somatic nervous system, autonomic nervous system, enteric nervous system, skeletal muscle, smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, glands, homeostasis, reflex, procedural memory.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (277, 100, 'The Central and Peripheral Nervous Systems', 'The Central and Peripheral Nervous Systems', '# Summary

This subsection introduces the major anatomical divisions and structural components of the nervous system: the central nervous system (CNS)—comprising the brain and spinal cord contained within the cranial cavity and vertebral cavity—and the peripheral nervous system (PNS)—all nervous tissue beyond these cavities. Nervous tissue contains two cell types: neurons (functionally responsible for communication) and glial cells (providing structural support). Neurons consist of a soma (cell body), an axon (the fiber connecting a neuron to its target), and dendrites (processes receiving input from other neurons). Nervous tissue regions are classified as gray matter (containing neuron cell bodies and dendrites) or white matter (composed of axons insulated by myelin, a lipid-rich substance). Terminology for neuron clusters and axon bundles differs by location: in the CNS, a localized collection of neuron cell bodies is called a nucleus and a bundle of axons is called a tract; in the PNS, these structures are called a ganglion and a nerve, respectively (exemplified by the optic nerve in the PNS becoming the optic tract in the CNS at the optic chiasm). MRI imaging can distinguish gray matter from white matter because myelin in white matter appears fatty and differs in appearance from non-fatty gray matter.

**Search terms:** central nervous system (CNS), peripheral nervous system (PNS), brain, spinal cord, neurons, glial cells, soma, axon, dendrite, gray matter, white matter, myelin, nucleus, ganglion, tract, nerve, optic nerve, optic tract, optic chiasm, basal ganglia, basal nuclei, MRI.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (278, 101, 'Graded Potentials', 'Graded Potentials', '# Summary: Graded Potentials

Graded potentials are local changes in membrane potential associated with dendrites, determined in magnitude by stimulus size and classified as depolarizing or hyperpolarizing. Depolarizing graded potentials result from Na+ or Ca2+ entering the cell, while hyperpolarizing graded potentials result from K+ leaving or Cl− entering the cell. Three functional types are distinguished: generator potentials (in sensory neuron dendrites influencing axon action potentials), receptor potentials (in sensory receptor cells causing neurotransmitter release), and postsynaptic potentials (PSPs)—further subdivided into excitatory postsynaptic potentials (EPSPs, depolarizing toward threshold) and inhibitory postsynaptic potentials (IPSPs, hyperpolarizing away from threshold). Graded potentials summate (combine) at the initial segment (in sensory neurons) or axon hillock (in other neurons), where high-density voltage-gated Na+ channels initiate action potentials; summation can be spatial (multiple inputs at different locations) or temporal (multiple signals separated in time). Chemical synapses and electrical synapses represent two connection types; chemical synapses involve neurotransmitter release from presynaptic elements across the synaptic cleft to postsynaptic receptors, exemplified by the neuromuscular junction (NMJ) with its characteristic components: motor neuron axon terminals, acetylcholine, synaptic cleft, nicotinic acetylcholine receptors, sarcolemma, and acetylcholinesterase elimination.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (279, 102, 'Glial Cells', 'Glial Cells', 'Glial cells (neuroglia or glia) are supporting cells in nervous tissue that assist neurons in communication; six types exist—four in the CNS (astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, microglia, ependymal cells) and two in the PNS (satellite cells, Schwann cells). Astrocytes are star-shaped CNS cells with processes extending to neurons, blood vessels, and the pia mater; they maintain extracellular chemical concentration, remove excess signaling molecules, react to tissue damage, and contribute to the blood-brain barrier (BBB), which restricts passage of most substances from circulating blood into the CNS while allowing glucose, amino acids, water, gases, and ions to cross via active transport. Oligodendrocytes insulate multiple axon segments in the CNS with myelin, while microglia (possibly derived from macrophages) perform immune surveillance and phagocytosis as CNS-resident macrophages. Ependymal cells line the ventricles and filter blood at the choroid plexus to produce cerebrospinal fluid (CSF), functioning as a component of or breakdown point in the BBB. In the PNS, satellite cells in sensory and autonomic ganglia provide support similar to astrocytes, while Schwann cells wrap around single axon segments to provide myelin insulation; myelin is a lipid-rich sheath composed of glial cell membrane phospholipids and integral proteins that facilitates electrical signal transmission along axons.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (280, 103, 'Electrically Active Cell Membranes', 'Electrically Active Cell Membranes', 'This subsection introduces the structural and functional basis of electrically active cell membranes, focusing on how ions are regulated across the phospholipid bilayer to generate transmembrane potentials and action potentials. Key structures include the cell membrane (phospholipid bilayer), ion channels (pores selective for specific charged particles), the sodium/potassium pump (an ATPase carrier protein that moves Na+ out and K+ into cells against concentration gradients), and various gated channel types. The textbook establishes that charged particles cannot cross the hydrophobic core unaided and require passive transport channels or active transport pumps; the sodium/potassium pump maintains concentration gradients by using ATP energy to move ions against their gradients. Ion channel selectivity operates through two mechanisms: electrochemical exclusion (charge-specific interaction between hydrophilic amino acids and ions) and size exclusion (pore diameter specificity based on ion diameter when dissociated from water). Gated channels are categorized by their opening mechanisms: ligand-gated channels (opened by neurotransmitter binding, also called ionotropic receptors), mechanically gated channels (opened by physical membrane distortion, associated with somatosensation), voltage-gated channels (responding to changes in membrane electrical properties), and leakage channels (randomly gated, contributing to resting transmembrane voltage). Search terms: cell membrane, phospholipid bilayer, ion channels, sodium/potassium pump, ATPase, Na+, K+, transmembrane potential, action potential, electrochemical exclusion, size exclusion, nonspecific channel, ligand-gated channel, ionotropic receptor, neurotransmitter, mechanically gated channel, voltage-gated channel, leakage channel, resting transmembrane voltage.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (281, 103, 'The Membrane Potential', 'The Membrane Potential', '# Summary: The Membrane Potential

This subsection introduces the **membrane potential**—a distribution of electrical charge across the cell membrane measured in millivolts (mV), with the intracellular side compared to the extracellular side (set as zero reference). The **resting membrane potential** is -70 mV, maintained by ion concentration gradients: Na+ is 10 times more concentrated outside the cell than inside, while K+ is more concentrated inside; the cytosol contains high concentrations of anions (phosphate ions and negatively charged proteins). **Leakage channels** allow Na+ to slowly enter and K+ to slowly exit, while the **Na+/K+ pump** restores these ions, creating a dynamic steady state. When a **Na+ channel opens** (ligand-gated, mechanically-gated, or voltage-gated), Na+ rushes in driven by its concentration gradient, causing **depolarization** (membrane potential moves toward zero, reaching +30 mV); this triggers **voltage-gated K+ channels** to open at -50 mV, allowing K+ to exit and causing **repolarization** back toward -70 mV, with an overshoot into **hyperpolarization** (-70 mV to below). The **threshold** is -55 mV; depolarization reaching this voltage opens voltage-gated Na+ channels and initiates an action potential following an "all or none" principle. The **voltage-gated Na+ channel** contains an **activation gate** (opens at -55 mV) and **inactivation gate** (closes after milliseconds), while the **voltage-gated K+ channel** has a single gate (sensitive to -50 mV, slower to open). The **absolute refractory period** occurs when the Na+ channel inactivation gate is closed; the **relative refractory period** follows during', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (282, 104, 'Causes of Neurological Deficits', 'Causes of Neurological Deficits', 'This subsection introduces major causes of neurological deficits affecting the brain and spinal cord, including circulatory, infectious, traumatic, degenerative, and developmental etiologies. Acute circulatory damage occurs through stroke (cerebrovascular accident/CVA), which has two types: ischemic stroke (caused by blocked or narrowed vessels, embolus, blood clot, fat deposit, or hypovolemia) and hemorrhagic stroke (bleeding from damaged blood vessels causing physical pressure, edema, and compromised blood flow). Transient ischemic attack (TIA) differs from stroke by resolving within 24 hours; stroke effects persist at least 24 hours. Widespread damage results from infectious diseases (involving astrocytes and microglia), blunt force trauma, and neurodegenerative diseases (Alzheimer''s disease, Parkinson''s disease, Huntington''s disease, amyotrophic lateral sclerosis/ALS, Creutzfeldt–Jacob disease, multiple sclerosis/MS)—where neurons die in Alzheimer''s, Parkinson''s, and ALS, while myelin is affected in MS. Developmental disorders arise from genetic and environmental factors disrupting CNS development; the textbook illustrates how stroke-induced cell death in the fusiform gyrus causes prosopagnosia, whereas autism spectrum disorder (ASD) presents similar deficits without visible fusiform gyrus damage, suggesting improper connectivity. The neurological exam helps differentiate acute events (stroke) from chronic or global conditions (trauma) and clarifies whether deficits reflect discrete or widespread neurological loss.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (283, 104, 'Neuroanatomy and the Neurological Exam', 'Neuroanatomy and the Neurological Exam', 'This subsection introduces the neurological exam as a clinical tool based on the principle of localization of function—the concept that circumscribed CNS locations are responsible for specific functions. The five major sections of the neurological exam are: (1) mental status exam, which assesses cerebrum and cerebral cortex functions including language localized to the left hemisphere between the superior temporal lobe and posterior frontal lobe with connections through the inferior parietal lobe; (2) cranial nerve exam, which tests nerves connecting to the diencephalon and brain stem; (3) coordination exam and gait exam, which assess cerebellum function; and (4) motor and sensory exams, which are associated with the spinal cord and spinal nerves. The textbook explicitly states that deficits uncovered by mental status examination usually point to damage to the left cerebral cortex, and that sensory deficits suggest problems with sensory systems between the spinal cord and brain. Students would search for: localization of function, mental status exam, cranial nerve exam, coordination exam, gait exam, motor exam, sensory exam, cerebrum, cerebral cortex, language function, left hemisphere, superior temporal lobe, posterior frontal lobe, inferior parietal lobe, diencephalon, brain stem, cerebellum, spinal cord, and spinal nerves.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (284, 105, 'Coordination and Alternating Movement', 'Coordination and Alternating Movement', 'The coordination exam assesses cerebellar function through subtests targeting appendicular and axial musculature, requiring normal motor control from the cerebrum and sensory input from somatic, visual, and vestibular systems. Key structures and processes include: tremor check (arm extension), rebound response (cerebellum comparing cerebral motor command with proprioceptive feedback; red nucleus sending signals to lower motor neurons; cerebellar input preventing continued contraction), and check reflex (elbow flexion against resistance). Alternating movement tests include finger-to-nose test (switching between biceps and triceps brachii; motor cortex communicating with cerebellum through pons; thalamic feedback; visual cortex processing in cerebrocerebellum), rapid alternating movements of upper extremities (finger-to-thumb, hand-patting), lower extremities (heel-to-shin sliding), and speech (repeating "lah-kah-pah" to alternate tongue, lips, and palate movements). The cerebrocerebellum (lateral cerebellum) receives input through the cortico-ponto-cerebellar pathway and plans movement commands; the vestibulocerebellum (flocculonodular lobe) receives vestibular projections.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (285, 105, 'Location and Connections of the Cerebellum', 'Location and Connections of the Cerebellum', 'The cerebellum, located on the dorsal surface of the brain stem centered on the pons, connects to the brain stem through three white matter bundles: the middle cerebellar peduncle (MCP) connects to the pons, the superior cerebellar peduncle (SCP) connects to the midbrain and forebrain, and the inferior cerebellar peduncle (ICP) connects to the medulla. The MCP is part of the cortico-ponto-cerebellar pathway that carries motor commands from the precentral gyrus and visual cortex input to the lateral cerebellar regions, while the ICP conveys sensory input including proprioception from the spinal cord and vestibular sensations from the inner ear. The SCP is the major cerebellar output, projecting to the red nucleus and thalamus, which return processing to the motor cortex and send corrective motor commands to the spinal cord via the rubrospinal tract. The cerebellum is functionally divided into three regions: the vermis (spinocerebellum) and flocculonodular lobe (vestibulocerebellum) in the midline coordinate balance and gait through axial musculature control, while the lateral hemispheres (cerebrocerebellum) plan motor functions targeting appendicular musculature. Search terms: cerebellum, pons, middle cerebellar peduncle (MCP), superior cerebellar peduncle (SCP), inferior cerebellar peduncle (ICP), cortico-ponto-cerebellar pathway, vermis, flocculonodular lobe, spinocerebellum, vestibulocerebellum, cerebrocerebellum, red nucleus, rubrospinal tract, motor coordination, gait.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (286, 105, 'Posture and Gait', 'Posture and Gait', '# Summary: Posture and Gait

This subsection covers clinical assessment of posture and gait as components of the neurological exam that test spinocerebellum and vestibulocerebellum function. Key examination subtests include: station (patient standing with eyes open to assess foot placement and balance; patient hops on one foot), which differs from the Romberg test (eyes closed, assesses proprioceptive deficits); normal walking (observing foot placement and arm movement); tandem gait (heel-to-toe walking); and walking on heels or toes only. Ataxia—a cerebellar movement disorder presenting as loss of coordination in voluntary movements—can result from cerebellar damage, sensory deficits (proprioception, equilibrium), or vestibular dysfunction, and may be caused by exogenous substances (alcohol, ketamine, mercury), focal lesions (strokes of cerebellar arteries, tumors, trauma, MS), or genetic disorders (cerebellar degeneration, spinal cord degeneration, brain malformation, Wilson''s disease). Abnormal findings include wide stance (feet placed far apart), which indicates impaired cerebellar function because the cerebellum coordinates proprioceptive, vestibular, and visual information for balance and motor control; widened stance compensates by increasing base of support.

**Search terms:** station subtest, Romberg test, tandem gait, ataxia, cerebellar damage, spinocerebellum, vestibulocerebellum, proprioceptive deficits, wide stance, cerebellar arteries, Wilson''s disease', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (287, 106, 'Gaze Control', 'Gaze Control', 'This subsection covers the neural control of eye movements through three cranial nerves—the oculomotor (CN III), trochlear (CN IV), and abducens (CN VI) nerves—which innervate the extraocular muscles: lateral rectus, medial rectus, superior rectus, inferior rectus, and superior oblique. The abducens nucleus (superior medulla) controls the lateral rectus; the oculomotor nucleus (midbrain) controls the medial rectus and other extraocular muscles; and the trochlear nerve controls the superior oblique for intorsion. Conjugate gaze (coordinated eye movement in the same direction) is mediated by the medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF), which connects the superior colliculus, paramedian pontine reticular formation (PPRF), and the oculomotor, trochlear, and abducens nuclei to coordinate saccades and horizontal/vertical eye movements. The subsection describes convergence (nonconjugate adduction of both eyes) and accommodation (lens shape change via parasympathetic oculomotor fibers), collectively termed the accommodation–convergence reflex, as well as the vestibulo-ocular reflex (VOR), which uses semicircular canal input to stabilize the visual field during head rotation by coordinating lateral and medial rectus muscle contraction through abducens and oculomotor nuclei. Clinical testing involves tracking a pen through the visual field to assess conjugate movements and detect internuclear ophthalmoplegia (failure of conjugate gaze causing diplopia) or VOR deficits from vestibular or brain stem damage.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (288, 106, 'Motor Nerves of the Neck', 'Motor Nerves of the Neck', 'The accessory nerve (spinal accessory nerve) innervates the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles, which are key structures for head and neck positioning. Bilateral sternocleidomastoid contraction flexes the head forward; unilateral contraction causes rotation to the opposite side, while the trapezius acts as an antagonist to produce neck extension and hyperextension. Both muscles also receive input from cervical spinal nerves: the sternocleidomastoid receives primarily sensory projections from spinal nerves, whereas the trapezius receives motor input from the spinal cord and has lateral insertions to the clavicle and scapula. Clinical testing includes neck flexion and extension against resistance, shoulder shrugging to assess scapula and clavicle elevation, and lateral neck flexion toward the shoulder, with asymmetry suggesting damage on the weaker side. Accessory nerve deficits may affect head orientation. Search terms: accessory nerve, spinal accessory nerve, sternocleidomastoid, trapezius, cervical spinal nerves, motor exam, neck flexion, neck extension, shoulder shrug, scapula elevation.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (289, 106, 'Nerves of the Face and Oral Cavity', 'Nerves of the Face and Oral Cavity', 'This subsection covers clinical examination of cranial nerves associated with the oral cavity and face, including the facial nerve (CN VII), glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX), vagus nerve (CN X), and hypoglossal nerve (CN XII). The facial and glossopharyngeal nerves convey gustatory stimulation and are tested by applying salty, sour, bitter, or sweet stimuli to the tongue; the glossopharyngeal nerve also relays general sensations from the pharyngeal walls and contributes to the gag reflex, whose motor response is mediated through the vagus nerve. The vagus nerve directly stimulates contraction of skeletal muscles in the pharynx and larynx for swallowing and speech, tested by having the patient repeat consonant sounds like "lah-kah-pah" while observing soft palate movement. The facial and glossopharyngeal nerves initiate salivation through parasympathetic fibers: facial nerve fibers synapse in the pterygopalatine ganglion (projecting to submandibular and sublingual glands) and glossopharyngeal fibers synapse in the otic ganglion (projecting to the parotid gland). The hypoglossal nerve controls tongue muscles (except the palatoglossus, controlled by the vagus nerve) and is tested by the "stick out your tongue" exam; the genioglossus muscle is responsible for tongue protrusion, and unilateral nerve deficits cause the tongue to deviate toward the affected side. The facial nerve controls muscles of facial expression and is tested through strength testing, including having the patient squeeze eyes shut against examiner resistance.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (290, 106, 'Sensory Nerves', 'Sensory Nerves', 'This subsection covers examination of the sensory cranial nerves (CN I, II, VIII, V, VII, IX) and their associated pathways. Key structures include the olfactory epithelium and olfactory bulb (CN I); optic nerve, optic chiasm, optic canals, medial retinae, orbit, and diencephalon (CN II); vestibulocochlear nerve, inner ear, bony labyrinth, vestibule, utricle, saccule, semicircular canals, cochlea, internal auditory meatus, and medulla nuclei (CN VIII); and trigeminal system nuclei—mesencephalic nucleus, chief nucleus (pons), and spinal trigeminal nucleus (medulla)—which relay information to the thalamus and postcentral gyrus. Testing procedures include olfactory discrimination (coffee, mint), visual acuity via Snellen chart, visual field assessment, ophthalmoscopic inspection of the optic disk, equilibrium testing via Romberg test and vestibulo-ocular reflex, hearing via Rinne test and Weber test using tuning forks, and trigeminal sensory discrimination using a cotton-tipped applicator. The textbook explicitly states that the chief nucleus relays light touch and mandibular proprioception analogously to the dorsal column pathway, the spinal trigeminal nucleus relays crude touch, pain, and temperature analogously to the spinothalamic pathway, conductive hearing depends on ossicle vibration while sensorineural hearing depends on inner ear and cranial nerve transmission, and damage to the spinal trigeminal nucleus may indicate concurrent medullary involvement affecting cardiovascular, respiratory, and digestive regulation.', 4);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (291, 107, 'Cognitive Abilities', 'Cognitive Abilities', '# Summary: Cognitive Abilities Assessment in the Mental Status Exam

The mental status exam assesses four groups of cognitive abilities: orientation and memory, language and speech, sensorium, and judgment and abstract reasoning. Orientation tests awareness of time (date), place (location), and person (identity) through questions like "Do you know what the date is?" and "What is your name?"; memory is assessed via the three-word recall test, reciting months in reverse order, spelling words backwards, and reciting number sequences. Memory is largely a function of the temporal lobe, hippocampus, and amygdala; the case of patient Henry Molaison (HM) demonstrated that the hippocampus and medial temporal lobe structures consolidate short-term memories (localized to the prefrontal lobe) into long-term episodic memory (stored in multimodal integration areas of the cerebral cortex), as HM developed anterograde amnesia after bilateral medial temporal lobe removal but retained short-term and procedural memory. Language assessment includes comprehension subtests (following verbal instructions) and fluency subtests (describing images, reciting sentences); Broca''s area (lateral frontal lobe, anterior to motor cortex for head/neck) produces expressive aphasia (non-fluent speech production), Wernicke''s area (end of lateral sulcus, anterior to visual cortex) produces receptive aphasia (loss of language comprehension), and white matter tracts connecting these regions mediate conduction aphasia. The sensorium encompasses conscious sensory perception through primary and association cortical areas; praxis tests verbal-to-motor transformation, and gnosis includes stereognosis (object identification via somatosensory manipulation) and graphesthesia (recognizing written symbols on skin). Judgment and abstract reasoning, localized to the prefrontal cortex (anterior to motor association areas), are assessed through problem-solving questions', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (292, 107, 'Functions of the Cerebral Cortex', 'Functions of the Cerebral Cortex', 'The cerebral cortex, a thin layer of gray matter approximately 2.55 mm thick on the cerebrum''s surface, is the seat of higher mental functions including memory, learning, language, and conscious perception that are assessed in the mental status exam. Korbinian Brodmann''s cytoarchitectural study identified approximately 50 distinct cortical regions based on neuronal size, shape, and number; these Brodmann areas correlate strongly with functional localization, including primary cortical areas (Brodmann areas 1, 2, 3, 4, 17, and 22) and association areas. Primary areas receive sensory information from the thalamus for conscious perception or send descending motor commands to the brain stem and spinal cord; the primary somatosensory cortex (Brodmann areas 1–3 in the postcentral gyrus) processes submodalities of somatosensation (touch, vibration, pain, temperature, proprioception) organized by the sensory homunculus, while the primary motor cortex (area 4 in the precentral gyrus) and premotor cortex (area 6) control movement. Integrative areas located between sensory and motor domains integrate multisensory information; for example, the posterior parietal cortex, lying between the somatosensory and visual cortices, coordinates visual and motor functions such as reaching to grasp an object by integrating proprioceptive feedback and sensory information about object weight. **Search terms:** cerebral cortex, cerebrum, Brodmann areas, primary cortical areas, association areas, integrative areas, primary somatosensory cortex, sensory homunculus, primary motor cortex, premotor cortex, primary visual cortex, primary auditory cortex, posterior parietal cortex, thalamus, brain stem, spinal cord.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (293, 108, 'Muscle Strength and Voluntary Movement', 'Muscle Strength and Voluntary Movement', 'This subsection introduces the anatomical and functional basis of the motor exam, centered on the two-neuron motor pathway: the upper motor neuron (UMN) from the precentral gyrus of the frontal lobe via the corticospinal tract (which decussates in the medulla and travels through lateral or anterior spinal cord columns) and the lower motor neuron (LMN) in the ventral horn that projects to skeletal muscle. The motor exam assesses muscle structure through inspection and palpation, evaluates muscle tone (hypotonicity or flaccidity indicates LMN dysfunction) through passive range of motion at the elbow, wrist, knee, and ankle, and tests muscle strength through resistance testing; lateral strength deficits indicate unilateral corticospinal tract lesions while global weakness suggests diffuse motor system pathology, with UMN lesions (cerebral palsy, MS, stroke) identified by positive pronator drift. Reflex testing—including deep tendon reflexes (stretch reflexes like the knee-jerk reflex and ankle reflex) and superficial reflexes (plantar reflex/Babinski sign, abdominal reflexes)—evaluates LMN function; the muscle spindle sends a signal through the dorsal root that synapses directly on the ventral horn motor neuron, causing muscle contraction, and the descending corticospinal tract modifies the plantar reflex response. Search terms: precentral gyrus, primary motor cortex, corticospinal tract, decussate, medulla, spinal cord white matter, ventral horn, upper motor neuron (UMN), lower motor neuron (LMN), muscle tone, hypotonicity, flaccidity, pronator drift, deep tendon reflex, stretch reflex, knee-jerk reflex, superficial reflex, plantar reflex, Babinski sign, muscle spindle', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (294, 108, 'Sensory Modalities and Location', 'Sensory Modalities and Location', 'This subsection covers the sensory examination component of the neurological exam, focusing on testing somatic senses—those consciously perceived—through dermatomes that connect to the postcentral gyrus. Key anatomical structures and concepts include: dermatomes (topographically organized regions innervated by spinal nerves, exemplified by the eighth cervical nerve innervating the medial forearm and fingers), the dorsal column pathway (conveying fine touch, vibration, and proprioception; ascending ipsilateral and decussating in the medulla), and the spinothalamic pathway (conveying pain and temperature; decussating at level of entry and ascending contralaterally). Testing modalities include light touch (cotton-tipped applicator), pain perception (broken applicator end), vibration (tuning fork on bone prominences like the distal ulna), tactile movement (stimulus drawn 2–3 cm across skin), and proprioception (passive finger/toe movement). Discrimination subtests include two-point discrimination (using forceps or calipers to assess receptive field density, with failure indicating dorsal column deficit), double simultaneous stimulation (bilateral stimuli detecting contralateral posterior parietal lobe damage), and the Romberg test (assessing dorsal column proprioception and spinocerebellar tract function via balance with eyes closed). Search terms: dermatomes, postcentral gyrus, spinal nerves, dorsal column pathway, spinothalamic pathway, light touch, pain perception, vibration, proprioception, two-point discrimination, double simultaneous stimulation, Romberg test, receptive fields, posterior parietal lobe.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (295, 109, 'External Female Genitals', 'External Female Genitals', 'This subsection introduces the external female genitalia (vulva) and internal reproductive structures, including the mons pubis, labia majora, labia minora, clitoris, hymen, vagina, and ovaries. Key anatomical relationships include: the labia minora encircle the clitoris and protect the urethra and reproductive tract entrance; the clitoris originates from the same cells as the glans penis; the hymen partially covers the vaginal opening; Bartholin''s glands and lesser vestibular glands (located near the clitoris) secrete mucus; the vagina is a muscular canal lined with fibrous adventitia, smooth muscle, and mucous membrane with rugae that allow expansion; the fornix (superior vagina) meets the uterine cervix; and the ovaries are supported by the mesovarium (connected to the broad ligament), suspensory ligament (containing ovarian blood and lymph vessels), and ovarian ligament (attached to the uterus). The subsection also describes ovarian internal anatomy: the ovarian surface epithelium, tunica albuginea, cortex (containing ovarian stroma where oocytes develop within follicles), and medulla (containing blood vessels, lymph vessels, and nerves). Additionally, it explains that Lactobacillus bacteria maintain vaginal pH below 4.5 to protect against pathogens, and that douching disrupts this protective microbial flora.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (296, 109, 'Hormonal Birth Control', 'Hormonal Birth Control', 'Birth control pills prevent pregnancy by exploiting the negative feedback system of the ovarian and menstrual cycles through constant levels of estrogen and progesterone, which inhibit FSH and LH release from the hypothalamus and pituitary. Without FSH, follicles fail to mature; without the LH surge, ovulation does not occur. The estrogen in birth control pills causes reduced endometrial thickening compared to normal cycles, making implantation less likely. Standard formulations contain 21 active hormone-containing pills and 7 inactive (placebo) pills; the hormone decline during placebo week triggers lighter menses due to reduced endometrial development. Newer continuous-dose formulations deliver low-dose estrogen and progesterone for 365 days, preventing menses entirely with no reported adverse health effects. Skipping pills or taking them late can disrupt negative feedback, allowing FSH and LH to increase and potentially trigger ovulation, so consistent adherence to pill schedules is critical for contraceptive efficacy.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (297, 109, 'The Breasts', 'The Breasts', '# Summary: The Breasts

The breasts are accessory organs of the female reproductive system whose primary function is lactation. Key anatomical structures include the nipple, areola (25–100 mm diameter), areolar glands, mammary glands (modified sweat glands), 15–20 lactiferous ducts, lactiferous sinuses, glandular lobes, alveoli (milk-secreting cell clusters), myoepithelial cells, fat tissue, and suspensory ligaments (connective tissue connecting breast tissue to skin dermis). Milk produced in the alveoli is pushed by contracting myoepithelial cells to the lactiferous sinuses, then exits through lactiferous ducts via the nipple during suckling. Breast tissue responds to estrogen and progesterone fluctuations during the menstrual cycle, causing swelling and tenderness especially in the secretory phase; pregnancy hormones further develop mammary tissue and enlarge the breasts. Breast size is determined by surrounding fat tissue and does not affect milk production capacity. Search terms: breasts, nipple, areola, areolar glands, mammary glands, lactiferous ducts, lactiferous sinuses, alveoli, myoepithelial cells, suspensory ligaments, lactation, estrogen, progesterone.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (298, 109, 'The Menstrual Cycle', 'The Menstrual Cycle', 'The menstrual cycle is a series of changes in which the uterine lining is shed, rebuilds, and prepares for implantation, consisting of three phases: menses phase, proliferative phase, and secretory phase. The cycle averages 28 days (range 21–32 days) and is timed from the first day of menses; hormones produced by granulosa and theca cells of the ovary control all three menstrual phases. The menses phase (2–7 days) occurs during the early follicular phase when progesterone, FSH, and LH levels are low; declining progesterone from corpus luteum degradation triggers shedding of the stratum functionalis of the endometrium. The proliferative phase begins when menstrual flow ceases and granulosa and theca cells of tertiary follicles produce increased estrogen, which stimulates endometrial rebuilding; high estrogen causes negative feedback decreasing FSH (resulting in atresia of all but one tertiary follicle), then positive feedback stimulating the LH surge that triggers ovulation on day 14, and also increases uterine tube contractions and decreases vaginal acidity. The secretory phase begins after ovulation when luteinization of granulosa cells forms the corpus luteum, which produces progesterone; progesterone causes endometrial glands to secrete glycogen-rich fluid and spiral arteries to develop in the thickened stratum functionalis to prepare for implantation. If no pregnancy occurs within 10–12 days, the corpus luteum degrades to corpus albicans, estrogen and progesterone levels fall, prostaglandins cause spiral artery constriction reducing oxygen supply, endometrial tissue dies, and menses begins the next cycle.', 4);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (299, 109, 'The Ovarian Cycle', 'The Ovarian Cycle', '# Summary: The Ovarian Cycle

The ovarian cycle is a roughly 28-day cycle of predictable changes in oocytes and ovarian follicles that encompasses two interrelated processes: oogenesis (production of gametes) and folliculogenesis (growth and development of ovarian follicles). Oogenesis begins with oogonia (ovarian stem cells formed during fetal development) that divide via mitosis and form primary oocytes in the fetal ovary prior to birth; primary oocytes arrest in meiosis I and resume meiosis at puberty, with numbers declining from one to two million in infants to approximately 400,000 at puberty to zero by the end of menopause. A surge of luteinizing hormone triggers the resumption of meiosis in a primary oocyte, initiating the transition to a secondary oocyte; unequal cytoplasmic division produces the larger secondary oocyte (which leaves the ovary during ovulation) and the smaller first polar body (which disintegrates). The secondary oocyte completes meiosis II only if penetrated by sperm, producing one haploid ovum that becomes a diploid zygote upon fertilization; the ovum represents a brief transitional haploid stage between the diploid oocyte and diploid zygote. The female gamete''s abundant cytoplasm supplies nutrients to the developing zygote between fertilization and implantation, while sperm contribute only DNA; consequently, all cytoplasmic organelles in the developing embryo are egg-derived, and mitochondrial DNA is maternally inherited.

**Search terms:** ovarian cycle, oogenesis, folliculogenesis, oogonia, primary oocyte, secondary oocyte, ovulation, luteinizing hormone, meiosis I, meiosis II, first polar body, ovum, zygote, menopause,', 5);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (300, 109, 'The Uterine Tubes', 'The Uterine Tubes', 'The uterine tubes (fallopian tubes, oviducts) transport the oocyte from the ovary to the uterus and consist of three anatomical regions: the isthmus (narrow medial end connected to the uterus), the ampulla (middle region where fertilization typically occurs), and the infundibulum (wide distal end with finger-like fimbriae). The tube wall contains three layers—outer serosa, middle smooth muscle layer, and inner mucosal layer with ciliated cells and mucus-secreting cells. Oocyte transport depends on two coordinated mechanisms: high estrogen concentrations around ovulation induce smooth muscle contractions (every 4–8 seconds) that sweep the ovary and pelvic cavity, while ciliated cells beat toward the uterus to generate current that pulls the oocyte–granulosa cell complex into and through the tube. Fertilization occurs in the ampulla; if successful, the zygote divides during transit and implants in the uterus; if unfertilized, the oocyte degrades in the tube or uterus. The open-ended structure of uterine tubes permits bacterial ascension from the vagina through the uterus into the tubes and pelvic cavity, risking sepsis; sexually transmitted infections (gonorrhea, chlamydia) increase risk for pelvic inflammatory disease (PID), which even when resolved can cause scar tissue formation leading to infertility. **Search terms:** uterine tubes, fallopian tubes, oviducts, isthmus, ampulla, infundibulum, fimbriae, serosa, smooth muscle layer, mucosal layer, ciliated cells, secondary oocyte, granulosa cells, estrogen, fertilization, zygote, pelvic inflammatory disease, PID, gonorrhea, chlamydia, sepsis.', 6);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (301, 109, 'The Uterus and Cervix', 'The Uterus and Cervix', '# Summary: The Uterus and Cervix

The uterus is a muscular organ (approximately 5 cm wide by 7 cm long when not pregnant) composed of three layers: the perimetrium (serous membrane covering the exterior), the myometrium (thick smooth muscle layer responsible for contractions), and the endometrium (innermost layer). The uterus is divided into the fundus (superior portion above the uterine tubes), the body of uterus or corpus (middle section), and the cervix (narrow inferior portion projecting into the vagina); the cervix produces estrogen-responsive mucus secretions that facilitate sperm movement. The broad ligament, round ligament, and uterosacral ligament maintain uterine position within the abdominopelvic cavity. The endometrium consists of two layers—the stratum basalis (adjacent to myometrium, does not shed during menses) and the stratum functionalis (thickens in response to estrogen and progesterone, contains spiral arteries supplied by the uterine artery, and sheds during menstruation if fertilization does not occur). During the follicular phase, tertiary follicles secrete estrogen causing stratum functionalis thickening; during the luteal phase, progesterone from the corpus luteum maintains the thickened stratum functionalis for implantation; if no embryo implants, corpus luteum degradation ceases progesterone production, prostaglandins cause spiral arteries to constrict and rupture, and the stratum functionalis sheds during menses.

**Search terms:** uterus, cervix, fundus, body of uterus, corpus, perimetrium, myometrium, endometrium, stratum basalis, stratum functionalis, broad ligament, round ligament, uterosacral ligament, spiral arteries, menarche, men', 7);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (302, 110, 'Sperm Transport', 'Sperm Transport', 'This subsection describes the pathway and mechanisms of sperm transport from the testes to ejaculation, introducing key anatomical structures: seminiferous tubules, epididymis (head and tail), ductus deferens (vas deferens), spermatic cord, ampulla, ejaculatory duct, prostate gland, prostatic urethra, seminal vesicles, and bulbourethral glands (Cowper''s glands). Sperm move from seminiferous tubules through the epididymis (taking ~12 days) via smooth muscle contraction, where they mature and acquire motility; during ejaculation, smooth muscle contraction pushes sperm from the epididymis tail into the ductus deferens, which extends through the inguinal canal and pelvic cavity to the ampulla posterior to the bladder. The three accessory glands contribute to semen composition: seminal vesicles (60% of semen volume) add fructose for ATP generation; the prostate gland surrounds the prostatic urethra and secretes alkaline fluid that coagulates then decoagulates semen; bulbourethral glands release lubricating fluid (pre-ejaculate) that may contain sperm. The subsection also covers vasectomy (surgical interruption of the ductus deferens), benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) causing urethral constriction, and prostate cancer detection and characteristics.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (303, 110, 'Structure of Formed Sperm', 'Structure of Formed Sperm', 'Formed sperm (spermatozoa) consist of three distinctive regions—head, mid-piece, and tail—each with specialized structures and functions. The head contains a compact haploid nucleus with minimal cytoplasm and is capped by the acrosome, which contains lysosomal enzymes required for fertilization; the mid-piece is filled with tightly packed mitochondria that produce ATP; and the tail contains the flagellum, which extends from the neck and mid-piece and enables sperm motility. The flagellum''s central strand, the axial filament, is formed from one centriole during the final stages of spermatogenesis. Approximately 100 to 300 million sperm are produced daily, with each sperm cell being 85,000 times smaller in volume than the female gamete. After formation in the seminiferous tubule lumen, sperm are released and move along ducts toward the epididymis for further maturation. Search terms: sperm structure, head, mid-piece, tail, acrosome, lysosomal enzymes, mitochondria, flagellum, axial filament, centriole, spermatogenesis, spermatozoa, epididymis.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (304, 110, 'The Penis', 'The Penis', '# Summary: The Penis

The penis is the male organ of copulation composed of three erectile tissue chambers: two larger corpora cavernosa (which form the bulk of the penis) and the smaller corpus spongiosum (which surrounds the penile urethra). Key anatomical structures include the glans penis (highly innervated end that influences ejaculation likelihood), the prepuce or foreskin (which lubricates and protects the glans and contains dense nerve endings), and the penile shaft (which surrounds the urethra). Penile erection results from vasocongestion: nitric oxide (NO) released from nerve endings near blood vessels in the corpora cavernosa and spongiosum activates smooth muscle relaxation in penile arteries, causing vasodilation; this increased arterial blood flow fills the erectile chambers and compresses penile venules, preventing venous drainage and producing erection. Sexual arousal and REM sleep both induce erection; circumcision surgically removes the prepuce. Testosterone, a steroid hormone androgen produced by Leydig cells (interstitial cells located between seminiferous tubules), is secreted beginning at the seventh week of male embryonic development and reaches peak concentrations in the second trimester; testosterone is necessary for anatomical differentiation of male sexual organs, and its increase during puberty activates physical changes and initiates spermatogenesis.

**Search terms:** penis, shaft, urethra, corpus cavernosum, corpora cavernosa, corpus spongiosum, penile urethra, glans penis, prepuce, foreskin, circumcision, vasocongestion, nitric oxide, penile arteries, erectile chambers, penile venules, erection, sexual arousal, REM sleep, testosterone, androgen, Leydig cells, interstitial cells, seminiferous tubules, spermatogenesis, male', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (305, 111, 'Development of the Sexual Organs in the Embryo and Fetus', 'Development of the Sexual Organs in the Embryo and Fetus', 'This subsection explains mammalian sex determination and the embryonic development of male and female reproductive structures. Key anatomical structures include the Y chromosome, SRY (Sex-determining Region of the Y chromosome) gene, bipotential gonads, germ cells (spermatogonia and oogonia), Leydig cells, sustentacular cells, glans penis, glans clitoris, Müllerian duct, and Wolffian duct, along with internal structures including the uterus, uterine tubes, vagina, epididymis, ductus deferens, and seminal vesicles. The textbook establishes that the presence of the Y chromosome and SRY gene initiates male development; SRY recruits genes to develop testes and suppresses female development genes, while the absence of SRY results in female development with oogonia and primordial follicles forming in the primitive ovary. Testosterone secreted by Leydig cells influences bipotential tissues to develop male characteristics (e.g., glans penis formation), while without testosterone these tissues differentiate into female structures (e.g., clitoris). For internal reproductive structures, sustentacular cell secretions degrade the Müllerian duct in males while testosterone stimulates Wolffian duct growth; without sustentacular cell secretion and testosterone, the Müllerian duct develops and the Wolffian duct degrades, resulting in female development. Chromosomal intersex conditions (XXY, XYY, XO) can result from nondisjunction during meiosis.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (306, 111, 'Further Sexual Development Occurs at Puberty', 'Further Sexual Development Occurs at Puberty', 'Puberty is the stage of sexual maturation controlled by a concerted hormonal release from the hypothalamus (GnRH), anterior pituitary (LH and FSH), and gonads (testosterone or estrogen), which initiates maturation of the reproductive systems and development of secondary sex characteristics. Key structures and processes include: the hypothalamus and pituitary''s negative feedback sensitivity to sex steroid hormones (androgens and estrogens), the gonads'' increased responsiveness to gonadotropins (FSH and LH), spermatogenesis and folliculogenesis, menarche, and growth spurts occurring at different ages in males and females. In females, secondary sexual characteristics include breast development, deposition of fat in breasts and hips, broadening of the pelvis, and growth of axillary and pubic hair; in males, they include increased larynx size, deepening of voice, increased muscular development, and growth of facial, axillary, and pubic hair. Testosterone stimulates growth of the larynx and thickening and lengthening of the vocal folds, causing voice pitch to drop. Factors affecting puberty onset include genetics, environment, psychological stress, nutrition, and body fat (leptin secretion by adipose cells), with menarche historically decreasing from approximately 17 years in 1860 to 12.75 years by 1960. Search terms: puberty, sexual maturation, GnRH, LH, FSH, testosterone, estrogen, secondary sex characteristics, spermatogenesis, folliculogenesis, menarche, negative feedback, gonadotropins, growth spurt, leptin.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (307, 112, 'Fetal “Breathing”', 'Fetal “Breathing”', 'Fetal breathing movements, observable from 20–21 weeks of development, involve muscle contractions that inhale and exhale amniotic fluid, pulmonary surfactant, and mucus; these movements are non-continuous and influenced by maternal factors including hyperglycemia (which increases frequency), hypoglycemia (which decreases frequency), and tobacco use (which lowers rates). The textbook proposes that fetal breathing movements may tone respiratory muscles and promote alveoli formation and maturation, serving as a sign of robust fetal health. Prior to birth, the lungs are filled with amniotic fluid, mucus, and surfactant; during passage through the birth canal, thoracic cavity compression expels much of this fluid, with remaining fluid rapidly absorbed after birth. The first inhalation occurs within 10 seconds after birth and inflates the lungs; pulmonary surfactant is critical for this inflation because it reduces surface tension of the alveoli. Preterm birth around 26 weeks frequently results in severe respiratory distress because sufficient pulmonary surfactant has not been produced and gas exchange surfaces have not formed adequately; a full complement of functional alveoli does not appear until around 8 years of age. Search terms: fetal breathing movements, amniotic fluid, pulmonary surfactant, alveoli, hyperglycemia, hypoglycemia, tobacco use, birth canal, thoracic cavity compression, preterm birth, respiratory distress, surface tension.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (308, 112, 'Time Line', 'Time Line', '# Summary: Embryonic Development of the Respiratory System—Time Line

Respiratory system development begins at week 4 of gestation with ectodermal tissue from the anterior head region invaginating posteriorly to form olfactory pits, which fuse with endodermal tissue of the developing pharynx; olfactory pits enlarge to become the nasal cavity. The lung bud, a dome-shaped structure bulging from the foregut (endoderm inferior to pharyngeal pouches), extends longitudinally to form the laryngotracheal bud; the proximal portion becomes the trachea while the distal end forms bronchial buds, which branch progressively until all segmental bronchi form by week 16. By week 16, respiratory bronchioles form (marking completion of major airway structures), followed by vascularization, alveolar duct formation, and alveolar precursor development; type I and type II pneumocytes differentiate around week 19, with type II cells secreting pulmonary surfactant beginning around week 19. From week 24 to term, major growth and maturation occur: alveolar precursors develop further, pulmonary surfactant production increases (reaching adequate levels for lung compliance by month 8), pulmonary capillaries expand to create gas exchange surface area, and the respiratory membrane develops. The critical milestone occurs at week 28 when sufficient alveolar precursors mature to allow independent breathing in premature infants; however, alveoli continue developing into childhood, with a full complement of functional alveoli not appearing until approximately 8 years of age.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (309, 113, 'Gas Exchange', 'Gas Exchange', 'This subsection introduces gas exchange principles and mechanisms through key concepts: **pressure**, **partial pressure (Px)**, **atmospheric pressure**, **Dalton''s law**, and **Henry''s law**. Students searching for content on gas exchange will find information on **external respiration** (gas exchange in alveoli of lungs at the respiratory membrane) and **internal respiration** (gas exchange in tissues), both occurring via **simple diffusion** along **partial pressure gradients**. The textbook establishes that gases move from higher to lower partial pressure areas, with movement rate proportional to the pressure difference; oxygen partial pressure in alveoli (~104 mm Hg) exceeds that in pulmonary capillaries (~40 mm Hg), creating a 64 mm Hg gradient that drives oxygen diffusion, while carbon dioxide partial pressure in capillaries (~45 mm Hg) exceeds alveolar air (~40 mm Hg), driving CO₂ diffusion in the opposite direction. Key anatomical structures include the **respiratory membrane**, **alveoli**, **pulmonary capillaries**, **pulmonary artery**, **pulmonary veins**, **bronchioles**, **pulmonary arterioles**, **erythrocytes**, and **hemoglobin**; the textbook specifies that the **pulmonary artery carries deoxygenated blood** into the lungs and branches into **pulmonary capillaries** that create the **respiratory membrane with the alveoli**, while **oxygenated hemoglobin** returns via **pulmonary veins**. **Ventilation** (air movement into/out of lungs) and **perfusion** (blood flow in pulmonary capillaries) must be compatible for efficient gas exchange; **greater partial pressure of carbon dioxide in alveoli causes bronchioles to increase diameter**, and **greater partial pressure of oxygen in alve', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (310, 114, 'High Altitude Effects', 'High Altitude Effects', '# High Altitude Effects Summary

This subsection explains how increased altitude decreases atmospheric pressure and partial pressure of oxygen, reducing hemoglobin saturation (67% at 19,000 feet versus 98% at sea level) and limiting oxygen diffusion across the respiratory membrane into pulmonary capillaries. At high altitudes, tissues maintain adequate oxygen during rest through two mechanisms: greater proportional oxygen release from hemoglobin and increased BPG production by erythrocytes enhancing oxygen dissociation. Physical exertion at high altitude causes altitude sickness because the small venous oxygen reserve cannot support increased metabolic demand, whereas sea level provides large oxygen reserves. Acute mountain sickness (AMS) results from acute exposure to high altitudes (typically ≥2400 meters/8000 feet) due to low partial pressure of oxygen and low blood oxygen levels, presenting with nausea, vomiting, fatigue, lightheadedness, and potentially pulmonary or cerebral edema; treatment requires descent, though supplemental oxygen and pharmacologic treatments improve symptoms. Acclimatization—the respiratory system''s adjustment to chronic high altitude exposure—occurs when low tissue oxygen stimulates the kidneys to produce erythropoietin (EPO), which stimulates erythrocyte production, increasing circulating hemoglobin to transport available oxygen and enabling physical exertion without AMS. Key search terms: altitude, atmospheric pressure, partial pressure of oxygen, hemoglobin saturation, respiratory membrane, alveoli, BPG, erythrocytes, acute mountain sickness (AMS), altitude sickness, acclimatization, erythropoietin (EPO), micturition, venous oxygen reserve.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (311, 115, 'Conducting Zone', 'Conducting Zone', '# Summary: Conducting Zone

The conducting zone comprises structures that route air into and out of the respiratory system while removing debris, pathogens, and warming and humidifying incoming air. Key anatomical structures include the external nose (root, bridge, dorsum nasi, apex, alae, nares, philtrum), nasal skeleton (nasal bones, septal cartilage, alar cartilage), nasal cavity (separated by nasal septum formed of septal cartilage anteriorly and perpendicular plate of ethmoid bone and vomer posteriorly), nasal conchae (superior, middle, and inferior), paranasal sinuses (frontal, maxillary, sphenoidal, ethmoidal), pharynx (nasopharynx, oropharynx, laryngopharynx), larynx (thyroid cartilage, cricoid cartilage, epiglottis, arytenoids, corniculates, cuneiforms), glottis (vestibular folds, true vocal cords), trachea, primary bronchi, bronchial tree, bronchioles, and terminal bronchioles. The nasal epithelium contains pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium with goblet cells, olfactory epithelium, sebaceous glands, and hair follicles; capillaries beneath the epithelium warm air by convection; serous and mucus-producing cells secrete lysozyme and defensins with antibacterial properties. The epiglottis attaches to the thyroid cartilage and covers the tracheal opening; during swallowing, the uvula and soft palate swing upward to close the nasopharynx. The trachea is formed by 16–20 C-shaped hyaline cartilage rings connected by the fibroelastic membrane (composed of trachealis muscle and elastic', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (312, 115, 'Respiratory Zone', 'Respiratory Zone', 'The respiratory zone comprises structures directly involved in gas exchange, beginning where terminal bronchioles join respiratory bronchioles (the smallest bronchiole type), which lead to alveolar ducts composed of smooth muscle and connective tissue that open into alveolar sacs—clusters of individual alveoli approximately 200 μm in diameter with elastic walls. The alveolar wall contains three major cell types: type I alveolar cells (squamous epithelial cells constituting 97% of alveolar surface area and highly permeable to gases), type II alveolar cells (which secrete pulmonary surfactant to reduce surface tension), and alveolar macrophages (phagocytic immune cells that remove debris and pathogens); alveoli are connected by alveolar pores that maintain equal air pressure. The respiratory membrane—formed by the simple squamous epithelium of type I alveolar cells attached to a thin elastic basement membrane and bordering capillary endothelial membranes—is approximately 0.5 μm thick and allows gases to cross by simple diffusion, enabling oxygen uptake and CO₂ release. The subsection also covers asthma, a chronic disease characterized by inflammation, edema, and bronchospasms (constriction of bronchioles) that can be triggered by environmental factors, respiratory infections, exercise, or stress, presenting with symptoms including coughing, shortness of breath, wheezing, and chest tightness. Search terms: respiratory zone, respiratory bronchiole, alveolar duct, alveolus, alveolar sac, alveolar pores, type I alveolar cells, type II alveolar cells, pulmonary surfactant, alveolar macrophage, respiratory membrane, asthma, bronchospasm, human rhinovirus C, Mycoplasma pneumoniae, Chlamydia pneumoniae.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (313, 116, 'Blood Supply and Nervous Innervation of the Lungs', 'Blood Supply and Nervous Innervation of the Lungs', 'This subsection covers the blood supply and nervous innervation of the lungs, introducing key structures including the pulmonary artery, pulmonary capillary network, pulmonary veins, respiratory membrane, pulmonary plexus, vagus nerve, and thoracic ganglia (second to fifth). The pulmonary artery arises from the pulmonary trunk, carries deoxygenated blood to the alveoli, branches progressively smaller following the bronchi, and becomes the pulmonary capillary network near the alveoli; the pulmonary capillary network consists of thin-walled vessels lacking smooth muscle that form the respiratory membrane where the capillary wall meets the alveolar wall; oxygenated blood drains via pulmonary veins through the hilum. The parasympathetic nervous system causes bronchoconstriction while the sympathetic nervous system stimulates bronchodilation; sensory nerve fibers arise from the vagus nerve and thoracic ganglia (T2-T5), converge at the pulmonary plexus at the lung root/hilum, then follow the bronchi to innervate muscle fibers, glands, and blood vessels. Autonomic nervous system control enables reflexes such as coughing and regulation of oxygen and carbon dioxide levels.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (314, 116, 'Gross Anatomy of the Lungs', 'Gross Anatomy of the Lungs', 'The lungs are pyramid-shaped, paired organs connected to the trachea by the right and left bronchi, bordered inferiorly by the diaphragm, and enclosed by the pleurae. The right lung is shorter and wider than the left lung; the left lung features a cardiac notch that accommodates the heart, with the apex as the superior region and the base as the inferior region near the diaphragm. The right lung contains three lobes (superior, middle, inferior) and the left lung contains two lobes (superior, inferior), separated by fissures and subdivided into bronchopulmonary segments. Each bronchopulmonary segment receives air from its own tertiary bronchus and blood supply from its own artery; diseased segments can be surgically removed with minimal impact on neighboring segments. Lobes further subdivide into pulmonary lobules formed as bronchi branch into bronchioles, with each lobule receiving its own large bronchiole and separated from adjacent lobules by interlobular septa composed of connective tissue. Search terms: lungs, trachea, bronchi, diaphragm, pleurae, mediastinum, cardiac notch, apex, base, lobes, fissures, bronchopulmonary segment, tertiary bronchus, pulmonary lobule, bronchioles, interlobular septum.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (315, 116, 'Pleura of the Lungs', 'Pleura of the Lungs', 'The pleura is a serous membrane consisting of two layers that enclose each lung: the visceral pleura (superficial layer that lines the lung fissures) and the parietal pleura (outer layer that connects to the thoracic wall, mediastinum, and diaphragm). The right and left pleurae are separated by the mediastinum, and the two pleural layers connect at the hilum and form the pleural cavity between them. Pleural fluid is secreted by mesothelial cells and lubricates the pleural surfaces to reduce friction during breathing while creating surface tension that maintains lung position against the thoracic wall. The pleurae perform two major functions: producing pleural fluid and creating cavities that separate major organs to prevent interference from organ movement and prevent infection spread. The adhesive properties of pleural fluid cause the lungs to enlarge when the thoracic wall expands during ventilation, allowing air filling.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (316, 117, 'Pulmonary Ventilation', 'Pulmonary Ventilation', 'Pulmonary ventilation comprises two major steps—inspiration and expiration—driven by pressure gradients where air flows from higher to lower pressure areas. During normal inspiration, the diaphragm contracts and moves inferiorly while external intercostal muscles contract to move ribs upward and outward, expanding the thoracic cavity and decreasing intra-alveolar pressure below atmospheric pressure to draw air in. Normal expiration is passive, relying on lung tissue elasticity to recoil as the diaphragm and intercostal muscles relax, increasing intrapulmonary pressure above atmospheric pressure to expel air. The textbook distinguishes four breathing modes: eupnea (quiet breathing at rest using diaphragm and external intercostals), diaphragmatic breathing (deep breaths), costal breathing (shallow breaths), and hyperpnea (forced breathing during exercise or singing). During forced breathing, accessory muscles including the scalenes (neck), obliques (abdomen), and internal intercostals contract to further increase lung volume during inspiration and actively compress the rib cage during expiration. Thoracic wall compliance directly influences the lungs'' capacity to expand, as pleural fluid''s adhesive force couples thoracic cavity expansion to lung expansion.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (317, 117, 'Respiratory Rate and Control of Ventilation', 'Respiratory Rate and Control of Ventilation', 'This subsection introduces the neural and chemical control mechanisms regulating respiratory rate and ventilation depth. Key anatomical structures include the medulla oblongata (containing the dorsal respiratory group [DRG] and ventral respiratory group [VRG]), pontine respiratory group (PRG) with its apneustic and pneumotaxic centers, central chemoreceptors (brain and brainstem), peripheral chemoreceptors (carotid arteries and aortic arch), aortic body, carotid body, hypothalamus, and cortical areas. The DRG stimulates the diaphragm and intercostal muscles to contract during inspiration; when DRG activity ceases, these muscles relax, producing expiration. The VRG stimulates accessory muscles for forced inspiration and forced expiration. The apneustic center controls inspiration depth by stimulating the DRG, while the pneumotaxic center inhibits DRG activity to control overall respiratory rate. Carbon dioxide concentration is the primary stimulus for respiration; increased CO₂ diffuses across the blood-brain barrier, increases hydrogen ions, and triggers central chemoreceptors to stimulate respiratory centers, increasing rate and depth of respiration. Peripheral chemoreceptors sense arterial hydrogen ion concentration and blood oxygen levels (≤60 mm Hg), signaling respiratory centers to increase ventilation. The hypothalamus and limbic system regulate respiration in response to emotions, pain, and body temperature. Search terms: respiratory rate, respiratory center, medulla oblongata, dorsal respiratory group, ventral respiratory group, pontine respiratory group, apneustic center, pneumotaxic center, chemoreceptors, carbon dioxide, hydrogen ions, pH, blood oxygen levels, diaphragm, intercostal muscles, accessory muscles, pulmonary ventilation, inspiration, expiration.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (318, 117, 'Respiratory Volumes and Capacities', 'Respiratory Volumes and Capacities', 'This subsection introduces four major respiratory volumes—tidal volume (TV, ~500 mL during quiet breathing), expiratory reserve volume (ERV, up to 1200 mL for males), inspiratory reserve volume (IRV, air brought in during forced inspiration), and residual volume (RV, air remaining after maximal exhalation)—and explains that residual volume prevents alveolar collapse. Respiratory capacities combine two or more volumes: total lung capacity (TLC = TV + ERV + IRV + RV; ~6000 mL males, ~4200 mL females), vital capacity (VC = TV + ERV + IRV; 4000–5000 mL), inspiratory capacity (IC = TV + IRV), and functional residual capacity (FRC = ERV + RV). The subsection defines anatomical dead space as airway air that never reaches the alveoli and does not participate in gas exchange, and alveolar dead space as nonfunctional air within diseased or poorly perfused alveoli; total dead space combines both types. Spirometry testing uses these measurements to diagnose respiratory diseases and assess treatment effectiveness. Search terms: tidal volume, expiratory reserve volume, inspiratory reserve volume, residual volume, total lung capacity, vital capacity, inspiratory capacity, functional residual capacity, anatomical dead space, alveolar dead space, total dead space, spirometry.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (319, 118, 'Carbon Dioxide Transport in the Blood', 'Carbon Dioxide Transport in the Blood', 'This subsection describes three mechanisms of carbon dioxide transport in blood: dissolved CO₂ in plasma (7–10%), bicarbonate (HCO₃⁻) in plasma (70%), and carbaminohemoglobin binding to hemoglobin (20%). Carbonic anhydrase (CA) catalyzes the formation of carbonic acid (H₂CO₃) from CO₂ and water in erythrocytes, which dissociates into bicarbonate (HCO₃⁻) and hydrogen (H⁺) ions; bicarbonate accumulates in red blood cells and moves into plasma via the chloride shift (exchange with Cl⁻ ions). Carbon dioxide binds to amino acid moieties on the globin portions of hemoglobin to form carbaminohemoglobin, distinct from oxygen binding to iron; this binding and dissociation depend on partial pressure of carbon dioxide. The Haldane effect describes how oxygen saturation and partial pressure of oxygen influence hemoglobin''s affinity for carbon dioxide: oxygenated hemoglobin does not readily bind CO₂, while deoxygenated hemoglobin (low partial pressure of oxygen) readily binds CO₂. At the pulmonary capillaries, dissolved CO₂ and carbaminohemoglobin release CO₂ across the respiratory membrane into alveoli for exhalation; at tissue capillaries, the lower partial pressure of CO₂ in blood causes CO₂ to diffuse from tissues into blood and bind to hemoglobin.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (320, 118, 'Oxygen Transport in the Blood', 'Oxygen Transport in the Blood', 'This subsection covers oxygen transport in blood, focusing on hemoglobin''s role as the primary oxygen carrier in erythrocytes. Key structures include erythrocytes (red blood cells), hemoglobin (a metalloprotein with quaternary structure composed of four subunits), heme (the iron-containing portion that binds oxygen), and oxyhemoglobin (HbO₂, formed when oxygen binds to hemoglobin). The textbook explains that each hemoglobin molecule carries up to four oxygen molecules, and binding of the first oxygen causes conformational change that facilitates binding of subsequent molecules—a mechanism that reverses during oxygen dissociation at tissues. The oxygen–hemoglobin saturation/dissociation curve describes how partial pressure of oxygen determines the degree of binding at the respiratory membrane and dissociation at body tissues, with hemoglobin saturation normally ranging from 95–99% in healthy individuals. Multiple factors regulate oxygen delivery: partial pressure differences between capillaries (~100 mm Hg) and tissues (highly active tissues like muscle ~20 mm Hg); temperature (higher temperature promotes dissociation); 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate (BPG), a glycolysis byproduct stimulated by androgens, epinephrine, thyroid hormones, and growth hormone, which promotes oxygen dissociation; and the Bohr effect, wherein lower (more acidic) blood pH promotes oxygen dissociation while higher (more basic) pH inhibits it. The subsection also addresses fetal oxygen transport, explaining that fetal hemoglobin has greater oxygen affinity than maternal hemoglobin due to structural differences in two of its four subunits, compensating for the lower partial pressure gradient (~35–50 mm Hg maternal to ~20 mm Hg fetal) at the placenta and chorion.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (321, 119, 'Cortical Processing', 'Cortical Processing', 'This subsection introduces cortical processing of sensory information through topographic mapping and sensory integration. Key structures include the somatosensory cortex, sensory homunculus, primary visual cortex, occipital lobe, dorsal column system (fasciculus gracilis, fasciculus cuneatus), medial lemniscus, trigeminal nuclei, optic chiasm, retina, and fovea. The textbook establishes that sensory axons maintain positional correspondence with receptor cells, allowing the cerebral cortex to preserve sensory topography: lower body axons travel in the fasciculus gracilis while upper body axons travel in the fasciculus cuneatus, and these relationships are maintained through the medial lemniscus and thalamus to the somatosensory cortex. In the somatosensory cortex, external genitals, feet, and lower legs map to the medial gyrus within the longitudinal fissure, with the body represented medial to lateral from lower to upper body, while sensitive areas (fingers, lower face) are exaggerated and less sensitive areas (shoulders, back) are compressed. The visual pathway maintains topography from retina through optic chiasm to primary visual cortex, with the right visual field processed in the left visual cortex and left visual field in the right visual cortex; superior and inferior visual information are inverted such that the superior visual cortex processes the inferior visual field. Sensory processing proceeds from primary sensory cortex through association areas to multimodal integration areas, and the visual cortex demonstrates plasticity in adapting to altered visual input, while distinct sensory modalities are integrated into a seamless perceptual experience.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (322, 119, 'Sensory Pathways', 'Sensory Pathways', '# Summary: Sensory Pathways

This subsection describes how the CNS processes somatic sensory information through ascending pathways and tracts. Key structures include the **dorsal column system** (composed of **fasciculus gracilis** and **fasciculus cuneatus**), **spinothalamic tract**, **trigeminal pathway**, and specialized sensory systems for **gustation**, **audition**, **vestibular function**, and **vision**. The **dorsal column system** begins with **dorsal root ganglion neurons** entering the **dorsal root** and **dorsal column white matter**; axons synapse in the **nucleus gracilis** and **nucleus cuneatus** of the **medulla**, then decussate and ascend as the **medial lemniscus** to the **thalamus**, projecting finally to the **postcentral gyrus** of the **cerebral cortex**. The **spinothalamic tract** originates in **dorsal root ganglion** neurons synapsing in the **dorsal horn**; second neurons decussate within the **spinal cord** and ascend to the **thalamus**, then project to the **postcentral gyrus**. The **dorsal column system** mediates **touch sensations** and **proprioception**, while the **spinothalamic tract** mediates **pain** and **temperature sensations**. The **trigeminal pathway** carries somatosensory information from the face, head, mouth, and nasal cavity via the **trigeminal ganglion**, **spinal trigeminal nucleus**, **chief sensory nucleus**, and **mesencephalic nuclei**, with second neurons decussating along the **trigeminothalamic tract** to the **thalamus** and **primary somatosensory cortex**. **Gustation** travels via **facial** and **glossopharyngeal', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (323, 120, 'Cortical Responses', 'Cortical Responses', 'This subsection introduces cortical motor processing, beginning with how sensory perception progresses through primary, associative, and integrative sensory areas to initiate motor processing in the frontal lobe. Key structures include the prefrontal cortex (responsible for executive functions, working memory, attention, and personality), secondary motor cortices (premotor cortex and supplemental motor area), frontal eye fields, Broca''s area, and primary motor cortex located in the precentral gyrus. The prefrontal areas project into the secondary motor cortices; the premotor cortex controls core muscle movements for posture, while the supplemental motor area plans and coordinates movement and manages learned sequential movements. The primary motor cortex receives input from planning areas and outputs to spinal cord neurons to stimulate skeletal muscle contraction, organized as a motor homunculus with topographical representation where muscles with greater innervation and fine, agile movements (fingers, lower face) occupy exaggerated cortical space compared to "power muscles" (buttock, back). Search terms: prefrontal cortex, executive functions, premotor cortex, supplemental motor area, primary motor cortex, precentral gyrus, motor homunculus, frontal eye fields, Broca''s area, Phineas Gage.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (324, 120, 'Descending Pathways', 'Descending Pathways', 'This subsection introduces the descending motor pathways that control voluntary skeletal muscle movement: the corticobulbar tract and corticospinal tract, both originating from Betz cells in the primary motor cortex and synapsing with lower motor neurons in the brain stem or spinal cord. The corticobulbar tract is ipsilateral (projects to the same side), while the corticospinal tract is largely contralateral (crosses the midline at the pyramidal decussation in the medulla). The corticospinal tract divides into two functional divisions: the lateral corticospinal tract controls appendicular muscles (arms and legs) via the lateral column of the spinal cord, with wider ventral horns in the cervical and lumbar enlargements reflecting fine motor control; the anterior corticospinal tract controls axial trunk muscles via the medial ventral horn and exhibits bilateral innervation through both contralateral crossing and ipsilateral collateral branches to coordinate postural movements. Key search terms: Betz cells, primary motor cortex, corticobulbar tract, corticospinal tract, pyramidal decussation, lateral corticospinal tract, anterior corticospinal tract, cerebral peduncles, internal capsule, pyramids, cervical enlargement, lumbar enlargement, ventral horn, appendicular muscles, axial muscles.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (325, 120, 'Extrapyramidal Controls', 'Extrapyramidal Controls', 'The extrapyramidal system comprises descending brain-to-spinal cord pathways outside the corticospinal tract, including the tectospinal tract (projecting from midbrain to spinal cord, driven by superior colliculus), reticulospinal tract (connecting reticular system to spinal cord, influencing trunk and proximal limb muscles, muscle tone, and autonomic functions), and vestibulospinal tract (connecting vestibular system brain stem nuclei to spinal cord, modulating posture and balance based on equilibrium information). The secondary motor cortices, basal nuclei, and subcortical structures influence extrapyramidal pathways to modulate spine and cranium movements. The cerebellum compares cerebral motor commands with proprioceptive feedback by receiving corticospinal fiber branches that synapse in the pons and dorsal column system collaterals that project to the medulla; conflicts between motor commands and proprioceptive information cause the cerebellum to stimulate the red nucleus, which sends corrective commands via the rubrospinal tract to the spinal cord. Cerebellar dysfunction impairs coordination and balance, as demonstrated by alcohol''s inhibition of proprioceptive feedback interpretation and medication side effects affecting motor control regions.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (326, 120, 'Ventral Horn Output', 'Ventral Horn Output', 'This subsection introduces lower motor neurons located in the ventral horn of the spinal cord that innervate skeletal muscles through long axons extending via the ventral nerve root and spinal nerve. Key structures include motor units (a motor neuron and all muscle fibers it controls), the neuromuscular junction (where motor neuron synaptic end bulbs release acetylcholine to depolarize the muscle fiber sarcolemma), and interneurons in the dorsal horn that provide inhibitory control. The textbook describes multiple reflex types: withdrawal reflexes (hot-stove reflex, painful stimulus reflex) where sensory neurons activate motor neurons to contract agonist muscles while interneurons inhibit antagonist motor neurons; stretch reflexes where muscle spindle receptors directly activate muscle contraction and inhibit antagonist motor neurons; and specialized reflexes including the corneal reflex (trigeminal or optic nerve sensory input, facial nerve motor output to orbicularis oculi) and the Babinski reflex. Search terms: lower motor neurons, ventral horn, motor unit, neuromuscular junction, acetylcholine, sarcolemma, interneuron, dorsal horn, withdrawal reflex, stretch reflex, muscle spindle receptor, corneal reflex, eye blink reflex, trigeminal nerve, facial nerve, orbicularis oculi, Babinski reflex.', 4);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (327, 121, 'Sensory Modalities', 'Sensory Modalities', 'This subsection introduces sensory modalities—the ways sensory information is encoded and transduced—and distinguishes between general senses (distributed throughout the body; examples: mechanoreceptors in skin, muscles, blood vessel walls) and special senses (dedicated organs: eye, inner ear, tongue, nose). Key structures and processes include: taste (gustation) with five recognized tastes (sweet, salty, sour, bitter, umami) and a possible sixth (fats/lipids); papillae (circumvallate, foliate, filiform, fungiform) containing taste buds with gustatory receptor cells that release neurotransmitters; salty taste triggered by Na+ diffusion into gustatory cells causing depolarization; sour taste triggered by H+ concentration causing depolarization; sweet, bitter, and umami tastes mediated by G protein–coupled receptors (sweet activated by glucose, bitter by alkaloids and other molecules, umami by L-glutamate); and cranial nerve innervation (facial nerve connects to anterior third of tongue, glossopharyngeal nerve to posterior two-thirds, vagus nerve to extreme posterior). Smell (olfaction) involves olfactory receptor neurons in the olfactory epithelium (superior nasal cavity) with dendrites extending into mucus; odorant molecules bind G protein–coupled receptors on olfactory dendrites, producing graded membrane potentials; olfactory neuron axons extend through the cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone to the olfactory bulb on the ventral frontal lobe, then project to primary olfactory cortex (inferior and medial temporal lobe), limbic system, and hypothalamus—notably, olfaction does not synapse in the thalamus before reaching cerebral cortex. Search terms: sensory modalities, general sense, special sense, gustation, taste buds, papillae, gustatory', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (328, 121, 'Sensory Nerves', 'Sensory Nerves', 'This subsection introduces the structural organization and routing of sensory nerves from peripheral receptors to the central nervous system. Key anatomical structures include spinal nerves (containing afferent axons from sensory receptors and efferent axons to effectors), dorsal roots (sensory neuron axons only), ventral roots (motor neuron axons only), dorsal root ganglia, posterior (dorsal) horn, anterior (ventral) horn, posterior (dorsal) column white matter, and cranial nerves (olfactory, optic, vestibulocochlear, trigeminal, facial, glossopharyngeal, and vagus nerves). The textbook establishes that sensory axons exhibit topographical arrangement in special senses; spinal nerves split into dorsal and ventral roots near the spinal cord; sensory branches synapse with local neurons in the dorsal root ganglion, posterior horn, or anterior horn, or travel to other spinal cord levels, or project into the posterior column to connect with the brain. Spinal sensory pathways are contralateral (right body to left brain; left body to right brain), whereas cranial nerve systems are mostly ipsilateral (right head to right brain). The trigeminal system conveys somatosensation for the face; some cranial nerves contain only sensory axons while others contain both sensory and motor axons.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (329, 121, 'Sensory Receptors', 'Sensory Receptors', '# Summary: Sensory Receptors

This subsection introduces sensory receptors as specialized cells in the peripheral nervous system that detect environmental stimuli. Receptors are classified by three criteria: (1) **cell type and structural organization**—free nerve endings (e.g., pain and temperature receptors in skin dermis), encapsulated nerve endings (e.g., lamellated corpuscles responding to pressure and touch), or specialized receptor cells (e.g., photoreceptors in the retina); (2) **location relative to stimuli**—exteroceptors (in skin for external stimuli), interoceptors (in internal organs like aorta and carotid sinus), or proprioceptors (near muscles interpreting tissue position); and (3) **functional type based on stimulus transduction**—chemoreceptors (taste, smell), osmoreceptors (solute concentration), nociceptors (pain from tissue damage chemicals), mechanoreceptors (pressure, vibration, sound, balance), and thermoreceptors (heat or cold). Stimuli include ions and macromolecules affecting transmembrane receptor proteins, physical environmental variations, and electromagnetic radiation (visible light in humans). Search terms: sensory receptors, free nerve endings, encapsulated nerve endings, photoreceptor, exteroceptor, interoceptor, proprioceptor, chemoreceptor, osmoreceptor, nociceptor, mechanoreceptor, thermoreceptor, stimulus transduction, lamellated corpuscles.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (330, 122, 'Classification of Connective Tissues', 'Classification of Connective Tissues', 'Connective tissues are classified into three broad categories—connective tissue proper, supportive connective tissue, and fluid connective tissue—based on the characteristics of their ground substance and types of fibers in the matrix. Connective tissue proper comprises loose connective tissue (areolar, adipose, reticular) with loosely organized fibers and large spaces, and dense connective tissue (dense regular, elastic, dense-irregular) with fiber bundles that provide tensile strength, elasticity, and protection. Supportive connective tissue includes cartilage (hyaline, fibrocartilage, elastic) and bone, which contain few distinct cell types and densely packed fibers; bone is characterized by a rigid, calcified matrix due to deposited calcium salts. Fluid connective tissue—blood and lymph—consists of various specialized cells circulating in a watery fluid containing salts, nutrients, and dissolved proteins. All connective tissues share three characteristic components: cells, ground substance, and protein fibers, with the amount and structure of each component correlating to tissue function.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (331, 122, 'Connective Tissue Proper', 'Connective Tissue Proper', 'Connective tissue proper contains fibroblasts (most abundant), fibrocytes, adipocytes, mesenchymal cells, and immune cells (macrophages, mast cells, lymphocytes, plasma cells, phagocytic cells); fibroblasts secrete polysaccharides and proteins that form ground substance (made of hyaluronic acid and proteoglycans), which combines with embedded fibrous proteins to create the extracellular matrix. Three fiber types secreted by fibroblasts—collagen fibers (provide tensile strength to ligaments and tendons), elastic fibers (contain elastin; return to original shape after stretching; found in skin and vertebral column ligaments), and reticular fibers (narrow, branching network; anchor parenchyma in soft organs like liver and spleen)—are embedded in ground substance. Loose connective tissue (found between organs; absorbs shock and binds tissues) includes adipose tissue (white adipocytes store single large lipid droplets for insulation and mechanical protection; brown adipocytes store multiple droplets and are thermogenic), areolar tissue (random web-like distribution; fills spaces between muscle fibers and surrounds vessels), and reticular tissue (mesh-like framework for lymphatic tissue, spleen, liver). Dense connective tissue contains more collagen fibers than loose connective tissue and displays greater resistance to stretching; dense regular connective tissue (parallel fibers; forms ligaments and tendons) and dense regular elastic tissue (contains elastin fibers; found in vocal folds and vertebral column ligaments) provide directional strength, while dense irregular connective tissue (random fiber direction; found in skin dermis and arterial walls) provides multidirectional strength.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (332, 122, 'Supportive Connective Tissues', 'Supportive Connective Tissues', 'This subsection introduces the two major supportive connective tissues—cartilage and bone—that maintain posture and protect internal organs. Cartilage structure features chondroitin sulfates forming proteoglycans, chondrocytes occupying lacunae, and an encapsulating perichondrium; cartilage is avascular, causing slow nutrient diffusion and slow healing. The three cartilage types are: hyaline cartilage (most common, clear appearance, found in rib cage, nose, and moveable joints; serves as embryonic skeleton template and growth plate), fibrocartilage (tough, thick collagen bundles; found in menisci and intervertebral discs), and elastic cartilage (contains elastic fibers; found in external ear). Bone is the hardest connective tissue with a matrix of collagen fibers embedded in mineralized ground substance containing hydroxyapatite; osteocytes occupy lacunae arranged in concentric circles around a central canal; bone is highly vascularized and heals quickly unlike cartilage. Bone structure includes cancellous bone (sponge-like, contains trabeculae, lighter, found in bone interiors and long bone ends) and compact bone. Search terms: cartilage, bone, chondroitin sulfates, proteoglycans, chondrocytes, lacunae, perichondrium, hyaline cartilage, fibrocartilage, elastic cartilage, osteocytes, hydroxyapatite, cancellous bone, trabeculae, compact bone.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (333, 123, 'Cell to Cell Junctions', 'Cell to Cell Junctions', 'Epithelial tissues are held together by three basic types of cell-to-cell junctions: tight junctions, anchoring junctions, and gap junctions. Tight junctions block movement of substances through extracellular space between adjacent epithelial cells, separating cells into apical and basal compartments and enabling epithelia to act as selective barriers. Anchoring junctions—comprising desmosomes, hemidesmosomes, and adherens junctions—stabilize epithelial tissues on lateral and basal cell surfaces; desmosomes use the adhesion molecule cadherin to link adjacent cells, hemidesmosomes use integrins to link cells to the extracellular matrix (basal lamina), and adherens junctions use either cadherins or integrins and contain the contractile protein actin on the cytoplasmic surface, influencing epithelial tissue shape and folding. Gap junctions form intercellular passageways that facilitate movement of small molecules and ions between adjacent cell cytoplasm, enabling electrical and metabolic coupling that coordinates function across large cell groups. Search terms: tight junctions, anchoring junctions, gap junctions, desmosomes, hemidesmosomes, adherens junctions, cadherin, integrins, actin, basal lamina, apical and basal compartments, selective barriers, intercellular passageway.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (334, 123, 'Classification of Epithelial Tissues', 'Classification of Epithelial Tissues', 'Epithelial tissues are classified by cell shape (squamous, cuboidal, columnar) and number of layers (simple, stratified, pseudostratified, transitional), with all cells in simple epithelium resting on the basal lamina while only basal cells contact it in stratified epithelium. Simple squamous epithelium lines lymphatic and cardiovascular vessels (endothelium), lung alveoli, kidney tubules, and capillaries, and forms the mesothelium of serous membranes; simple cuboidal epithelium lines kidney tubules and gland ducts and is active in secretion and absorption; simple columnar epithelium lines digestive system sections and female reproductive tract and is active in absorption and secretion, while ciliated columnar epithelium in fallopian tubes and respiratory system uses cilia to remove particulate matter. Pseudostratified columnar epithelium appears stratified but consists of a single layer with nuclei at different levels in the respiratory tract, and both simple and pseudostratified columnar epithelia are heterogeneous, containing goblet cells that secrete mucus. Stratified epithelium protects against physical and chemical wear and tear; stratified squamous epithelium (the most common type) is keratinized in mammalian skin and unkeratinized in the mouth cavity lining, while stratified cuboidal and columnar epithelia are uncommon. Transitional epithelium, found only in the ureters and urinary bladder, changes shape as the bladder fills—displaying convoluted cuboidal apical cells with umbrella-shaped surfaces when empty and transitioning to squamous cells when distended with urine.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (335, 123, 'Glandular Epithelium', 'Glandular Epithelium', 'Glandular epithelium comprises endocrine glands (ductless structures that release hormones into interstitial fluid and bloodstream to reach target cells with hormone receptors) and exocrine glands (duct-bearing structures that release mucous, sweat, saliva, and breast milk to epithelial surfaces or external environments). Exocrine glands are classified structurally as unicellular (goblet cells in small and large intestine) or multicellular (serous glands, simple glands with single ducts, compound glands with branched ducts, tubular glands with straight or coiled ducts, and alveolar/acinar glands like the exocrine pancreas; tubuloalveolar glands combine tubes and pockets). Secretion modes include merocrine secretion (most common; vesicles release contents by exocytosis at apical surface; eccrine sweat glands and mucous glands containing mucin exemplify this), apocrine secretion (apical cell portion pinches off; apocrine sweat glands in axillary and genital areas release fatty secretions), and holocrine secretion (entire cell ruptures and is destroyed; sebaceous glands producing skin and hair oils exemplify this). Glands are further classified by secretion product: serous glands produce watery, enzyme-rich secretions (alpha amylase); mucous glands produce viscous mucin-rich secretions; mixed exocrine glands contain both serous and mucous components and are common in salivary glands. Endocrine gland examples include anterior pituitary, thymus, adrenal cortex, and gonads.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (336, 124, 'Tissue Injury and Repair', 'Tissue Injury and Repair', 'This subsection introduces inflammation as the body''s standard initial response to injury (biological, chemical, physical, or radiation burns), which limits injury extent, eliminates the injury cause, and initiates tissue repair and regeneration. Key entities include necrosis (accidental cell death that causes inflammation) versus apoptosis (programmed cell death that does not initiate inflammation); the four cardinal signs of inflammation (redness, swelling, pain, local heat); and inflammatory mediators including histamine (released by degranulating mast cells), prostaglandins (released from injured cells), and neutrophils and macrophages (recruited to the inflammation site). The subsection details the inflammatory cascade: damaged cells release chemical signals causing local vasodilation; histamine and inflammatory mediators increase blood vessel permeability, allowing fluid and white blood cells into interstitial tissue spaces, causing edema and pain; NSAIDs inhibit prostaglandin synthesis to reduce pain and inflammation; antihistamines block histamine receptors. The tissue repair phase includes clotting (coagulation) forming fibrin networks and scabs, fibroblast replacement of collagen and extracellular material, angiogenesis producing granulation tissue and vascularization, and clot retraction and dissolution. Healing outcomes include primary union (edges close together) versus secondary union (wound contraction in gaping wounds), with scar formation when granulation tissue is extensive; regeneration is the addition of new cells of the same type as injured cells.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (337, 124, 'Tissue and Aging', 'Tissue and Aging', 'This subsection addresses senescence and its effects across all cells, tissues, and organs, with variability based on genetic makeup and lifestyle. Key anatomical structures affected include skin, hair follicles, elastic and collagen fibers, bones, fibrous cartilage disks of the spine, joints, muscles, blood vessels, airways, brain, and spinal cord. Outward signs of aging include skin thinning and drying (reducing elasticity and contributing to wrinkles and high blood pressure), graying hair (due to follicles producing less melanin), loss of muscle tone and facial flabbiness, decreased overall height (from bone calcium loss), joint stiffening (from cartilage loss), and sensory deterioration requiring glasses and hearing aids. At the cellular level, telomeres shorten with each cell division, and alterations in cell membranes reduce efficient transport of oxygen and nutrients into cells and removal of carbon dioxide and waste products, potentially causing abnormal cell function and diseases including arthritis, memory issues, and some cancers. Interior signs of aging include slower wound healing, higher infection frequency (from reduced immune system capacity), and increased incidence of heart diseases, respiratory syndromes, and type 2 diabetes. The textbook emphasizes that exercise and healthy lifestyle choices can slow deterioration associated with aging.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (338, 125, 'Embryonic Origin of Tissues', 'Embryonic Origin of Tissues', NULL, 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (339, 125, 'Tissue Membranes', 'Tissue Membranes', '# Tissue Membranes Summary

Tissue membranes are thin layers of cells covering the body exterior, organs, internal passageways, and moveable joint cavities, divided into two basic types: connective tissue membranes and epithelial membranes. Connective tissue membranes form solely from connective tissue and encapsulate organs such as the kidneys; synovial membranes, a connective tissue membrane type, line freely movable joints (shoulder, elbow, knee) where fibroblasts release hyaluronan to form synovial fluid, a lubricant that enables frictionless bone movement and exchanges water and nutrients with blood. Epithelial membranes consist of epithelium attached to connective tissue and include three subtypes: mucous membranes (mucosae) that line digestive, respiratory, excretory, and reproductive tracts with mucus covering the epithelial layer and lamina propria supporting it; serous membranes composed of mesodermally derived mesothelium supported by connective tissue that line coelomic cavities and cover internal organs, including the pleura (lungs), pericardium (heart), and peritoneum (abdominal cavity with mesenteries); and the cutaneous membrane (skin), a stratified squamous epithelial membrane with keratinized cells on its apical surface protecting against desiccation and pathogens. Serous fluid secreted by mesothelium lubricates serous membranes to reduce friction between organs. Histology enables detection of membrane structural disruptions indicating injury or disease.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (340, 126, 'Antidiuretic Hormone (ADH)', 'Antidiuretic Hormone (ADH)', 'This subsection covers antidiuretic hormone (ADH) and endothelins as key regulators of kidney function and water balance. ADH, a 9-amino acid peptide released by the posterior pituitary, promotes water recovery and decreases urine volume by stimulating aquaporin protein movement into the apical cell membrane of principal cells of the collecting ducts, enabling transcellular water movement from the collecting duct lumen into the interstitial space of the kidney medulla by osmosis, after which water enters vasa recta capillaries to return to circulation; ADH maintains plasma osmolarity and blood pressure. Endothelins are 21-amino acid peptide vasoconstrictors produced by endothelial cells of renal blood vessels, mesangial cells, and cells of the DCT; angiotensin II, bradykinin, and epinephrine stimulate endothelin release. In healthy people, endothelins do not typically influence blood pressure, but in diabetic kidney disease, chronically elevated endothelin causes sodium retention and diminishes GFR by damaging podocytes and vasoconstricting both afferent and efferent arterioles. Search terms: antidiuretic hormone (ADH), posterior pituitary, aquaporin proteins, principal cells, collecting ducts, plasma osmolarity, endothelins, vasoconstrictors, angiotensin II, bradykinin, epinephrine, GFR, podocytes, afferent arterioles, efferent arterioles, diabetic kidney disease.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (341, 126, 'Natriuretic Hormones', 'Natriuretic Hormones', 'Natriuretic hormones are peptides that stimulate sodium excretion by inhibiting aldosterone release and ADH release, thereby inhibiting Na+ and water recovery in the collecting ducts and distal convoluted tubule (DCT). Atrial natriuretic hormone (ANH), a 28-amino acid peptide produced by heart atria in response to atrial wall over-stretching, increases glomerular filtration rate (GFR) through vasodilation of the afferent arteriole and vasoconstriction of the efferent arteriole, leading to increased loss of water and sodium in forming urine. B-type natriuretic peptide (BNP), a 32-amino acid peptide produced in the heart ventricles, has lower receptor affinity than ANH and provides "fine tuning" of blood pressure regulation; its longer biologic half-life makes it a diagnostic marker for congestive heart failure. Search terms: natriuretic hormones, atrial natriuretic hormone (ANH), B-type natriuretic peptide (BNP), aldosterone inhibition, ADH inhibition, sodium excretion, water reabsorption, GFR, afferent arteriole vasodilation, efferent arteriole vasoconstriction, congestive heart failure.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (342, 127, 'Renal Hilum', 'Renal Hilum', 'The renal hilum serves as the entry and exit site for renal arteries, renal veins, nerves, lymphatics, and ureters; the renal pelvis emerges from the hilum, formed from major and minor calyxes, and uses smooth muscle peristalsis to funnel urine into the ureter. Renal arteries branch directly from the descending aorta and divide sequentially into segmental arteries, interlobar arteries, arcuate arteries, cortical radiate arteries, and afferent arterioles that service approximately 1.3 million nephrons per kidney. Nephrons, the functional units of the kidney, consist of a renal corpuscle (glomerulus and Bowman''s capsule) where afferent arterioles form high-pressure capillaries (~200 µm diameter) that filter blood, followed by an efferent arteriole that drains into peritubular capillaries and vasa recta for solute and water recovery. The kidney''s vascular arrangement constitutes a portal system—the only one where an arteriole connects two capillary beds—with renal veins returning blood directly to the inferior vena cava. The cortex contains all renal corpuscles, proximal convoluted tubules (PCTs), and distal convoluted tubules, while cortical nephrons have short loops of Henle confined to the cortex and juxtamedullary nephrons (~15%) have long loops of Henle extending into the medulla.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (343, 128, 'Nephrons: The Functional Unit', 'Nephrons: The Functional Unit', 'Nephrons are the functional units of the kidney that modify blood filtrate into urine through three principal functions: filtration, reabsorption, and secretion; they also regulate blood pressure (via renin production), red blood cell production (via EPO), and calcium absorption (via vitamin D conversion). The renal corpuscle comprises the glomerulus (a high-pressure capillary bed between afferent and efferent arterioles) surrounded by Bowman''s capsule, which captures 10–20% of plasma filtrate and directs it to the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT); the filtration membrane consists of fenestrations in glomerular capillaries, podocytes with filtration slits, basement membrane, and negatively charged proteins that permit passage of substances <4 nm (readily) to 8 nm (mostly) but exclude blood cells and large proteins. The juxtaglomerular apparatus (JGA)—located where the afferent and efferent arterioles contact the distal convoluted tubule (DCT)—contains macula densa cells that monitor Na+ concentration and fluid flow rate, releasing ATP and adenosine to regulate juxtaglomerular cell contraction/relaxation and glomerular filtration rate (GFR) in response to blood osmolarity changes; macula densa cells also regulate renin release, which cleaves angiotensinogen to produce angiotensin I, converted by ACE (from lungs) to angiotensin II, a systemic vasoconstrictor that stimulates aldosterone release from the adrenal cortex to increase Na+ reabsorption and water retention. The PCT (simple cuboidal epithelium with brush border microvilli) actively transports solutes (Na+, Cl–, glucose); the loop of Henle has descending thick (simple cuboidal) and thin (simple squ', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (344, 129, 'Glomerular Filtration Rate (GFR)', 'Glomerular Filtration Rate (GFR)', 'This subsection defines glomerular filtration rate (GFR) as the volume of filtrate formed by both kidneys per minute, with normal values of approximately 125 mL/min in males and 105 mL/min in females, corresponding to 180 L/day and 150 L/day respectively, of which 99% is reabsorbed to yield 1–2 liters of daily urine production. Key structures and processes include the glomerulus, Bowman''s capsule, filtration membrane, podocyte processes, and capillary endothelial fenestrations, which together function as a semipermeable barrier that constrains solute movement by particle size. GFR is influenced by hydrostatic pressure and colloid osmotic pressure (osmotic pressure) on either side of the glomerular capillary membrane; glomerular filtration occurs when glomerular hydrostatic pressure exceeds luminal hydrostatic pressure of Bowman''s capsule. The filtration membrane prevents passage of red and white blood cells, platelets, albumins, and large proteins, which remain in the capillary and create an average colloid osmotic pressure of 30 mm Hg, while Bowman''s space has osmotic pressure near zero. Net filtration pressure (NFP) results from the sum of osmotic and hydrostatic influences and equals approximately 10 mm Hg. Disorders involving excess protein passage through filtration slits cause deficiency of circulating plasma proteins (principally albumin), decreased blood osmotic pressure, and systemic edema as water is lost from circulation to interstitial tissues and cells.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (345, 129, 'Net Filtration Pressure (NFP)', 'Net Filtration Pressure (NFP)', 'Net filtration pressure (NFP) determines filtration rates through the kidney and is calculated as NFP = glomerular blood hydrostatic pressure (GBHP) – [capsular hydrostatic pressure (CHP) + blood colloid osmotic pressure (BCOP)], yielding approximately 10 mm Hg (55 – [15 + 30]). The low net pressure across the filtration membrane means minor changes in blood osmolarity or capillary blood pressure cause major changes in filtrate formation. Smooth muscle in the afferent capillaries autoregulates: contracting when blood pressure rises to limit increased blood flow and filtration rate, and relaxing when blood pressure drops to maintain steady flow and filtration rate despite systemic blood pressure changes. Mean arterial blood pressure must remain above approximately 60 mm Hg to maintain adequate glomerular filtration; pressures below this threshold impair renal function and cause shock. Glomerular filtration rate (GFR) assesses kidney excretory function and is estimated by measuring inulin (a plant polysaccharide neither reabsorbed nor secreted by the kidney) or more commonly by measuring creatinine (a protein-derived molecule from muscle metabolism that is not reabsorbed and only slightly secreted by the nephron). Search terms: net filtration pressure, glomerular blood hydrostatic pressure, capsular hydrostatic pressure, blood colloid osmotic pressure, afferent capillaries, autoregulation, mean arterial blood pressure, glomerular filtration rate, inulin, creatinine.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (346, 130, 'Diuretics and Fluid Volume', 'Diuretics and Fluid Volume', 'This subsection covers diuretics and the regulation of fluid volume and electrolyte composition in the urinary system. Key structures and processes include: diuretics (caffeine, alcohol, hydrochlorothiazide, mannitol) that increase urine volume through vasodilation, ADH inhibition, or Na+/Cl– symporter inhibition in the DCT and collecting duct; osmotic diuretics that promote water loss by osmosis; and the regulation of sodium, potassium, chloride, calcium, and phosphate through mechanisms including the renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system, aldosterone stimulation of Na+ uptake in the DCT and collecting ducts, and PTH stimulation of Ca++ reabsorption in the DCT. The subsection also addresses acid–base homeostasis, explaining that the PCT actively secretes H+ as Na+ is reabsorbed, intercalated cells in the collecting ducts use proton pumps to actively secrete H+ into forming urine, and H+ is buffered by bicarbonate, dihydrogen phosphate ion, or ammonia. Search terms: diuretic, caffeine, alcohol, hydrochlorothiazide, GFR, ADH, posterior pituitary, collecting duct, DCT, Na+/Cl– symporter, osmotic diuretic, mannitol, glucose, polyuria, polydipsia, polyphagia, sodium, potassium, chloride, aldosterone, PTH, calcium, phosphate, calcitriol, vitamin D, acid–base homeostasis, PCT, intercalated cells, proton pump, bicarbonate, ammonia, urine pH.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (347, 130, 'Volume-sensing Mechanisms', 'Volume-sensing Mechanisms', 'This subsection explains how the body senses and regulates blood volume and pressure through multiple integrated mechanisms. Key structures include baroreceptors in the aorta and carotid sinuses, granular cells in the afferent arteriole of the juxtaglomerular apparatus (JGA), atrial cardiomyocytes, and the collecting ducts and distal convoluted tubule (DCT) of the kidney. The primary sensing mechanism involves baroreceptors detecting blood pressure changes and signaling the central nervous system: increased pressure triggers vasodilation of afferent arterioles, increased GFR, and water loss; decreased pressure triggers sympathetic stimulation and vasoconstriction, decreasing GFR and water loss. Decreased blood pressure also stimulates granular cells to release renin, which activates angiotensin II to produce vasoconstriction and triggers aldosterone secretion, increasing Na+ and water retention; progesterone similarly binds aldosterone receptors in the collecting duct with weaker effects. Atrial cardiomyocytes respond to increased stretch by secreting ANH, which opposes aldosterone by inhibiting Na+ recovery in the DCT and collecting ducts, causing Na+ and water loss and declining blood volume. When blood volume drops 5–10 percent, the posterior pituitary rapidly increases ADH (vasopressin) release, causing immediate vasoconstriction and activating aquaporin channels in collecting ducts to recover water and restore vascular volume. Search terms: baroreceptors, aorta, carotid sinuses, juxtaglomerular apparatus, granular cells, afferent arteriole, GFR, renin, angiotensin II, aldosterone, progesterone, ANH, DCT, collecting duct, ADH, vasopressin, aquaporin channels.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (348, 131, 'Sympathetic Nerves', 'Sympathetic Nerves', '# Summary: Regulation of Renal Blood Flow — Sympathetic Nerves

The kidneys are innervated by sympathetic neurons via the celiac plexus and splanchnic nerves; reduction of sympathetic stimulation causes vasodilation and increased renal blood flow at rest, while increased sympathetic activity causes vasoconstriction of afferent arterioles (via norepinephrine) and stimulates the adrenal medulla to release epinephrine, redirecting blood away from the kidneys during stress. When blood pressure falls, sympathetic nerves stimulate renin release, which increases angiotensin II production (a powerful vasoconstrictor) and aldosterone production to retain Na+ and water. The kidneys maintain stable glomerular filtration rate (GFR) across varying blood pressures through two autoregulatory mechanisms: the myogenic mechanism, in which afferent arteriolar smooth muscle cells contract when stretched (increased pressure) and relax when unstretched (decreased pressure); and the tubuloglomerular feedback mechanism, involving the juxtaglomerular apparatus (JGA) and macula densa cells of the distal convoluted tubule (DCT) that respond to fluid flow rate and Na+ concentration. Macula densa cells release ATP and adenosine as paracrine factors to stimulate juxtaglomerular cells to constrict the afferent arteriole when GFR increases, while nitric oxide (NO) relaxes the afferent arteriole to fine-tune this response. Only a 10 mm Hg pressure differential across the glomerulus is required for normal GFR, making renal blood flow highly sensitive to small changes in afferent arterial pressure.

**Search terms:** sympathetic innervation, celiac plexus, splanchnic nerves, afferent arteriole, vasoconstriction,', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (349, 132, 'Blood Pressure Regulation', 'Blood Pressure Regulation', 'This subsection explains how the kidneys regulate blood pressure through water and sodium reabsorption and the renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system. Key structures and processes include: ADH stimulation of aquaporin channels in the collecting ducts for water recovery; osmotic reabsorption of Na+ followed by water; and the renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system involving the kidneys, lungs, liver, and adrenal cortex. The liver synthesizes and secretes angiotensinogen; when blood pressure is low, the kidney releases renin, which converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I; ACE produced in the lung converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II, which causes immediate vasoconstriction and stimulates the adrenal cortex to release aldosterone for longer-term Na+ and water reabsorption. Loss of renal function impairs vascular volume control, causing hypotension or hypertension that can lead to stroke, heart attack, and aneurysm formation; renal failure also reduces EPO production, causing anemia. Search terms: renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system, angiotensinogen, renin, angiotensin I, angiotensin II, ACE, aldosterone, aquaporin channels, ADH, Na+ reabsorption, blood pressure regulation, vasoconstriction, renal failure, hypotension, hypertension.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (350, 132, 'Vitamin D Synthesis', 'Vitamin D Synthesis', 'This subsection covers two major homeostatic functions of the kidney: vitamin D synthesis and erythropoietin (EPO) production. Vitamin D activation occurs through hydroxylation in the kidney, converting calcidiol to calcitriol (1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol), which regulates Ca++ absorption in the digestive tract, Ca++ reabsorption in the kidney, and maintains serum Ca++ and phosphate concentrations; inadequate Ca++ causes osteoporosis, osteomalacia, rickets, and impairs cell proliferation, neuromuscular function, blood clotting, and inflammatory response. EPO, a 193-amino acid protein produced 85% by the kidney and 15% by the liver, stimulates red blood cell formation in the bone marrow and increases in response to lower oxygen partial pressure at high altitude, increased oxygen demands during aerobic exercise, and erythrocyte loss from bleeding or disease. Renal failure results in loss of EPO production and anemia, impairing oxygen delivery and potentially becoming life-threatening. Search terms: vitamin D, calcidiol, calcitriol, hydroxylation, calcium (Ca++), phosphate, EPO, erythropoietin, erythropoiesis, bone marrow, kidney, liver, altitude, aerobic exercise, renal failure, anemia.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (351, 133, 'Collecting Ducts and Recovery of Water', 'Collecting Ducts and Recovery of Water', 'The collecting ducts contain principal cells and intercalated cells that regulate urine volume, osmolarity, and blood pH through solute and water transport. Principal cells possess channels for sodium and potassium recovery/loss and express receptors for ADH (vasopressin) and aldosterone; ADH stimulates insertion of aquaporin water channels into the apical membrane, enabling osmotic water reabsorption that concentrates urine and regulates blood osmolarity and blood pressure, while aldosterone stimulates manufacture of Na+/K+ channels and Na+/K+ ATPase pumps on the basal membrane to increase Na+ recovery, with water following passively and K+ being secreted in proportion to Na+ recovery. Intercalated cells secrete or absorb acid and bicarbonate; specifically, they reabsorb K+ and HCO3– while secreting H+, lowering plasma acidity and increasing urine acidity. ADH is released by the posterior pituitary in response to osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus detecting elevated plasma osmolarity, while aldosterone is secreted by the adrenal cortex in response to angiotensin II stimulation; angiotensin II functions as a vasoconstrictor to immediately increase blood pressure and stimulates aldosterone production for longer-lasting blood pressure support through vascular volume maintenance. Symport channels move Na+ and Cl– together, and the Na+/K+ ATPase pumps Na+ out while pumping K+ into the forming urine in the opposite direction.', 1);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (352, 133, 'Mechanisms of Recovery', 'Mechanisms of Recovery', 'This subsection describes five membrane transport mechanisms—active transport, diffusion, facilitated diffusion, secondary active transport, and osmosis—that enable tubular reabsorption and secretion in the kidney. Active transport uses ATP energy to move substances against concentration gradients and requires specific membrane receptors; the Na+/K+ pump exemplifies this by moving Na+ out and K+ into cells in opposite directions. Symport and antiport mechanisms involve two substances sharing a channel protein, with symport moving substances in the same direction and antiport in opposite directions; secondary active transport occurs when active transport powers another substance''s movement, as exemplified by glucose reabsorption via Na+/glucose symport on the luminal surface coupled with Na+/K+ ATPases on the basal membrane. The kidneys reabsorb Ca++, Na+, glucose, and amino acids to maintain homeostatic plasma concentrations, while secreting urea, K+, ammonia, creatinine, and drugs as waste; acid–base balance is maintained as the lungs eliminate H+ and the kidneys secrete or reabsorb H+ and HCO3–. ADH induces insertion of urea transporters and aquaporin channel proteins, and quantitative 24-hour filtration and reabsorption data show near-complete recovery of glucose, proteins, and bicarbonate versus partial recovery of water, sodium, and potassium, with creatinine undergoing no reabsorption.', 2);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (353, 133, 'Reabsorption and Secretion in the Distal Convoluted Tubule', 'Reabsorption and Secretion in the Distal Convoluted Tubule', 'This subsection covers reabsorption and secretion mechanisms in the distal convoluted tubule (DCT) and collecting duct. Key structures and processes include: the DCT recovers 10–15 percent of filtered water before urine enters the collecting ducts; aldosterone increases Na+/K+ ATPase in the basal membrane of the DCT and collecting duct; Na+ movement out of the lumen creates a negative charge that promotes Cl– movement across tight junctions via paracellular route into the interstitial space; peritubular capillaries receive solutes and water, returning them to circulation. Ca++ reabsorption involves parathyroid hormone (PTH) receptors in DCT cells that, when bound to PTH, induce insertion of calcium channels on the luminal surface to enhance Ca++ recovery; the electrochemical gradient from Na+ pumping attracts Ca++ into the cell; and calcitriol (1,25 dihydroxyvitamin D) induces calcium-binding proteins that transport Ca++ into the cell and aid exocytosis across the basolateral membrane. Search terms: distal convoluted tubule, collecting duct, aldosterone, Na+/K+ ATPase, sodium reabsorption, chloride reabsorption, calcium reabsorption, parathyroid hormone, PTH receptors, calcium channels, calcitriol, vitamin D, calcium-binding proteins, peritubular capillaries.', 3);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (354, 133, 'Reabsorption and Secretion in the Loop of Henle', 'Reabsorption and Secretion in the Loop of Henle', '# Summary: Reabsorption and Secretion in the Loop of Henle

This subsection introduces the **loop of Henle** (consisting of thick and thin descending and ascending sections), **vasa recta**, and the **countercurrent multiplier system** that enable recovery of sodium and water filtered by the glomerulus. The **descending limb** contains **simple squamous epithelial cells** with **aquaporin channel proteins** that permit osmosis, allowing water reabsorption (up to 15% of filtered water) as osmolarity increases from ~300 to ~1200 mOsmol/kg; the **thick ascending limb** is lined with **simple cuboidal epithelium** lacking aquaporin proteins and is impermeable to water, but actively reabsorbs **Na⁺** and **Cl⁻** via **Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase pumps** in the basal membrane and **Na⁺/Cl⁻ symporters** in the apical membrane, creating a hyposmotic filtrate (~50–100 mOsmol/L) while establishing a hyperosmotic medullary interstitium. **Leaky tight junctions** between ascending loop cells allow paracellular movement of solutes; **K⁺** returns to the lumen via leaky channels, leaving a negatively charged interstitium that attracts cations (Na⁺, K⁺, Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺) paracellularly. The subsection explains how **urea** (converted from ammonia by liver hepatocytes) freely diffuses into the descending loop and is reabsorbed at the **collecting duct**, and how **vasa recta** recover solutes and water by flowing slowly to preserve the osmolar gradient and prevent blood cell', 4);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO subsections(subsection_id, section_id, title, display_label, summary, subsection_order) VALUES (355, 133, 'Reabsorption and Secretion in the PCT', 'Reabsorption and Secretion in the PCT', 'This subsection introduces reabsorption and secretion mechanisms in the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT), the first site where forming urine is modified. Key structures include the PCT''s simple cuboidal endothelial cells with apical and basal surfaces, peritubular and vasa recta capillaries, and transport proteins including symporters, antiporters, aquaporins, and carbonic anhydrase. The subsection establishes that approximately 67% of water, sodium, and potassium; nearly 100% of glucose, amino acids, and vitamins; 50% of chloride; and variable quantities of calcium and magnesium are reabsorbed in the PCT and returned to circulation. Transport mechanisms include obligatory water reabsorption (water follows Na+ passively), Na+/glucose symport on the apical membrane coupled with facilitated diffusion on the basal membrane, Na+/K+ ATPase active exchange on the basal surface, and Na+/H+ antiport for bicarbonate recovery. Bicarbonate recovery depends on carbonic anhydrase catalyzing the conversion of HCO3– and H+ to CO2 and water in the lumen, with CO2 diffusing into cells where the reverse reaction regenerates HCO3– for cotransport with Na+ across the basal membrane; this process is vital for acid-base balance. Searchable entities: proximal convoluted tubule (PCT), reabsorption, secretion, obligatory water reabsorption, symport, antiport, active transport, facilitated diffusion, aquaporin, carbonic anhydrase, Na+/K+ ATPase, Na+/H+ antiporter, transport maximum (Tm), glycosuria, bicarbonate recovery, apical surface, basal surface.', 5);

